ew or significantly modified business-critical applications must undergo performance and load testing to validate capacity requirements and identify scaling characteristics under expected and peak loads.</p><p><strong>5.8 Supplier Capacity Management:</strong> For services dependent on third-party suppliers or cloud providers, contractual agreements (SLAs) must include explicit capacity and performance commitments. The responsible team shall monitor supplier performance against these commitments and manage supplier reviews to ensure capacity obligations are met.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Capacity Management activities shall be conducted in a manner that supports compliance with all relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, ensuring sufficient capacity to maintain data availability requirements stipulated in service level agreements (SLAs), data processing agreements (DPAs), and regulatory frameworks (e.g., data residency laws). The Capacity Management process shall provide documented evidence for audits demonstrating proactive control over resource availability, a key component of security and service continuity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Officer (CIO) / Head of IT:</strong> Ultimately accountable for the effectiveness of the Capacity Management process. Responsible for approving the annual Capacity Plan and allocating necessary budget and resources.</p><p><strong>Capacity Manager (Designated Role):</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day operation of the Capacity Management process. Duties include producing capacity reports, maintaining the Capacity Plan, analyzing trends, chairing capacity review meetings, and acting as the central point of contact for all capacity-related issues.</p><p><strong>IT Operations Teams:</strong> Responsible for the implementation of monitoring, collection of capacity data, responding to capacity alerts, and executing approved capacity-related changes (e.g., upgrades, scaling actions).</p><p><strong>Application and Service Owners:</strong> Responsible for providing business demand forecasts, participating in capacity impact assessments for changes, and validating that service capacity meets business requirements.</p><p><strong>Procurement/Finance:</strong> Responsible for facilitating the procurement of hardware, software, or cloud resources as specified in the approved Capacity Plan.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory for all individuals and systems within its scope. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The CIO, in conjunction with Information Security, is authorized to monitor compliance and enforce this policy. Deliberate circumvention of capacity controls or willful neglect of capacity warnings that lead to a service outage shall be treated as a serious security and operational incident.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Capacity Management Policy shall be reviewed by the CIO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the business model, technology landscape, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version, revision date, and change history shall be meticulously maintained.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.403385,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 20:03:58.67,NULL
8,3,Incident Response Policy,"<h2>Purpose</h2><p>This Incident Response Policy establishes the formal authority, structure, and mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, respond to, contain, eradicate, and recover from information security incidents in a timely, effective, and legally compliant manner. The policy mandates a consistent, documented approach to minimize operational, financial, reputational, and legal impact, and to ensure continuous improvement of the organization's security posture.</p>

<h2>Scope</h2><p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, entrusted to, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to data, systems, networks, applications, and physical facilities. It is binding for all personnel (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents) who access, use, or manage these assets. The policy governs all phases of incident response, from preparation and detection through post-incident review.</p>

<h2>Definitions</h2><ul><li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> Any attempted or actual unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, or destruction of information, or interference with system operations in an information system, constituting a violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies, acceptable use policies, or standard security practices.</li><li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A confirmed incident where sensitive, protected, or confidential data has been accessed, disclosed, or exfiltrated in an unauthorized manner.</li><li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The formally designated, cross-functional team responsible for managing the response to a security incident.</li><li><strong>Containment:</strong> The immediate actions taken to limit the scope and impact of an incident.</li><li><strong>Forensic Evidence:</strong> Data collected and preserved in a legally admissible manner to support incident analysis, recovery, and potential legal proceedings.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to formal assignment and initiation of response actions.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Resolve (MTTR):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to closure, including full containment, eradication, and recovery.</li></ul>

<h2>Policy Statement</h2><p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive, structured, and continuous approach to information security incident management. All personnel shall immediately report suspected or confirmed security incidents in accordance with this policy. The organization shall maintain a formal, tested Incident Response Plan (IRP) and dedicate appropriate resources to ensure rapid detection, effective response, thorough remediation, and organizational learning from all incidents.</p>

<h2>Policy Requirements</h2><h3>5.1 Incident Response Plan (IRP) Maintenance</h3><p>A comprehensive, written Incident Response Plan (IRP) shall be developed, approved by the CISO, and maintained by the Information Security team. The IRP must be reviewed and updated at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in infrastructure, or regulatory update. The IRP shall be made available to all members of the Incident Response Team and key stakeholders.</p><h3>5.2 Incident Classification and Prioritization</h3><p>All detected incidents shall be classified according to a predefined severity matrix based on impact criteria (e.g., data sensitivity, system criticality, financial loss, regulatory impact) and urgency. Severity levels (e.g., Critical, High, Medium, Low) shall dictate escalation paths, response timelines, and resource allocation. Classification must occur within the MTTA target defined in the IRP.</p><h3>5.3 Detection and Reporting</h3><p>All personnel are required to report any suspected security incident immediately to the designated Security Operations Center (SOC) or via the approved incident reporting channel. Automated detection systems (SIEM, IDS/IPS, EDR) shall be configured to generate alerts for anomalous activity. Logs from all critical systems must be centrally collected and retained for a minimum of 90 days to facilitate detection and investigation.</p><h3>5.4 Response and Containment Procedures</h3><p>Upon declaration of an incident, the IRT shall execute predefined containment strategies to isolate affected systems and prevent further damage. Containment actions may include network segmentation, disabling accounts, taking systems offline, or applying firewall rules. All containment actions must be documented, and their potential business impact assessed prior to execution where feasible.</p><h3>5.5 Eradication, Recovery, and Forensics</h3><p>Following containment, the root cause of the incident shall be identified and eradicated. This may involve removing malware, patching vulnerabilities, or reconfiguring systems. Recovery shall restore systems and data from known-good, validated backups. A forensic evidence chain of custody shall be maintained for all incidents classified as High or Critical. Forensic images of affected systems must be created prior to eradication if legally or investigatively required.</p><h3>5.6 Communication and Notification</h3><p>Internal and external communications during an incident shall follow strict protocols defined in the IRP. Unauthorized public communication is strictly prohibited. Legal and Compliance teams must be engaged immediately to determine if regulatory, contractual, or legal notification obligations (e.g., to data subjects, regulators, partners) are triggered. All notifications must be documented.</p><h3>5.7 Post-Incident Analysis and Reporting</h3><p>A formal Post-Incident Review (PIR) report shall be completed for every High or Critical severity incident, and for Medium severity incidents at the CISO's discretion. The report must include a timeline, root cause analysis, impact assessment, effectiveness of response actions, and specific corrective actions to prevent recurrence. Lessons learned shall be integrated into security controls, policies, and training programs.</p><h3>5.8 Training and Testing</h3><p>All members of the IRT shall receive annual role-specific training on incident response procedures. A tabletop exercise simulating a significant security incident shall be conducted at least annually. A full-scale technical simulation or red team exercise shall be conducted at least biennially. Results from all tests and exercises shall be used to update the IRP.</p>

<h2>Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2><p>All incident response activities shall be conducted in compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, state laws), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and contractual SLAs. Evidence preservation shall adhere to legal standards for admissibility. The Legal Department must be consulted prior to any external communications regarding an incident that may have legal or regulatory implications.</p>

<h2>Roles and Responsibilities</h2><p><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provides ultimate authority and resources for the IRP; is informed of Critical incidents.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the IRP; declares major incidents; chairs the IRT; approves final PIR reports.</p><p><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Lead:</strong> Manages the tactical response during an incident; coordinates team activities; serves as primary point of contact.</p><p><strong>Information Security Analysts:</strong> Execute technical investigation, containment, eradication, and forensic analysis.</p><p><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Implements containment and recovery actions on infrastructure; provides system expertise.</p><p><strong>Legal & Compliance:</strong> Advises on regulatory notification requirements, legal risks, and evidence handling.</p><p><strong>Corporate Communications / PR:</strong> Manages all external and internal stakeholder communications as directed by the IRT Lead and Legal.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete security awareness training including incident reporting procedures; are required to report suspected incidents immediately.</p>

<h2>Enforcement</h2><p>Compliance with this Incident Response Policy is mandatory for all covered individuals and entities. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and potential legal liability. Failure to report a suspected incident, or interference with an incident response investigation, is considered a serious violation.</p>

<h2>Review and Revision</h2><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, legal and regulatory requirements, technology infrastructure, or lessons learned from incident response activities. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company's policy management system.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Purpose</h2><p>This Incident Response Policy establishes the formal authority, structure, and mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, respond to, contain, eradicate, and recover from information security incidents in a timely, effective, and legally compliant manner. The policy mandates a consistent, documented approach to minimize operational, financial, reputational, and legal impact, and to ensure continuous improvement of the organization's security posture.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Scope</h2><p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, entrusted to, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to data, systems, networks, applications, and physical facilities. It is binding for all personnel (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents) who access, use, or manage these assets. The policy governs all phases of incident response, from preparation and detection through post-incident review.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Definitions</h2><ul><li><strong>Security Incident:</strong> Any attempted or actual unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, or destruction of information, or interference with system operations in an information system, constituting a violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies, acceptable use policies, or standard security practices.</li><li><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A confirmed incident where sensitive, protected, or confidential data has been accessed, disclosed, or exfiltrated in an unauthorized manner.</li><li><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT):</strong> The formally designated, cross-functional team responsible for managing the response to a security incident.</li><li><strong>Containment:</strong> The immediate actions taken to limit the scope and impact of an incident.</li><li><strong>Forensic Evidence:</strong> Data collected and preserved in a legally admissible manner to support incident analysis, recovery, and potential legal proceedings.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Acknowledge (MTTA):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to formal assignment and initiation of response actions.</li><li><strong>Mean Time to Resolve (MTTR):</strong> The target maximum elapsed time from incident detection to closure, including full containment, eradication, and recovery.</li></ul>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Policy Statement</h2><p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive, structured, and continuous approach to information security incident management. All personnel shall immediately report suspected or confirmed security incidents in accordance with this policy. The organization shall maintain a formal, tested Incident Response Plan (IRP) and dedicate appropriate resources to ensure rapid detection, effective response, thorough remediation, and organizational learning from all incidents.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Policy Requirements</h2><h3>5.1 Incident Response Plan (IRP) Maintenance</h3><p>A comprehensive, written Incident Response Plan (IRP) shall be developed, approved by the CISO, and maintained by the Information Security team. The IRP must be reviewed and updated at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in infrastructure, or regulatory update. The IRP shall be made available to all members of the Incident Response Team and key stakeholders.</p><h3>5.2 Incident Classification and Prioritization</h3><p>All detected incidents shall be classified according to a predefined severity matrix based on impact criteria (e.g., data sensitivity, system criticality, financial loss, regulatory impact) and urgency. Severity levels (e.g., Critical, High, Medium, Low) shall dictate escalation paths, response timelines, and resource allocation. Classification must occur within the MTTA target defined in the IRP.</p><h3>5.3 Detection and Reporting</h3><p>All personnel are required to report any suspected security incident immediately to the designated Security Operations Center (SOC) or via the approved incident reporting channel. Automated detection systems (SIEM, IDS/IPS, EDR) shall be configured to generate alerts for anomalous activity. Logs from all critical systems must be centrally collected and retained for a minimum of 90 days to facilitate detection and investigation.</p><h3>5.4 Response and Containment Procedures</h3><p>Upon declaration of an incident, the IRT shall execute predefined containment strategies to isolate affected systems and prevent further damage. Containment actions may include network segmentation, disabling accounts, taking systems offline, or applying firewall rules. All containment actions must be documented, and their potential business impact assessed prior to execution where feasible.</p><h3>5.5 Eradication, Recovery, and Forensics</h3><p>Following containment, the root cause of the incident shall be identified and eradicated. This may involve removing malware, patching vulnerabilities, or reconfiguring systems. Recovery shall restore systems and data from known-good, validated backups. A forensic evidence chain of custody shall be maintained for all incidents classified as High or Critical. Forensic images of affected systems must be created prior to eradication if legally or investigatively required.</p><h3>5.6 Communication and Notification</h3><p>Internal and external communications during an incident shall follow strict protocols defined in the IRP. Unauthorized public communication is strictly prohibited. Legal and Compliance teams must be engaged immediately to determine if regulatory, contractual, or legal notification obligations (e.g., to data subjects, regulators, partners) are triggered. All notifications must be documented.</p><h3>5.7 Post-Incident Analysis and Reporting</h3><p>A formal Post-Incident Review (PIR) report shall be completed for every High or Critical severity incident, and for Medium severity incidents at the CISO's discretion. The report must include a timeline, root cause analysis, impact assessment, effectiveness of response actions, and specific corrective actions to prevent recurrence. Lessons learned shall be integrated into security controls, policies, and training programs.</p><h3>5.8 Training and Testing</h3><p>All members of the IRT shall receive annual role-specific training on incident response procedures. A tabletop exercise simulating a significant security incident shall be conducted at least annually. A full-scale technical simulation or red team exercise shall be conducted at least biennially. Results from all tests and exercises shall be used to update the IRP.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Compliance and Legal Requirements</h2><p>All incident response activities shall be conducted in compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, state laws), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS), and contractual SLAs. Evidence preservation shall adhere to legal standards for admissibility. The Legal Department must be consulted prior to any external communications regarding an incident that may have legal or regulatory implications.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Roles and Responsibilities</h2><p><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provides ultimate authority and resources for the IRP; is informed of Critical incidents.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the IRP; declares major incidents; chairs the IRT; approves final PIR reports.</p><p><strong>Incident Response Team (IRT) Lead:</strong> Manages the tactical response during an incident; coordinates team activities; serves as primary point of contact.</p><p><strong>Information Security Analysts:</strong> Execute technical investigation, containment, eradication, and forensic analysis.</p><p><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Implements containment and recovery actions on infrastructure; provides system expertise.</p><p><strong>Legal & Compliance:</strong> Advises on regulatory notification requirements, legal risks, and evidence handling.</p><p><strong>Corporate Communications / PR:</strong> Manages all external and internal stakeholder communications as directed by the IRT Lead and Legal.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete security awareness training including incident reporting procedures; are required to report suspected incidents immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Enforcement</h2><p>Compliance with this Incident Response Policy is mandatory for all covered individuals and entities. Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and potential legal liability. Failure to report a suspected incident, or interference with an incident response investigation, is considered a serious violation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<h2>Review and Revision</h2><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Governance Committee at least annually. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, legal and regulatory requirements, technology infrastructure, or lessons learned from incident response activities. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company's policy management system.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:54.71159,"""[\""ISO 27001 / SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 20:04:38.515,NULL
35,38635,Firewall Configuration Policy,"<p>This Firewall Configuration Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure design, implementation, management, and monitoring of all firewall systems at [Company Name]. The policy enforces a defense-in-depth architecture to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets and network infrastructure from unauthorized access, misuse, or compromise.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all network firewall devices, virtual firewalls, cloud-native firewall services (e.g., AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs, GCP Firewall Rules), web application firewalls (WAFs), and next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) deployed within or protecting any [Company Name] network environment, including corporate data centers, cloud platforms, and branch offices. It encompasses all personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers responsible for the configuration, administration, or management of these systems.</p>

<p><strong>Firewall:</strong> A network security system that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user or process is granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform its function.<br><strong>Baseline Configuration:</strong> A standardized, secure configuration template for a specific type of firewall, against which all operational configurations must be assessed.<br><strong>Change Management:</strong> A formal process for controlling modifications to firewall rules, configurations, and firmware to ensure they are reviewed, approved, documented, and tested.<br><strong>Implicit Deny:</strong> A firewall rule that blocks all traffic not explicitly permitted by preceding allow rules.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all firewall configurations shall enforce a principle of least privilege and a default-deny posture. All rule changes must follow a formal change management process. Unauthorized modification, bypassing, or disabling of firewall protections is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Baseline Configuration & Hardening</strong><br>All firewalls shall be deployed with a company-approved, hardened baseline configuration. This baseline must disable all unnecessary services, use strong, unique administrative credentials, and implement role-based access control (RBAC) for management interfaces. Default passwords and accounts shall be changed immediately upon installation.</p><p><strong>5.2 Rule Set Management & Least Privilege</strong><br>Firewall rule sets shall be reviewed and pruned quarterly to remove obsolete rules. All rules must have a documented business justification, owner, and expiration date. Rules shall be as specific as possible, specifying source IP, destination IP, destination port, and protocol. The use of ""ANY"" in source, destination, or service fields is forbidden unless explicitly justified and approved by the CISO.</p><p><strong>5.3 Default-Deny Posture & Rule Order</strong><br>All firewalls must implement an implicit deny rule as the final rule in every rule base. Inbound internet traffic shall be restricted to only authorized services published through a DMZ architecture. Direct inbound connections from the internet to internal networks are prohibited.</p><p><strong>5.4 Change Management & Documentation</strong><br>All modifications to firewall rules, policies, or firmware must be submitted through the formal IT Change Management process. Changes require pre-implementation review, risk assessment, and approval from the Network Security team. A complete audit trail, including the requester, approver, date, time, and reason for change, must be maintained for a minimum of seven years.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Alerting</strong><br>All firewalls shall be configured to generate detailed logs for all allowed and denied traffic, administrative access, and configuration changes. Logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts shall be configured for critical security events, including but not limited to: policy modification, login failures, and detected intrusion attempts.</p><p><strong>5.6 Segmentation & Zone Defense</strong><br>Network segmentation shall be enforced using firewalls to create security zones (e.g., DMZ, Internal, Management, Production). Traffic between zones of differing trust levels must traverse a firewall and be explicitly permitted by rule. Direct network pathways that bypass firewall inspection are strictly forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.7 Vulnerability & Patch Management</strong><br>Firewall operating systems and firmware shall be included in the enterprise vulnerability management program. Critical and high-severity security patches must be applied within the timeframe defined by the corporate Patch Management Policy, following approved change procedures.</p><p><strong>5.8 Cryptographic Controls for Management</strong><br>All administrative access to firewalls (including GUI, CLI, and API) must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Insecure management protocols such as Telnet and HTTP are prohibited.</p>

<p>Firewall configurations shall be designed and maintained to meet the control requirements of ISO 27001 (A.13.1 Network Security Management), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6, CC6.7), and PCI DSS (Requirements 1.1 - 1.4). Regular audits and evidence collection (e.g., rule sets, change logs, review reports) shall be performed to demonstrate compliance with these frameworks and any applicable data protection regulations.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, enforcement, and overall firewall security posture.<br><strong>Network Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the operational implementation, daily management, rule review, logging, and incident response related to firewalls.<br><strong>System & Cloud Administrators:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy when configuring virtual or cloud-native firewall components within their environments.<br><strong>IT Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for reviewing and approving all firewall change requests as part of the formal change management process.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance and conduct periodic technical audits of firewall configurations. Non-compliant configurations will be documented, and responsible parties will be required to remediate findings within a mandated timeframe.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made available to all relevant personnel.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Firewall Configuration Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure design, implementation, management, and monitoring of all firewall systems at [Company Name]. The policy enforces a defense-in-depth architecture to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets and network infrastructure from unauthorized access, misuse, or compromise.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all network firewall devices, virtual firewalls, cloud-native firewall services (e.g., AWS Security Groups, Azure NSGs, GCP Firewall Rules), web application firewalls (WAFs), and next-generation firewalls (NGFWs) deployed within or protecting any [Company Name] network environment, including corporate data centers, cloud platforms, and branch offices. It encompasses all personnel, contractors, and third-party service providers responsible for the configuration, administration, or management of these systems.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Firewall:</strong> A network security system that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> A security concept in which a user or process is granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform its function.<br><strong>Baseline Configuration:</strong> A standardized, secure configuration template for a specific type of firewall, against which all operational configurations must be assessed.<br><strong>Change Management:</strong> A formal process for controlling modifications to firewall rules, configurations, and firmware to ensure they are reviewed, approved, documented, and tested.<br><strong>Implicit Deny:</strong> A firewall rule that blocks all traffic not explicitly permitted by preceding allow rules.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all firewall configurations shall enforce a principle of least privilege and a default-deny posture. All rule changes must follow a formal change management process. Unauthorized modification, bypassing, or disabling of firewall protections is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Baseline Configuration & Hardening</strong><br>All firewalls shall be deployed with a company-approved, hardened baseline configuration. This baseline must disable all unnecessary services, use strong, unique administrative credentials, and implement role-based access control (RBAC) for management interfaces. Default passwords and accounts shall be changed immediately upon installation.</p><p><strong>5.2 Rule Set Management & Least Privilege</strong><br>Firewall rule sets shall be reviewed and pruned quarterly to remove obsolete rules. All rules must have a documented business justification, owner, and expiration date. Rules shall be as specific as possible, specifying source IP, destination IP, destination port, and protocol. The use of \\\""ANY\\\"" in source, destination, or service fields is forbidden unless explicitly justified and approved by the CISO.</p><p><strong>5.3 Default-Deny Posture & Rule Order</strong><br>All firewalls must implement an implicit deny rule as the final rule in every rule base. Inbound internet traffic shall be restricted to only authorized services published through a DMZ architecture. Direct inbound connections from the internet to internal networks are prohibited.</p><p><strong>5.4 Change Management & Documentation</strong><br>All modifications to firewall rules, policies, or firmware must be submitted through the formal IT Change Management process. Changes require pre-implementation review, risk assessment, and approval from the Network Security team. A complete audit trail, including the requester, approver, date, time, and reason for change, must be maintained for a minimum of seven years.</p><p><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring & Alerting</strong><br>All firewalls shall be configured to generate detailed logs for all allowed and denied traffic, administrative access, and configuration changes. Logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts shall be configured for critical security events, including but not limited to: policy modification, login failures, and detected intrusion attempts.</p><p><strong>5.6 Segmentation & Zone Defense</strong><br>Network segmentation shall be enforced using firewalls to create security zones (e.g., DMZ, Internal, Management, Production). Traffic between zones of differing trust levels must traverse a firewall and be explicitly permitted by rule. Direct network pathways that bypass firewall inspection are strictly forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.7 Vulnerability & Patch Management</strong><br>Firewall operating systems and firmware shall be included in the enterprise vulnerability management program. Critical and high-severity security patches must be applied within the timeframe defined by the corporate Patch Management Policy, following approved change procedures.</p><p><strong>5.8 Cryptographic Controls for Management</strong><br>All administrative access to firewalls (including GUI, CLI, and API) must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Insecure management protocols such as Telnet and HTTP are prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Firewall configurations shall be designed and maintained to meet the control requirements of ISO 27001 (A.13.1 Network Security Management), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6, CC6.7), and PCI DSS (Requirements 1.1 - 1.4). Regular audits and evidence collection (e.g., rule sets, change logs, review reports) shall be performed to demonstrate compliance with these frameworks and any applicable data protection regulations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, enforcement, and overall firewall security posture.<br><strong>Network Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the operational implementation, daily management, rule review, logging, and incident response related to firewalls.<br><strong>System & Cloud Administrators:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy when configuring virtual or cloud-native firewall components within their environments.<br><strong>IT Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for reviewing and approving all firewall change requests as part of the formal change management process.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance and conduct periodic technical audits of firewall configurations. Non-compliant configurations will be documented, and responsible parties will be required to remediate findings within a mandated timeframe.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made available to all relevant personnel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.92538,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""PCI DSS\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 20:05:10.158,NULL
33,507965,Password Policy,"<p>This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of authentication credentials at [Company Name]. Its primary objective is to safeguard information assets by ensuring that passwords are robust, confidential, and resistant to unauthorized access, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel, including but not limited to employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who access, create, or manage any [Company Name] information system, network, application, database, or data repository. This policy governs all authentication credentials, including but not limited to user passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, and API keys, irrespective of the system's location (on-premises, cloud, or hybrid).</p>

<p><strong>Password:</strong> A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used to authenticate identity and authorize access to information resources.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Password Manager:</strong> A software application designed to store and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault.</p><p><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application or system to interact with other systems or resources.</p><p><strong>Password Hash:</strong> A cryptographically scrambled representation of a password, generated via a one-way function.</p><p><strong>Brute-Force Attack:</strong> An attempt to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, and protected according to the stringent requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or compromised passwords is strictly prohibited. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls to maintain the security posture of the organization.</p>

<h4>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h4><p>All user-created passwords must meet the following minimum complexity requirements:</p><ul><li><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> 14 characters for standard user accounts; 20 characters for privileged administrative accounts.</li><li><strong>Character Diversity:</strong> Must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: uppercase letters (A-Z), lowercase letters (a-z), numerals (0-9), and special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & *).</li><li><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must not contain the user's account name, common dictionary words, simple patterns (e.g., ""123456"", ""qwerty""), or repetitive characters (e.g., ""aaaaaa"").</li></ul><h4>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h4><p>The following controls govern password creation, change, and expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial Provisioning:</strong> All initial passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. The user must change this password upon first login.</li><li><strong>Password Expiration:</strong> Standard user passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Privileged account passwords must be changed at least every 60 days. Service account passwords shall be rotated at least every 180 days or immediately upon personnel change.</li><li><strong>Password History:</strong> Systems shall enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent reuse.</li></ul><h4>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h4><p>All passwords and secrets shall be protected according to the following technical mandates:</p><ul><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2 with sufficient iteration counts).</li><li><strong>Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels (e.g., TLS 1.2 or higher).</li><li><strong>Local Storage Prohibition:</strong> Writing down passwords on paper, storing them in unencrypted files (e.g., text files, spreadsheets), or saving them in browser password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution is strictly forbidden.</li></ul><h4>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h4><p>Authentication systems shall implement the following controls:</p><ul><li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all remote network access (VPN), access to cloud administration portals, and for all users with privileged access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators).</li><li><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts, the user account shall be automatically locked for a minimum period of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and alerted to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</li><li><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall automatically terminate after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically secure and invalidated upon logout.</li></ul><h4>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h4><p>Technical systems must be configured to enforce this policy:</p><ul><li><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system where technically feasible.</li><li><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> Automated tools shall be deployed to periodically scan for and alert on the use of default, weak, or shared passwords, particularly for service accounts.</li><li><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events (successes, failures, password changes, account lockouts) must be logged with sufficient detail (timestamp, source IP, user ID) and retained for a minimum of 90 days for analysis and audit purposes.</li></ul>

<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with the following frameworks and regulations:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), and A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights).</li><li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Security (CC6.1 - Logical Access), Availability, and Confidentiality criteria pertaining to access controls and authentication.</li><li><strong>PCI DSS v4.0:</strong> Requirements 8.3 (Secure all individual non-console administrative access and all remote access using MFA) and 8.3.6 (Minimum password complexity and strength).</li><li><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Article 32 (Security of Processing), mandating appropriate technical measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk.</li></ul><p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and legal liability for [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, monitoring, and technical enforcement of this policy. This includes configuring IAM systems and reviewing authentication logs.</p><p><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all systems, servers, and applications under their management.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding, adhering to, and reporting any suspected violations of this policy. This includes creating strong passwords, safeguarding them, and using MFA where required.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for ensuring policy acknowledgment is part of the onboarding and offboarding processes.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and potential legal action. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disable accounts found in violation of this policy to mitigate risk. All violations shall be documented and reported in accordance with the Incident Response Policy.</p>

<p>This Password Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in compliance landscape, or major technological shift. Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for formal approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Password Policy establishes the mandatory technical and administrative controls for the creation, management, protection, and secure disposal of authentication credentials at [Company Name]. Its primary objective is to safeguard information assets by ensuring that passwords are robust, confidential, and resistant to unauthorized access, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel, including but not limited to employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who access, create, or manage any [Company Name] information system, network, application, database, or data repository. This policy governs all authentication credentials, including but not limited to user passwords, service account passwords, application secrets, and API keys, irrespective of the system's location (on-premises, cloud, or hybrid).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Password:</strong> A confidential sequence of characters, known only to the user and the system, used to authenticate identity and authorize access to information resources.</p><p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.</p><p><strong>Password Manager:</strong> A software application designed to store and manage online credentials in an encrypted vault.</p><p><strong>Service Account:</strong> A non-human account used by an application or system to interact with other systems or resources.</p><p><strong>Password Hash:</strong> A cryptographically scrambled representation of a password, generated via a one-way function.</p><p><strong>Brute-Force Attack:</strong> An attempt to discover a password by systematically trying every possible combination of characters.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all authentication credentials shall be created, managed, and protected according to the stringent requirements defined in this policy. The use of weak, default, shared, or compromised passwords is strictly prohibited. All personnel are accountable for adhering to these controls to maintain the security posture of the organization.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Password Composition and Complexity</h4><p>All user-created passwords must meet the following minimum complexity requirements:</p><ul><li><strong>Minimum Length:</strong> 14 characters for standard user accounts; 20 characters for privileged administrative accounts.</li><li><strong>Character Diversity:</strong> Must include characters from at least three of the following four categories: uppercase letters (A-Z), lowercase letters (a-z), numerals (0-9), and special characters (e.g., ! @ # $ % ^ & *).</li><li><strong>Prohibited Elements:</strong> Passwords must not contain the user's account name, common dictionary words, simple patterns (e.g., \\\""123456\\\"", \\\""qwerty\\\""), or repetitive characters (e.g., \\\""aaaaaa\\\"").</li></ul><h4>5.2 Password Lifecycle Management</h4><p>The following controls govern password creation, change, and expiration:</p><ul><li><strong>Initial Provisioning:</strong> All initial passwords shall be system-generated, unique, and transmitted via a secure, out-of-band method. The user must change this password upon first login.</li><li><strong>Password Expiration:</strong> Standard user passwords must be changed at least every 90 days. Privileged account passwords must be changed at least every 60 days. Service account passwords shall be rotated at least every 180 days or immediately upon personnel change.</li><li><strong>Password History:</strong> Systems shall enforce a password history of at least the previous 10 passwords to prevent reuse.</li></ul><h4>5.3 Password Protection and Storage</h4><p>All passwords and secrets shall be protected according to the following technical mandates:</p><ul><li><strong>Storage:</strong> Passwords shall never be stored in plaintext. All passwords at rest must be hashed using a strong, industry-approved cryptographic hashing algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2 with sufficient iteration counts).</li><li><strong>Transmission:</strong> Passwords shall only be transmitted over encrypted channels (e.g., TLS 1.2 or higher).</li><li><strong>Local Storage Prohibition:</strong> Writing down passwords on paper, storing them in unencrypted files (e.g., text files, spreadsheets), or saving them in browser password managers not integrated with the corporate-approved solution is strictly forbidden.</li></ul><h4>5.4 Authentication and Access Controls</h4><p>Authentication systems shall implement the following controls:</p><ul><li><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> MFA is mandatory for all remote network access (VPN), access to cloud administration portals, and for all users with privileged access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators).</li><li><strong>Account Lockout:</strong> After 5 consecutive failed authentication attempts, the user account shall be automatically locked for a minimum period of 15 minutes. Lockout events must be logged and alerted to the Security Operations Center (SOC).</li><li><strong>Session Management:</strong> Interactive user sessions shall automatically terminate after 15 minutes of inactivity. Session tokens must be cryptographically secure and invalidated upon logout.</li></ul><h4>5.5 Administrative and Technical Enforcement</h4><p>Technical systems must be configured to enforce this policy:</p><ul><li><strong>Centralized Identity Management:</strong> All authentication shall be managed through a centralized Identity and Access Management (IAM) system where technically feasible.</li><li><strong>Automated Compliance Checking:</strong> Automated tools shall be deployed to periodically scan for and alert on the use of default, weak, or shared passwords, particularly for service accounts.</li><li><strong>Logging and Monitoring:</strong> All authentication events (successes, failures, password changes, account lockouts) must be logged with sufficient detail (timestamp, source IP, user ID) and retained for a minimum of 90 days for analysis and audit purposes.</li></ul>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with the following frameworks and regulations:</p><ul><li><strong>ISO 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.15 (Identity Management), A.5.16 (Identity, Authentication & Access), A.5.17 (Authentication Information), and A.8.2 (Privileged Access Rights).</li><li><strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria:</strong> Security (CC6.1 - Logical Access), Availability, and Confidentiality criteria pertaining to access controls and authentication.</li><li><strong>PCI DSS v4.0:</strong> Requirements 8.3 (Secure all individual non-console administrative access and all remote access using MFA) and 8.3.6 (Minimum password complexity and strength).</li><li><strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Article 32 (Security of Processing), mandating appropriate technical measures to ensure a level of security appropriate to the risk.</li></ul><p>Non-compliance with this policy may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and legal liability for [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.</p><p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, monitoring, and technical enforcement of this policy. This includes configuring IAM systems and reviewing authentication logs.</p><p><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing the technical controls specified in Section 5 across all systems, servers, and applications under their management.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding, adhering to, and reporting any suspected violations of this policy. This includes creating strong passwords, safeguarding them, and using MFA where required.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for ensuring policy acknowledgment is part of the onboarding and offboarding processes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and potential legal action. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disable accounts found in violation of this policy to mitigate risk. All violations shall be documented and reported in accordance with the Incident Response Policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Password Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following any significant security incident, change in compliance landscape, or major technological shift. Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for formal approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.719889,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""PCI DSS\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 20:05:50.697,NULL
34,775612,Wireless Security Policy,"<p>This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store [Company Name] information assets. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications, in alignment with [Company Name]'s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>

<p>This policy applies universally across [Company Name], encompassing all subsidiaries, business units, and physical locations. It governs all individuals (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties), all corporate-owned or managed wireless infrastructure (e.g., access points, controllers, bridges), and all devices (corporate-issued or personal) that connect to [Company Name] wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc, tethering) to access corporate resources. This includes, but is not limited to, Wi-Fi (802.11 standards), Bluetooth, cellular data modems, and any emerging wireless technologies used for business purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):</strong> A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant devices to connect to a wired network.<br><br><strong>Enterprise Wireless Network:</strong> The centrally managed, authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed by [Company Name] IT for general corporate use.<br><br><strong>Guest Wireless Network:</strong> A segregated and controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, contractors, or other non-employees, with strictly limited access to corporate resources.<br><br><strong>Rogue Access Point:</strong> Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without explicit authorization from the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br><br><strong>WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):</strong> The current mandatory security certification program for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks, utilizing 256-bit encryption and 802.1X/EAP for authentication.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies is a privilege granted under strict controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving [Company Name] data or systems must be encrypted, authenticated, and monitored. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure or the circumvention of security controls is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Authorization and Architecture</strong><br>5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, controllers, and management systems, must be approved, procured, and deployed solely by the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br>5.1.2 A formal network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure, including coverage areas, channels, and power settings, shall be maintained and reviewed annually.<br>5.1.3 The implementation of personal wireless access points, wireless routers, or ad-hoc wireless networks within corporate facilities is expressly forbidden.<br><br><strong>5.2 Encryption and Authentication Standards</strong><br>5.2.1 All enterprise wireless networks shall utilize WPA3-Enterprise with AES-256-GCM encryption. Transitional use of WPA2-Enterprise is permitted only for legacy device support and must be documented with a risk-accepted migration plan.<br>5.2.2 The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for any corporate purpose is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Authentication for the enterprise network must leverage a centralized identity provider (e.g., RADIUS server) integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory, Azure AD) using 802.1X/EAP-TLS or EAP-PEAP.<br>5.2.4 Guest network access shall be secured via a captive portal with unique, time-limited credentials or a separate PSK that is rotated at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Device Configuration and Hardening</strong><br>5.3.1 All authorized access points shall have default administrative credentials changed, unnecessary services disabled, and firmware updated to vendor-supported versions within 30 days of release for critical security patches.<br>5.3.2 Service Set Identifiers (SSIDs) shall not contain information identifying [Company Name], its location, or function (e.g., ""[Company Name]_Finance"").<br>5.3.3 Wireless signal strength shall be tuned to minimize propagation beyond physical premises boundaries where feasible.<br><br><strong>5.4 Monitoring and Intrusion Detection</strong><br>5.4.1 A Wireless Intrusion Detection/Prevention System (WIDS/WIPS) shall be deployed to continuously monitor the radio frequency (RF) spectrum for rogue devices, misconfigured clients, and attack patterns.<br>5.4.2 All authentication successes and failures, as well as administrative access to wireless infrastructure, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system with a minimum retention period of 90 days.<br>5.4.3 The IT Department shall conduct periodic wireless vulnerability assessments and penetration tests at least annually to identify security weaknesses.<br><br><strong>5.5 Endpoint and Client Security</strong><br>5.5.1 Corporate-managed devices must have wireless interfaces configured via a Mobile Device Management (MDM) or equivalent policy to automatically connect only to approved [Company Name] SSIDs.<br>5.5.2 Users are prohibited from configuring corporate devices to connect to untrusted or public wireless networks for accessing sensitive corporate data unless using a mandated, company-provided VPN.<br>5.5.3 The use of personal devices (BYOD) to connect to the enterprise wireless network requires enrollment in the BYOD program, which mandates device health checks and containerization of corporate data.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to support compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). Specific controls within this policy directly address ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, particularly A.8.1 (Asset Management), A.8.2 (Information Classification), A.8.9 (Wireless Access Control), A.8.12 (Data Leakage Prevention), A.8.15 (Logging), and A.8.16 (Monitoring). For SOC 2, this policy supports the Trust Services Criteria of Security (CC6.1, CC6.3, CC6.6, CC6.7) and Confidentiality. Deviations from this policy for business necessity require a formal risk assessment and documented exception approved by the CISO.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Bears ultimate responsibility for the approval, oversight, and enforcement of this policy.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, maintenance, and monitoring of all authorized wireless infrastructure. This includes managing the WIDS/WIPS, applying patches, and responding to incidents.<br><br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for policy review, conducting security assessments, analyzing WIDS/WIPS alerts, and investigating policy violations.<br><br><strong>All Users:</strong> Required to comply with all aspects of this policy, use only authorized wireless networks for business, and report any suspected security incidents or unauthorized wireless devices immediately.</p>

<p>Compliance with this policy will be enforced through a combination of technical controls, automated monitoring, and administrative procedures. Violations of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately disconnect any unauthorized wireless device or network posing a security risk. All users shall be required to formally acknowledge receipt and understanding of this policy upon hire and annually thereafter.</p>

<p>This Wireless Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in threat landscape, or updates to relevant standards (ISO 27001, NIST, etc.). Proposed revisions must be submitted to the CISO for approval. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the corporate policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Wireless Security Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure deployment, configuration, management, and monitoring of all wireless networks and devices that access, transmit, or store [Company Name] information assets. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems from threats inherent in wireless communications, in alignment with [Company Name]'s overall Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally across [Company Name], encompassing all subsidiaries, business units, and physical locations. It governs all individuals (employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties), all corporate-owned or managed wireless infrastructure (e.g., access points, controllers, bridges), and all devices (corporate-issued or personal) that connect to [Company Name] wireless networks or that create wireless connections (e.g., ad-hoc, tethering) to access corporate resources. This includes, but is not limited to, Wi-Fi (802.11 standards), Bluetooth, cellular data modems, and any emerging wireless technologies used for business purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Access Point (AP) / Wireless Access Point (WAP):</strong> A networking hardware device that allows Wi-Fi compliant devices to connect to a wired network.<br><br><strong>Enterprise Wireless Network:</strong> The centrally managed, authorized wireless network infrastructure deployed by [Company Name] IT for general corporate use.<br><br><strong>Guest Wireless Network:</strong> A segregated and controlled wireless network provisioned for visitors, contractors, or other non-employees, with strictly limited access to corporate resources.<br><br><strong>Rogue Access Point:</strong> Any wireless access point installed on the corporate network or within corporate facilities without explicit authorization from the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br><br><strong>WPA3-Enterprise (Wi-Fi Protected Access 3 Enterprise):</strong> The current mandatory security certification program for securing enterprise-grade Wi-Fi networks, utilizing 256-bit encryption and 802.1X/EAP for authentication.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all wireless network access serving corporate functions shall be implemented and maintained with security as the paramount design principle. The use of wireless technologies is a privilege granted under strict controls to mitigate risks of unauthorized access, data interception, and network compromise. All wireless communications involving [Company Name] data or systems must be encrypted, authenticated, and monitored. The deployment of unauthorized wireless infrastructure or the circumvention of security controls is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Authorization and Architecture</strong><br>5.1.1 All wireless infrastructure, including access points, controllers, and management systems, must be approved, procured, and deployed solely by the Information Technology (IT) Department.<br>5.1.2 A formal network architecture diagram detailing all wireless infrastructure, including coverage areas, channels, and power settings, shal