 processing initiation. For Sensitive Personal Data, an additional condition under applicable law must be satisfied. Reliance on consent requires a clear, affirmative action; pre-ticked boxes or inactivity does not constitute valid consent.</p><h4>5.2 Data Collection and Purpose Limitation</h4><p>Personal Data shall be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in a manner incompatible with those purposes. At the point of collection, Data Subjects shall be provided with a privacy notice containing all information required by law, including the identity of the controller, purposes of processing, legal basis, data retention periods, and Data Subject rights.</p><h4>5.3 Data Minimization and Accuracy</h4><p>Collected Personal Data shall be adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary for the stated purposes. Procedures shall be established to ensure Personal Data is kept accurate and, where necessary, up to date. Inaccurate data shall be rectified or erased without undue delay.</p><h4>5.4 Data Subject Rights Management</h4><p>A formal process shall be implemented to facilitate and log Data Subject rights requests (access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection). All requests shall be acknowledged within the timeframe mandated by law (e.g., one month under GDPR) and fulfilled using secure, verifiable methods. The ability to fulfill these rights shall be engineered into system design where feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Data Security and Confidentiality</h4><p>Personal Data shall be protected against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage. This mandate requires the implementation of encryption for data at rest (using AES-256 or equivalent) and in transit (using TLS 1.2 or higher), strict access controls based on the principle of least privilege, comprehensive logging of access and modifications, and regular security testing of applications and infrastructure handling Personal Data.</p><h4>5.6 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h4><p>Personal Data shall not be kept in a form which permits identification of Data Subjects for longer than is necessary for the purposes for which it is processed. A formal data retention schedule, mapping data types to specific retention periods and legal justifications, shall be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data shall be securely and irreversibly destroyed using cryptographic erasure for electronic media or cross-cut shredding for physical media.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Cross-Border Data Transfers</h4><p>Any transfer of Personal Data to a third party (Processor or Controller) shall be governed by a written agreement that imposes data protection obligations equivalent to those in this policy. Transfers of Personal Data outside the jurisdiction of origin shall only occur if an approved transfer mechanism (e.g., Standard Contractual Clauses, Binding Corporate Rules, or an Adequacy Decision) is in place and documented prior to the transfer.</p><h4>5.8 Data Protection by Design and by Default</h4><p>Data protection measures shall be integrated into the development of business processes and information systems from the initial design stage (Privacy by Design). Default system settings shall ensure that, by default, only Personal Data necessary for each specific purpose is processed (Privacy by Default). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for any high-risk processing activity prior to its commencement.</p><h4>5.9 Breach Notification</h4><p>A documented incident response plan specific to data breaches shall be maintained. In the event of a suspected or confirmed breach of Personal Data, the DPO/Privacy Officer must be notified immediately. The organization shall, without undue delay and in accordance with legal timeframes (e.g., 72 hours under GDPR), assess the breach and, where required by law, notify the relevant supervisory authority and affected Data Subjects.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection frameworks, including but not limited to the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA)/California Privacy Rights Act (CPRA), and other applicable global, federal, and state regulations. Adherence to the control requirements of SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria for Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, and Privacy) and the Annex A controls of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 is mandated. All Personnel are responsible for understanding and complying with the legal obligations relevant to their role.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO)/Privacy Officer:</strong> Oversees policy implementation, conducts DPIAs, serves as the point of contact for supervisory authorities and Data Subjects, and provides mandatory privacy training. The DPO shall report directly to the highest level of management.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical and organizational security measures required by this policy, including encryption, access controls, and logging infrastructure.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must complete annual privacy and security training, process Personal Data only in accordance with authorized procedures, and immediately report any suspected policy violation or data breach to the DPO or their manager.</p><p><strong>Heads of Business Units:</strong> Accountable for ensuring privacy controls are integrated into their departmental processes and for authorizing the lawful basis for processing activities under their purview.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Violations of this policy will result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to legal prosecution. The DPO and CISO are authorized to suspend any processing activity deemed non-compliant until corrective actions are verified. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued engagement with [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the DPO and CISO, or more frequently in response to significant changes in law, technology, business operations, or the risk environment. All revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The current version of this policy shall be made readily available to all Personnel and, where appropriate, to Data Subjects.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:57.352899,"""[\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 20:07:09.749,NULL
12,7,Risk Management Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Risk Management Policy is to establish a formal, structured, and continuous process for identifying, assessing, treating, monitoring, and communicating information security risks across [Company Name]. This policy mandates a systematic approach to safeguard the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets, ensure business continuity, and provide a foundation for informed decision-making. It is designed to fulfill the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (Clause 6.1) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Risk Assessment).</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to hardware, software, data, networks, and personnel. It is binding for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third parties who access, manage, or support [Company Name]'s information systems and processes. The risk management process shall encompass all organizational activities, business processes, and technological environments.</p>

<p><strong>Risk:</strong> The effect of uncertainty on objectives, expressed in terms of likelihood and consequence of an information security event.<br><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The overall process of risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process to modify risk through risk avoidance, mitigation, transfer, or acceptance.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The level of risk remaining after risk treatment measures have been applied.<br><strong>Risk Owner:</strong> The individual or entity with the accountability and authority to manage a specific risk.<br><strong>Inherent Risk:</strong> The level of risk before any controls are applied.<br><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The amount and type of risk that [Company Name] is willing to pursue or retain.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, documented Information Security Risk Management process. This process shall be integrated into all significant business decisions and operational activities. All identified risks shall be formally assessed, treated according to established criteria, and continuously monitored. Risk acceptance decisions shall be made only by authorized personnel and shall be formally documented. Failure to adhere to this policy and its associated procedures is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Risk Assessment Framework</h4><p>A formal risk assessment methodology, approved by the CISO, shall be documented and utilized. This methodology must define scales for likelihood, impact, and risk level calculation (e.g., 5x5 matrix). Risk assessments shall be conducted at planned intervals and upon significant changes to the organization, assets, or threat landscape.</p><h4>5.2 Risk Identification</h4><p>All information assets shall be inventoried and classified. Threats and vulnerabilities relevant to these assets shall be identified using sources including, but not limited to, internal audits, threat intelligence feeds, vulnerability scans, and incident reports. This process shall consider internal and external context as defined in ISO 27001.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Analysis and Evaluation</h4><p>Identified risks shall be analyzed to determine their inherent likelihood and impact on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Risks shall be evaluated against the organization's defined risk acceptance criteria and risk appetite. All risks evaluated above the acceptance threshold shall be documented in the Risk Register.</p><h4>5.4 Risk Treatment</h4><p>For each risk requiring treatment, a risk treatment plan shall be documented. Treatment options must be selected from: a) <strong>Mitigate</strong> (implementing security controls), b) <strong>Transfer</strong> (e.g., cyber insurance), c) <strong>Avoid</strong> (discontinuing the activity), or d) <strong>Accept</strong> (formal, documented acceptance by the risk owner and CISO). The selection of controls to mitigate risk shall be justified and mapped to the Statement of Applicability (SoA).</p><h4>5.5 Risk Register Maintenance</h4><p>A centralized Risk Register shall be maintained. Each entry shall include, at minimum: risk description, asset(s) affected, inherent risk rating, risk owner, treatment plan, target residual risk, current status, and review date. The Risk Register shall be a controlled document with access restricted to authorized personnel.</p><h4>5.6 Residual Risk and Acceptance</h4><p>Following the implementation of treatment plans, the residual risk level shall be re-assessed. Any decision to accept residual risk that remains above the defined risk appetite must be formally documented, including justification, and approved by the CISO and the relevant business executive. Such acceptances shall be reviewed at least annually.</p><h4>5.7 Monitoring and Review</h4><p>The risk environment and the effectiveness of implemented controls shall be monitored continuously. The entire Risk Management process and the Risk Register shall be formally reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant incidents or changes. Results of these reviews shall be reported to senior management.</p>

<p>The Risk Management process shall be designed to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to information security and data protection (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable). Risk assessments must explicitly consider compliance failures as a source of risk with significant financial and reputational impact. Evidence of the risk management process (assessments, treatment plans, review records) shall be maintained to demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 requirements for audits.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Senior Management:</strong> Establish and approve the organization's risk appetite and provide overall governance and resources for the risk management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns and administers this policy, defines the risk methodology, maintains the Risk Register, and reports on risk status. Final authority for operational risk acceptance.<br><strong>Risk Owners (Business/System Owners):</strong> Responsible for identifying risks within their domain, implementing approved treatment plans, and managing accepted risks.<br><strong>Information Technology & Security Teams:</strong> Responsible for executing technical risk assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans), implementing technical controls, and providing subject matter expertise.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report identified security risks or weaknesses to their manager or through the designated incident reporting channel.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Deliberate failure to report significant risks, or the falsification of risk assessment data, is considered gross misconduct. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance with this policy and conduct periodic reviews of risk management activities.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Risk Management Policy is to establish a formal, structured, and continuous process for identifying, assessing, treating, monitoring, and communicating information security risks across [Company Name]. This policy mandates a systematic approach to safeguard the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets, ensure business continuity, and provide a foundation for informed decision-making. It is designed to fulfill the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (Clause 6.1) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Risk Assessment).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including but not limited to hardware, software, data, networks, and personnel. It is binding for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third parties who access, manage, or support [Company Name]'s information systems and processes. The risk management process shall encompass all organizational activities, business processes, and technological environments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Risk:</strong> The effect of uncertainty on objectives, expressed in terms of likelihood and consequence of an information security event.<br><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The overall process of risk identification, analysis, and evaluation.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process to modify risk through risk avoidance, mitigation, transfer, or acceptance.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The level of risk remaining after risk treatment measures have been applied.<br><strong>Risk Owner:</strong> The individual or entity with the accountability and authority to manage a specific risk.<br><strong>Inherent Risk:</strong> The level of risk before any controls are applied.<br><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The amount and type of risk that [Company Name] is willing to pursue or retain.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, documented Information Security Risk Management process. This process shall be integrated into all significant business decisions and operational activities. All identified risks shall be formally assessed, treated according to established criteria, and continuously monitored. Risk acceptance decisions shall be made only by authorized personnel and shall be formally documented. Failure to adhere to this policy and its associated procedures is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Risk Assessment Framework</h4><p>A formal risk assessment methodology, approved by the CISO, shall be documented and utilized. This methodology must define scales for likelihood, impact, and risk level calculation (e.g., 5x5 matrix). Risk assessments shall be conducted at planned intervals and upon significant changes to the organization, assets, or threat landscape.</p><h4>5.2 Risk Identification</h4><p>All information assets shall be inventoried and classified. Threats and vulnerabilities relevant to these assets shall be identified using sources including, but not limited to, internal audits, threat intelligence feeds, vulnerability scans, and incident reports. This process shall consider internal and external context as defined in ISO 27001.</p><h4>5.3 Risk Analysis and Evaluation</h4><p>Identified risks shall be analyzed to determine their inherent likelihood and impact on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Risks shall be evaluated against the organization's defined risk acceptance criteria and risk appetite. All risks evaluated above the acceptance threshold shall be documented in the Risk Register.</p><h4>5.4 Risk Treatment</h4><p>For each risk requiring treatment, a risk treatment plan shall be documented. Treatment options must be selected from: a) <strong>Mitigate</strong> (implementing security controls), b) <strong>Transfer</strong> (e.g., cyber insurance), c) <strong>Avoid</strong> (discontinuing the activity), or d) <strong>Accept</strong> (formal, documented acceptance by the risk owner and CISO). The selection of controls to mitigate risk shall be justified and mapped to the Statement of Applicability (SoA).</p><h4>5.5 Risk Register Maintenance</h4><p>A centralized Risk Register shall be maintained. Each entry shall include, at minimum: risk description, asset(s) affected, inherent risk rating, risk owner, treatment plan, target residual risk, current status, and review date. The Risk Register shall be a controlled document with access restricted to authorized personnel.</p><h4>5.6 Residual Risk and Acceptance</h4><p>Following the implementation of treatment plans, the residual risk level shall be re-assessed. Any decision to accept residual risk that remains above the defined risk appetite must be formally documented, including justification, and approved by the CISO and the relevant business executive. Such acceptances shall be reviewed at least annually.</p><h4>5.7 Monitoring and Review</h4><p>The risk environment and the effectiveness of implemented controls shall be monitored continuously. The entire Risk Management process and the Risk Register shall be formally reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant incidents or changes. Results of these reviews shall be reported to senior management.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The Risk Management process shall be designed to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to information security and data protection (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable). Risk assessments must explicitly consider compliance failures as a source of risk with significant financial and reputational impact. Evidence of the risk management process (assessments, treatment plans, review records) shall be maintained to demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 requirements for audits.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Board of Directors / Senior Management:</strong> Establish and approve the organization's risk appetite and provide overall governance and resources for the risk management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns and administers this policy, defines the risk methodology, maintains the Risk Register, and reports on risk status. Final authority for operational risk acceptance.<br><strong>Risk Owners (Business/System Owners):</strong> Responsible for identifying risks within their domain, implementing approved treatment plans, and managing accepted risks.<br><strong>Information Technology & Security Teams:</strong> Responsible for executing technical risk assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans), implementing technical controls, and providing subject matter expertise.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report identified security risks or weaknesses to their manager or through the designated incident reporting channel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Deliberate failure to report significant risks, or the falsification of risk assessment data, is considered gross misconduct. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor compliance with this policy and conduct periodic reviews of risk management activities.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:55.645013,"""[\""ISO 27001 / SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:41:19.196,NULL
14,9,Human Resources Security Policy,"<p>This Human Resources Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and procedures for managing personnel throughout the employment lifecycle at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets by mitigating risks associated with human factors, ensuring compliance with contractual and regulatory obligations, and embedding security responsibilities into all roles.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all individuals who have, or will have, access to [Company Name] information assets, information processing facilities, or sensitive data. This includes, but is not limited to: full-time and part-time employees, contractors, temporary staff, consultants, interns, and third-party personnel (""Covered Personnel""). The policy governs all phases of the employment relationship: pre-employment, active employment, and termination or change of employment.</p>

<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service owned or used by [Company Name] that has value to the organization.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that any user, process, or program shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its function.<br/><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of separating critical functions among different individuals to reduce the risk of error or fraud.<br/><strong>Formal Sanction Process:</strong> A documented disciplinary procedure for personnel who violate security policies.<br/><strong>Confidentiality Agreement:</strong> A legally binding contract wherein the signee agrees to protect confidential information.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that security responsibilities shall be integrated into all human resource management practices. All Covered Personnel shall be subject to defined security controls prior to, during, and upon termination of their engagement to ensure they are suitable for the roles they are assigned, understand their security obligations, and are prevented from causing malicious or accidental damage to information assets.</p>

<h4>5.1 Pre-Employment Screening</h4><p>All candidates for employment, contractors, and third-party users shall undergo background verification checks commensurate with the business requirements, the classification of information to be accessed, and perceived risks. Checks must include, at a minimum: verification of academic and professional qualifications, validation of employment history for the past three years, and identity verification. For roles with access to highly sensitive systems or data, additional checks such as credit history or enhanced criminal record checks shall be conducted with candidate consent and in accordance with applicable laws.</p><h4>5.2 Terms and Conditions of Employment</h4><p>The contractual terms and conditions for all Covered Personnel shall explicitly state their and [Company Name]'s responsibilities for information security. This shall include: agreement to comply with all applicable security policies and procedures, a legally enforceable confidentiality or non-disclosure agreement (NDA), and acknowledgment that access rights are granted based on the principle of least privilege and may be monitored in accordance with the law.</p><h4>5.3 Management Responsibilities</h4><p>Department managers and project supervisors are responsible for: defining and documenting the security roles and responsibilities for their staff; ensuring personnel understand their security duties before granting access to systems or data; and applying the principles of least privilege and segregation of duties in the design of all roles and access profiles.</p><h4>5.4 Information Security Awareness, Education, and Training</h4><p>All Covered Personnel shall receive formal information security awareness training upon induction and annually thereafter. Training content must be role-specific and cover: acceptable use of assets, password hygiene, incident reporting procedures, clear desk/clear screen policy, secure remote working, and social engineering threats. Completion of training shall be recorded and monitored for compliance.</p><h4>5.5 Disciplinary Process</h4><p>A formal disciplinary process shall be established and communicated to address security breaches committed by personnel. The process shall provide for graduated sanctions, up to and including termination of employment and legal action, and shall be applied consistently and fairly in all cases of policy violation.</p><h4>5.6 Termination or Change of Employment</h4><p>Upon termination of employment or change of job responsibilities, the following controls shall be enforced: all access rights to information assets, premises, and systems shall be revoked immediately, synchronously with the HR notification; all company-owned physical and logical assets (e.g., laptops, mobile devices, ID badges, access tokens, software licenses) shall be returned and accounted for; and a formal exit interview shall be conducted to reiterate ongoing confidentiality obligations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5 (Information Security Policies), A.6 (Organization of Information Security), A.7 (Human Resource Security), and A.13 (Communications Security). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. All personnel screening and disciplinary actions shall be carried out in full compliance with relevant labor laws, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), and privacy statutes.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Responsible for executing pre-employment screening, managing the contractual process including NDAs, facilitating security training, and initiating access revocation upon termination in coordination with IT.<br/><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and de-provisioning access rights based on authorized requests from HR and management, in accordance with least privilege.<br/><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for defining role-based security requirements, ensuring their team's compliance with this policy, and reporting security incidents involving their personnel.<br/><strong>All Covered Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and adhering to all aspects of this policy and associated procedures.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and potential legal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately suspend or revoke system access for any individual suspected of a serious policy violation pending investigation. Monitoring of system access and user activity will be conducted to ensure compliance with this policy.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the HR Director at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in business operations, technology, or regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Covered Personnel through the company's official policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Human Resources Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and procedures for managing personnel throughout the employment lifecycle at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets by mitigating risks associated with human factors, ensuring compliance with contractual and regulatory obligations, and embedding security responsibilities into all roles.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all individuals who have, or will have, access to [Company Name] information assets, information processing facilities, or sensitive data. This includes, but is not limited to: full-time and part-time employees, contractors, temporary staff, consultants, interns, and third-party personnel (\\\""Covered Personnel\\\""). The policy governs all phases of the employment relationship: pre-employment, active employment, and termination or change of employment.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service owned or used by [Company Name] that has value to the organization.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that any user, process, or program shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform its function.<br/><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of separating critical functions among different individuals to reduce the risk of error or fraud.<br/><strong>Formal Sanction Process:</strong> A documented disciplinary procedure for personnel who violate security policies.<br/><strong>Confidentiality Agreement:</strong> A legally binding contract wherein the signee agrees to protect confidential information.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that security responsibilities shall be integrated into all human resource management practices. All Covered Personnel shall be subject to defined security controls prior to, during, and upon termination of their engagement to ensure they are suitable for the roles they are assigned, understand their security obligations, and are prevented from causing malicious or accidental damage to information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Pre-Employment Screening</h4><p>All candidates for employment, contractors, and third-party users shall undergo background verification checks commensurate with the business requirements, the classification of information to be accessed, and perceived risks. Checks must include, at a minimum: verification of academic and professional qualifications, validation of employment history for the past three years, and identity verification. For roles with access to highly sensitive systems or data, additional checks such as credit history or enhanced criminal record checks shall be conducted with candidate consent and in accordance with applicable laws.</p><h4>5.2 Terms and Conditions of Employment</h4><p>The contractual terms and conditions for all Covered Personnel shall explicitly state their and [Company Name]'s responsibilities for information security. This shall include: agreement to comply with all applicable security policies and procedures, a legally enforceable confidentiality or non-disclosure agreement (NDA), and acknowledgment that access rights are granted based on the principle of least privilege and may be monitored in accordance with the law.</p><h4>5.3 Management Responsibilities</h4><p>Department managers and project supervisors are responsible for: defining and documenting the security roles and responsibilities for their staff; ensuring personnel understand their security duties before granting access to systems or data; and applying the principles of least privilege and segregation of duties in the design of all roles and access profiles.</p><h4>5.4 Information Security Awareness, Education, and Training</h4><p>All Covered Personnel shall receive formal information security awareness training upon induction and annually thereafter. Training content must be role-specific and cover: acceptable use of assets, password hygiene, incident reporting procedures, clear desk/clear screen policy, secure remote working, and social engineering threats. Completion of training shall be recorded and monitored for compliance.</p><h4>5.5 Disciplinary Process</h4><p>A formal disciplinary process shall be established and communicated to address security breaches committed by personnel. The process shall provide for graduated sanctions, up to and including termination of employment and legal action, and shall be applied consistently and fairly in all cases of policy violation.</p><h4>5.6 Termination or Change of Employment</h4><p>Upon termination of employment or change of job responsibilities, the following controls shall be enforced: all access rights to information assets, premises, and systems shall be revoked immediately, synchronously with the HR notification; all company-owned physical and logical assets (e.g., laptops, mobile devices, ID badges, access tokens, software licenses) shall be returned and accounted for; and a formal exit interview shall be conducted to reiterate ongoing confidentiality obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls, specifically A.5 (Information Security Policies), A.6 (Organization of Information Security), A.7 (Human Resource Security), and A.13 (Communications Security). It also aligns with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. All personnel screening and disciplinary actions shall be carried out in full compliance with relevant labor laws, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), and privacy statutes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the approval, implementation, and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Responsible for executing pre-employment screening, managing the contractual process including NDAs, facilitating security training, and initiating access revocation upon termination in coordination with IT.<br/><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and de-provisioning access rights based on authorized requests from HR and management, in accordance with least privilege.<br/><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for defining role-based security requirements, ensuring their team's compliance with this policy, and reporting security incidents involving their personnel.<br/><strong>All Covered Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and adhering to all aspects of this policy and associated procedures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and potential legal liability. The IT Department is authorized to immediately suspend or revoke system access for any individual suspected of a serious policy violation pending investigation. Monitoring of system access and user activity will be conducted to ensure compliance with this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the HR Director at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in business operations, technology, or regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Covered Personnel through the company's official policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:56.064036,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:41:50.681,NULL
22,30001,Data Classification Policy,"<p>This Data Classification Policy establishes the framework for categorizing [Company Name] information assets based on their sensitivity, value, and criticality to the organization. The purpose is to ensure that information receives an appropriate level of protection in accordance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, thereby safeguarding confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format (e.g., electronic, physical, verbal) or location (e.g., on-premises, cloud, third-party). It is mandatory for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel who create, access, handle, store, or transmit [Company Name] data.</p>

<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Data whose unauthorized disclosure could cause severe or catastrophic damage to [Company Name], including but not limited to trade secrets, source code, pre-release financial data, and certain personal data. This classification aligns with ISO 27001 'confidential' and SOC 2 'restricted' categories.<br><br><strong>Internal Use Information:</strong> Data intended for use within [Company Name] and authorized third parties. Unauthorized disclosure could cause moderate risk or inconvenience. Examples include internal policies, non-sensitive project plans, and operational procedures.<br><br><strong>Public Information:</strong> Data explicitly approved for public release. Unauthorized disclosure poses no material risk to [Company Name]. Examples include marketing materials and published press releases.<br><br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, or repository that stores, processes, or transmits information valuable to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business executive or department head accountable for a specific information asset, responsible for defining its classification and protection requirements.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be classified according to their sensitivity and business impact. Protection controls shall be implemented commensurate with the assigned classification level. Failure to classify and protect information in accordance with this policy is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Classification Levels and Criteria</strong><br>All information shall be classified into one of three levels:<br>• <strong>Confidential:</strong> Data protected by statute, regulation, or contract (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA); trade secrets; authentication credentials; and any data whose compromise could cause severe financial, reputational, or legal harm.<br>• <strong>Internal Use:</strong> Operational business data, internal communications, and information not intended for public disclosure but whose exposure would not cause severe harm.<br>• <strong>Public:</strong> Information explicitly approved for public dissemination.<br><br><strong>5.2 Classification and Labeling Procedures</strong><br>Data Owners shall formally classify information at the time of creation or significant modification. Electronic documents and data repositories shall be digitally labeled with the classification (e.g., via metadata headers, watermarking, or folder naming conventions). Physical media and hardcopy documents shall bear visible classification labels (e.g., stamps, headers).<br><br><strong>5.3 Handling and Storage Requirements</strong><br>• <strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent and in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. Storage is permitted only on approved, access-controlled systems. Physical copies must be stored in locked containers.<br>• <strong>Internal Use Data:</strong> Shall be stored on authorized [Company Name] managed systems. Encryption is recommended for data in transit across untrusted networks.<br>• <strong>Public Data:</strong> No specific storage encryption is required, but integrity controls must be applied to prevent unauthorized modification.<br><br><strong>5.4 Access Control and Authorization</strong><br>Access to information shall be granted on a need-to-know basis. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented for all systems storing Confidential or Internal Use data. Access reviews for Confidential data shall be conducted quarterly. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all access to systems housing Confidential data.<br><br><strong>5.5 Transmission and Sharing Controls</strong><br>The transmission of Confidential data over public networks is forbidden unless encrypted via approved enterprise VPN or TLS 1.2+. Sharing Confidential data with external parties requires a valid Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) and the use of approved secure file transfer mechanisms. Email of Confidential data is strictly prohibited unless the message and attachments are encrypted end-to-end.<br><br><strong>5.6 Retention and Disposal</strong><br>All data shall be retained in accordance with the Records Retention Schedule. Confidential data shall be disposed of using secure deletion methods that render data unrecoverable (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization for electronic data, cross-cut shredding for physical documents). Logs of disposal activities for Confidential data shall be maintained.<br><br><strong>5.7 Monitoring and Logging</strong><br>All access attempts, modifications, and transfers of Confidential data shall be logged. Logs shall include user identity, timestamp, action, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated and protected from tampering, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and reviewed for anomalies weekly.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with relevant frameworks and regulations, including but not limited to:<br>• <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.9 (Inventory of information and other associated assets), A.5.10 (Classification of information), A.5.12 (Classification of information), and A.8.2 (Information classification).<br>• <strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security):</strong> CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls), CC6.6 (Logical access security measures to protect against threats), and CC6.8 (Data classification).<br>• <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Principles of data minimization and integrity and confidentiality (Article 5).<br>• <strong>Other Jurisdictional Laws:</strong> All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to data protection laws applicable to the data they handle.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for this policy; oversees implementation and enforcement.<br><br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Responsible for classifying information assets under their purview; defining access requirements; and authorizing exceptions.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls (encryption, access management, logging) specified in Section 5.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to handle all [Company Name] information according to its classification; complete mandatory data handling training; and report any suspected mishandling or loss of data.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The IT Department is authorized to technically enforce controls (e.g., block unencrypted transfers, revoke access) to prevent policy violations. All violations shall be reported to the CISO and investigated per the Incident Response Policy.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Data Classification Policy establishes the framework for categorizing [Company Name] information assets based on their sensitivity, value, and criticality to the organization. The purpose is to ensure that information receives an appropriate level of protection in accordance with legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, thereby safeguarding confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format (e.g., electronic, physical, verbal) or location (e.g., on-premises, cloud, third-party). It is mandatory for all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel who create, access, handle, store, or transmit [Company Name] data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Data whose unauthorized disclosure could cause severe or catastrophic damage to [Company Name], including but not limited to trade secrets, source code, pre-release financial data, and certain personal data. This classification aligns with ISO 27001 'confidential' and SOC 2 'restricted' categories.<br><br><strong>Internal Use Information:</strong> Data intended for use within [Company Name] and authorized third parties. Unauthorized disclosure could cause moderate risk or inconvenience. Examples include internal policies, non-sensitive project plans, and operational procedures.<br><br><strong>Public Information:</strong> Data explicitly approved for public release. Unauthorized disclosure poses no material risk to [Company Name]. Examples include marketing materials and published press releases.<br><br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, or repository that stores, processes, or transmits information valuable to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Data Owner:</strong> The business executive or department head accountable for a specific information asset, responsible for defining its classification and protection requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be classified according to their sensitivity and business impact. Protection controls shall be implemented commensurate with the assigned classification level. Failure to classify and protect information in accordance with this policy is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Classification Levels and Criteria</strong><br>All information shall be classified into one of three levels:<br>• <strong>Confidential:</strong> Data protected by statute, regulation, or contract (e.g., GDPR, HIPAA); trade secrets; authentication credentials; and any data whose compromise could cause severe financial, reputational, or legal harm.<br>• <strong>Internal Use:</strong> Operational business data, internal communications, and information not intended for public disclosure but whose exposure would not cause severe harm.<br>• <strong>Public:</strong> Information explicitly approved for public dissemination.<br><br><strong>5.2 Classification and Labeling Procedures</strong><br>Data Owners shall formally classify information at the time of creation or significant modification. Electronic documents and data repositories shall be digitally labeled with the classification (e.g., via metadata headers, watermarking, or folder naming conventions). Physical media and hardcopy documents shall bear visible classification labels (e.g., stamps, headers).<br><br><strong>5.3 Handling and Storage Requirements</strong><br>• <strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent and in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher. Storage is permitted only on approved, access-controlled systems. Physical copies must be stored in locked containers.<br>• <strong>Internal Use Data:</strong> Shall be stored on authorized [Company Name] managed systems. Encryption is recommended for data in transit across untrusted networks.<br>• <strong>Public Data:</strong> No specific storage encryption is required, but integrity controls must be applied to prevent unauthorized modification.<br><br><strong>5.4 Access Control and Authorization</strong><br>Access to information shall be granted on a need-to-know basis. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented for all systems storing Confidential or Internal Use data. Access reviews for Confidential data shall be conducted quarterly. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all access to systems housing Confidential data.<br><br><strong>5.5 Transmission and Sharing Controls</strong><br>The transmission of Confidential data over public networks is forbidden unless encrypted via approved enterprise VPN or TLS 1.2+. Sharing Confidential data with external parties requires a valid Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) and the use of approved secure file transfer mechanisms. Email of Confidential data is strictly prohibited unless the message and attachments are encrypted end-to-end.<br><br><strong>5.6 Retention and Disposal</strong><br>All data shall be retained in accordance with the Records Retention Schedule. Confidential data shall be disposed of using secure deletion methods that render data unrecoverable (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization for electronic data, cross-cut shredding for physical documents). Logs of disposal activities for Confidential data shall be maintained.<br><br><strong>5.7 Monitoring and Logging</strong><br>All access attempts, modifications, and transfers of Confidential data shall be logged. Logs shall include user identity, timestamp, action, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated and protected from tampering, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and reviewed for anomalies weekly.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to align with and support compliance with relevant frameworks and regulations, including but not limited to:<br>• <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Controls A.5.9 (Inventory of information and other associated assets), A.5.10 (Classification of information), A.5.12 (Classification of information), and A.8.2 (Information classification).<br>• <strong>SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security):</strong> CC6.1 (Logical and physical access controls), CC6.6 (Logical access security measures to protect against threats), and CC6.8 (Data classification).<br>• <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Principles of data minimization and integrity and confidentiality (Article 5).<br>• <strong>Other Jurisdictional Laws:</strong> All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to data protection laws applicable to the data they handle.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for this policy; oversees implementation and enforcement.<br><br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Responsible for classifying information assets under their purview; defining access requirements; and authorizing exceptions.<br><br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical controls (encryption, access management, logging) specified in Section 5.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to handle all [Company Name] information according to its classification; complete mandatory data handling training; and report any suspected mishandling or loss of data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. The IT Department is authorized to technically enforce controls (e.g., block unencrypted transfers, revoke access) to prevent policy violations. All violations shall be reported to the CISO and investigated per the Incident Response Policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. Proposed revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:57.770065,"""[\""Both\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:42:25.922,NULL
23,60001,Mobile Device and BYOD Policy,"<p>This Mobile Device and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy establishes the mandatory security and management controls for all mobile devices that access, store, process, or transmit [Company Name] information assets. The purpose is to protect corporate data from unauthorized access, loss, or compromise while enabling secure productivity on approved mobile platforms, in alignment with [Company Name]'s risk management framework.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (""Users"") who use any mobile device to connect to [Company Name]'s internal networks, systems, applications, or data. It encompasses both company-provided mobile devices and personally owned devices (BYOD) used for business purposes. This policy covers all forms of mobile computing and communication devices including, but not limited to: smartphones, tablets, portable storage media (e.g., USB drives), and wearable technology with data connectivity.</p>

<p><strong>Mobile Device:</strong> Any portable computing device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting data wirelessly.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned mobile device authorized for limited business use.<br/><strong>Mobile Device Management (MDM):</strong> A centralized software platform used to enforce security policies, configure settings, and manage mobile devices.<br/><strong>Containerization:</strong> A security method that isolates corporate applications and data within a secure, encrypted container on a device.<br/><strong>Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM):</strong> An integrated suite of services, typically including MDM, Mobile Application Management (MAM), and identity management, for securing the mobile enterprise.<br/><strong>Rooted/Jailbroken Device:</strong> A device where the operating system's inherent security restrictions have been removed, compromising its integrity.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all mobile access to corporate resources shall be strictly controlled and secured. The use of mobile devices for business purposes is a privilege contingent upon full compliance with this policy. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, manage, and, if necessary, remotely wipe data from any device registered under this policy to protect corporate information assets.</p>

<h4>5.1 Device Registration and Management</h4><p>All devices used to access [Company Name] resources must be registered with and managed by the approved Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM) solution. Enrollment is mandatory prior to the first access attempt. Rooted or jailbroken devices are strictly prohibited from accessing any corporate resource.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication and Access Control</h4><p>Device-level authentication via a strong passcode, biometric lock, or PIN of at least six alphanumeric characters is mandatory. Inactivity lock must be configured to engage after a maximum of five minutes. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is required for all remote access to corporate email, VPNs, and cloud applications.</p><h4>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h4><p>Full-disk encryption (FDE) or file-based encryption must be enabled on all mobile devices storing [Company Name] data. For data in transit, TLS 1.2 or higher must be used. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted public Wi-Fi networks for transmitting corporate data is strictly forbidden; an approved corporate VPN must be utilized on untrusted networks.</p><h4>5.4 Application Security and Management</h4><p>Only applications vetted and provisioned through the official corporate application catalog or public app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store) may be installed on devices used for business. Side-loading of applications from unofficial sources is prohibited. Corporate data shall be confined within managed, encrypted application containers where technically feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Physical Security and Loss Procedures</h4><p>Users are personally responsible for the physical security of their mobile devices. Any loss, theft, or suspected compromise of a registered device must be reported to the Information Security Department immediately, triggering a potential remote wipe procedure.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring and Logging</h4><p>All access to corporate data from mobile devices is subject to logging and monitoring. The EMM/MDM solution shall maintain audit logs of device compliance status, security incidents, application installations, and data access patterns for a minimum of 90 days.</p><h4>5.7 BYOD-Specific Provisions</h4><p>For BYOD participants, a clear separation between personal and corporate data must be maintained, typically through containerization. [Company Name]'s right to perform a selective remote wipe of the corporate container and data only, upon separation or security incident, must be formally acknowledged by the user during enrollment.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.6.2.1, A.8.1.1, A.8.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Non-compliance may result in access revocation, disciplinary action, and legal liability for the user, particularly concerning data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) triggered by policy violations.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, EMM/MDM management, security monitoring, and incident response.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for technical implementation, device provisioning, and user support for the EMM/MDM platform.<br/><strong>Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, safeguarding devices, and reporting security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Responsible for enforcing policy compliance within their teams and authorizing business use of mobile devices.</p>

<p>Any user found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately and without prior notice disable access or initiate a remote wipe of any device deemed non-compliant or posing an imminent security risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology, or relevant regulations. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). The latest version of this policy is the only version that is in effect.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Mobile Device and Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Policy establishes the mandatory security and management controls for all mobile devices that access, store, process, or transmit [Company Name] information assets. The purpose is to protect corporate data from unauthorized access, loss, or compromise while enabling secure productivity on approved mobile platforms, in alignment with [Company Name]'s risk management framework.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (\\\""Users\\\"") who use any mobile device to connect to [Company Name]'s internal networks, systems, applications, or data. It encompasses both company-provided mobile devices and personally owned devices (BYOD) used for business purposes. This policy covers all forms of mobile computing and communication devices including, but not limited to: smartphones, tablets, portable storage media (e.g., USB drives), and wearable technology with data connectivity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Mobile Device:</strong> Any portable computing device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting data wirelessly.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned mobile device authorized for limited business use.<br/><strong>Mobile Device Management (MDM):</strong> A centralized software platform used to enforce security policies, configure settings, and manage mobile devices.<br/><strong>Containerization:</strong> A security method that isolates corporate applications and data within a secure, encrypted container on a device.<br/><strong>Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM):</strong> An integrated suite of services, typically including MDM, Mobile Application Management (MAM), and identity management, for securing the mobile enterprise.<br/><strong>Rooted/Jailbroken Device:</strong> A device where the operating system's inherent security restrictions have been removed, compromising its integrity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all mobile access to corporate resources shall be strictly controlled and secured. The use of mobile devices for business purposes is a privilege contingent upon full compliance with this policy. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, manage, and, if necessary, remotely wipe data from any device registered under this policy to protect corporate information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Device Registration and Management</h4><p>All devices used to access [Company Name] resources must be registered with and managed by the approved Enterprise Mobility Management (EMM) solution. Enrollment is mandatory prior to the first access attempt. Rooted or jailbroken devices are strictly prohibited from accessing any corporate resource.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication and Access Control</h4><p>Device-level authentication via a strong passcode, biometric lock, or PIN of at least six alphanumeric characters is mandatory. Inactivity lock must be configured to engage after a maximum of five minutes. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is required for all remote access to corporate email, VPNs, and cloud applications.</p><h4>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h4><p>Full-disk encryption (FDE) or file-based encryption must be enabled on all mobile devices storing [Company Name] data. For data in transit, TLS 1.2 or higher must be used. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted public Wi-Fi networks for transmitting corporate data is strictly forbidden; an approved corporate VPN must be utilized on untrusted networks.</p><h4>5.4 Application Security and Management</h4><p>Only applications vetted and provisioned through the official corporate application catalog or public app stores (Google Play, Apple App Store) may be installed on devices used for business. Side-loading of applications from unofficial sources is prohibited. Corporate data shall be confined within managed, encrypted application containers where technically feasible.</p><h4>5.5 Physical Security and Loss Procedures</h4><p>Users are personally responsible for the physical security of their mobile devices. Any loss, theft, or suspected compromise of a registered device must be reported to the Information Security Department immediately, triggering a potential remote wipe procedure.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring and Logging</h4><p>All access to corporate data from mobile devices is subject to logging and monitoring. The EMM/MDM solution shall maintain audit logs of device compliance status, security incidents, application installations, and data access patterns for a minimum of 90 days.</p><h4>5.7 BYOD-Specific Provisions</h4><p>For BYOD participants, a clear separation between personal and corporate data must be maintained, typically through containerization. [Company Name]'s right to perform a selective remote wipe of the corporate container and data only, upon separation or security incident, must be formally acknowledged by the user during enrollment.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.6.2.1, A.8.1.1, A.8.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality). Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Non-compliance may result in access revocation, disciplinary action, and legal liability for the user, particularly concerning data breach notification laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) triggered by policy violations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, EMM/MDM management, security monitoring, and incident response.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for technical implementation, device provisioning, and user support for the EMM/MDM platform.<br/><strong>Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, safeguarding devices, and reporting security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Responsible for enforcing policy compliance within their teams and authorizing business use of mobile devices.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any user found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately and without prior notice disable access or initiate a remote wipe of any device deemed non-compliant or posing an imminent security risk to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology, or relevant regulations. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). The latest version of this policy is the only version that is in effect.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:57.989549,"""[\""Both\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:42:56.379,NULL
28,593939,Backup and Recovery Policy,"<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for creating, managing, testing, and restoring backups of [Company Name]'s information assets. The purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in the event of data loss, corruption, system failure, or disaster, thereby supporting business continuity and compliance with regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, applications, databases, and data repositories owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or hosted by third-party service providers on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or higher, including but not limited to corporate data, customer data, employee data, and intellectual property. All personnel, contractors, and third parties with access to [Company Name] systems are bound by this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Backup:</strong> A copy of data and system configurations created and retained for the purpose of restoration.<br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.<br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable delay for restoring operations after an incident.<br><strong>Full Backup:</strong> A complete copy of all specified data.<br><strong>Incremental Backup:</strong> A copy of only the data that has changed since the last backup of any type.<br><strong>Differential Backup:</strong> A copy of data that has changed since the last full backup.<br><strong>Off-site Storage:</strong> A geographically separate location from the primary data center, used for storing backup media.<br><strong>Data Restoration:</strong> The process of returning data from a backup to an operational state.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a formal, risk-based backup and recovery program to protect its critical information assets. All systems handling data covered under this policy's scope shall have defined Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) and Recovery Time Objectives (RTO). The creation, storage, security, and testing of backups are strictly required activities. Unauthorized access to, modification of, or destruction of backup data is expressly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Backup Strategy and Scheduling</strong><br>All critical systems and data shall be backed up according to a documented schedule aligned with their business impact assessment. Full system backups must be performed at least weekly. Incremental or differential backups must be performed at least daily. Backup schedules and methods must be documented in system-specific runbooks.</p><p><strong>5.2 Data Coverage and Retention</strong><br>Backups must capture all application data, databases, system configurations, and security settings necessary for full restoration. Backup retention periods shall be defined based on legal, regulatory, and business requirements, but shall not be less than 30 days for operational recovery and 7 years for data subject to specific compliance mandates (e.g., HIPAA, financial regulations).</p><p><strong>5.3 Encryption and Security of Backup Data</strong><br>All backup data, whether in transit or at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys shall be managed separately from the encrypted backup data. Access to backup systems, management consoles, and restoration capabilities shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege and require multi-factor authentication (MFA).</p><p><strong>5.4 Storage and Geographic Separation</strong><br>A minimum of one copy of backup data must be stored in a geographically separate location (off-site) from the primary production data center to mitigate risks from local disasters. The integrity of off-site backups must be verified periodically. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for at least one backup copy is required to protect against ransomware and malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Backup Integrity and Monitoring</strong><br>Automated monitoring must be implemented to verify the success or failure of all backup jobs. Backup logs must be retained and reviewed regularly for anomalies. Integrity checks (e.g., checksum validation) must be performed on backup data sets at least monthly to ensure data is not corrupted and is recoverable.</p><p><strong>5.6 Restoration Testing and Validation</strong><br>A formal restoration testing program shall be executed. Full restoration tests for critical systems must be conducted at least annually. Test results, including achieved RTO and RPO, must be documented, reviewed by management, and used to refine procedures. The ability to restore individual files or data objects must be tested quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Cloud Services</strong><br>For data processed or stored by third-party providers (e.g., SaaS, IaaS), [Company Name] shall ensure contractual agreements mandate compliance with this policy's backup, encryption, and testing requirements. The company retains the right to audit or receive evidence of the provider's backup and recovery capabilities.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO 27001:2022 (A.5.29 Information security during disruption, A.8.13 Information backup, A.8.14 Redundancy of information processing facilities) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Availability, Confidentiality, Security). For environments processing Protected Health Information (PHI), backup procedures shall enforce the HIPAA Security Rule requirements for data backup, disaster recovery, and emergency mode operation plans (45 CFR § 164.308(a)(7)(ii)(A) & § 164.310(a)(2)(i)). All activities must comply with relevant data protection laws regarding the storage and cross-border transfer of backup data.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>IT Operations/System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the implementation, execution, and daily monitoring of backup jobs and infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Business Unit Heads:</strong> Responsible for defining the criticality (RPO/RTO) and legal retention requirements for their data.<br><strong>Compliance Officer:</strong> Responsible for ensuring backup and recovery processes meet applicable legal and regulatory frameworks.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for storing business-critical data only on systems covered by this policy and reporting any suspected data loss immediately.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of backup procedures, unauthorized restoration attempts, or tampering with backup data is considered a serious security incident and will be investigated accordingly. Third-party providers in violation of contractual obligations derived from this policy may be subject to penalties and contract termination.</p>

<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the IT environment, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders, and personnel shall be required to re-acknowledge the policy as stipulated by the company's security awareness program.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for creating, managing, testing, and restoring backups of [Company Name]'s information assets. The purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in the event of data loss, corruption, system failure, or disaster, thereby supporting business continuity and compliance with regulatory obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information systems, applications, databases, and data repositories owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or hosted by third-party service providers on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all data classified as Confidential, Internal Use, or higher, including but not limited to corporate data, customer data, employee data, and intellectual property. All personnel, contractors, and third parties with access to [Company Name] systems are bound by this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Backup:</strong> A copy of data and system configurations created and retained for the purpose of restoration.<br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time.<br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable delay for restoring operations after an incident.<br><strong>Full Backup:</strong> A complete copy of all specified data.<br><strong>Incremental Backup:</strong> A copy of only the data that has changed since the last backup of any type.<br><strong>Differential Backup:</strong> A copy of data that has changed since the last full backup.<br><strong>Off-site Storage:</strong> A geographically separate location from the primary data center, used for storing backup media.<br><strong>Data Restoration:</strong> The process of returning data from a backup to an operational state.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates a formal, risk-based backup and recovery program to protect its critical information assets. All systems handling data covered under this policy's scope shall have defined Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) and Recovery Time Objectives (RTO). The creation, storage, security, and testing of backups are strictly required activities. Unauthorized access to, modification of, or destruction of backup data is expressly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Backup Strategy and Scheduling</strong><br>All critical systems and data shall be backed up according to a documented schedule aligned with their business impact assessment. Full system backups must be performed at least weekly. Incremental or differential backups must be performed at least daily. Backup schedules and methods must be documented in system-specific runbooks.</p><p><strong>5.2 Data Coverage and Retention</strong><br>Backups must capture all application data, databases, system configurations, and security settings necessary for full restoration. Backup retention periods shall be defined based on legal, regulatory, and business requirements, but shall not be less than 30 days for operational recovery and 7 years for data subject to specific compliance mandates (e.g., HIPAA, financial regulations).</p><p><strong>5.3 Encryption and Security of Backup Data</strong><br>All backup data, whether in transit or at rest, must be encrypted using strong, industry-accepted cryptographic standards (e.g., AES-256). Encryption keys shall be managed separately from the encrypted backup data. Access to backup systems, management consoles, and restoration capabilities shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege and require multi-factor authentication (MFA).</p><p><strong>5.4 Storage and Geographic Separation</strong><br>A minimum of one copy of backup data must be stored in a geographically separate location (off-site) from the primary production data center to mitigate risks from local disasters. The integrity of off-site backups must be verified periodically. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for at least one backup copy is required to protect against ransomware and malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Backup Integrity and Monitoring</strong><br>Automated monitoring must be implemented to verify the success or failure of all backup jobs. Backup logs must be retained and reviewed regularly for anomalies. Integrity checks (e.g., checksum validation) must be performed on backup data sets at least monthly to ensure data is not corrupted and is recoverable.</p><p><strong>5.6 Restoration Testing and Validation</strong><br>A formal restoration testing program shall be executed. Full restoration tests for critical systems must be conducted at least annually. Test results, including achieved RTO and RPO, must be documented, reviewed by management, and used to refine procedures. The ability to restore individual files or data objects must be tested quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Cloud Services</strong><br>For data processed or stored by third-party providers (e.g., SaaS, IaaS), [Company Name] shall ensure contractual agreements mandate compliance with this policy's backup, encryption, and testing requirements. The company retains the right to audit or receive evidence of the provider's backup and recovery capabilities.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to align with control objectives from ISO 27001:2022 (A.5.29 Information security during disruption, A.8.13 Information backup, A.8.14 Redundancy of information processing facilities) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Availability, Confidentiality, Security). For environments processing Protected Health Information (PHI), backup procedures shall enforce the HIPAA Security Rule requirements for data backup, disaster recovery, and emergency mode operation plans (45 CFR § 164.308(a)(7)(ii)(A) & § 164.310(a)(2)(i)). All activities must comply with relevant data protection laws regarding the storage and cross-border transfer of backup data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>IT Operations/System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for the implementation, execution, and daily monitoring of backup jobs and infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Business Unit Heads:</strong> Responsible for defining the criticality (RPO/RTO) and legal retention requirements for their data.<br><strong>Compliance Officer:</strong> Responsible for ensuring backup and recovery processes meet applicable legal and regulatory frameworks.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for storing business-critical data only on systems covered by this policy and reporting any suspected data loss immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of backup procedures, unauthorized restoration attempts, or tampering with backup data is considered a serious security incident and will be investigated accordingly. Third-party providers in violation of contractual obligations derived from this policy may be subject to penalties and contract termination.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Backup and Recovery Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the IT environment, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders, and personnel shall be required to re-acknowledge the policy as stipulated by the company's security awareness program.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.194158,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""HIPAA\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:43:29.947,NULL
24,927462,Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy,"<p>This Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy establishes mandatory physical and logical security controls to prevent unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, [Company Name]'s sensitive and confidential information. The policy mandates procedures to secure workspaces and information systems when unattended, thereby mitigating risks of data breach, theft, or visual hacking in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.11.2.9) and SOC 2 (CC6.1) requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively ""Personnel"") who access [Company Name] facilities, information assets, or systems. It encompasses all physical workspaces (e.g., offices, cubicles, meeting rooms, hot desks) and all logical access points (e.g., workstations, laptops, mobile devices, virtual desktops) owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Clean Desk:</strong> A state where all physical sensitive or confidential materials are removed from a workspace and secured when the workspace is unattended.<br/><strong>Clear Screen:</strong> A state where an information system session is locked, requiring re-authentication, or terminated when the device is unattended.<br/><strong>Sensitive Information:</strong> Any data classified as Confidential or Restricted per the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to financial records, intellectual property, customer data, employee records, and strategic plans.<br/><strong>Unattended Workspace:</strong> Any workspace where the authorized user is not physically present and cannot directly observe or control access to the workspace or device.<br/><strong>Session Lock:</strong> A mechanism that conceals all information on a display and requires re-authentication to resume activity.</p>

<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the leaving of sensitive information in an unsecured state in any unattended workspace. All Personnel shall maintain a clean desk and enforce a clear screen as defined in this policy. Failure to comply constitutes a security violation and may result in disciplinary action.</p>

<h4>5.1 Clean Desk Requirements</h4><p>5.1.1 At the end of the workday and whenever leaving a workspace unattended for any period, Personnel shall remove all physical documents containing sensitive information from the desk surface and store them in a locked filing cabinet or other approved secure storage.<br/>5.1.2 Sensitive information shall not be left on printers, copiers, fax machines, or in shared work areas. Documents must be retrieved immediately after printing or processing.<br/>5.1.3 Whiteboards, flip charts, or other writing surfaces in meeting rooms or workspaces shall be erased of all sensitive information immediately following use.<br/>5.1.4 Portable storage media (e.g., USB drives, external hard disks) containing sensitive information shall not be left unattended on desks and must be stored securely when not in active use.</p><h4>5.2 Clear Screen Requirements</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall manually initiate a session lock (e.g., Windows Key + L, Ctrl+Alt+Del Lock) or enable a screensaver with password protection that activates after a maximum of <strong>15 minutes</strong> of inactivity.<br/>5.2.2 All workstations, laptops, and mobile devices used for company business must be configured to automatically lock after a period of inactivity not to exceed 15 minutes. Exceptions require written approval from the Information Security team.<br/>5.2.3 When leaving a device unattended, even for short periods, Personnel must manually lock the session. Devices left logged in and unattended are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.4 For shared workstations or kiosks, the session must be fully logged off at the conclusion of use.</p><h4>5.3 Workspace Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Laptops and mobile devices shall be physically secured with a locking cable when left at a desk in a secure office, or stored in a locked drawer or cabinet when left in an unsecured or public area.<br/>5.3.2 Keys to locked storage, and access cards/badges, shall not be left unattended on desks or in unsecured locations.<br/>5.3.3 Personnel are responsible for ensuring meeting rooms are cleared of all sensitive materials and whiteboards are erased at the conclusion of meetings.</p><h4>5.4 Remote Work and Flexible Workspaces</h4><p>5.4.1 This policy applies with equal force to home offices, co-working spaces, and other remote work locations.<br/>5.4.2 Personnel working remotely are responsible for ensuring family members, visitors, or cleaning staff cannot access sensitive physical or digital information.<br/>5.4.3 Screens must be positioned to prevent ""shoulder surfing"" and windows or blinds should be used to obscure the view from outside the residence.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable) by implementing appropriate technical and organizational measures to protect personal data. Adherence supports audit requirements for ISO 27001 control A.11.2.9 (Clear desk and clear screen policy) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1) and Confidentiality. Non-compliance may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for daily compliance with all clean desk and clear screen procedures for their assigned workspaces and devices.<br/><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for promoting awareness, monitoring compliance within their teams, and addressing violations.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Is responsible for defining the policy, configuring automatic session lock settings on corporate devices, conducting periodic spot checks/audits, and investigating violations.<br/><strong>Facilities/Office Management:</strong> Are responsible for providing adequate secure storage (locked cabinets) and supporting the enforcement of clean desk procedures in common areas.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy will be treated as a security incident. The Information Security Department shall conduct periodic unannounced physical audits of workspaces. Personnel found in violation may be subject to corrective action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, in accordance with the Acceptable Use Policy and disciplinary procedures. Repeated or willful violations will result in escalated disciplinary measures.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be communicated to all Personnel.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Clean Desk and Clear Screen Policy establishes mandatory physical and logical security controls to prevent unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, [Company Name]'s sensitive and confidential information. The policy mandates procedures to secure workspaces and information systems when unattended, thereby mitigating risks of data breach, theft, or visual hacking in alignment with ISO 27001 (A.11.2.9) and SOC 2 (CC6.1) requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively \\\""Personnel\\\"") who access [Company Name] facilities, information assets, or systems. It encompasses all physical workspaces (e.g., offices, cubicles, meeting rooms, hot desks) and all logical access points (e.g., workstations, laptops, mobile devices, virtual desktops) owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Clean Desk:</strong> A state where all physical sensitive or confidential materials are removed from a workspace and secured when the workspace is unattended.<br/><strong>Clear Screen:</strong> A state where an information system session is locked, requiring re-authentication, or terminated when the device is unattended.<br/><strong>Sensitive Information:</strong> Any data classified as Confidential or Restricted per the Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to financial records, intellectual property, customer data, employee records, and strategic plans.<br/><strong>Unattended Workspace:</strong> Any workspace where the authorized user is not physically present and cannot directly observe or control access to the workspace or device.<br/><strong>Session Lock:</strong> A mechanism that conceals all information on a display and requires re-authentication to resume activity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the leaving of sensitive information in an unsecured state in any unattended workspace. All Personnel shall maintain a clean desk and enforce a clear screen as defined in this policy. Failure to comply constitutes a security violation and may result in disciplinary action.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Clean Desk Requirements</h4><p>5.1.1 At the end of the workday and whenever leaving a workspace unattended for any period, Personnel shall remove all physical documents containing sensitive information from the desk surface and store them in a locked filing cabinet or other approved secure storage.<br/>5.1.2 Sensitive information shall not be left on printers, copiers, fax machines, or in shared work areas. Documents must be retrieved immediately after printing or processing.<br/>5.1.3 Whiteboards, flip charts, or other writing surfaces in meeting rooms or workspaces shall be erased of all sensitive information immediately following use.<br/>5.1.4 Portable storage media (e.g., USB drives, external hard disks) containing sensitive information shall not be left unattended on desks and must be stored securely when not in active use.</p><h4>5.2 Clear Screen Requirements</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall manually initiate a session lock (e.g., Windows Key + L, Ctrl+Alt+Del Lock) or enable a screensaver with password protection that activates after a maximum of <strong>15 minutes</strong> of inactivity.<br/>5.2.2 All workstations, laptops, and mobile devices used for company business must be configured to automatically lock after a period of inactivity not to exceed 15 minutes. Exceptions require written approval from the Information Security team.<br/>5.2.3 When leaving a device unattended, even for short periods, Personnel must manually lock the session. Devices left logged in and unattended are strictly prohibited.<br/>5.2.4 For shared workstations or kiosks, the session must be fully logged off at the conclusion of use.</p><h4>5.3 Workspace Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Laptops and mobile devices shall be physically secured with a locking cable when left at a desk in a secure office, or stored in a locked drawer or cabinet when left in an unsecured or public area.<br/>5.3.2 Keys to locked storage, and access cards/badges, shall not be left unattended on desks or in unsecured locations.<br/>5.3.3 Personnel are responsible for ensuring meeting rooms are cleared of all sensitive materials and whiteboards are erased at the conclusion of meetings.</p><h4>5.4 Remote Work and Flexible Workspaces</h4><p>5.4.1 This policy applies with equal force to home offices, co-working spaces, and other remote work locations.<br/>5.4.2 Personnel working remotely are responsible for ensuring family members, visitors, or cleaning staff cannot access sensitive physical or digital information.<br/>5.4.3 Screens must be positioned to prevent \\\""shoulder surfing\\\"" and windows or blinds should be used to obscure the view from outside the residence.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA where applicable) by implementing appropriate technical and organizational measures to protect personal data. Adherence supports audit requirements for ISO 27001 control A.11.2.9 (Clear desk and clear screen policy) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security (CC6.1) and Confidentiality. Non-compliance may result in regulatory penalties, audit findings, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for daily compliance with all clean desk and clear screen procedures for their assigned workspaces and devices.<br/><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for promoting awareness, monitoring compliance within their teams, and addressing violations.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Is responsible for defining the policy, configuring automatic session lock settings on corporate devices, conducting periodic spot checks/audits, and investigating violations.<br/><strong>Facilities/Office Management:</strong> Are responsible for providing adequate secure storage (locked cabinets) and supporting the enforcement of clean desk procedures in common areas.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Violations of this policy will be treated as a security incident. The Information Security Department shall conduct periodic unannounced physical audits of workspaces. Personnel found in violation may be subject to corrective action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, in accordance with the Acceptable Use Policy and disciplinary procedures. Repeated or willful violations will result in escalated disciplinary measures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be communicated to all Personnel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:21.737347,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:44:02.16,NULL
25,147380,Malware Protection Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Malware Protection Policy is to establish the mandatory controls and procedures for [Company Name] to prevent, detect, contain, and eradicate malicious software (malware) across all information systems and assets. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems, and to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, ""Personnel"") who access, manage, or operate any [Company Name] information asset. It encompasses all company-owned or managed endpoints (e.g., workstations, laptops, servers, mobile devices), network infrastructure, and cloud-based environments, regardless of location.</p>

<p><strong>Malware:</strong> Malicious software designed to infiltrate, damage, or disable computer systems, including but not limited to viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware, spyware, adware, rootkits, and keyloggers.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP):</strong> A centralized security solution deployed on endpoints to prevent file-based malware, detect malicious activity, and provide investigation and remediation capabilities.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):</strong> A security solution that continuously monitors and collects endpoint data, uses rules-based automated response, and provides deep visibility for analysis of security incidents.</p><p><strong>Signature-Based Detection:</strong> A method of identifying malware by comparing files against a database of known malicious signatures or patterns.</p><p><strong>Heuristic/Behavioral Analysis:</strong> A method of detecting malware by analyzing the behavior or characteristics of software to identify suspicious activity indicative of new or unknown threats.</p>

<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the intentional introduction of malware into its information systems and environments. The organization mandates a defense-in-depth strategy, employing multiple, layered technical and administrative controls to mitigate the risk of malware infection. All Personnel are responsible for adhering to the security practices defined in this policy to safeguard organizational assets.</p>

<h4>5.1. Mandatory Endpoint Protection</h4><p>All company-managed endpoints (servers, workstations, laptops) must have the officially sanctioned Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP) installed, actively running, and kept up-to-date. The disabling, tampering with, or unauthorized removal of this software is strictly prohibited.</p><h4>5.2. Signature and Engine Updates</h4><p>The malware detection signatures and scanning engines for all protective software must be configured to update automatically at a minimum frequency of every 24 hours. Manual updates shall be performed if automatic updates fail.</p><h4>5.3. Real-Time Scanning</h4><p>Real-time (on-access) scanning must be enabled on all endpoints. This scanning shall inspect files upon creation, opening, closing, or execution to prevent malware from being written to or run from local storage, network drives, or removable media.</p><h4>5.4. Scheduled Full System Scans</h4><p>A comprehensive, scheduled full-system scan must be performed on all endpoints at least weekly. Scans must be configured to run during off-peak hours to minimize performance impact. Logs of all scans, including items detected and actions taken, must be retained.</p><h4>5.5. Automated Quarantine and Remediation</h4><p>All protective software must be configured to automatically quarantine any file identified as confirmed malware. Quarantined files must be moved to a secure, isolated location where they cannot be executed. Remediation actions (cleaning, deletion) shall be performed according to a defined process managed by the IT Security team.</p><h4>5.6. Email and Web Gateway Filtering</h4><p>All inbound and outbound email must pass through a gateway solution that scans attachments and links for malware. All web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) must be proxied and filtered through a solution capable of detecting and blocking malware from web downloads and drive-by downloads.</p><h4>5.7. Application Control and Execution Restriction</h4><p>Where technically feasible, application whitelisting or execution control policies shall be implemented on critical servers and endpoints (e.g., point-of-sale systems, domain controllers) to prevent the execution of unauthorized software, including malware.</p><h4>5.8. Patch Management Integration</h4><p>The malware protection strategy shall be integrated with the enterprise patch management program. All operating systems and applications must be patched promptly in accordance with the Patch Management Policy to remediate vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malware.</p><h4>5.9. Centralized Logging and Alerting</h4><p>All malware detection events, including blocked attempts, quarantined files, and scan results, must be logged centrally to a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts for critical detections (e.g., ransomware, worm activity) must be configured for immediate notification of the Security Operations Center (SOC) or IT Security team.</p><h4>5.10. Removable Media Controls</h4><p>The use of removable media (USB drives, external hard drives) must be controlled. All such media must be automatically scanned for malware upon insertion into any company endpoint. The use of unapproved or un-scanned removable media on company systems is forbidden.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.7 Protection against malware), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (PR.IP-1, DE.CM-4). Adherence is mandatory to meet contractual obligations with clients and partners, and to satisfy regulatory requirements concerning data protection and breach notification.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>IT Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the selection, deployment, configuration, monitoring, and tuning of all malware protection technologies. They shall investigate and respond to all malware incidents.<br><strong>IT Operations/Endpoint Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring the mandated protection software is installed, functional, and updated on all assets under their management.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must exercise caution with email attachments and web links, report any suspected malware activity immediately to the IT Service Desk, and shall not attempt to disable or circumvent security controls.</p>

<p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Deliberate acts that introduce malware or disable protective controls will be treated as a serious security violation.</p>

<p>This Malware Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated representative at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Malware Protection Policy is to establish the mandatory controls and procedures for [Company Name] to prevent, detect, contain, and eradicate malicious software (malware) across all information systems and assets. This policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems, and to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") who access, manage, or operate any [Company Name] information asset. It encompasses all company-owned or managed endpoints (e.g., workstations, laptops, servers, mobile devices), network infrastructure, and cloud-based environments, regardless of location.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Malware:</strong> Malicious software designed to infiltrate, damage, or disable computer systems, including but not limited to viruses, worms, trojans, ransomware, spyware, adware, rootkits, and keyloggers.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP):</strong> A centralized security solution deployed on endpoints to prevent file-based malware, detect malicious activity, and provide investigation and remediation capabilities.</p><p><strong>Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR):</strong> A security solution that continuously monitors and collects endpoint data, uses rules-based automated response, and provides deep visibility for analysis of security incidents.</p><p><strong>Signature-Based Detection:</strong> A method of identifying malware by comparing files against a database of known malicious signatures or patterns.</p><p><strong>Heuristic/Behavioral Analysis:</strong> A method of detecting malware by analyzing the behavior or characteristics of software to identify suspicious activity indicative of new or unknown threats.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the intentional introduction of malware into its information systems and environments. The organization mandates a defense-in-depth strategy, employing multiple, layered technical and administrative controls to mitigate the risk of malware infection. All Personnel are responsible for adhering to the security practices defined in this policy to safeguard organizational assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1. Mandatory Endpoint Protection</h4><p>All company-managed endpoints (servers, workstations, laptops) must have the officially sanctioned Endpoint Protection Platform (EPP) installed, actively running, and kept up-to-date. The disabling, tampering with, or unauthorized removal of this software is strictly prohibited.</p><h4>5.2. Signature and Engine Updates</h4><p>The malware detection signatures and scanning engines for all protective software must be configured to update automatically at a minimum frequency of every 24 hours. Manual updates shall be performed if automatic updates fail.</p><h4>5.3. Real-Time Scanning</h4><p>Real-time (on-access) scanning must be enabled on all endpoints. This scanning shall inspect files upon creation, opening, closing, or execution to prevent malware from being written to or run from local storage, network drives, or removable media.</p><h4>5.4. Scheduled Full System Scans</h4><p>A comprehensive, scheduled full-system scan must be performed on all endpoints at least weekly. Scans must be configured to run during off-peak hours to minimize performance impact. Logs of all scans, including items detected and actions taken, must be retained.</p><h4>5.5. Automated Quarantine and Remediation</h4><p>All protective software must be configured to automatically quarantine any file identified as confirmed malware. Quarantined files must be moved to a secure, isolated location where they cannot be executed. Remediation actions (cleaning, deletion) shall be performed according to a defined process managed by the IT Security team.</p><h4>5.6. Email and Web Gateway Filtering</h4><p>All inbound and outbound email must pass through a gateway solution that scans attachments and links for malware. All web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) must be proxied and filtered through a solution capable of detecting and blocking malware from web downloads and drive-by downloads.</p><h4>5.7. Application Control and Execution Restriction</h4><p>Where technically feasible, application whitelisting or execution control policies shall be implemented on critical servers and endpoints (e.g., point-of-sale systems, domain controllers) to prevent the execution of unauthorized software, including malware.</p><h4>5.8. Patch Management Integration</h4><p>The malware protection strategy shall be integrated with the enterprise patch management program. All operating systems and applications must be patched promptly in accordance with the Patch Management Policy to remediate vulnerabilities that could be exploited by malware.</p><h4>5.9. Centralized Logging and Alerting</h4><p>All malware detection events, including blocked attempts, quarantined files, and scan results, must be logged centrally to a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system. Alerts for critical detections (e.g., ransomware, worm activity) must be configured for immediate notification of the Security Operations Center (SOC) or IT Security team.</p><h4>5.10. Removable Media Controls</h4><p>The use of removable media (USB drives, external hard drives) must be controlled. All such media must be automatically scanned for malware upon insertion into any company endpoint. The use of unapproved or un-scanned removable media on company systems is forbidden.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy supports compliance with control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.7 Protection against malware), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (PR.IP-1, DE.CM-4). Adherence is mandatory to meet contractual obligations with clients and partners, and to satisfy regulatory requirements concerning data protection and breach notification.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>IT Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the selection, deployment, configuration, monitoring, and tuning of all malware protection technologies. They shall investigate and respond to all malware incidents.<br><strong>IT Operations/Endpoint Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring the mandated protection software is installed, functional, and updated on all assets under their management.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must exercise caution with email attachments and web links, report any suspected malware activity immediately to the IT Service Desk, and shall not attempt to disable or circumvent security controls.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Deliberate acts that introduce malware or disable protective controls will be treated as a serious security violation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Malware Protection Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated representative at least annually, or following significant security incidents or changes in the threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:21.867899,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""NIST CSF\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:44:35.68,NULL
26,751705,Vulnerability Management Policy,"<p>This Vulnerability Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, assessment, prioritization, remediation, and reporting of security vulnerabilities within [Company Name]'s information assets, systems, and networks. The policy is designed to proactively manage risk, protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network infrastructure, applications (including third-party and cloud-hosted), and data owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who develop, deploy, manage, or have access to [Company Name]'s technological environment. This policy is global in nature and applies to all subsidiaries and business units.</p>

<p><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited by a threat source.<br><strong>Threat:</strong> Any circumstance or event with the potential to adversely impact organizational operations, assets, or individuals through unauthorized access, destruction, disclosure, or modification of information.<br><strong>Risk:</strong> The potential for loss, damage, or destruction of an asset as a result of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> The process of fixing a vulnerability to eliminate or mitigate the associated risk. This includes patching, configuration changes, or implementing compensating controls.<br><strong>Critical/High-Risk Vulnerability:</strong> A vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 7.0 or higher, or otherwise designated as such by the Information Security team based on contextual business risk.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> The business unit or individual responsible for the business function supported by an information asset and accountable for its security.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a continuous and risk-based vulnerability management program. All technology assets within scope shall be regularly assessed for security vulnerabilities. Identified vulnerabilities shall be evaluated, prioritized based on severity and business impact, and remediated within defined timeframes. The failure to adhere to remediation timelines for critical and high-risk vulnerabilities is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability Discovery and Identification</strong><br>5.1.1 Automated vulnerability scanning of all in-scope internal and external network assets shall be conducted at least monthly.<br>5.1.2 Authenticated scanning, where technically feasible, shall be performed to identify vulnerabilities at the host and application layer.<br>5.1.3 All new or significantly modified applications must undergo static application security testing (SAST) and/or dynamic application security testing (DAST) prior to deployment to production.<br>5.1.4 External penetration tests, conducted by a qualified independent third party, shall be performed at least annually, or after any significant change to the external attack surface.<br><br><strong>5.2 Vulnerability Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br>5.2.1 All discovered vulnerabilities shall be cataloged in a centralized vulnerability management system.<br>5.2.2 Vulnerabilities shall be assigned a risk rating based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) and contextual business factors, including asset criticality and threat intelligence.<br>5.2.3 A formal risk acceptance process is required for any vulnerability that will not be remediated within the standard timeframe. Risk acceptance must be documented, approved by the Asset Owner and the CISO (or delegate), and reviewed quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Vulnerability Remediation</strong><br>5.3.1 Critical and High-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS >= 7.0) must be remediated within 30 calendar days of identification or vendor patch availability, whichever is later.<br>5.3.2 Medium-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS 4.0 - 6.9) must be remediated within 90 calendar days.<br>5.3.3 Low-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS < 4.0) shall be remediated based on a scheduled review or as part of normal maintenance cycles.<br>5.3.4 Emergency patches for critical, actively exploited vulnerabilities (e.g., zero-days) shall be applied within 7 calendar days, following an expedited change management process.<br>5.3.5 When immediate remediation is not possible, compensating controls (e.g., network segmentation, intrusion prevention rules) must be implemented and documented until a permanent fix is deployed.<br><br><strong>5.4 Verification and Reporting</strong><br>5.4.1 All remediation actions must be verified through re-scanning or other validation techniques to confirm closure.<br>5.4.2 A comprehensive vulnerability management report, detailing scan coverage, vulnerability metrics, remediation status, and aging of open vulnerabilities, shall be generated and reviewed by senior management at least quarterly.<br>5.4.3 All scanning, assessment, and remediation activities shall be logged, with logs retained for a minimum of one year to support audit requirements.</p>

<p>The Vulnerability Management Program shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.8, A.5.7, A.5.29), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and PCI DSS (Requirements 6.1, 6.2, 11.2). The program must also consider and align with other applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements pertaining to information security and data protection. Evidence of compliance, including scan reports, risk acceptance forms, and management reviews, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy and program. Approves risk exceptions and provides management oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Operates the vulnerability management program; conducts scans, coordinates testing, manages the vulnerability database, and tracks remediation.<br><strong>Asset Owners / System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved remediation actions (e.g., applying patches) within mandated timeframes for assets under their control.<br><strong>IT Operations / Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for facilitating the timely testing and deployment of security patches through the change management process.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report suspected security vulnerabilities to the Information Security team immediately.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Deliberate failure to remediate a known critical vulnerability, or the circumvention of security controls to avoid detection, will be treated as a serious security violation. The Information Security team is authorized to isolate or disconnect non-compliant systems from the network to prevent potential compromise.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology environment, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Vulnerability Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, assessment, prioritization, remediation, and reporting of security vulnerabilities within [Company Name]'s information assets, systems, and networks. The policy is designed to proactively manage risk, protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data, and ensure compliance with regulatory obligations and contractual commitments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network infrastructure, applications (including third-party and cloud-hosted), and data owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who develop, deploy, manage, or have access to [Company Name]'s technological environment. This policy is global in nature and applies to all subsidiaries and business units.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited by a threat source.<br><strong>Threat:</strong> Any circumstance or event with the potential to adversely impact organizational operations, assets, or individuals through unauthorized access, destruction, disclosure, or modification of information.<br><strong>Risk:</strong> The potential for loss, damage, or destruction of an asset as a result of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.<br><strong>Remediation:</strong> The process of fixing a vulnerability to eliminate or mitigate the associated risk. This includes patching, configuration changes, or implementing compensating controls.<br><strong>Critical/High-Risk Vulnerability:</strong> A vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 7.0 or higher, or otherwise designated as such by the Information Security team based on contextual business risk.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> The business unit or individual responsible for the business function supported by an information asset and accountable for its security.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates a continuous and risk-based vulnerability management program. All technology assets within scope shall be regularly assessed for security vulnerabilities. Identified vulnerabilities shall be evaluated, prioritized based on severity and business impact, and remediated within defined timeframes. The failure to adhere to remediation timelines for critical and high-risk vulnerabilities is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability Discovery and Identification</strong><br>5.1.1 Automated vulnerability scanning of all in-scope internal and external network assets shall be conducted at least monthly.<br>5.1.2 Authenticated scanning, where technically feasible, shall be performed to identify vulnerabilities at the host and application layer.<br>5.1.3 All new or significantly modified applications must undergo static application security testing (SAST) and/or dynamic application security testing (DAST) prior to deployment to production.<br>5.1.4 External penetration tests, conducted by a qualified independent third party, shall be performed at least annually, or after any significant change to the external attack surface.<br><br><strong>5.2 Vulnerability Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br>5.2.1 All discovered vulnerabilities shall be cataloged in a centralized vulnerability management system.<br>5.2.2 Vulnerabilities shall be assigned a risk rating based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) and contextual business factors, including asset criticality and threat intelligence.<br>5.2.3 A formal risk acceptance process is required for any vulnerability that will not be remediated within the standard timeframe. Risk acceptance must be documented, approved by the Asset Owner and the CISO (or delegate), and reviewed quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.3 Vulnerability Remediation</strong><br>5.3.1 Critical and High-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS >= 7.0) must be remediated within 30 calendar days of identification or vendor patch availability, whichever is later.<br>5.3.2 Medium-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS 4.0 - 6.9) must be remediated within 90 calendar days.<br>5.3.3 Low-Risk vulnerabilities (CVSS < 4.0) shall be remediated based on a scheduled review or as part of normal maintenance cycles.<br>5.3.4 Emergency patches for critical, actively exploited vulnerabilities (e.g., zero-days) shall be applied within 7 calendar days, following an expedited change management process.<br>5.3.5 When immediate remediation is not possible, compensating controls (e.g., network segmentation, intrusion prevention rules) must be implemented and documented until a permanent fix is deployed.<br><br><strong>5.4 Verification and Reporting</strong><br>5.4.1 All remediation actions must be verified through re-scanning or other validation techniques to confirm closure.<br>5.4.2 A comprehensive vulnerability management report, detailing scan coverage, vulnerability metrics, remediation status, and aging of open vulnerabilities, shall be generated and reviewed by senior management at least quarterly.<br>5.4.3 All scanning, assessment, and remediation activities shall be logged, with logs retained for a minimum of one year to support audit requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The Vulnerability Management Program shall be designed and operated to meet the control objectives of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.8, A.5.7, A.5.29), SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1), and PCI DSS (Requirements 6.1, 6.2, 11.2). The program must also consider and align with other applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements pertaining to information security and data protection. Evidence of compliance, including scan reports, risk acceptance forms, and management reviews, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy and program. Approves risk exceptions and provides management oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Operates the vulnerability management program; conducts scans, coordinates testing, manages the vulnerability database, and tracks remediation.<br><strong>Asset Owners / System Administrators:</strong> Responsible for implementing approved remediation actions (e.g., applying patches) within mandated timeframes for assets under their control.<br><strong>IT Operations / Change Advisory Board (CAB):</strong> Responsible for facilitating the timely testing and deployment of security patches through the change management process.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report suspected security vulnerabilities to the Information Security team immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Deliberate failure to remediate a known critical vulnerability, or the circumvention of security controls to avoid detection, will be treated as a serious security violation. The Information Security team is authorized to isolate or disconnect non-compliant systems from the network to prevent potential compromise.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology environment, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:21.977925,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""PCI DSS\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:45:08.683,NULL
41,33245,Data Breach Notification Policy,"<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy establishes the mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, report, assess, and respond to data breaches involving Personal Data or Protected Health Information (PHI). The purpose is to ensure timely and compliant notification to regulatory authorities, affected individuals, and other stakeholders as required by applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, thereby mitigating legal, financial, and reputational harm.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively, ""Personnel"") of [Company Name] who have access to company information systems or data. It covers all Personal Data and PHI processed by [Company Name], regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other) or location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party services). This policy extends to all subsidiaries and business units under the control of [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A security incident leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to Personal Data or PHI transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.<br><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person as defined under the GDPR and other applicable data protection laws.<br><strong>Protected Health Information (PHI):</strong> Individually identifiable health information as defined by HIPAA.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.<br><strong>Supervisory Authority:</strong> An independent public authority established under the GDPR (e.g., a Data Protection Authority).<br><strong>Notification Timeline:</strong> The legally mandated period within which a breach must be reported to authorities and/or affected individuals.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a zero-tolerance approach to the mismanagement of data breach incidents. All Personnel shall immediately report any suspected or confirmed data breach. [Company Name] shall investigate all reported incidents promptly, document findings rigorously, and execute notification procedures in strict compliance with all applicable legal and regulatory frameworks, including but not limited to GDPR, HIPAA, and state-level breach notification laws. Failure to adhere to this policy constitutes a serious violation of company rules and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Breach Detection and Initial Reporting:</strong> Any Personnel who suspects a data breach shall immediately, and in no case later than one (1) hour after discovery, report the incident to the Information Security Team via the designated secure channel (e.g., a dedicated ticketing system or hotline). Initial reports must include the time of discovery, systems/data involved, and the nature of the suspected breach.<br><br><strong>5.2 Incident Triage and Classification:</strong> Upon notification, the Information Security Team shall initiate a formal incident response. A preliminary assessment must be completed within four (4) hours to classify the incident's severity (e.g., Low, Medium, High, Critical) based on data sensitivity, volume of records impacted, and likelihood of harm to data subjects.<br><br><strong>5.3 Formal Investigation and Documentation:</strong> For all confirmed breaches, a formal investigation shall be conducted. This investigation must document: the date, time, and duration of the breach; the categories and approximate number of data subjects and records concerned; the likely consequences of the breach; and the measures taken or proposed to address the breach. All investigative actions and findings shall be logged in the organization's Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.<br><br><strong>5.4 Breach Assessment and Legal Determination:</strong> The Data Protection Officer (DPO) or Legal Counsel, in conjunction with the CISO, shall determine within forty-eight (48) hours of a confirmed breach whether the incident is likely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals. This assessment shall dictate the notification requirements under GDPR Article 33/34, HIPAA, and other relevant laws.<br><br><strong>5.5 Regulatory Notification Procedures:</strong> If notification is required, the DPO shall ensure notification is made to the relevant Supervisory Authority (e.g., for GDPR) within seventy-two (72) hours of becoming aware of the breach. Notifications to the Secretary of Health and Human Services (for HIPAA) shall be made without unreasonable delay and no later than sixty (60) calendar days after discovery. All notifications must include the information documented per section 5.3.<br><br><strong>5.6 Individual Notification Procedures:</strong> Where a breach is likely to result in a high risk to individuals, affected data subjects shall be notified without undue delay. Notifications shall be delivered in clear and plain language, describing the nature of the breach, the categories of data involved, recommended mitigation steps for the individual, and contact details for [Company Name]'s DPO. Communication methods shall be direct (e.g., email, letter) unless demonstrably disproportionate effort is required, in which case a public communication may be authorized by the DPO.<br><br><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Media Communication:</strong> All external communications regarding a data breach, including press releases and notifications to business partners or data processors, must be pre-approved by the CISO, DPO, and Legal Counsel. A single, authorized spokesperson shall be designated for all media inquiries.<br><br><strong>5.8 Post-Breach Remediation and Review:</strong> Following containment and notification, the Information Security Team shall conduct a root cause analysis. Corrective and preventive actions (e.g., patching systems, updating access controls, enhancing monitoring) shall be implemented. A full incident report, including lessons learned, shall be presented to senior management within thirty (30) days of breach closure.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with, but not limited to, the following frameworks and regulations:<br>- <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Articles 33 and 34 (Notification obligations).<br>- <strong>Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA):</strong> Breach Notification Rule (45 CFR §§ 164.400-414).<br>- <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Control objectives A.5.24 (Information security incident response planning), A.5.25 (Assessment and decision on information security events), A.5.26 (Response to information security incidents), and A.5.27 (Learning from information security incidents).<br>- <strong>State-Level Laws:</strong> Applicable state data breach notification statutes (e.g., California Consumer Privacy Act/CPRA, New York SHIELD Act).<br>In the event of conflicting requirements, the most stringent applicable law shall govern [Company Name]'s actions.</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must immediately report any suspected data breach as defined in Section 5.1.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for initial triage, investigation, containment, technical remediation, and documentation of the breach.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has overall authority for the execution of this policy, coordinates the incident response, and is the primary liaison with executive management.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO) / Legal Counsel:</strong> Responsible for the legal assessment of the breach, determining notification obligations, and managing all regulatory and individual communications.<br><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Must be informed of significant breaches without delay and are responsible for approving resource allocation for breach response and remediation.</p>

<p>Personnel found to have violated this policy—including, but not limited to, failing to report a suspected breach in a timely manner, impeding an investigation, or making unauthorized external communications—shall be subject to disciplinary action. Such action may include performance improvement plans, suspension, revocation of system access, termination of employment, and pursuit of legal action. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued access to [Company Name] information assets.</p>

<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and DPO, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to applicable laws and regulations. All revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Personnel through the company's policy management portal.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy establishes the mandatory procedures for [Company Name] to detect, report, assess, and respond to data breaches involving Personal Data or Protected Health Information (PHI). The purpose is to ensure timely and compliant notification to regulatory authorities, affected individuals, and other stakeholders as required by applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, thereby mitigating legal, financial, and reputational harm.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") of [Company Name] who have access to company information systems or data. It covers all Personal Data and PHI processed by [Company Name], regardless of the data format (electronic, paper, or other) or location (on-premises, cloud, or third-party services). This policy extends to all subsidiaries and business units under the control of [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Data Breach:</strong> A security incident leading to the accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorized disclosure of, or access to Personal Data or PHI transmitted, stored, or otherwise processed.<br><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person as defined under the GDPR and other applicable data protection laws.<br><strong>Protected Health Information (PHI):</strong> Individually identifiable health information as defined by HIPAA.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Data Controller.<br><strong>Supervisory Authority:</strong> An independent public authority established under the GDPR (e.g., a Data Protection Authority).<br><strong>Notification Timeline:</strong> The legally mandated period within which a breach must be reported to authorities and/or affected individuals.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates a zero-tolerance approach to the mismanagement of data breach incidents. All Personnel shall immediately report any suspected or confirmed data breach. [Company Name] shall investigate all reported incidents promptly, document findings rigorously, and execute notification procedures in strict compliance with all applicable legal and regulatory frameworks, including but not limited to GDPR, HIPAA, and state-level breach notification laws. Failure to adhere to this policy constitutes a serious violation of company rules and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Breach Detection and Initial Reporting:</strong> Any Personnel who suspects a data breach shall immediately, and in no case later than one (1) hour after discovery, report the incident to the Information Security Team via the designated secure channel (e.g., a dedicated ticketing system or hotline). Initial reports must include the time of discovery, systems/data involved, and the nature of the suspected breach.<br><br><strong>5.2 Incident Triage and Classification:</strong> Upon notification, the Information Security Team shall initiate a formal incident response. A preliminary assessment must be completed within four (4) hours to classify the incident's severity (e.g., Low, Medium, High, Critical) based on data sensitivity, volume of records impacted, and likelihood of harm to data subjects.<br><br><strong>5.3 Formal Investigation and Documentation:</strong> For all confirmed breaches, a formal investigation shall be conducted. This investigation must document: the date, time, and duration of the breach; the categories and approximate number of data subjects and records concerned; the likely consequences of the breach; and the measures taken or proposed to address the breach. All investigative actions and findings shall be logged in the organization's Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.<br><br><strong>5.4 Breach Assessment and Legal Determination:</strong> The Data Protection Officer (DPO) or Legal Counsel, in conjunction with the CISO, shall determine within forty-eight (48) hours of a confirmed breach whether the incident is likely to result in a risk to the rights and freedoms of individuals. This assessment shall dictate the notification requirements under GDPR Article 33/34, HIPAA, and other relevant laws.<br><br><strong>5.5 Regulatory Notification Procedures:</strong> If notification is required, the DPO shall ensure notification is made to the relevant Supervisory Authority (e.g., for GDPR) within seventy-two (72) hours of becoming aware of the breach. Notifications to the Secretary of Health and Human Services (for HIPAA) shall be made without unreasonable delay and no later than sixty (60) calendar days after discovery. All notifications must include the information documented per section 5.3.<br><br><strong>5.6 Individual Notification Procedures:</strong> Where a breach is likely to result in a high risk to individuals, affected data subjects shall be notified without undue delay. Notifications shall be delivered in clear and plain language, describing the nature of the breach, the categories of data involved, recommended mitigation steps for the individual, and contact details for [Company Name]'s DPO. Communication methods shall be direct (e.g., email, letter) unless demonstrably disproportionate effort is required, in which case a public communication may be authorized by the DPO.<br><br><strong>5.7 Third-Party and Media Communication:</strong> All external communications regarding a data breach, including press releases and notifications to business partners or data processors, must be pre-approved by the CISO, DPO, and Legal Counsel. A single, authorized spokesperson shall be designated for all media inquiries.<br><br><strong>5.8 Post-Breach Remediation and Review:</strong> Following containment and notification, the Information Security Team shall conduct a root cause analysis. Corrective and preventive actions (e.g., patching systems, updating access controls, enhancing monitoring) shall be implemented. A full incident report, including lessons learned, shall be presented to senior management within thirty (30) days of breach closure.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with, but not limited to, the following frameworks and regulations:<br>- <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR):</strong> Articles 33 and 34 (Notification obligations).<br>- <strong>Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA):</strong> Breach Notification Rule (45 CFR §§ 164.400-414).<br>- <strong>ISO/IEC 27001:2022:</strong> Control objectives A.5.24 (Information security incident response planning), A.5.25 (Assessment and decision on information security events), A.5.26 (Response to information security incidents), and A.5.27 (Learning from information security incidents).<br>- <strong>State-Level Laws:</strong> Applicable state data breach notification statutes (e.g., California Consumer Privacy Act/CPRA, New York SHIELD Act).<br>In the event of conflicting requirements, the most stringent applicable law shall govern [Company Name]'s actions.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must immediately report any suspected data breach as defined in Section 5.1.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for initial triage, investigation, containment, technical remediation, and documentation of the breach.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Has overall authority for the execution of this policy, coordinates the incident response, and is the primary liaison with executive management.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO) / Legal Counsel:</strong> Responsible for the legal assessment of the breach, determining notification obligations, and managing all regulatory and individual communications.<br><strong>Executive Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Must be informed of significant breaches without delay and are responsible for approving resource allocation for breach response and remediation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Personnel found to have violated this policy—including, but not limited to, failing to report a suspected breach in a timely manner, impeding an investigation, or making unauthorized external communications—shall be subject to disciplinary action. Such action may include performance improvement plans, suspension, revocation of system access, termination of employment, and pursuit of legal action. Compliance with this policy is a condition of employment and continued access to [Company Name] information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Data Breach Notification Policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and DPO, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to applicable laws and regulations. All revisions must be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made accessible to all Personnel through the company's policy management portal.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.570773,"""[\""GDPR\"",\""HIPAA\"",\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:45:46.823,NULL
42,515080,Records Management Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Records Management Policy is to establish a formal framework for the systematic control of [Company Name]'s information assets throughout their lifecycle. This policy mandates the creation, classification, retention, storage, security, retrieval, and final disposition of all records to ensure legal and regulatory compliance, operational efficiency, and the protection of sensitive information. It is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.8.2, A.8.3) and SOC 2 (CC Series 3.0) concerning information classification, handling, and retention.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all records, in any media or format, created, received, or maintained by [Company Name] in the course of its business operations. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic documents, databases, email, paper files, audio/video recordings, and system logs. It applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other third parties who create, handle, or manage [Company Name] records.</p>

<p><strong>Record:</strong> Recorded information, regardless of medium or format, created, received, and maintained as evidence and information by [Company Name] in pursuance of legal obligations or in the transaction of business.<br><strong>Records Lifecycle:</strong> The stages through which a record passes: creation, classification, use, storage, retrieval, and final disposition (destruction or permanent archiving).<br><strong>Retention Period:</strong> The legally or operationally mandated minimum duration for which a specific record series must be retained.<br><strong>Legal Hold:</strong> A process that suspends the normal disposition of records relevant to anticipated or ongoing litigation, audit, or investigation.<br><strong>Disposition:</strong> The final action taken with a record at the end of its retention period, which shall be either secure destruction or transfer to an archive.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive records management program. All records shall be accurately classified, securely stored, retained for defined periods, and disposed of in a controlled and documented manner. The unauthorized creation, alteration, deletion, or disclosure of records is strictly prohibited. This program is designed to ensure the integrity, availability, and confidentiality of records in compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations.</p>

<h4>5.1 Records Classification and Labeling</h4><p>All records shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Classification metadata must be embedded within electronic records or clearly marked on physical records. Automated classification tools shall be employed where feasible for electronic data.</p><h4>5.2 Records Creation and Capture</h4><p>Records of all significant business transactions, decisions, and communications must be created and captured in the designated corporate system of record. Ad-hoc storage in personal drives or unauthorized cloud services is forbidden. Email deemed a record must be archived in accordance with the corporate email retention policy.</p><h4>5.3 Retention Schedule Management</h4><p>A formal, documented Retention Schedule, approved by Legal and Compliance, shall define the minimum retention period and final disposition action for every record series. This schedule must be reviewed annually and updated to reflect changes in law or business practice. Records shall not be destroyed before the expiration of their mandated retention period.</p><h4>5.4 Secure Storage and Access Control</h4><p>Records must be stored in authorized repositories with access controls enforcing the principle of least privilege. Confidential and Restricted records must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent. Physical records must be stored in locked, access-controlled facilities. Regular integrity checks (e.g., checksums) shall be performed on vital electronic records.</p><h4>5.5 Legal Hold Process</h4><p>Upon notification from Legal Counsel, a formal Legal Hold shall be initiated. The IT Department must immediately suspend any automated deletion processes for implicated records and ensure their preservation. A register of all active Legal Holds shall be maintained.</p><h4>5.6 Secure Disposition</h4><p>At the end of the retention period, records must be disposed of securely. Electronic records shall be purged using a method that renders recovery infeasible (e.g., cryptographic erasure, NIST 800-88 purge). Physical records shall be cross-cut shredded or incinerated by an approved vendor. A Certificate of Destruction must be obtained and retained for all third-party disposals.</p><h4>5.7 Audit Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All access, modification, deletion, and export activities within designated recordkeeping systems must be logged. Logs must capture user identity, timestamp, action, and record identifier. These logs shall be retained for a minimum of one year and protected from tampering. Regular audits of access logs shall be conducted to detect unauthorized activity.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual frameworks, which may include but are not limited to: GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA, SOX, SEC Rule 17a-4, and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying applicable requirements and integrating them into the Retention Schedule and handling procedures. Failure to comply with this policy may result in legal sanctions, financial penalties, and reputational damage.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Overall governance and enforcement of this policy.<br><strong>Records Manager:</strong> Operational administration of the records management program, maintenance of the Retention Schedule.<br><strong>Department Heads:</strong> Ensure staff within their department comply with records management procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for creating, classifying, and handling records in accordance with this policy and associated procedures.<br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Issue Legal Holds and provide guidance on retention periods and legal obligations.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls for secure storage, access, logging, and disposition of electronic records.</p>

<p>Any employee found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Contractors and third parties in violation may have their agreements terminated. Deliberate destruction of records subject to a Legal Hold, or falsification of records, may result in criminal prosecution. All suspected policy violations must be reported immediately to the Information Security Office.</p>

<p>This Records Management Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes in business operations, technology, or applicable law. Revisions must be approved by the designated governance committee. The latest approved version shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Records Management Policy is to establish a formal framework for the systematic control of [Company Name]'s information assets throughout their lifecycle. This policy mandates the creation, classification, retention, storage, security, retrieval, and final disposition of all records to ensure legal and regulatory compliance, operational efficiency, and the protection of sensitive information. It is designed to satisfy the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.8.2, A.8.3) and SOC 2 (CC Series 3.0) concerning information classification, handling, and retention.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all records, in any media or format, created, received, or maintained by [Company Name] in the course of its business operations. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic documents, databases, email, paper files, audio/video recordings, and system logs. It applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other third parties who create, handle, or manage [Company Name] records.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Record:</strong> Recorded information, regardless of medium or format, created, received, and maintained as evidence and information by [Company Name] in pursuance of legal obligations or in the transaction of business.<br><strong>Records Lifecycle:</strong> The stages through which a record passes: creation, classification, use, storage, retrieval, and final disposition (destruction or permanent archiving).<br><strong>Retention Period:</strong> The legally or operationally mandated minimum duration for which a specific record series must be retained.<br><strong>Legal Hold:</strong> A process that suspends the normal disposition of records relevant to anticipated or ongoing litigation, audit, or investigation.<br><strong>Disposition:</strong> The final action taken with a record at the end of its retention period, which shall be either secure destruction or transfer to an archive.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive records management program. All records shall be accurately classified, securely stored, retained for defined periods, and disposed of in a controlled and documented manner. The unauthorized creation, alteration, deletion, or disclosure of records is strictly prohibited. This program is designed to ensure the integrity, availability, and confidentiality of records in compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Records Classification and Labeling</h4><p>All records shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Classification metadata must be embedded within electronic records or clearly marked on physical records. Automated classification tools shall be employed where feasible for electronic data.</p><h4>5.2 Records Creation and Capture</h4><p>Records of all significant business transactions, decisions, and communications must be created and captured in the designated corporate system of record. Ad-hoc storage in personal drives or unauthorized cloud services is forbidden. Email deemed a record must be archived in accordance with the corporate email retention policy.</p><h4>5.3 Retention Schedule Management</h4><p>A formal, documented Retention Schedule, approved by Legal and Compliance, shall define the minimum retention period and final disposition action for every record series. This schedule must be reviewed annually and updated to reflect changes in law or business practice. Records shall not be destroyed before the expiration of their mandated retention period.</p><h4>5.4 Secure Storage and Access Control</h4><p>Records must be stored in authorized repositories with access controls enforcing the principle of least privilege. Confidential and Restricted records must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or equivalent. Physical records must be stored in locked, access-controlled facilities. Regular integrity checks (e.g., checksums) shall be performed on vital electronic records.</p><h4>5.5 Legal Hold Process</h4><p>Upon notification from Legal Counsel, a formal Legal Hold shall be initiated. The IT Department must immediately suspend any automated deletion processes for implicated records and ensure their preservation. A register of all active Legal Holds shall be maintained.</p><h4>5.6 Secure Disposition</h4><p>At the end of the retention period, records must be disposed of securely. Electronic records shall be purged using a method that renders recovery infeasible (e.g., cryptographic erasure, NIST 800-88 purge). Physical records shall be cross-cut shredded or incinerated by an approved vendor. A Certificate of Destruction must be obtained and retained for all third-party disposals.</p><h4>5.7 Audit Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All access, modification, deletion, and export activities within designated recordkeeping systems must be logged. Logs must capture user identity, timestamp, action, and record identifier. These logs shall be retained for a minimum of one year and protected from tampering. Regular audits of access logs shall be conducted to detect unauthorized activity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual frameworks, which may include but are not limited to: GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA, SOX, SEC Rule 17a-4, and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure. The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying applicable requirements and integrating them into the Retention Schedule and handling procedures. Failure to comply with this policy may result in legal sanctions, financial penalties, and reputational damage.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Overall governance and enforcement of this policy.<br><strong>Records Manager:</strong> Operational administration of the records management program, maintenance of the Retention Schedule.<br><strong>Department Heads:</strong> Ensure staff within their department comply with records management procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for creating, classifying, and handling records in accordance with this policy and associated procedures.<br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Issue Legal Holds and provide guidance on retention periods and legal obligations.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls for secure storage, access, logging, and disposition of electronic records.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Contractors and third parties in violation may have their agreements terminated. Deliberate destruction of records subject to a Legal Hold, or falsification of records, may result in criminal prosecution. All suspected policy violations must be reported immediately to the Information Security Office.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Records Management Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes in business operations, technology, or applicable law. Revisions must be approved by the designated governance committee. The latest approved version shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.677681,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:46:18.257,NULL
43,449810,Code of Conduct,"<p>This Code of Conduct establishes the mandatory ethical and professional standards of behavior for all personnel of [Company Name], including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other individuals acting on behalf of the organization. Its purpose is to protect the company's assets, reputation, and legal standing by defining acceptable conduct, fostering a secure and respectful work environment, and ensuring compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including those stipulated by SOC 2 and ISO 27001 frameworks.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals who access, use, or manage [Company Name] information assets, systems, networks, or facilities. This includes, but is not limited to, full-time and part-time employees, contractors, vendors, interns, and board members. The policy governs conduct during all company-related activities, whether conducted on company premises, remotely, or using personal devices for business purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, clients, or business partners, including trade secrets, financial data, source code, system designs, customer lists, and pre-release product information.<br><br><strong>Conflict of Interest:</strong> A situation where an individual's personal interests could improperly influence, or appear to influence, their professional judgment or duties to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Information Assets:</strong> All data, systems, hardware, software, and networks owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Personnel:</strong> All individuals covered under the Scope of this policy.<br><br><strong>Protected Classes:</strong> Categories protected from discrimination under applicable law, which may include race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, or veteran status.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Personnel shall conduct business with the highest degree of integrity, ethics, and professionalism. Adherence to this Code of Conduct is a condition of employment or engagement. Personnel are strictly prohibited from engaging in any activity that compromises the security, confidentiality, integrity, or availability of company information assets, violates the law, or damages the company's reputation. All Personnel are responsible for understanding and complying with this policy and are required to report any suspected violations.</p>

<h4>5.1 Ethical Business Practices and Conflicts of Interest</h4><p>5.1.1 Personnel shall avoid all conflicts of interest. Any actual or potential conflict must be disclosed in writing to the Chief Compliance Officer or designated manager immediately upon identification.<br>5.1.2 Offering, giving, soliciting, or accepting any form of bribe, kickback, or other improper inducement is strictly forbidden.<br>5.1.3 Personnel shall maintain accurate and complete business records. Falsification of records, timesheets, expense reports, or financial data is prohibited.</p><h4>5.2 Protection of Company Assets and Information Security</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall access Information Assets only to the extent required to perform their authorized job duties (Principle of Least Privilege).<br>5.2.2 All Confidential Information must be classified, handled, stored, and transmitted according to the company's Data Classification and Handling Policy. Unauthorized disclosure is prohibited.<br>5.2.3 Personnel shall use only company-approved software, hardware, and cloud services for business activities. The installation of unlicensed or unauthorized software is forbidden.<br>5.2.4 All workstations must be secured with a password-protected screensaver or locked when unattended. Mobile devices containing company data must employ encryption and remote wipe capabilities.</p><h4>5.3 Workplace Conduct and Harassment</h4><p>5.3.1 [Company Name] maintains a zero-tolerance policy for harassment, discrimination, or bullying based on any Protected Class or characteristic.<br>5.3.2 All workplace communications, whether verbal, written, or digital, shall be professional and respectful. The use of company communication systems for harassment, hate speech, or the dissemination of offensive material is prohibited.</p><h4>5.4 Compliance with Laws and Regulations</h4><p>5.4.1 Personnel must comply with all applicable local, national, and international laws and regulations in every jurisdiction where [Company Name] operates.<br>5.4.2 Personnel involved in software development or handling of personal data shall adhere to all relevant intellectual property laws, export controls, and data privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA).</p><h4>5.5 Reporting and Non-Retaliation</h4><p>5.5.1 Personnel are required to promptly report any known or suspected violation of this Code, or any illegal or unethical conduct, through the designated reporting channel (e.g., Ethics Hotline, direct manager, Legal Department).<br>5.5.2 [Company Name] strictly prohibits retaliation against any individual who, in good faith, reports a concern or participates in an investigation. Any act of retaliation will result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>

<p>This Code of Conduct is designed to ensure organizational compliance with key control objectives from ISO 27001 (A.6.1.1 Information security roles and responsibilities, A.7.1.1 Screening, A.8.1.1 Asset inventory) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy). Violations may constitute breaches of employment contracts, vendor agreements, and applicable law, potentially resulting in civil or criminal liability for the individual and [Company Name]. All Personnel shall complete mandatory annual training and acknowledge their understanding of this policy.</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this Code; reporting violations; and completing required training.<br><br><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for enforcing this Code within their teams, modeling appropriate behavior, and addressing concerns promptly.<br><br><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Is responsible for onboarding training, investigating reports of harassment or discrimination, and managing disciplinary actions in consultation with Legal.<br><br><strong>Information Security/IT Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls to enforce asset protection requirements (e.g., access controls, encryption).<br><br><strong>Chief Compliance Officer / Legal Department:</strong> Has ultimate authority for policy interpretation, managing the reporting and investigation process for ethical violations, and ensuring overall policy compliance.</p>

<p>Violations of this Code of Conduct will result in disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the offense. Actions may include, but are not limited to: mandatory corrective training, formal written warning, suspension of privileges, financial penalties, demotion, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal action. The specific disciplinary measures shall be determined by HR and the Legal Department in accordance with internal procedures and applicable law.</p>

<p>This Code of Conduct shall be reviewed at least annually by the Chief Compliance Officer and the Legal Department to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with changes in the legal landscape, industry standards, and [Company Name]'s operational environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version shall be made accessible to all Personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Code of Conduct establishes the mandatory ethical and professional standards of behavior for all personnel of [Company Name], including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other individuals acting on behalf of the organization. Its purpose is to protect the company's assets, reputation, and legal standing by defining acceptable conduct, fostering a secure and respectful work environment, and ensuring compliance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including those stipulated by SOC 2 and ISO 27001 frameworks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals who access, use, or manage [Company Name] information assets, systems, networks, or facilities. This includes, but is not limited to, full-time and part-time employees, contractors, vendors, interns, and board members. The policy governs conduct during all company-related activities, whether conducted on company premises, remotely, or using personal devices for business purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, clients, or business partners, including trade secrets, financial data, source code, system designs, customer lists, and pre-release product information.<br><br><strong>Conflict of Interest:</strong> A situation where an individual's personal interests could improperly influence, or appear to influence, their professional judgment or duties to [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Information Assets:</strong> All data, systems, hardware, software, and networks owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name].<br><br><strong>Personnel:</strong> All individuals covered under the Scope of this policy.<br><br><strong>Protected Classes:</strong> Categories protected from discrimination under applicable law, which may include race, color, religion, sex, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, sexual orientation, gender identity, or veteran status.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Personnel shall conduct business with the highest degree of integrity, ethics, and professionalism. Adherence to this Code of Conduct is a condition of employment or engagement. Personnel are strictly prohibited from engaging in any activity that compromises the security, confidentiality, integrity, or availability of company information assets, violates the law, or damages the company's reputation. All Personnel are responsible for understanding and complying with this policy and are required to report any suspected violations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Ethical Business Practices and Conflicts of Interest</h4><p>5.1.1 Personnel shall avoid all conflicts of interest. Any actual or potential conflict must be disclosed in writing to the Chief Compliance Officer or designated manager immediately upon identification.<br>5.1.2 Offering, giving, soliciting, or accepting any form of bribe, kickback, or other improper inducement is strictly forbidden.<br>5.1.3 Personnel shall maintain accurate and complete business records. Falsification of records, timesheets, expense reports, or financial data is prohibited.</p><h4>5.2 Protection of Company Assets and Information Security</h4><p>5.2.1 Personnel shall access Information Assets only to the extent required to perform their authorized job duties (Principle of Least Privilege).<br>5.2.2 All Confidential Information must be classified, handled, stored, and transmitted according to the company's Data Classification and Handling Policy. Unauthorized disclosure is prohibited.<br>5.2.3 Personnel shall use only company-approved software, hardware, and cloud services for business activities. The installation of unlicensed or unauthorized software is forbidden.<br>5.2.4 All workstations must be secured with a password-protected screensaver or locked when unattended. Mobile devices containing company data must employ encryption and remote wipe capabilities.</p><h4>5.3 Workplace Conduct and Harassment</h4><p>5.3.1 [Company Name] maintains a zero-tolerance policy for harassment, discrimination, or bullying based on any Protected Class or characteristic.<br>5.3.2 All workplace communications, whether verbal, written, or digital, shall be professional and respectful. The use of company communication systems for harassment, hate speech, or the dissemination of offensive material is prohibited.</p><h4>5.4 Compliance with Laws and Regulations</h4><p>5.4.1 Personnel must comply with all applicable local, national, and international laws and regulations in every jurisdiction where [Company Name] operates.<br>5.4.2 Personnel involved in software development or handling of personal data shall adhere to all relevant intellectual property laws, export controls, and data privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA).</p><h4>5.5 Reporting and Non-Retaliation</h4><p>5.5.1 Personnel are required to promptly report any known or suspected violation of this Code, or any illegal or unethical conduct, through the designated reporting channel (e.g., Ethics Hotline, direct manager, Legal Department).<br>5.5.2 [Company Name] strictly prohibits retaliation against any individual who, in good faith, reports a concern or participates in an investigation. Any act of retaliation will result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Code of Conduct is designed to ensure organizational compliance with key control objectives from ISO 27001 (A.6.1.1 Information security roles and responsibilities, A.7.1.1 Screening, A.8.1.1 Asset inventory) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality, and Privacy). Violations may constitute breaches of employment contracts, vendor agreements, and applicable law, potentially resulting in civil or criminal liability for the individual and [Company Name]. All Personnel shall complete mandatory annual training and acknowledge their understanding of this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this Code; reporting violations; and completing required training.<br><br><strong>Managers and Team Leads:</strong> Are responsible for enforcing this Code within their teams, modeling appropriate behavior, and addressing concerns promptly.<br><br><strong>Human Resources (HR) Department:</strong> Is responsible for onboarding training, investigating reports of harassment or discrimination, and managing disciplinary actions in consultation with Legal.<br><br><strong>Information Security/IT Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls to enforce asset protection requirements (e.g., access controls, encryption).<br><br><strong>Chief Compliance Officer / Legal Department:</strong> Has ultimate authority for policy interpretation, managing the reporting and investigation process for ethical violations, and ensuring overall policy compliance.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Violations of this Code of Conduct will result in disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the offense. Actions may include, but are not limited to: mandatory corrective training, formal written warning, suspension of privileges, financial penalties, demotion, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal action. The specific disciplinary measures shall be determined by HR and the Legal Department in accordance with internal procedures and applicable law.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Code of Conduct shall be reviewed at least annually by the Chief Compliance Officer and the Legal Department to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with changes in the legal landscape, industry standards, and [Company Name]'s operational environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version shall be made accessible to all Personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.780443,"""[\""SOC 2\"",\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:46:52.748,NULL
40,722099,Data Loss Prevention (DLP) Policy,"<p>This Data Loss Prevention (DLP) Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to proactively identify, monitor, and protect the sensitive data of [Company Name] from unauthorized access, exfiltration, or accidental disclosure. The policy is designed to safeguard information assets, ensure regulatory compliance, and mitigate risks associated with data breaches.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, networks, endpoints, and cloud environments owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who create, access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. The policy covers data at rest, in transit, and in use, regardless of location or format.</p>

<p><strong>Data Loss Prevention (DLP):</strong> A set of technologies, processes, and policies designed to detect and prevent the unauthorized transmission or exfiltration of sensitive data.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> Information classified as Confidential or Restricted per the [Company Name] Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to Personally Identifiable Information (PII), Protected Health Information (PHI), financial records, intellectual property, and authentication credentials.<br><strong>Data Exfiltration:</strong> The unauthorized transfer of data from within [Company Name]'s controlled environment to an external destination.<br><strong>Endpoint:</strong> Any device that can connect to the corporate network, including but not limited to laptops, desktops, mobile phones, and tablets.<br><strong>DLP Agent:</strong> Software installed on an endpoint to enforce DLP policies locally.</p>

<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the unauthorized disclosure, transmission, or exfiltration of sensitive data. All personnel shall adhere to the technical and procedural controls mandated herein to prevent data loss. The organization is committed to implementing a comprehensive DLP program that aligns with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of its information assets.</p>

<h4>5.1 Data Classification and Discovery</h4><p>5.1.1 All DLP controls shall be based on the data classification levels defined in the [Company Name] Data Classification Policy.<br>5.1.2 Automated discovery tools must be deployed to scan all data repositories, including file shares, databases, and cloud storage, to identify and inventory stored sensitive data at least quarterly.<br>5.1.3 A data map detailing the location, type, volume, and custodian of all sensitive data assets shall be maintained and reviewed annually.</p><h4>5.2 Network DLP Controls</h4><p>5.2.1 All outbound network traffic (e.g., HTTP/S, FTP, SMTP, IM) must be inspected by a network-based DLP solution at the network perimeter.<br>5.2.2 The DLP solution must be configured to block, in real-time, any attempt to transmit sensitive data to unauthorized external destinations, including personal email, cloud storage, and public websites.<br>5.2.3 All encrypted traffic (TLS/SSL) shall be subject to DLP inspection via a man-in-the-middle proxy with appropriate decryption and re-encryption capabilities, following a documented risk assessment and legal review.</p><h4>5.3 Endpoint DLP Controls</h4><p>5.3.1 A DLP agent must be installed and actively running on all corporate-managed endpoints, including those used for remote work.<br>5.3.2 The endpoint DLP agent shall enforce policies to prevent copying of sensitive data to unauthorized removable media (e.g., USB drives, external hard drives).<br>5.3.3 The agent must monitor and control data transfer attempts via local applications (e.g., instant messaging, unauthorized cloud sync clients) and must log all policy violations.</p><h4>5.4 Cloud and Email DLP</h4><p>5.4.1 For sanctioned cloud applications (e.g., Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Salesforce), native or integrated cloud DLP capabilities must be enabled and configured to match corporate policy.<br>5.4.2 All outbound email must be scanned by a DLP solution. Emails containing sensitive data shall be automatically encrypted or blocked if sent to external recipients without a valid business justification and approved secure method.<br>5.4.3 Automated alerts must be generated for any attempted upload of sensitive data to unsanctioned cloud applications (Shadow IT).</p><h4>5.5 Incident Response and Logging</h4><p>5.5.1 All DLP events (attempted violations, blocks, alerts) must be logged with immutable timestamps, user identity, data involved, source, and destination.<br>5.5.2 DLP logs shall be centrally aggregated into the Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation and compliance audits.<br>5.5.3 A formal process must be established to triage, investigate, and remediate all DLP alerts. Confirmed incidents shall be escalated per the [Company Name] Incident Response Plan.</p><h4>5.6 Policy Configuration and Management</h4><p>5.6.1 DLP policy rules shall be reviewed, tested, and updated at least semi-annually or following significant changes to business processes or data types.<br>5.6.2 All changes to DLP policy configurations must follow the formal Change Management process and require approval from the Information Security team.<br>5.6.3 DLP systems must be integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory) to ensure user-based policy enforcement and accurate attribution.</p>

<p>The DLP program shall be designed to support compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, and PCI-DSS. The technical controls and logging mechanisms mandated in Section 5 are essential for demonstrating compliance with ISO 27001 Annex A controls (e.g., A.8.2.3, A.13.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. Regular audits will verify the effectiveness of these controls.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the DLP program's effectiveness and compliance.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, monitoring, and tuning of DLP systems and policies.<br><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Responsible for the deployment, maintenance, and availability of DLP infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Custodians:</strong> Responsible for classifying data and approving DLP policy exceptions for their respective data sets.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with this policy, complete associated training, and report any suspected data loss incidents immediately.</p>

<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may lead to legal prosecution. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor, intercept, and block any data transmissions to enforce this policy. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO or designated delegate.</p>

<p>This Data Loss Prevention Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually or in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Data Loss Prevention (DLP) Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to proactively identify, monitor, and protect the sensitive data of [Company Name] from unauthorized access, exfiltration, or accidental disclosure. The policy is designed to safeguard information assets, ensure regulatory compliance, and mitigate risks associated with data breaches.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information systems, networks, endpoints, and cloud environments owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name]. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party agents who create, access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. The policy covers data at rest, in transit, and in use, regardless of location or format.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Data Loss Prevention (DLP):</strong> A set of technologies, processes, and policies designed to detect and prevent the unauthorized transmission or exfiltration of sensitive data.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> Information classified as Confidential or Restricted per the [Company Name] Data Classification Policy, including but not limited to Personally Identifiable Information (PII), Protected Health Information (PHI), financial records, intellectual property, and authentication credentials.<br><strong>Data Exfiltration:</strong> The unauthorized transfer of data from within [Company Name]'s controlled environment to an external destination.<br><strong>Endpoint:</strong> Any device that can connect to the corporate network, including but not limited to laptops, desktops, mobile phones, and tablets.<br><strong>DLP Agent:</strong> Software installed on an endpoint to enforce DLP policies locally.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] strictly prohibits the unauthorized disclosure, transmission, or exfiltration of sensitive data. All personnel shall adhere to the technical and procedural controls mandated herein to prevent data loss. The organization is committed to implementing a comprehensive DLP program that aligns with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria to ensure the confidentiality and integrity of its information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Data Classification and Discovery</h4><p>5.1.1 All DLP controls shall be based on the data classification levels defined in the [Company Name] Data Classification Policy.<br>5.1.2 Automated discovery tools must be deployed to scan all data repositories, including file shares, databases, and cloud storage, to identify and inventory stored sensitive data at least quarterly.<br>5.1.3 A data map detailing the location, type, volume, and custodian of all sensitive data assets shall be maintained and reviewed annually.</p><h4>5.2 Network DLP Controls</h4><p>5.2.1 All outbound network traffic (e.g., HTTP/S, FTP, SMTP, IM) must be inspected by a network-based DLP solution at the network perimeter.<br>5.2.2 The DLP solution must be configured to block, in real-time, any attempt to transmit sensitive data to unauthorized external destinations, including personal email, cloud storage, and public websites.<br>5.2.3 All encrypted traffic (TLS/SSL) shall be subject to DLP inspection via a man-in-the-middle proxy with appropriate decryption and re-encryption capabilities, following a documented risk assessment and legal review.</p><h4>5.3 Endpoint DLP