ent process and require approval from the Information Security team.<br>5.6.3 DLP systems must be integrated with the corporate directory (e.g., Active Directory) to ensure user-based policy enforcement and accurate attribution.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The DLP program shall be designed to support compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, and PCI-DSS. The technical controls and logging mechanisms mandated in Section 5 are essential for demonstrating compliance with ISO 27001 Annex A controls (e.g., A.8.2.3, A.13.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. Regular audits will verify the effectiveness of these controls.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the DLP program's effectiveness and compliance.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, monitoring, and tuning of DLP systems and policies.<br><strong>IT Operations:</strong> Responsible for the deployment, maintenance, and availability of DLP infrastructure.<br><strong>Data Owners/Custodians:</strong> Responsible for classifying data and approving DLP policy exceptions for their respective data sets.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with this policy, complete associated training, and report any suspected data loss incidents immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and may lead to legal prosecution. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor, intercept, and block any data transmissions to enforce this policy. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the CISO or designated delegate.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Data Loss Prevention Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually or in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.460871,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:49:11.545,NULL
45,170568,Security Awareness Training Policy,"<p>This Security Awareness Training Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for a formal, continuous, and measurable security awareness and training program at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure all personnel understand their role in protecting information assets, recognize security threats, and comply with applicable security policies, procedures, and legal obligations. This policy is designed to cultivate a security-conscious culture and reduce human-related security risks.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals who are granted access to [Company Name] information systems, data, or facilities (collectively referred to as ""Personnel""). The policy encompasses all training activities, materials, delivery methods, and compliance measurements related to information security awareness.</p>

<p><strong>Annual Security Training:</strong> A comprehensive, mandatory training module covering core security topics, required to be completed by all Personnel within a defined calendar period.<br/><strong>Phishing Simulation:</strong> A controlled, internal campaign that mimics real-world phishing attacks to test and improve Personnel's ability to identify malicious messages.<br/><strong>Role-Based Training:</strong> Specialized security training tailored to the specific duties and risks associated with a particular job function (e.g., system administrators, developers, HR personnel).<br/><strong>Security Culture:</strong> The collective attitudes, perceptions, and behaviors within the organization regarding the protection of information assets.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Personnel shall participate in an ongoing security awareness and training program. This program is a critical component of the organization's defense-in-depth strategy and is essential for compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, HIPAA, and other regulatory frameworks. Failure to complete required training shall result in disciplinary action, up to and including revocation of system access and termination of employment or contract.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Training Curriculum and Content</strong><br/>The Information Security Department, in collaboration with Human Resources, shall develop and maintain a formal security awareness curriculum. This curriculum must be updated at least annually and shall include, at a minimum, coverage of: password hygiene, phishing and social engineering, physical security, acceptable use of assets, data classification and handling, incident reporting procedures, mobile device security, and remote work security. All training content shall be role-appropriate and reflect current threat intelligence.</p><p><strong>5.2 Training Frequency and Scheduling</strong><br/>All new Personnel must complete initial security awareness training within 30 days of hire or contract commencement. All Personnel shall complete an Annual Security Training module. Personnel in high-risk roles (as defined by the Information Security Department) shall complete additional, role-based training at least semi-annually.</p><p><strong>5.3 Training Delivery and Tracking</strong><br/>All formal training shall be delivered through the company's approved Learning Management System (LMS) or a similarly controlled platform that provides verifiable completion records, including user identity, completion timestamp, and assessment scores. Instructor-led sessions must maintain a signed attendance roster. Training completion records shall be retained for a minimum of three years for audit purposes.</p><p><strong>5.4 Phishing Awareness Program</strong><br/>The Information Security Department shall conduct regular, unannounced Phishing Simulations targeting all Personnel with email access. The frequency shall be no less than quarterly. Personnel who fail a simulation shall be automatically enrolled in remedial training. Aggregate metrics (click rates, report rates) shall be tracked and reported to management.</p><p><strong>5.5 Knowledge Assessments and Effectiveness</strong><br/>All mandatory training modules shall include a knowledge assessment (e.g., quiz) with a minimum passing score of 80%. Personnel failing to achieve the passing score on the first attempt must re-take the assessment until successful. The Information Security Department shall analyze training and phishing metrics annually to measure program effectiveness and identify areas for improvement.</p><p><strong>5.6 Communication and Reinforcement</strong><br/>The Information Security Department shall issue regular security awareness communications (e.g., monthly newsletters, intranet articles, posters) to reinforce key concepts and highlight emerging threats between formal training sessions.</p>

<p>The security awareness training program shall be designed to satisfy explicit and implicit requirements of relevant standards and regulations, including but not limited to: ISO 27001:2022 Annex A control 7.2.2 (Information security awareness, education, and training), SOC 2 Common Criteria CC2.2 (Communication and Information), and HIPAA Security Rule §164.308(a)(5) (Security Awareness and Training). Training records are considered confidential and shall be protected as such; they shall be made available to auditors and regulators upon request.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the approval, oversight, and effectiveness of the security awareness program.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for developing the training curriculum, conducting phishing simulations, tracking compliance, measuring effectiveness, and reporting metrics.<br/><strong>Human Resources Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring new hires are enrolled in initial training and for enforcing disciplinary actions related to non-compliance.<br/><strong>Department Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for ensuring their staff complete all required training within mandated deadlines and for fostering a security-conscious environment within their teams.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for completing all assigned security training promptly, applying the knowledge gained, and reporting suspected security incidents.</p>

<p>Personnel who fail to complete mandatory security awareness training by the assigned deadline shall have their network access automatically suspended until the training is completed. Repeated failures or willful neglect of security training requirements shall be considered a violation of the Acceptable Use Policy and will be subject to formal disciplinary action, which may include written warnings, suspension of privileges, or termination of employment or contract, in accordance with [Company Name]'s disciplinary procedures.</p>

<p>This Security Awareness Training Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology environment, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made available to all Personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Security Awareness Training Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for a formal, continuous, and measurable security awareness and training program at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure all personnel understand their role in protecting information assets, recognize security threats, and comply with applicable security policies, procedures, and legal obligations. This policy is designed to cultivate a security-conscious culture and reduce human-related security risks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals who are granted access to [Company Name] information systems, data, or facilities (collectively referred to as \\\""Personnel\\\""). The policy encompasses all training activities, materials, delivery methods, and compliance measurements related to information security awareness.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Annual Security Training:</strong> A comprehensive, mandatory training module covering core security topics, required to be completed by all Personnel within a defined calendar period.<br/><strong>Phishing Simulation:</strong> A controlled, internal campaign that mimics real-world phishing attacks to test and improve Personnel's ability to identify malicious messages.<br/><strong>Role-Based Training:</strong> Specialized security training tailored to the specific duties and risks associated with a particular job function (e.g., system administrators, developers, HR personnel).<br/><strong>Security Culture:</strong> The collective attitudes, perceptions, and behaviors within the organization regarding the protection of information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Personnel shall participate in an ongoing security awareness and training program. This program is a critical component of the organization's defense-in-depth strategy and is essential for compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, HIPAA, and other regulatory frameworks. Failure to complete required training shall result in disciplinary action, up to and including revocation of system access and termination of employment or contract.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Training Curriculum and Content</strong><br/>The Information Security Department, in collaboration with Human Resources, shall develop and maintain a formal security awareness curriculum. This curriculum must be updated at least annually and shall include, at a minimum, coverage of: password hygiene, phishing and social engineering, physical security, acceptable use of assets, data classification and handling, incident reporting procedures, mobile device security, and remote work security. All training content shall be role-appropriate and reflect current threat intelligence.</p><p><strong>5.2 Training Frequency and Scheduling</strong><br/>All new Personnel must complete initial security awareness training within 30 days of hire or contract commencement. All Personnel shall complete an Annual Security Training module. Personnel in high-risk roles (as defined by the Information Security Department) shall complete additional, role-based training at least semi-annually.</p><p><strong>5.3 Training Delivery and Tracking</strong><br/>All formal training shall be delivered through the company's approved Learning Management System (LMS) or a similarly controlled platform that provides verifiable completion records, including user identity, completion timestamp, and assessment scores. Instructor-led sessions must maintain a signed attendance roster. Training completion records shall be retained for a minimum of three years for audit purposes.</p><p><strong>5.4 Phishing Awareness Program</strong><br/>The Information Security Department shall conduct regular, unannounced Phishing Simulations targeting all Personnel with email access. The frequency shall be no less than quarterly. Personnel who fail a simulation shall be automatically enrolled in remedial training. Aggregate metrics (click rates, report rates) shall be tracked and reported to management.</p><p><strong>5.5 Knowledge Assessments and Effectiveness</strong><br/>All mandatory training modules shall include a knowledge assessment (e.g., quiz) with a minimum passing score of 80%. Personnel failing to achieve the passing score on the first attempt must re-take the assessment until successful. The Information Security Department shall analyze training and phishing metrics annually to measure program effectiveness and identify areas for improvement.</p><p><strong>5.6 Communication and Reinforcement</strong><br/>The Information Security Department shall issue regular security awareness communications (e.g., monthly newsletters, intranet articles, posters) to reinforce key concepts and highlight emerging threats between formal training sessions.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The security awareness training program shall be designed to satisfy explicit and implicit requirements of relevant standards and regulations, including but not limited to: ISO 27001:2022 Annex A control 7.2.2 (Information security awareness, education, and training), SOC 2 Common Criteria CC2.2 (Communication and Information), and HIPAA Security Rule §164.308(a)(5) (Security Awareness and Training). Training records are considered confidential and shall be protected as such; they shall be made available to auditors and regulators upon request.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the approval, oversight, and effectiveness of the security awareness program.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for developing the training curriculum, conducting phishing simulations, tracking compliance, measuring effectiveness, and reporting metrics.<br/><strong>Human Resources Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring new hires are enrolled in initial training and for enforcing disciplinary actions related to non-compliance.<br/><strong>Department Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for ensuring their staff complete all required training within mandated deadlines and for fostering a security-conscious environment within their teams.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for completing all assigned security training promptly, applying the knowledge gained, and reporting suspected security incidents.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Personnel who fail to complete mandatory security awareness training by the assigned deadline shall have their network access automatically suspended until the training is completed. Repeated failures or willful neglect of security training requirements shall be considered a violation of the Acceptable Use Policy and will be subject to formal disciplinary action, which may include written warnings, suspension of privileges, or termination of employment or contract, in accordance with [Company Name]'s disciplinary procedures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Security Awareness Training Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology environment, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy shall be made available to all Personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.994313,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""HIPAA\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:47:22.122,NULL
46,855932,Social Media Policy,"<p>This Social Media Policy establishes the mandatory governance, security, and acceptable use requirements for all activities involving [Company Name]'s official social media accounts, employee use of social media in a professional capacity, and any public communications that may be associated with [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect corporate information assets, safeguard the company's reputation, and ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including those stipulated by ISO/IEC 27001:2022.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all [Company Name] personnel (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all information assets owned by or entrusted to [Company Name]. It governs:</p><ul><li>Official corporate social media accounts and pages.</li><li>Any professional use of social media platforms that references [Company Name], its business, clients, partners, or employees.</li><li>Personal use of social media that could reasonably be linked to an individual's affiliation with [Company Name] and that may impact the company's security, reputation, or legal standing.</li></ul>

<p><strong>Social Media:</strong> Any digital platform, website, or application that enables users to create and share content or to participate in social networking. This includes, but is not limited to, platforms such as LinkedIn, X (formerly Twitter), Facebook, Instagram, YouTube, TikTok, and professional forums.</p><p><strong>Official Corporate Account:</strong> Any social media account created to represent [Company Name], a department, or a business unit, and is used for official business communications.</p><p><strong>Brand Ambassador:</strong> An employee authorized by the Marketing or Communications Department to represent [Company Name] on social media in a professional capacity.</p><p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name]'s business, including trade secrets, financial data, client lists, internal project details, unreleased product information, and any information protected by non-disclosure agreements (NDAs).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all social media activities involving its personnel, information, or representation shall be conducted in a secure, professional, and legally compliant manner. The use of social media must not compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of corporate information assets, nor damage the company's reputation. All personnel are personally accountable for their online conduct as it relates to [Company Name].</p>

<h4>5.1 Account Establishment and Management</h4><p>The creation of any Official Corporate Account <strong>must</strong> receive prior written approval from the Head of Marketing and the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). All such accounts shall be registered using a corporate-managed email address and enrolled in the company's centralized identity and access management (IAM) system. Credentials for these accounts <strong>are strictly prohibited</strong> from being stored in plaintext or shared via unsecured channels (e.g., email, instant messaging).</p><h4>5.2 Authentication and Access Control</h4><p>Access to Official Corporate Accounts <strong>shall</strong> be provisioned based on the principle of least privilege. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) <strong>must</strong> be enabled on all social media platform accounts where the feature is available. Access rights <strong>must</strong> be reviewed quarterly by the account custodian and the IT Security team, with immediate revocation upon role change or termination.</p><h4>5.3 Content Classification and Posting</h4><p>All content intended for posting on Official Corporate Accounts <strong>must</strong> be classified prior to publication. Content containing or referencing Confidential Information <strong>is forbidden</strong>. A formal review and approval workflow, managed by the Communications Department, <strong>shall</strong> be followed for all posts. Drafts and scheduled posts <strong>must</strong> be stored within the approved social media management platform, not on personal devices or cloud storage.</p><h4>5.4 Employee Conduct and Disclaimers</h4><p>Employees acting as Brand Ambassadors or discussing [Company Name] in a professional context <strong>must</strong> include a clear disclaimer in their profile or relevant posts stating that views expressed are their own. Engaging in online debates, disparaging remarks about competitors, clients, or colleagues, or posting discriminatory content <strong>is strictly prohibited</strong>. Employees <strong>shall not</strong> imply they are speaking on [Company Name]'s official behalf without explicit authorization.</p><h4>5.5 Security and Incident Reporting</h4><p>Any suspected compromise of an Official Corporate Account (e.g., unauthorized login, strange posts) <strong>must</strong> be reported immediately to the IT Security Incident Response Team. Personal devices used to access corporate accounts <strong>must</strong> comply with the Mobile Device Management (MDM) policy, including enforced encryption and the ability for remote wipe. The use of public or unsecured Wi-Fi networks to manage corporate social media accounts <strong>is forbidden</strong> without an established and approved VPN connection.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring and Logging</h4><p>All administrative actions on Official Corporate Accounts (e.g., posting, editing, user management) <strong>shall</strong> be logged. Logs <strong>must</strong> include user identity, timestamp, action performed, and IP address. These logs <strong>shall</strong> be retained for a minimum of 90 days and be made available for security audits and forensic investigations. The Communications Department <strong>shall</strong> conduct monthly audits of account permissions and posted content for policy compliance.</p>

<p>Social media activities <strong>must</strong> adhere to all applicable laws and regulations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), copyright and trademark laws, financial disclosure regulations (e.g., SEC Rule 10b-5), and advertising standards. The Legal Department <strong>shall</strong> be consulted prior to any campaign involving contests, user-generated content, or influencer partnerships. This policy supports controls from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A, including A.6.2 (Teleworking), A.7.2 (During Employment), A.8.1 (Responsibility for Assets), A.9.2 (User Access Management), A.13.2 (Information Transfer), and A.16.1 (Management of Information Security Incidents).</p>

<p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this policy. Must report policy violations or security incidents.</p><p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the enforcement and interpretation of this policy's security controls. Responsible for risk assessment and audit coordination.</p><p><strong>Head of Marketing / Communications:</strong> Responsible for the strategic use of Official Corporate Accounts, content approval workflows, and brand consistency. Designates Brand Ambassadors.</p><p><strong>IT Security Team:</strong> Responsible for implementing technical controls (MFA, logging), conducting access reviews, and responding to security incidents involving social media accounts.</p><p><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for advising on regulatory compliance, intellectual property, and liability related to social media use.</p><p><strong>People & Culture / HR:</strong> Responsible for communicating this policy to employees and integrating it into the employee onboarding process. Manages disciplinary actions for violations.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy <strong>shall</strong> result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal action. The CISO and Head of Human Resources <strong>are authorized</strong> to investigate suspected violations. The company reserves the right to monitor activity on corporate-owned devices and networks, and on Official Corporate Accounts, to ensure compliance with this policy.</p>

<p>This policy <strong>shall</strong> be reviewed annually by the CISO and the Head of Communications, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, business operations, or regulatory requirements. All revisions <strong>must</strong> be approved by the CISO and General Counsel. The latest version of this policy is the only authoritative version and will be maintained in the company's central policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Social Media Policy establishes the mandatory governance, security, and acceptable use requirements for all activities involving [Company Name]'s official social media accounts, employee use of social media in a professional capacity, and any public communications that may be associated with [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect corporate information assets, safeguard the company's reputation, and ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including those stipulated by ISO/IEC 27001:2022.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all [Company Name] personnel (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all information assets owned by or entrusted to [Company Name]. It governs:</p><ul><li>Official corporate social media accounts and pages.</li><li>Any professional use of social media platforms that references [Company Name], its business, clients, partners, or employees.</li><li>Personal use of social media that could reasonably be linked to an individual's affiliation with [Company Name] and that may impact the company's security, reputation, or legal standing.</li></ul>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Social Media:</strong> Any digital platform, website, or application that enables users to create and share content or to participate in social networking. This includes, but is not limited to, platforms such as LinkedIn, X (formerly Twitter), Facebook, Instagram, YouTube, TikTok, and professional forums.</p><p><strong>Official Corporate Account:</strong> Any social media account created to represent [Company Name], a department, or a business unit, and is used for official business communications.</p><p><strong>Brand Ambassador:</strong> An employee authorized by the Marketing or Communications Department to represent [Company Name] on social media in a professional capacity.</p><p><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name]'s business, including trade secrets, financial data, client lists, internal project details, unreleased product information, and any information protected by non-disclosure agreements (NDAs).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all social media activities involving its personnel, information, or representation shall be conducted in a secure, professional, and legally compliant manner. The use of social media must not compromise the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of corporate information assets, nor damage the company's reputation. All personnel are personally accountable for their online conduct as it relates to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Account Establishment and Management</h4><p>The creation of any Official Corporate Account <strong>must</strong> receive prior written approval from the Head of Marketing and the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO). All such accounts shall be registered using a corporate-managed email address and enrolled in the company's centralized identity and access management (IAM) system. Credentials for these accounts <strong>are strictly prohibited</strong> from being stored in plai