"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.095216,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:48:00.251,NULL
39,977900,Open Source Software Policy,"<p>This Open Source Software (OSS) Policy establishes the mandatory governance, security, and compliance framework for the evaluation, approval, acquisition, use, and management of all open source software within [Company Name]. The policy is designed to mitigate risks associated with license non-compliance, security vulnerabilities, and operational instability while enabling the strategic benefits of OSS. It ensures alignment with [Company Name]'s information security objectives and compliance with ISO 27001 (A.8.1.3, A.12.6.2, A.14.2.1) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1) requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively ""Personnel"") who develop, procure, integrate, deploy, or manage software on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all OSS components, including but not limited to: libraries, frameworks, packages, modules, snippets, and modified versions of OSS (""forks"") used in any [Company Name] system, application, service, or product, whether for internal use, embedded in commercial offerings, or deployed in cloud environments.</p>

<p><strong>Open Source Software (OSS):</strong> Software for which the human-readable source code is made available under a license that permits use, modification, and redistribution with or without fee. Common licenses include GNU General Public License (GPL), Apache License 2.0, MIT License, and BSD variants.<br><br><strong>Copyleft License:</strong> A type of OSS license (e.g., GPL, AGPL) that requires derivative works or combined works to be distributed under the same license terms.<br><br><strong>Permissive License:</strong> A type of OSS license (e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0) with minimal restrictions on use, modification, and redistribution, typically requiring only attribution.<br><br><strong>Software Bill of Materials (SBOM):</strong> A formal, machine-readable inventory of all software components, including OSS dependencies, their versions, and license details.<br><br><strong>Vulnerability Disclosure:</strong> The process of identifying, reporting, and remediating known security flaws in OSS components.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that the use of any Open Source Software shall be governed by a formal risk-based approval process. All OSS components must be inventoried, continuously monitored for security vulnerabilities, and managed in strict compliance with their respective license obligations. Unauthorized use, modification, or distribution of OSS is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Pre-Approval and Risk Assessment</h4><p>All proposed use of OSS, regardless of deployment context, must undergo a formal pre-approval review prior to download, integration, or deployment. The review shall include a comprehensive analysis of the OSS component's license terms, security posture, maintenance status, and architectural fit. The use of OSS under strong Copyleft licenses (e.g., GPLv3, AGPL) in commercial products or core infrastructure is expressly forbidden without written approval from the Legal and Information Security departments.</p><h4>5.2 License Compliance and Obligations</h4><p>Personnel must accurately identify and document the specific license(s) governing every OSS component. A complete and accurate Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) shall be maintained for all production systems and software products. All mandatory license obligations, including copyright notices, license text reproduction, and source code availability requirements, must be fulfilled prior to distribution or deployment. Automated license scanning tools shall be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to enforce this requirement.</p><h4>5.3 Security Vulnerability Management</h4><p>All approved OSS components shall be continuously monitored against authoritative vulnerability databases (e.g., NVD, GitHub Security Advisories). Critical or High-severity vulnerabilities identified in OSS dependencies must be remediated or a risk-accepted mitigation plan implemented within 7 calendar days of disclosure. The use of OSS components that are no longer maintained by the upstream project or community is strictly prohibited in production environments.</p><h4>5.4 Acquisition and Source Integrity</h4><p>OSS shall only be acquired from official, project-maintained repositories or vetted, organization-approved internal mirrors. Downloads from unofficial or untrusted third-party sources are forbidden. All OSS binaries integrated into production artifacts must be verified using cryptographic hashes or digital signatures provided by the official project to ensure integrity and provenance.</p><h4>5.5 Inventory and Lifecycle Management</h4><p>A centralized OSS registry, integrated with the SBOM, shall be maintained to track all approved components, their versions, associated licenses, and deployment locations. A formal process for periodic review and upgrade of OSS components shall be established to maintain supportability and security. End-of-life (EOL) OSS components must be removed or replaced according to a defined sunset schedule.</p><h4>5.6 Modification and Distribution</h4><p>Any modification to OSS source code (""forking"") requires explicit architectural and security review. Modified OSS must be clearly tagged and tracked separately from upstream sources. The distribution of any software containing OSS, either internally or to third parties, must be preceded by a final license compliance review to ensure all obligations are met.</p>

<p>This policy enforces controls to meet specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 Annex A, including A.8.1.3 (Acceptable use of assets), A.12.6.2 (Restrictions on software installation), and A.14.2.1 (Secure development policy). For SOC 2, it directly supports Criteria CC6.1 (Logical and Physical Access Controls) and CC7.1 (System Operations) by governing the software components that constitute the system. Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action from third-party rights holders, regulatory penalties, and material breaches of customer contracts.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval, enforcement, and oversight of the OSS security review process.<br><br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for interpreting OSS license terms, advising on compliance obligations, and approving high-risk license usage.<br><br><strong>Development/Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for initiating OSS approval requests, maintaining accurate SBOMs, and implementing vulnerability remediations within mandated timeframes.<br><br><strong>Platform/DevOps Teams:</strong> Responsible for maintaining the approved OSS sources, the centralized registry, and enforcing policy controls within CI/CD pipelines and deployment platforms.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with this policy and to report any suspected unapproved or non-compliant OSS use.</p>

<p>Personnel found to be in violation of this policy will be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may be held personally liable for damages incurred by [Company Name]. The Information Security Department is authorized to scan, audit, and quarantine any system or code repository to identify non-compliant OSS. Projects or products containing unapproved or non-compliant OSS shall be prohibited from deployment or release until all violations are remediated.</p>

<p>This Open Source Software Policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and Legal Department, or following a significant compliance incident or change in relevant law or regulation. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all Personnel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Open Source Software (OSS) Policy establishes the mandatory governance, security, and compliance framework for the evaluation, approval, acquisition, use, and management of all open source software within [Company Name]. The policy is designed to mitigate risks associated with license non-compliance, security vulnerabilities, and operational instability while enabling the strategic benefits of OSS. It ensures alignment with [Company Name]'s information security objectives and compliance with ISO 27001 (A.8.1.3, A.12.6.2, A.14.2.1) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC7.1) requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other personnel (collectively \\\""Personnel\\\"") who develop, procure, integrate, deploy, or manage software on behalf of [Company Name]. It encompasses all OSS components, including but not limited to: libraries, frameworks, packages, modules, snippets, and modified versions of OSS (\\\""forks\\\"") used in any [Company Name] system, application, service, or product, whether for internal use, embedded in commercial offerings, or deployed in cloud environments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Open Source Software (OSS):</strong> Software for which the human-readable source code is made available under a license that permits use, modification, and redistribution with or without fee. Common licenses include GNU General Public License (GPL), Apache License 2.0, MIT License, and BSD variants.<br><br><strong>Copyleft License:</strong> A type of OSS license (e.g., GPL, AGPL) that requires derivative works or combined works to be distributed under the same license terms.<br><br><strong>Permissive License:</strong> A type of OSS license (e.g., MIT, Apache 2.0) with minimal restrictions on use, modification, and redistribution, typically requiring only attribution.<br><br><strong>Software Bill of Materials (SBOM):</strong> A formal, machine-readable inventory of all software components, including OSS dependencies, their versions, and license details.<br><br><strong>Vulnerability Disclosure:</strong> The process of identifying, reporting, and remediating known security flaws in OSS components.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that the use of any Open Source Software shall be governed by a formal risk-based approval process. All OSS components must be inventoried, continuously monitored for security vulnerabilities, and managed in strict compliance with their respective license obligations. Unauthorized use, modification, or distribution of OSS is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Pre-Approval and Risk Assessment</h4><p>All proposed use of OSS, regardless of deployment context, must undergo a formal pre-approval review prior to download, integration, or deployment. The review shall include a comprehensive analysis of the OSS component's license terms, security posture, maintenance status, and architectural fit. The use of OSS under strong Copyleft licenses (e.g., GPLv3, AGPL) in commercial products or core infrastructure is expressly forbidden without written approval from the Legal and Information Security departments.</p><h4>5.2 License Compliance and Obligations</h4><p>Personnel must accurately identify and document the specific license(s) governing every OSS component. A complete and accurate Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) shall be maintained for all production systems and software products. All mandatory license obligations, including copyright notices, license text reproduction, and source code availability requirements, must be fulfilled prior to distribution or deployment. Automated license scanning tools shall be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to enforce this requirement.</p><h4>5.3 Security Vulnerability Management</h4><p>All approved OSS components shall be continuously monitored against authoritative vulnerability databases (e.g., NVD, GitHub Security Advisories). Critical or High-severity vulnerabilities identified in OSS dependencies must be remediated or a risk-accepted mitigation plan implemented within 7 calendar days of disclosure. The use of OSS components that are no longer maintained by the upstream project or community is strictly prohibited in production environments.</p><h4>5.4 Acquisition and Source Integrity</h4><p>OSS shall only be acquired from official, project-maintained repositories or vetted, organization-approved internal mirrors. Downloads from unofficial or untrusted third-party sources are forbidden. All OSS binaries integrated into production artifacts must be verified using cryptographic hashes or digital signatures provided by the official project to ensure integrity and provenance.</p><h4>5.5 Inventory and Lifecycle Management</h4><p>A centralized OSS registry, integrated with the SBOM, shall be maintained to track all approved components, their versions, associated licenses, and deployment locations. A formal process for periodic review and upgrade of OSS components shall be established to maintain supportability and security. End-of-life (EOL) OSS components must be removed or replaced according to a defined sunset schedule.</p><h4>5.6 Modification and Distribution</h4><p>Any modification to OSS source code (\\\""forking\\\"") requires explicit architectural and security review. Modified OSS must be clearly tagged and tracked separately from upstream sources. The distribution of any software containing OSS, either internally or to third parties, must be preceded by a final license compliance review to ensure all obligations are met.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy enforces controls to meet specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 Annex A, including A.8.1.3 (Acceptable use of assets), A.12.6.2 (Restrictions on software installation), and A.14.2.1 (Secure development policy). For SOC 2, it directly supports Criteria CC6.1 (Logical and Physical Access Controls) and CC7.1 (System Operations) by governing the software components that constitute the system. Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action from third-party rights holders, regulatory penalties, and material breaches of customer contracts.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval, enforcement, and oversight of the OSS security review process.<br><br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for interpreting OSS license terms, advising on compliance obligations, and approving high-risk license usage.<br><br><strong>Development/Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for initiating OSS approval requests, maintaining accurate SBOMs, and implementing vulnerability remediations within mandated timeframes.<br><br><strong>Platform/DevOps Teams:</strong> Responsible for maintaining the approved OSS sources, the centralized registry, and enforcing policy controls within CI/CD pipelines and deployment platforms.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with this policy and to report any suspected unapproved or non-compliant OSS use.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Personnel found to be in violation of this policy will be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may be held personally liable for damages incurred by [Company Name]. The Information Security Department is authorized to scan, audit, and quarantine any system or code repository to identify non-compliant OSS. Projects or products containing unapproved or non-compliant OSS shall be prohibited from deployment or release until all violations are remediated.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Open Source Software Policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and Legal Department, or following a significant compliance incident or change in relevant law or regulation. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all Personnel. The latest approved version is the only authoritative document.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.353395,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:48:36.303,NULL
49,393282,Segregation of Duties Policy,"<p>This Segregation of Duties (SoD) Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to prevent fraud, error, and abuse by ensuring that no single individual has control over all phases of a critical business process or information system. The policy mandates the separation of incompatible functions, particularly those involving authorization, custody, record-keeping, and reconciliation, to enforce internal controls and mitigate risks related to confidentiality, integrity, and availability as required by SOC 2 and ISO 27001.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers (""personnel"") who are involved in the design, implementation, operation, or management of [Company Name]'s information systems, financial processes, and critical business operations. It encompasses all systems, applications, databases, and platforms that process, store, or transmit company data, including cloud services and on-premises infrastructure.</p>

<p><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of splitting tasks and associated privileges for a specific business process among multiple users to prevent a single point of failure or control.<br><strong>Incompatible Functions:</strong> Two or more duties that, if combined, would increase the risk of fraud or error (e.g., the ability to create a vendor and approve payments to that vendor).<br><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> System permissions that exceed those of a standard user, including administrative, root, or super-user rights that can bypass normal controls.<br><strong>SoD Conflict:</strong> A situation where a single user role or individual possesses permissions that create a violation of this policy.<br><strong>Compensating Control:</strong> An alternative security measure implemented to mitigate risk when full SoD cannot be achieved.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall strictly enforce the segregation of duties across all critical systems and business processes. The combination of authorization, execution, custody, and reconciliation duties for any single transaction or system control is expressly prohibited. All SoD controls shall be designed, implemented, and monitored to meet the control objectives of SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, Privacy) and the requirements of ISO 27001 Annex A controls, particularly A.6.1.2 (Segregation of duties) and A.9.2.3 (Management of privileged access rights).</p>

<h4>5.1 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Design</h4><p>All system access shall be provisioned based on pre-defined job roles, not individual user requests. Role definitions must be documented and must explicitly exclude combinations of permissions that create SoD conflicts. The IT Security Department must review and approve all role designs prior to implementation.</p><h4>5.2 Identification of Incompatible Functions</h4><p>For all critical processes (e.g., financial disbursements, user access provisioning, code deployment, data management), process owners shall formally document the discrete duties and identify incompatible combinations. This SoD matrix shall be reviewed annually and after any significant process change.</p><h4>5.3 Automated SoD Enforcement in Systems</h4><p>Where technically feasible, SoD rules shall be enforced programmatically within the system. Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP), Identity and Access Management (IAM), and financial systems must be configured to prevent the assignment of conflicting roles or entitlements to a single user account. Any exceptions must be logged and require formal approval.</p><h4>5.4 User Access Reviews and Recertification</h4><p>Management shall conduct quarterly user access reviews for all systems in scope. Reviews must specifically analyze user permissions for SoD conflicts. Any conflicts discovered must be remediated within five (5) business days. Evidence of these reviews shall be retained for audit.</p><h4>5.5 Management of Privileged Access</h4><p>Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators, database administrators) shall be strictly segregated from business process owner accounts. A privileged user shall not use their administrative account to perform routine business transactions. Privileged session activity must be logged and monitored independently.</p><h4>5.6 SoD in Change Management</h4><p>The roles of development, testing, and production deployment must be segregated. Personnel who develop or test application code shall not have the ability to promote that code to the production environment. Access to production data by developers is strictly prohibited unless authorized via a formal, time-limited request with compensating controls.</p><h4>5.7 Exception Handling and Compensating Controls</h4><p>Any exception to this policy, such as in cases of limited staff, must be submitted via a formal risk-accepted exception request. All exceptions must have documented compensating controls (e.g., enhanced logging, managerial review of all transactions) approved by the CISO and the relevant process owner. Exceptions shall be reviewed every six months for validity.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC6.2, CC6.8) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls A.6.1.2 and A.9.2.3. Violations of this policy may also constitute non-compliance with regulatory frameworks including but not limited to Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX), GDPR, and HIPAA, where applicable. The Legal and Compliance departments shall be consulted to align SoD controls with all relevant statutory and contractual obligations.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors/Audit Committee:</strong> Provides oversight of the SoD control framework.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, defines technical standards, and approves compensating controls.<br><strong>Process Owners (e.g., CFO, Head of HR):</strong> Identify incompatible functions within their domains and certify user access.<br><strong>IT Security Department:</strong> Implements technical SoD controls in systems, conducts access reviews, and monitors for conflicts.<br><strong>Internal Audit:</strong> Independently tests and verifies the operating effectiveness of SoD controls.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must understand and adhere to SoD requirements for their roles and report suspected violations.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Deliberate circumvention of SoD controls, including collusion or the sharing of credentials, is considered gross misconduct. The IT Security Department is authorized to immediately revoke access privileges involved in a confirmed SoD violation.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Internal Audit function at least annually, or following any significant security incident, regulatory change, or major system implementation. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant personnel. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Segregation of Duties (SoD) Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to prevent fraud, error, and abuse by ensuring that no single individual has control over all phases of a critical business process or information system. The policy mandates the separation of incompatible functions, particularly those involving authorization, custody, record-keeping, and reconciliation, to enforce internal controls and mitigate risks related to confidentiality, integrity, and availability as required by SOC 2 and ISO 27001.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers (\\\""personnel\\\"") who are involved in the design, implementation, operation, or management of [Company Name]'s information systems, financial processes, and critical business operations. It encompasses all systems, applications, databases, and platforms that process, store, or transmit company data, including cloud services and on-premises infrastructure.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Segregation of Duties (SoD):</strong> The principle of splitting tasks and associated privileges for a specific business process among multiple users to prevent a single point of failure or control.<br><strong>Incompatible Functions:</strong> Two or more duties that, if combined, would increase the risk of fraud or error (e.g., the ability to create a vendor and approve payments to that vendor).<br><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> System permissions that exceed those of a standard user, including administrative, root, or super-user rights that can bypass normal controls.<br><strong>SoD Conflict:</strong> A situation where a single user role or individual possesses permissions that create a violation of this policy.<br><strong>Compensating Control:</strong> An alternative security measure implemented to mitigate risk when full SoD cannot be achieved.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] shall strictly enforce the segregation of duties across all critical systems and business processes. The combination of authorization, execution, custody, and reconciliation duties for any single transaction or system control is expressly prohibited. All SoD controls shall be designed, implemented, and monitored to meet the control objectives of SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Processing Integrity, Confidentiality, Privacy) and the requirements of ISO 27001 Annex A controls, particularly A.6.1.2 (Segregation of duties) and A.9.2.3 (Management of privileged access rights).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) Design</h4><p>All system access shall be provisioned based on pre-defined job roles, not individual user requests. Role definitions must be documented and must explicitly exclude combinations of permissions that create SoD conflicts. The IT Security Department must review and approve all role designs prior to implementation.</p><h4>5.2 Identification of Incompatible Functions</h4><p>For all critical processes (e.g., financial disbursements, user access provisioning, code deployment, data management), process owners shall formally document the discrete duties and identify incompatible combinations. This SoD matrix shall be reviewed annually and after any significant process change.</p><h4>5.3 Automated SoD Enforcement in Systems</h4><p>Where technically feasible, SoD rules shall be enforced programmatically within the system. Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP), Identity and Access Management (IAM), and financial systems must be configured to prevent the assignment of conflicting roles or entitlements to a single user account. Any exceptions must be logged and require formal approval.</p><h4>5.4 User Access Reviews and Recertification</h4><p>Management shall conduct quarterly user access reviews for all systems in scope. Reviews must specifically analyze user permissions for SoD conflicts. Any conflicts discovered must be remediated within five (5) business days. Evidence of these reviews shall be retained for audit.</p><h4>5.5 Management of Privileged Access</h4><p>Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators, database administrators) shall be strictly segregated from business process owner accounts. A privileged user shall not use their administrative account to perform routine business transactions. Privileged session activity must be logged and monitored independently.</p><h4>5.6 SoD in Change Management</h4><p>The roles of development, testing, and production deployment must be segregated. Personnel who develop or test application code shall not have the ability to promote that code to the production environment. Access to production data by developers is strictly prohibited unless authorized via a formal, time-limited request with compensating controls.</p><h4>5.7 Exception Handling and Compensating Controls</h4><p>Any exception to this policy, such as in cases of limited staff, must be submitted via a formal risk-accepted exception request. All exceptions must have documented compensating controls (e.g., enhanced logging, managerial review of all transactions) approved by the CISO and the relevant process owner. Exceptions shall be reviewed every six months for validity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC6.2, CC6.8) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls A.6.1.2 and A.9.2.3. Violations of this policy may also constitute non-compliance with regulatory frameworks including but not limited to Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX), GDPR, and HIPAA, where applicable. The Legal and Compliance departments shall be consulted to align SoD controls with all relevant statutory and contractual obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Board of Directors/Audit Committee:</strong> Provides oversight of the SoD control framework.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, defines technical standards, and approves compensating controls.<br><strong>Process Owners (e.g., CFO, Head of HR):</strong> Identify incompatible functions within their domains and certify user access.<br><strong>IT Security Department:</strong> Implements technical SoD controls in systems, conducts access reviews, and monitors for conflicts.<br><strong>Internal Audit:</strong> Independently tests and verifies the operating effectiveness of SoD controls.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must understand and adhere to SoD requirements for their roles and report suspected violations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Deliberate circumvention of SoD controls, including collusion or the sharing of credentials, is considered gross misconduct. The IT Security Department is authorized to immediately revoke access privileges involved in a confirmed SoD violation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Internal Audit function at least annually, or following any significant security incident, regulatory change, or major system implementation. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant personnel. The latest version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel through the company's policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.420942,"""[\""SOC 2\"",\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:49:44.81,NULL
50,880077,Intellectual Property Policy,"<p>This Intellectual Property (IP) Policy establishes the authoritative framework for the identification, classification, protection, and management of all intellectual property assets owned by, created for, or entrusted to [Company Name]. Its purpose is to safeguard proprietary information, preserve competitive advantage, ensure legal compliance, and define clear ownership and usage rights, thereby protecting the organization's most valuable intangible assets from unauthorized disclosure, misuse, or loss.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals and entities with access to [Company Name]'s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary, and contractors), consultants, vendors, business partners, and any third-party agents. It encompasses all forms of intellectual property, whether in physical or digital format, stored on corporate systems, personal devices used for business purposes, cloud environments, or in physical locations under the company's control. This includes all company facilities, data centers, and remote work environments.</p>

<p><strong>Intellectual Property (IP):</strong> Creations of the mind for which exclusive legal rights are recognized. For the purposes of this policy, this includes, but is not limited to: Trade Secrets, Copyrights, Patents, Trademarks, Proprietary Software Code, Technical Know-How, Business Processes, Research Data, and Confidential Business Information.<br><br><strong>Proprietary Information:</strong> Any non-public information that provides a business advantage, including source code, algorithms, customer lists, financial projections, strategic plans, and unpublished research.<br><br><strong>Classified IP:</strong> IP assets that have been formally categorized according to the Data Classification Policy (e.g., Confidential, Restricted).<br><br><strong>Authorized User:</strong> An individual who has been granted explicit, role-based access to specific IP assets following the principle of least privilege.<br><br><strong>Data Loss Prevention (DLP):</strong> A set of tools and processes designed to detect and prevent unauthorized exfiltration of sensitive data.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates the strict protection of all its intellectual property assets. All IP created by employees within the scope of their employment, using company resources, or relating to the company's business shall be the sole and exclusive property of [Company Name]. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, destruction, or transfer of any company IP is strictly prohibited. All personnel are legally and contractually obligated to uphold the confidentiality and integrity of these assets in perpetuity, even following termination of engagement.</p>

<h4>5.1 IP Identification and Classification</h4><p>All potential IP assets must be formally identified and recorded in the corporate IP register. Each asset shall be classified according to the Data Classification Policy (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Classification labels must be applied digitally via metadata and, where applicable, physically on documents.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authorization</h4><p>Access to IP shall be governed by strict role-based access control (RBAC) and the principle of least privilege. Access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for accessing systems housing Confidential or Restricted IP. All access rights must be reviewed quarterly and revoked immediately upon role change or termination.</p><h4>5.3 Storage and Encryption</h4><p>Confidential and Restricted IP must be stored exclusively on approved, centrally managed corporate systems (e.g., designated secure file shares, version control systems for code). Storage on unauthorized cloud services, personal devices, or removable media is forbidden. All IP at rest classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. IP in transit over public networks must be protected via TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><h4>5.4 Network and Endpoint Security</h4><p>Endpoints used to access IP must have company-managed endpoint detection and response (EDR) software, full-disk encryption, and enforced screen locking. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate systems containing high-value IP. All network traffic to and from IP repositories must be logged and monitored for anomalous activity.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Data Loss Prevention (DLP)</h4><p>A full-featured DLP solution must be deployed to monitor all egress channels (email, web upload, cloud sync, removable media) for unauthorized transmission of classified IP. All policy violations must generate immediate alerts to the Security Operations Center (SOC). Regular data discovery scans must be conducted to identify unsecured IP on corporate and cloud assets.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Source Code Protection</h4><p>All proprietary software source code is considered Restricted IP. Code must be stored in a centralized, access-controlled version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history shall be immutable and tied to an authenticated user. Code repositories must be scanned for secrets (API keys, passwords) pre-commit. External contributions require signed contributor license agreements (CLAs).</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Vendor Management</h4><p>Any disclosure of IP to a third party (vendor, partner, contractor) requires a fully executed Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) with confidentiality clauses that survive termination. Third-party access must be time-bound, audited, and provisioned through a secure, isolated method (e.g., virtual desktop). Vendor systems used to process company IP must meet or exceed the security requirements outlined in this policy.</p><h4>5.8 Employee Onboarding, Training, and Offboarding</h4><p>IP protection clauses must be included in all employment and contractor agreements. Annual mandatory security awareness training, with specific modules on IP protection, is required for all personnel. Upon termination, all access to IP must be revoked simultaneously with HR notification, and a formal return/attestation of all company IP (physical and digital) must be completed.</p><h4>5.9 Audit Logging and Incident Response</h4><p>All access, modification, and transfer events involving Confidential or Restricted IP must generate immutable audit logs. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 365 days and protected from tampering. Any suspected or actual loss, theft, or unauthorized disclosure of IP constitutes a security incident and must be reported immediately per the Incident Response Policy for containment, investigation, and legal notification if required.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant intellectual property laws (e.g., Copyright Act, Defend Trade Secrets Act), contractual obligations, and industry frameworks including ISO 27001 (A.8.2.1 Classification of information, A.13.2.1 Information transfer policies and procedures) and SOC 2 (Criteria CC6.1, CC7.1). The Legal Department is responsible for interpreting legal requirements. Violations may result in civil liability, criminal prosecution, and severe reputational damage to [Company Name]. All personnel are required to comply with export control regulations regarding the international transfer of technical data.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Executive Officer (CEO)/Board of Directors:</strong> Ultimate accountability for IP protection and governance.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of the technical and administrative controls mandated by this policy.<br><br><strong>Data/Asset Owners (Business Unit Leaders):</strong> Responsible for identifying IP within their domain, authorizing access, and defining classification levels.<br><br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for IP registration, contract/NDA management, and guiding the response to legal incidents involving IP.<br><br><strong>All Employees, Contractors, and Third Parties:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, completing mandatory training, and immediately reporting any suspected policy violations or loss of IP.</p>

<p>Any violation of this Intellectual Property Policy shall be considered a serious breach of company rules and may constitute a violation of law. Violations will result in disciplinary action, up to and including immediate termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal legal proceedings. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and the Legal Department, is authorized to investigate suspected violations, which may include forensic analysis of systems and networks.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and the Legal Department, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to applicable laws and standards. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive management team. The most current version of this policy is the only authoritative version and will be maintained in the official policy repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Intellectual Property (IP) Policy establishes the authoritative framework for the identification, classification, protection, and management of all intellectual property assets owned by, created for, or entrusted to [Company Name]. Its purpose is to safeguard proprietary information, preserve competitive advantage, ensure legal compliance, and define clear ownership and usage rights, thereby protecting the organization's most valuable intangible assets from unauthorized disclosure, misuse, or loss.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all individuals and entities with access to [Company Name]'s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary, and contractors), consultants, vendors, business partners, and any third-party agents. It encompasses all forms of intellectual property, whether in physical or digital format, stored on corporate systems, personal devices used for business purposes, cloud environments, or in physical locations under the company's control. This includes all company facilities, data centers, and remote work environments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Intellectual Property (IP):</strong> Creations of the mind for which exclusive legal rights are recognized. For the purposes of this policy, this includes, but is not limited to: Trade Secrets, Copyrights, Patents, Trademarks, Proprietary Software Code, Technical Know-How, Business Processes, Research Data, and Confidential Business Information.<br><br><strong>Proprietary Information:</strong> Any non-public information that provides a business advantage, including source code, algorithms, customer lists, financial projections, strategic plans, and unpublished research.<br><br><strong>Classified IP:</strong> IP assets that have been formally categorized according to the Data Classification Policy (e.g., Confidential, Restricted).<br><br><strong>Authorized User:</strong> An individual who has been granted explicit, role-based access to specific IP assets following the principle of least privilege.<br><br><strong>Data Loss Prevention (DLP):</strong> A set of tools and processes designed to detect and prevent unauthorized exfiltration of sensitive data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates the strict protection of all its intellectual property assets. All IP created by employees within the scope of their employment, using company resources, or relating to the company's business shall be the sole and exclusive property of [Company Name]. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, modification, destruction, or transfer of any company IP is strictly prohibited. All personnel are legally and contractually obligated to uphold the confidentiality and integrity of these assets in perpetuity, even following termination of engagement.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 IP Identification and Classification</h4><p>All potential IP assets must be formally identified and recorded in the corporate IP register. Each asset shall be classified according to the Data Classification Policy (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted). Classification labels must be applied digitally via metadata and, where applicable, physically on documents.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authorization</h4><p>Access to IP shall be governed by strict role-based access control (RBAC) and the principle of least privilege. Access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for accessing systems housing Confidential or Restricted IP. All access rights must be reviewed quarterly and revoked immediately upon role change or termination.</p><h4>5.3 Storage and Encryption</h4><p>Confidential and Restricted IP must be stored exclusively on approved, centrally managed corporate systems (e.g., designated secure file shares, version control systems for code). Storage on unauthorized cloud services, personal devices, or removable media is forbidden. All IP at rest classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. IP in transit over public networks must be protected via TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><h4>5.4 Network and Endpoint Security</h4><p>Endpoints used to access IP must have company-managed endpoint detection and response (EDR) software, full-disk encryption, and enforced screen locking. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate systems containing high-value IP. All network traffic to and from IP repositories must be logged and monitored for anomalous activity.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Data Loss Prevention (DLP)</h4><p>A full-featured DLP solution must be deployed to monitor all egress channels (email, web upload, cloud sync, removable media) for unauthorized transmission of classified IP. All policy violations must generate immediate alerts to the Security Operations Center (SOC). Regular data discovery scans must be conducted to identify unsecured IP on corporate and cloud assets.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Source Code Protection</h4><p>All proprietary software source code is considered Restricted IP. Code must be stored in a centralized, access-controlled version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history shall be immutable and tied to an authenticated user. Code repositories must be scanned for secrets (API keys, passwords) pre-commit. External contributions require signed contributor license agreements (CLAs).</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Vendor Management</h4><p>Any disclosure of IP to a third party (vendor, partner, contractor) requires a fully executed Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA) with confidentiality clauses that survive termination. Third-party access must be time-bound, audited, and provisioned through a secure, isolated method (e.g., virtual desktop). Vendor systems used to process company IP must meet or exceed the security requirements outlined in this policy.</p><h4>5.8 Employee Onboarding, Training, and Offboarding</h4><p>IP protection clauses must be included in all employment and contractor agreements. Annual mandatory security awareness training, with specific modules on IP protection, is required for all personnel. Upon termination, all access to IP must be revoked simultaneously with HR notification, and a formal return/attestation of all company IP (physical and digital) must be completed.</p><h4>5.9 Audit Logging and Incident Response</h4><p>All access, modification, and transfer events involving Confidential or Restricted IP must generate immutable audit logs. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 365 days and protected from tampering. Any suspected or actual loss, theft, or unauthorized disclosure of IP constitutes a security incident and must be reported immediately per the Incident Response Policy for containment, investigation, and legal notification if required.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant intellectual property laws (e.g., Copyright Act, Defend Trade Secrets Act), contractual obligations, and industry frameworks including ISO 27001 (A.8.2.1 Classification of information, A.13.2.1 Information transfer policies and procedures) and SOC 2 (Criteria CC6.1, CC7.1). The Legal Department is responsible for interpreting legal requirements. Violations may result in civil liability, criminal prosecution, and severe reputational damage to [Company Name]. All personnel are required to comply with export control regulations regarding the international transfer of technical data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Executive Officer (CEO)/Board of Directors:</strong> Ultimate accountability for IP protection and governance.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of the technical and administrative controls mandated by this policy.<br><br><strong>Data/Asset Owners (Business Unit Leaders):</strong> Responsible for identifying IP within their domain, authorizing access, and defining classification levels.<br><br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for IP registration, contract/NDA management, and guiding the response to legal incidents involving IP.<br><br><strong>All Employees, Contractors, and Third Parties:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, completing mandatory training, and immediately reporting any suspected policy violations or loss of IP.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any violation of this Intellectual Property Policy shall be considered a serious breach of company rules and may constitute a violation of law. Violations will result in disciplinary action, up to and including immediate termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal legal proceedings. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and the Legal Department, is authorized to investigate suspected violations, which may include forensic analysis of systems and networks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the CISO and the Legal Department, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to applicable laws and standards. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive management team. The most current version of this policy is the only authoritative version and will be maintained in the official policy repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.578414,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:50:24.851,NULL
51,364722,Cloud Security Policy,"<p>This Cloud Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for the secure adoption, operation, and management of cloud computing services at [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems hosted in cloud environments, ensure compliance with regulatory obligations, and align with the security requirements of the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 standard and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other third parties (collectively, ""Personnel"") who are involved in the procurement, provisioning, configuration, management, or use of any cloud computing service (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data or supports [Company Name] business processes. This includes all public, private, and hybrid cloud deployments.</p>

<p><strong>Cloud Service Provider (CSP):</strong> A third-party organization that provides cloud-based infrastructure, platforms, or software (e.g., AWS, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud Platform, Salesforce).<br/><strong>Shared Responsibility Model:</strong> The security and compliance framework that delineates the security obligations of the CSP and [Company Name] for a given cloud service.<br/><strong>Data Classification:</strong> The process of categorizing data based on its level of sensitivity (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br/><strong>Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM):</strong> Tools and processes used to identify and remediate risks through continuous assessment of cloud resource configurations against security policies.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any system access that allows for the modification of security controls, configuration of critical infrastructure, or access to sensitive data.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all use of cloud services shall adhere to the principle of least privilege, enforce data protection commensurate with data classification, and maintain a demonstrable security posture. All cloud deployments must be formally authorized, continuously monitored, and managed in accordance with this policy and the shared responsibility model. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Cloud Service Procurement & Authorization</strong><br/>5.1.1 All procurement or subscription to cloud services must undergo a formal security risk assessment and receive written approval from the Information Security Department prior to contract execution or provisioning.<br/>5.1.2 A shared responsibility matrix, clearly defining security obligations between [Company Name] and the CSP, must be documented and acknowledged for each approved service.<br/><br/><strong>5.2 Identity & Access Management (IAM)</strong><br/>5.2.1 All user access to cloud management consoles and workloads shall be authenticated using [Company Name]'s centralized identity provider (e.g., via SAML 2.0 or OpenID Connect). Local IAM users are forbidden for human access.<br/>5.2.2 The principle of least privilege shall be enforced. Access rights must be granted based on job function and reviewed at least quarterly.<br/>5.2.3 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled and enforced for all privileged user accounts and for all remote administrative access to cloud environments.<br/>5.2.4 Privileged access sessions (e.g., root, admin) must be logged, monitored, and require justification. Just-in-Time (JIT) access provisioning is mandated where supported.<br/><br/><strong>5.3 Data Protection & Encryption</strong><br/>5.3.1 All data classified as Confidential or Restricted must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or stronger encryption. The encryption keys shall be managed by [Company Name] using a certified Key Management Service (KMS) where feasible.<br/>5.3.2 All data in transit between [Company Name] networks and cloud services, and between cloud services, must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br/>5.3.3 Data residency and sovereignty requirements must be defined and enforced through CSP region selection and configuration controls.<br/><br/><strong>5.4 Network Security & Segmentation</strong><br/>5.4.1 All cloud virtual networks must be logically segmented. Direct public internet access to backend databases, storage, or management interfaces is strictly prohibited.<br/>5.4.2 Network Security Groups (NSGs), Firewalls, or Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) must be configured to deny all traffic by default, with rules explicitly defined and justified for allowed traffic.<br/>5.4.3 A dedicated, encrypted, and monitored connection (e.g., VPN, Direct Connect, ExpressRoute) must be established for any administrative or sensitive data transfer between [Company Name] premises and cloud environments.<br/><br/><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</strong><br/>5.5.1 Audit logging must be enabled for all security-relevant events, including authentication attempts, configuration changes, data access, and network traffic. Logs must be aggregated into a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.<br/>5.5.2 A Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) tool must be deployed to continuously assess configurations against this policy and industry benchmarks (e.g., CIS Benchmarks). Critical misconfigurations must be remediated within 24 hours.<br/>5.5.3 An incident response plan, integrated with the corporate IR plan, must be established for each major cloud environment, defining roles and procedures for containment, eradication, and recovery.<br/><br/><strong>5.6 Asset Management & Configuration</strong><br/>5.5.1 An authoritative inventory of all provisioned cloud assets (compute instances, storage, databases, etc.) must be maintained and updated automatically.<br/>5.5.2 All cloud workloads (e.g., virtual machines, containers) must be hardened using a standardized, security-approved image or template. Unnecessary services and ports shall be disabled.<br/>5.5.3 A formal patch management process must be applied to all [Company Name]-managed cloud assets. Critical security patches must be applied within 14 days of release by the CSP or vendor.</p>

<p>All cloud service engagements must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). The Information Security Department, in conjunction with Legal and Compliance, shall assess CSP agreements for compliance with [Company Name]'s data processing and security requirements. The CSP must provide independent third-party audit reports (e.g., SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001 certification) upon request, and any gaps identified must be formally risk-accepted or remediated.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, conducting risk assessments, monitoring compliance, and leading incident response in cloud environments.<br/><strong>Cloud Service Owners:</strong> Business or technical leaders responsible for a specific cloud service; accountable for ensuring the service is operated in compliance with this policy.<br/><strong>IT/Cloud Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure implementation, configuration, and maintenance of cloud resources in accordance with this policy and approved architectures.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to comply with this policy and report any suspected security incidents or policy violations related to cloud services.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately suspend or terminate access to any cloud resource that poses a critical security risk to [Company Name]. Non-compliant cloud services may be decommissioned at the discretion of the CISO.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes in cloud technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is the only version that is considered valid and enforceable.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Cloud Security Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for the secure adoption, operation, and management of cloud computing services at [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate data and systems hosted in cloud environments, ensure compliance with regulatory obligations, and align with the security requirements of the ISO/IEC 27001:2022 standard and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other third parties (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") who are involved in the procurement, provisioning, configuration, management, or use of any cloud computing service (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data or supports [Company Name] business processes. This includes all public, private, and hybrid cloud deployments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Cloud Service Provider (CSP):</strong> A third-party organization that provides cloud-based infrastructure, platforms, or software (e.g., AWS, Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud Platform, Salesforce).<br/><strong>Shared Responsibility Model:</strong> The security and compliance framework that delineates the security obligations of the CSP and [Company Name] for a given cloud service.<br/><strong>Data Classification:</strong> The process of categorizing data based on its level of sensitivity (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br/><strong>Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM):</strong> Tools and processes used to identify and remediate risks through continuous assessment of cloud resource configurations against security policies.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any system access that allows for the modification of security controls, configuration of critical infrastructure, or access to sensitive data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all use of cloud services shall adhere to the principle of least privilege, enforce data protection commensurate with data classification, and maintain a demonstrable security posture. All cloud deployments must be formally authorized, continuously monitored, and managed in accordance with this policy and the shared responsibility model. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Cloud Service Procurement & Authorization</strong><br/>5.1.1 All procurement or subscription to cloud services must undergo a formal security risk assessment and receive written approval from the Information Security Department prior to contract execution or provisioning.<br/>5.1.2 A shared responsibility matrix, clearly defining security obligations between [Company Name] and the CSP, must be documented and acknowledged for each approved service.<br/><br/><strong>5.2 Identity & Access Management (IAM)</strong><br/>5.2.1 All user access to cloud management consoles and workloads shall be authenticated using [Company Name]'s centralized identity provider (e.g., via SAML 2.0 or OpenID Connect). Local IAM users are forbidden for human access.<br/>5.2.2 The principle of least privilege shall be enforced. Access rights must be granted based on job function and reviewed at least quarterly.<br/>5.2.3 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled and enforced for all privileged user accounts and for all remote administrative access to cloud environments.<br/>5.2.4 Privileged access sessions (e.g., root, admin) must be logged, monitored, and require justification. Just-in-Time (JIT) access provisioning is mandated where supported.<br/><br/><strong>5.3 Data Protection & Encryption</strong><br/>5.3.1 All data classified as Confidential or Restricted must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or stronger encryption. The encryption keys shall be managed by [Company Name] using a certified Key Management Service (KMS) where feasible.<br/>5.3.2 All data in transit between [Company Name] networks and cloud services, and between cloud services, must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br/>5.3.3 Data residency and sovereignty requirements must be defined and enforced through CSP region selection and configuration controls.<br/><br/><strong>5.4 Network Security & Segmentation</strong><br/>5.4.1 All cloud virtual networks must be logically segmented. Direct public internet access to backend databases, storage, or management interfaces is strictly prohibited.<br/>5.4.2 Network Security Groups (NSGs), Firewalls, or Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) must be configured to deny all traffic by default, with rules explicitly defined and justified for allowed traffic.<br/>5.4.3 A dedicated, encrypted, and monitored connection (e.g., VPN, Direct Connect, ExpressRoute) must be established for any administrative or sensitive data transfer between [Company Name] premises and cloud environments.<br/><br/><strong>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</strong><br/>5.5.1 Audit logging must be enabled for all security-relevant events, including authentication attempts, configuration changes, data access, and network traffic. Logs must be aggregated into a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.<br/>5.5.2 A Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM) tool must be deployed to continuously assess configurations against this policy and industry benchmarks (e.g., CIS Benchmarks). Critical misconfigurations must be remediated within 24 hours.<br/>5.5.3 An incident response plan, integrated with the corporate IR plan, must be established for each major cloud environment, defining roles and procedures for containment, eradication, and recovery.<br/><br/><strong>5.6 Asset Management & Configuration</strong><br/>5.5.1 An authoritative inventory of all provisioned cloud assets (compute instances, storage, databases, etc.) must be maintained and updated automatically.<br/>5.5.2 All cloud workloads (e.g., virtual machines, containers) must be hardened using a standardized, security-approved image or template. Unnecessary services and ports shall be disabled.<br/>5.5.3 A formal patch management process must be applied to all [Company Name]-managed cloud assets. Critical security patches must be applied within 14 days of release by the CSP or vendor.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>All cloud service engagements must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). The Information Security Department, in conjunction with Legal and Compliance, shall assess CSP agreements for compliance with [Company Name]'s data processing and security requirements. The CSP must provide independent third-party audit reports (e.g., SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001 certification) upon request, and any gaps identified must be formally risk-accepted or remediated.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval and enforcement of this policy.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy development, conducting risk assessments, monitoring compliance, and leading incident response in cloud environments.<br/><strong>Cloud Service Owners:</strong> Business or technical leaders responsible for a specific cloud service; accountable for ensuring the service is operated in compliance with this policy.<br/><strong>IT/Cloud Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure implementation, configuration, and maintenance of cloud resources in accordance with this policy and approved architectures.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to comply with this policy and report any suspected security incidents or policy violations related to cloud services.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately suspend or terminate access to any cloud resource that poses a critical security risk to [Company Name]. Non-compliant cloud services may be decommissioned at the discretion of the CISO.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or following significant changes in cloud technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is the only version that is considered valid and enforceable.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.694372,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:51:02.193,NULL
53,317180,Supply Chain Security Policy,"<p>This Supply Chain Security Policy establishes the mandatory security requirements and controls for all third-party relationships, products, and services that interact with, support, or form part of [Company Name]'s information systems, data, or operational processes. The purpose is to mitigate risks associated with external dependencies, ensure the integrity and confidentiality of information throughout the supply chain, and maintain compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other relevant regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, and agents of [Company Name] involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party suppliers, vendors, contractors, and service providers (collectively, ""Suppliers""). It encompasses all hardware, software, cloud services, and outsourced processes that process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data or that are integrated into [Company Name]'s critical infrastructure.</p>

<p><strong>Supplier:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, software, or services to [Company Name].<br><strong>Critical Supplier:</strong> A Supplier whose failure or compromise would significantly impact the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of [Company Name]'s information assets or operations.<br><strong>Supply Chain Risk Management (SCRM):</strong> The systematic process for identifying, assessing, monitoring, and mitigating risks arising from the supply chain.<br><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A formal agreement defining the expected level of service, including security obligations.<br><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Supplier's security controls.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that security shall be a foundational component of all supplier relationships. All Suppliers must adhere to security standards commensurate with the sensitivity of the data accessed and the criticality of the services provided. The introduction of any third-party product or service without prior security due diligence and formal risk acceptance is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Supplier Security Due Diligence and Classification</strong><br>All prospective Suppliers shall undergo a formal security assessment prior to contract execution. Suppliers shall be classified (e.g., Critical, Standard, Low-Risk) based on the data sensitivity and service criticality. Critical Suppliers must demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 or equivalent frameworks through current certifications or a detailed assessment.</p><p><strong>5.2 Contractual Security Requirements</strong><br>All contracts with Suppliers must include explicit security clauses, including: confidentiality obligations; data protection and encryption requirements; incident notification timelines (not to exceed 24 hours for confirmed breaches); a Right-to-Audit clause; and clear termination procedures for security failures. For Critical Suppliers, these requirements must be encapsulated in a formal Security Addendum.</p><p><strong>5.3 Continuous Monitoring and Performance Management</strong><br>The performance and security posture of Critical Suppliers shall be monitored continuously. This includes regular review of security attestations (e.g., SOC 2 Type II reports), automated security scorecard assessments, and verification of compliance with agreed SLAs. Any degradation in security posture must trigger a formal review.</p><p><strong>5.4 Software and Hardware Integrity</strong><br>The acquisition of third-party software or hardware must include verification of integrity through checksums or digital signatures. Open-source software components must be inventoried and monitored for known vulnerabilities. The use of unauthorized or unvetted software from Suppliers within the production environment is forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.5 Incident Response and Notification</strong><br>Suppliers must have a documented incident response plan. In the event of a security incident affecting [Company Name] data or systems, the Supplier must notify the designated [Company Name] Security Contact within one (1) hour of initial detection and provide ongoing updates. Failure to report within the mandated timeline constitutes a material breach of contract.</p><p><strong>5.6 Data Protection and Encryption</strong><br>All [Company Name] data processed, stored, or transmitted by a Supplier must be protected using encryption. Data at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or stronger. Data in transit shall be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. Encryption keys managed by the Supplier for [Company Name] data must be protected under a formal key management policy.</p><p><strong>5.7 Access Control and Least Privilege</strong><br>Supplier personnel access to [Company Name] systems or data shall be granted on a need-to-know, least-privilege basis. All access must be authenticated using multi-factor authentication (MFA). Access reviews for Supplier accounts must be conducted quarterly, and all access must be revoked immediately upon contract termination.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with ISO 27001 controls A.15 (Supplier relationships) and SOC 2 criteria CC3.1, CC4.1, and CC5.1. All Supplier agreements must comply with applicable data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). The Legal and Compliance departments must review all contracts with Critical Suppliers to ensure regulatory obligations are contractually enforced.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval and oversight of the SCRM program.<br><strong>Procurement/Supply Chain Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring security due diligence is completed prior to supplier onboarding and contract signing.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for conducting supplier risk assessments, defining security requirements, and performing audits.<br><strong>Business Unit Owners:</strong> Accountable for the risks associated with Suppliers they engage and must participate in the risk acceptance process.<br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for incorporating mandated security and compliance clauses into all supplier contracts.</p>

<p>Any employee found to have bypassed supplier security due diligence processes or engaged a non-compliant Supplier shall be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination. Contracts with Suppliers that materially breach this policy's security requirements shall be subject to termination for cause. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disconnect or block any Supplier integration that poses an imminent security threat.</p>

<p>This Supply Chain Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Team at least annually, or following a significant supply chain security incident. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Supply Chain Security Policy establishes the mandatory security requirements and controls for all third-party relationships, products, and services that interact with, support, or form part of [Company Name]'s information systems, data, or operational processes. The purpose is to mitigate risks associated with external dependencies, ensure the integrity and confidentiality of information throughout the supply chain, and maintain compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other relevant regulatory obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, and agents of [Company Name] involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party suppliers, vendors, contractors, and service providers (collectively, \\\""Suppliers\\\""). It encompasses all hardware, software, cloud services, and outsourced processes that process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data or that are integrated into [Company Name]'s critical infrastructure.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Supplier:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, software, or services to [Company Name].<br><strong>Critical Supplier:</strong> A Supplier whose failure or compromise would significantly impact the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of [Company Name]'s information assets or operations.<br><strong>Supply Chain Risk Management (SCRM):</strong> The systematic process for identifying, assessing, monitoring, and mitigating risks arising from the supply chain.<br><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A formal agreement defining the expected level of service, including security obligations.<br><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Supplier's security controls.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that security shall be a foundational component of all supplier relationships. All Suppliers must adhere to security standards commensurate with the sensitivity of the data accessed and the criticality of the services provided. The introduction of any third-party product or service without prior security due diligence and formal risk acceptance is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Supplier Security Due Diligence and Classification</strong><br>All prospective Suppliers shall undergo a formal security assessment prior to contract execution. Suppliers shall be classified (e.g., Critical, Standard, Low-Risk) based on the data sensitivity and service criticality. Critical Suppliers must demonstrate compliance with ISO 27001 or equivalent frameworks through current certifications or a detailed assessment.</p><p><strong>5.2 Contractual Security Requirements</strong><br>All contracts with Suppliers must include explicit security clauses, including: confidentiality obligations; data protection and encryption requirements; incident notification timelines (not to exceed 24 hours for confirmed breaches); a Right-to-Audit clause; and clear termination procedures for security failures. For Critical Suppliers, these requirements must be encapsulated in a formal Security Addendum.</p><p><strong>5.3 Continuous Monitoring and Performance Management</strong><br>The performance and security posture of Critical Suppliers shall be monitored continuously. This includes regular review of security attestations (e.g., SOC 2 Type II reports), automated security scorecard assessments, and verification of compliance with agreed SLAs. Any degradation in security posture must trigger a formal review.</p><p><strong>5.4 Software and Hardware Integrity</strong><br>The acquisition of third-party software or hardware must include verification of integrity through checksums or digital signatures. Open-source software components must be inventoried and monitored for known vulnerabilities. The use of unauthorized or unvetted software from Suppliers within the production environment is forbidden.</p><p><strong>5.5 Incident Response and Notification</strong><br>Suppliers must have a documented incident response plan. In the event of a security incident affecting [Company Name] data or systems, the Supplier must notify the designated [Company Name] Security Contact within one (1) hour of initial detection and provide ongoing updates. Failure to report within the mandated timeline constitutes a material breach of contract.</p><p><strong>5.6 Data Protection and Encryption</strong><br>All [Company Name] data processed, stored, or transmitted by a Supplier must be protected using encryption. Data at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or stronger. Data in transit shall be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. Encryption keys managed by the Supplier for [Company Name] data must be protected under a formal key management policy.</p><p><strong>5.7 Access Control and Least Privilege</strong><br>Supplier personnel access to [Company Name] systems or data shall be granted on a need-to-know, least-privilege basis. All access must be authenticated using multi-factor authentication (MFA). Access reviews for Supplier accounts must be conducted quarterly, and all access must be revoked immediately upon contract termination.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with ISO 27001 controls A.15 (Supplier relationships) and SOC 2 criteria CC3.1, CC4.1, and CC5.1. All Supplier agreements must comply with applicable data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA). The Legal and Compliance departments must review all contracts with Critical Suppliers to ensure regulatory obligations are contractually enforced.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval and oversight of the SCRM program.<br><strong>Procurement/Supply Chain Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring security due diligence is completed prior to supplier onboarding and contract signing.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for conducting supplier risk assessments, defining security requirements, and performing audits.<br><strong>Business Unit Owners:</strong> Accountable for the risks associated with Suppliers they engage and must participate in the risk acceptance process.<br><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Responsible for incorporating mandated security and compliance clauses into all supplier contracts.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee found to have bypassed supplier security due diligence processes or engaged a non-compliant Supplier shall be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination. Contracts with Suppliers that materially breach this policy's security requirements shall be subject to termination for cause. The Information Security Team is authorized to immediately disconnect or block any Supplier integration that poses an imminent security threat.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Supply Chain Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Team at least annually, or following a significant supply chain security incident. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.917923,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:51:32.976,NULL
54,448836,Equipment Policy,"<p>This Equipment Policy establishes the mandatory controls for the secure acquisition, configuration, management, and disposal of all information processing equipment owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect corporate information assets from loss, theft, compromise, and unauthorized access, ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who are authorized to use, manage, or support any [Company Name] information processing equipment. This includes, but is not limited to, all company-issued and approved personal devices (BYOD) used for business purposes, such as laptops, desktops, servers, mobile devices (smartphones, tablets), networking hardware, storage devices, and portable media. The policy governs equipment throughout its entire lifecycle, from procurement to secure decommissioning.</p>

<p><strong>Information Processing Equipment:</strong> Any physical device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting [Company Name] information or connecting to corporate networks.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned device authorized by [Company Name] for business use.<br/><strong>Endpoint Protection:</strong> A suite of security software (e.g., antivirus, host firewall, intrusion detection) mandated for all endpoints.<br/><strong>Hardening:</strong> The process of configuring equipment to reduce its attack surface by disabling unnecessary services, applying security baselines, and removing default accounts.<br/><strong>Secure Sanitization:</strong> The irreversible destruction of data on storage media using approved methods (e.g., cryptographic erasure, physical destruction) prior to disposal or reuse.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information processing equipment shall be managed under a formal, risk-based lifecycle program. All equipment must be configured to a secure baseline, continuously monitored for compliance, and protected against physical and logical threats. The use of unauthorized equipment to access, store, or process corporate data is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Equipment Acquisition and Provisioning</h4><p>All equipment acquisitions must be approved by the Information Technology (IT) Department. Only equipment from the approved vendor list that meets defined security specifications shall be procured. Prior to deployment, all equipment must be hardened according to the corporate security baseline image, which includes disabling unnecessary ports/services, applying the latest security patches, and configuring mandatory security settings.</p><h4>5.2 Configuration and Hardening Standards</h4><p>A standardized, security-hardened configuration baseline, approved by the Information Security team, shall be applied to all equipment types (e.g., workstations, servers, network devices). This baseline mandates: full-disk encryption using FIPS 140-2 validated modules for all portable devices and media; automatic locking of sessions after 15 minutes of inactivity requiring re-authentication; and the removal or disabling of all default user accounts and passwords.</p><h4>5.3 Endpoint Protection and Monitoring</h4><p>Company-approved endpoint protection software with integrated antivirus, host-based firewall, and application control capabilities must be installed, kept active, and automatically updated on all endpoints. A centralized logging agent shall be deployed to transmit security events (e.g., logon attempts, policy changes, malware detection) to the Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system for continuous monitoring.</p><h4>5.4 Physical Security and Asset Management</h4><p>All equipment shall be recorded in the corporate asset inventory with details including owner, location, serial number, and configuration. Portable equipment must be physically secured when unattended. The removal of equipment from company premises requires prior authorization. Equipment used in public areas must employ privacy screens to prevent shoulder surfing.</p><h4>5.5 Network Connectivity and Wireless Access</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved, configured, and managed by the IT Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for corporate access is forbidden. Corporate Wi-Fi shall use WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Personal hotspot usage for accessing corporate resources is prohibited unless routed through an approved corporate VPN.</p><h4>5.6 Maintenance, Disposal, and Secure Sanitization</h4><p>All maintenance, including repairs and upgrades, must be performed by authorized personnel or vendors. Prior to disposal, reassignment, or return of equipment, all storage media must undergo secure sanitization. Hard drives and non-volatile storage shall be cryptographically erased using a tool that meets NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 1 ""Clear"" or ""Purge"" standards, or physically destroyed if the device is faulty or at end-of-life.</p><h4>5.7 Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) Requirements</h4><p>Personally owned devices authorized for business use must be enrolled in the Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) platform. This enrollment enforces mandatory security policies, including device encryption, remote wipe capability, and separation of corporate data within a secure container or workspace. The device must be kept updated with the latest operating system security patches.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO 27001 controls, specifically A.8 (Asset Management), A.9 (Access Control), A.11 (Physical and Environmental Security), and A.12 (Operations Security). It also addresses SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. Equipment handling must adhere to relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) regarding the secure storage and erasure of personal data. Evidence of compliance, including asset inventories, hardening checklists, and sanitization certificates, must be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining security baselines, approving endpoint protection solutions, and monitoring policy compliance.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for the procurement, provisioning, hardening, maintenance, and secure disposal of all corporate equipment in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Asset Owners/Department Heads:</strong> Accountable for ensuring equipment under their control is used in compliance with this policy and reported if lost or stolen.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use company equipment responsibly, adhere to physical security measures, and report any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</p>

<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Unauthorized equipment may be disconnected from the network, and data may be remotely wiped from non-compliant or lost devices. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>

<p>This Equipment Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be made available to all personnel through the corporate policy portal.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Equipment Policy establishes the mandatory controls for the secure acquisition, configuration, management, and disposal of all information processing equipment owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to protect corporate information assets from loss, theft, compromise, and unauthorized access, ensuring the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who are authorized to use, manage, or support any [Company Name] information processing equipment. This includes, but is not limited to, all company-issued and approved personal devices (BYOD) used for business purposes, such as laptops, desktops, servers, mobile devices (smartphones, tablets), networking hardware, storage devices, and portable media. The policy governs equipment throughout its entire lifecycle, from procurement to secure decommissioning.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Processing Equipment:</strong> Any physical device capable of storing, processing, or transmitting [Company Name] information or connecting to corporate networks.<br/><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned device authorized by [Company Name] for business use.<br/><strong>Endpoint Protection:</strong> A suite of security software (e.g.,