rsonally owned devices authorized for business use must be enrolled in the Mobile Device Management (MDM) or Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) platform. This enrollment enforces mandatory security policies, including device encryption, remote wipe capability, and separation of corporate data within a secure container or workspace. The device must be kept updated with the latest operating system security patches.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO 27001 controls, specifically A.8 (Asset Management), A.9 (Access Control), A.11 (Physical and Environmental Security), and A.12 (Operations Security). It also addresses SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. Equipment handling must adhere to relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) regarding the secure storage and erasure of personal data. Evidence of compliance, including asset inventories, hardening checklists, and sanitization certificates, must be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for defining security baselines, approving endpoint protection solutions, and monitoring policy compliance.<br/><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for the procurement, provisioning, hardening, maintenance, and secure disposal of all corporate equipment in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Asset Owners/Department Heads:</strong> Accountable for ensuring equipment under their control is used in compliance with this policy and reported if lost or stolen.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use company equipment responsibly, adhere to physical security measures, and report any suspected policy violations or security incidents immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. Unauthorized equipment may be disconnected from the network, and data may be remotely wiped from non-compliant or lost devices. Exceptions to this policy require a formal risk assessment and written approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Equipment Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or following significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is authoritative and will be made available to all personnel through the corporate policy portal.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:25.019452,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:52:06.927,NULL
55,15658,Cabling Security Policy,"<p>This Cabling Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures for the physical and logical security of all data and telecommunications cabling infrastructure at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information transmitted across the corporate network by mitigating risks associated with unauthorized access, interception, tampering, and physical damage to cabling systems. This policy is a foundational component of [Company Name]'s Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who design, install, maintain, access, or manage any data or telecommunications cabling infrastructure owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all copper (e.g., Cat 5e/6/6a/7) and fiber optic cabling, associated patch panels, wall outlets, cable trays, conduits, and demarcation points within all corporate facilities, data centers, wiring closets, and remote office locations.</p>

<p><strong>Demarcation Point (Demarc):</strong> The physical point where the service provider's network ends and [Company Name]'s internal network begins.<br/><strong>Intermediate Distribution Frame (IDF):</strong> A secondary communications room that connects end-user equipment to the Main Distribution Frame (MDF).<br/><strong>Main Distribution Frame (MDF):</strong> The primary communications room for a building, housing core networking equipment and the demarcation point.<br/><strong>Patch Panel:</strong> A hardware assembly containing ports used to connect and manage incoming and outgoing LAN cables.<br/><strong>Plenum-rated Cable:</strong> Cable jacketing material that meets specific fire-resistance and low-smoke toxicity standards for use in air-handling spaces.<br/><strong>Temporary Cable:</strong> Any cable run that is not installed within permanent pathways (e.g., conduit, cable tray) and is intended for use for less than 90 days.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all network and telecommunications cabling infrastructure shall be designed, installed, maintained, and decommissioned in a manner that prevents unauthorized physical and logical access, electromagnetic interference, and service disruption. The use of unsecured, ad-hoc, or non-compliant cabling is strictly prohibited. All cabling work must adhere to industry standards and the specific technical controls defined in this policy.</p>

<h4>5.1 Physical Pathway Security</h4><p>All permanent cabling shall be installed within secured, locked conduits, cable trays, or raised floors. Wiring closets (IDFs/MDFs) must remain locked at all times, with access logged and restricted to authorized personnel only. Cabling must not be run in public areas or ceilings in a manner that is accessible or visible.</p><h4>5.2 Cable Installation and Labeling Standards</h4><p>All cables must be installed in compliance with ANSI/TIA-568 and ISO/IEC 11801 standards. Every cable, patch panel port, and wall outlet shall be labeled with a unique, durable identifier following the corporate naming standard. Cable labels must remain legible for the lifecycle of the installation.</p><h4>5.3 Patch Panel and Port Management</h4><p>Unused ports on all network switches and patch panels shall be administratively disabled. Physical patch panels must be kept neat, with cables dressed and managed to prevent strain or accidental disconnection. A documented patch schedule, mapping physical ports to logical network segments, must be maintained and reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.4 Prohibition of Ad-Hoc and Temporary Cabling</h4><p>The use of temporary cabling runs across floors, under doors, or through ceilings is forbidden. Any exception for a genuine temporary need (not to exceed 90 days) requires written approval from the Network Operations team and must be clearly documented with a removal date.</p><h4>5.5 Cable Type and Environmental Specifications</h4><p>Cabling run within air plenums must be plenum-rated (CMP/OFNP). Outdoor cabling must be specifically rated for external use and protected from environmental hazards. All cabling must be tested and certified upon installation to meet performance specifications for its category/class.</p><h4>5.6 Decommissioning and Disposal</h4><p>Decommissioned cables must be physically removed from pathways and infrastructure. Cables containing sensitive data traces shall be securely destroyed (e.g., shredding) prior to disposal or recycling, in accordance with the Data Destruction Policy.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Contractor Work</h4><p>All third-party cabling work must be pre-approved by the IT Department. Contractors must provide proof of compliance with this policy and relevant standards. A member of the IT staff must supervise and validate the final installation before the cable plant is accepted into the production network.</p>

<p>This policy enforces controls to meet specific ISO 27001:2022 Annex A objectives, including <strong>A.7.4 (Physical Security Perimeter)</strong>, <strong>A.7.10 (Equipment Siting and Protection)</strong>, and <strong>A.8.9 (Information Leakage)</strong>. Compliance with this policy is mandatory to satisfy SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Availability. Violations may also constitute a breach of contractual obligations with clients and partners, and non-compliance with relevant data protection regulations.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval and enforcement.<br/><strong>IT Director / Network Operations Manager:</strong> Responsible for policy implementation, monitoring compliance, and managing exceptions.<br/><strong>Network Engineers & Technicians:</strong> Responsible for the daily execution of this policy, including installation, labeling, and maintenance of cabling infrastructure.<br/><strong>Facilities Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring physical security of pathways (conduits, rooms) and coordinating with IT for any construction affecting cabling.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any observed damage, tampering, or non-compliant cabling to the IT Department immediately.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Contractors and third parties in violation may have their access revoked and contracts terminated. The IT Department is authorized to immediately disconnect or remove any cabling found to be non-compliant with this policy to mitigate security risks.</p>

<p>This Cabling Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the IT Director at least annually, or following any significant change to the network infrastructure or relevant regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is the only version considered valid.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Cabling Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures for the physical and logical security of all data and telecommunications cabling infrastructure at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information transmitted across the corporate network by mitigating risks associated with unauthorized access, interception, tampering, and physical damage to cabling systems. This policy is a foundational component of [Company Name]'s Information Security Management System (ISMS).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who design, install, maintain, access, or manage any data or telecommunications cabling infrastructure owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all copper (e.g., Cat 5e/6/6a/7) and fiber optic cabling, associated patch panels, wall outlets, cable trays, conduits, and demarcation points within all corporate facilities, data centers, wiring closets, and remote office locations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Demarcation Point (Demarc):</strong> The physical point where the service provider's network ends and [Company Name]'s internal network begins.<br/><strong>Intermediate Distribution Frame (IDF):</strong> A secondary communications room that connects end-user equipment to the Main Distribution Frame (MDF).<br/><strong>Main Distribution Frame (MDF):</strong> The primary communications room for a building, housing core networking equipment and the demarcation point.<br/><strong>Patch Panel:</strong> A hardware assembly containing ports used to connect and manage incoming and outgoing LAN cables.<br/><strong>Plenum-rated Cable:</strong> Cable jacketing material that meets specific fire-resistance and low-smoke toxicity standards for use in air-handling spaces.<br/><strong>Temporary Cable:</strong> Any cable run that is not installed within permanent pathways (e.g., conduit, cable tray) and is intended for use for less than 90 days.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all network and telecommunications cabling infrastructure shall be designed, installed, maintained, and decommissioned in a manner that prevents unauthorized physical and logical access, electromagnetic interference, and service disruption. The use of unsecured, ad-hoc, or non-compliant cabling is strictly prohibited. All cabling work must adhere to industry standards and the specific technical controls defined in this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Physical Pathway Security</h4><p>All permanent cabling shall be installed within secured, locked conduits, cable trays, or raised floors. Wiring closets (IDFs/MDFs) must remain locked at all times, with access logged and restricted to authorized personnel only. Cabling must not be run in public areas or ceilings in a manner that is accessible or visible.</p><h4>5.2 Cable Installation and Labeling Standards</h4><p>All cables must be installed in compliance with ANSI/TIA-568 and ISO/IEC 11801 standards. Every cable, patch panel port, and wall outlet shall be labeled with a unique, durable identifier following the corporate naming standard. Cable labels must remain legible for the lifecycle of the installation.</p><h4>5.3 Patch Panel and Port Management</h4><p>Unused ports on all network switches and patch panels shall be administratively disabled. Physical patch panels must be kept neat, with cables dressed and managed to prevent strain or accidental disconnection. A documented patch schedule, mapping physical ports to logical network segments, must be maintained and reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.4 Prohibition of Ad-Hoc and Temporary Cabling</h4><p>The use of temporary cabling runs across floors, under doors, or through ceilings is forbidden. Any exception for a genuine temporary need (not to exceed 90 days) requires written approval from the Network Operations team and must be clearly documented with a removal date.</p><h4>5.5 Cable Type and Environmental Specifications</h4><p>Cabling run within air plenums must be plenum-rated (CMP/OFNP). Outdoor cabling must be specifically rated for external use and protected from environmental hazards. All cabling must be tested and certified upon installation to meet performance specifications for its category/class.</p><h4>5.6 Decommissioning and Disposal</h4><p>Decommissioned cables must be physically removed from pathways and infrastructure. Cables containing sensitive data traces shall be securely destroyed (e.g., shredding) prior to disposal or recycling, in accordance with the Data Destruction Policy.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party and Contractor Work</h4><p>All third-party cabling work must be pre-approved by the IT Department. Contractors must provide proof of compliance with this policy and relevant standards. A member of the IT staff must supervise and validate the final installation before the cable plant is accepted into the production network.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy enforces controls to meet specific ISO 27001:2022 Annex A objectives, including <strong>A.7.4 (Physical Security Perimeter)</strong>, <strong>A.7.10 (Equipment Siting and Protection)</strong>, and <strong>A.8.9 (Information Leakage)</strong>. Compliance with this policy is mandatory to satisfy SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Availability. Violations may also constitute a breach of contractual obligations with clients and partners, and non-compliance with relevant data protection regulations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for policy approval and enforcement.<br/><strong>IT Director / Network Operations Manager:</strong> Responsible for policy implementation, monitoring compliance, and managing exceptions.<br/><strong>Network Engineers & Technicians:</strong> Responsible for the daily execution of this policy, including installation, labeling, and maintenance of cabling infrastructure.<br/><strong>Facilities Management:</strong> Responsible for ensuring physical security of pathways (conduits, rooms) and coordinating with IT for any construction affecting cabling.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any observed damage, tampering, or non-compliant cabling to the IT Department immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment. Contractors and third parties in violation may have their access revoked and contracts terminated. The IT Department is authorized to immediately disconnect or remove any cabling found to be non-compliant with this policy to mitigate security risks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Cabling Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the IT Director at least annually, or following any significant change to the network infrastructure or relevant regulatory landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO. The latest version of this policy is the only version considered valid.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:25.122532,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:52:39.395,NULL
21,16,Logging and Monitoring Policy,"<p>This Logging and Monitoring Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the generation, collection, analysis, storage, and protection of security event logs across all [Company Name] information systems and networks. The purpose is to ensure the availability of reliable audit trails for the detection of security incidents, forensic investigation, regulatory compliance, and the demonstration of due diligence in safeguarding corporate assets and data.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network devices, applications, databases, and cloud services owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or that process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. This includes systems hosted on-premises, in third-party data centers, or within public cloud infrastructure (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS). All personnel, including employees, contractors, and third-party service providers with access to in-scope systems, are bound by this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Security Event:</strong> Any observable occurrence in a system or network that is relevant to security (e.g., login attempt, file access, configuration change, firewall rule modification).<br><strong>Log:</strong> A chronological record of security events.<br><strong>SIEM (Security Information and Event Management):</strong> The centralized platform for log aggregation, correlation, analysis, and alerting.<br><strong>Retention Period:</strong> The minimum duration for which log data must be preserved in an unaltered state.<br><strong>Privileged User:</strong> Any user account with elevated access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators, domain administrators).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates comprehensive logging and monitoring of all critical information systems to support security operations, incident response, and compliance obligations. Logging shall be enabled by default on all in-scope systems, and the integrity and confidentiality of log data shall be protected against unauthorized access, modification, or destruction. Continuous monitoring mechanisms shall be implemented to detect and alert on anomalous or malicious activities in a timely manner.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Log Generation and Content</strong><br>All systems must generate logs with, at a minimum, the following data elements for each security event: timestamp (synchronized to UTC), event source (hostname/IP), user identity (where applicable), event type/description, and outcome (success/failure). For privileged actions and access to sensitive data, logs must capture the specific command executed or data object accessed.</p><p><strong>5.2 Log Collection and Centralization</strong><br>All security event logs must be transmitted in real-time or near-real-time to the designated centralized SIEM platform. Local storage of logs on individual systems is strictly prohibited as the sole repository. Log transmission channels must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><p><strong>5.3 Log Protection and Integrity</strong><br>Write-once, read-many (WORM) storage or equivalent cryptographic integrity controls (e.g., hash-chaining) must be applied to the central log archive to prevent tampering, deletion, or alteration. Access to the SIEM and raw log data shall be restricted to authorized Security Operations Center (SOC) personnel only, based on the principle of least privilege.</p><p><strong>5.4 Log Retention</strong><br>A minimum retention period of 90 days for actively monitored logs in the SIEM and one (1) year for archived logs in immutable storage is mandated. Logs pertaining to financial transactions, regulated data (e.g., PII, PHI), or incident investigations must be retained for a period of seven (7) years or as required by applicable law or regulation, whichever is longer.</p><p><strong>5.5 Monitoring and Alerting</strong><br>The SOC must maintain 24/7 monitoring capabilities. Automated correlation rules and alerts must be configured to detect patterns indicative of security incidents, including but not limited to: multiple failed authentication attempts, unauthorized access attempts, privilege escalation, data exfiltration, and malware execution. All alerts must be triaged and documented in the incident management system.</p><p><strong>5.6 Regular Review and Audit</strong><br>Privileged user activity logs must be reviewed by a separate, independent party (e.g., a manager or internal audit) on a quarterly basis. System and application logs must be sampled and reviewed for anomalies at least monthly. The effectiveness of logging configurations and monitoring rules must be formally assessed during the annual internal audit.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.15 Logging, A.8.16 Monitoring) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC7.1, CC7.2, CC7.3). Logging practices must also adhere to all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) and industry-specific mandates (e.g., PCI DSS, HIPAA). The Legal and Compliance departments shall be consulted to determine specific retention and evidentiary requirements.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, implementation, and enforcement.<br><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the operational management of the SIEM, 24/7 monitoring, alert triage, and log analysis.<br><strong>System and Network Owners/Administrators:</strong> Responsible for ensuring logging is enabled and properly configured on all assets under their control and that logs are forwarded to the SIEM.<br><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and maintaining the underlying SIEM infrastructure and immutable log storage.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any suspected disabling or tampering of logging functions immediately to the SOC.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy, including but not limited to disabling logging, tampering with log data, or failing to forward logs to the central SIEM, may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Systems found to be non-compliant with logging requirements may be disconnected from the network until remediated.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or their designee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or regulatory environment. All revisions must be formally approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Logging and Monitoring Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the generation, collection, analysis, storage, and protection of security event logs across all [Company Name] information systems and networks. The purpose is to ensure the availability of reliable audit trails for the detection of security incidents, forensic investigation, regulatory compliance, and the demonstration of due diligence in safeguarding corporate assets and data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information systems, network devices, applications, databases, and cloud services owned, operated, or managed by [Company Name], or that process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. This includes systems hosted on-premises, in third-party data centers, or within public cloud infrastructure (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS). All personnel, including employees, contractors, and third-party service providers with access to in-scope systems, are bound by this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Security Event:</strong> Any observable occurrence in a system or network that is relevant to security (e.g., login attempt, file access, configuration change, firewall rule modification).<br><strong>Log:</strong> A chronological record of security events.<br><strong>SIEM (Security Information and Event Management):</strong> The centralized platform for log aggregation, correlation, analysis, and alerting.<br><strong>Retention Period:</strong> The minimum duration for which log data must be preserved in an unaltered state.<br><strong>Privileged User:</strong> Any user account with elevated access rights (e.g., system administrators, database administrators, domain administrators).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates comprehensive logging and monitoring of all critical information systems to support security operations, incident response, and compliance obligations. Logging shall be enabled by default on all in-scope systems, and the integrity and confidentiality of log data shall be protected against unauthorized access, modification, or destruction. Continuous monitoring mechanisms shall be implemented to detect and alert on anomalous or malicious activities in a timely manner.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Log Generation and Content</strong><br>All systems must generate logs with, at a minimum, the following data elements for each security event: timestamp (synchronized to UTC), event source (hostname/IP), user identity (where applicable), event type/description, and outcome (success/failure). For privileged actions and access to sensitive data, logs must capture the specific command executed or data object accessed.</p><p><strong>5.2 Log Collection and Centralization</strong><br>All security event logs must be transmitted in real-time or near-real-time to the designated centralized SIEM platform. Local storage of logs on individual systems is strictly prohibited as the sole repository. Log transmission channels must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p><p><strong>5.3 Log Protection and Integrity</strong><br>Write-once, read-many (WORM) storage or equivalent cryptographic integrity controls (e.g., hash-chaining) must be applied to the central log archive to prevent tampering, deletion, or alteration. Access to the SIEM and raw log data shall be restricted to authorized Security Operations Center (SOC) personnel only, based on the principle of least privilege.</p><p><strong>5.4 Log Retention</strong><br>A minimum retention period of 90 days for actively monitored logs in the SIEM and one (1) year for archived logs in immutable storage is mandated. Logs pertaining to financial transactions, regulated data (e.g., PII, PHI), or incident investigations must be retained for a period of seven (7) years or as required by applicable law or regulation, whichever is longer.</p><p><strong>5.5 Monitoring and Alerting</strong><br>The SOC must maintain 24/7 monitoring capabilities. Automated correlation rules and alerts must be configured to detect patterns indicative of security incidents, including but not limited to: multiple failed authentication attempts, unauthorized access attempts, privilege escalation, data exfiltration, and malware execution. All alerts must be triaged and documented in the incident management system.</p><p><strong>5.6 Regular Review and Audit</strong><br>Privileged user activity logs must be reviewed by a separate, independent party (e.g., a manager or internal audit) on a quarterly basis. System and application logs must be sampled and reviewed for anomalies at least monthly. The effectiveness of logging configurations and monitoring rules must be formally assessed during the annual internal audit.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.15 Logging, A.8.16 Monitoring) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC7.1, CC7.2, CC7.3). Logging practices must also adhere to all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) and industry-specific mandates (e.g., PCI DSS, HIPAA). The Legal and Compliance departments shall be consulted to determine specific retention and evidentiary requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for policy approval, implementation, and enforcement.<br><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the operational management of the SIEM, 24/7 monitoring, alert triage, and log analysis.<br><strong>System and Network Owners/Administrators:</strong> Responsible for ensuring logging is enabled and properly configured on all assets under their control and that logs are forwarded to the SIEM.<br><strong>IT Infrastructure Team:</strong> Responsible for provisioning and maintaining the underlying SIEM infrastructure and immutable log storage.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any suspected disabling or tampering of logging functions immediately to the SOC.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy, including but not limited to disabling logging, tampering with log data, or failing to forward logs to the central SIEM, may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Systems found to be non-compliant with logging requirements may be disconnected from the network until remediated.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or their designee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or regulatory environment. All revisions must be formally approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:57.561536,"""[\""Both\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:53:09.914,NULL
44,533399,Disciplinary Policy,"<p>This Disciplinary Policy establishes the formal framework for addressing violations of [Company Name]'s information security policies, standards, and procedures. Its purpose is to define clear, consistent, and legally defensible actions to deter misconduct, protect corporate assets, ensure compliance with regulatory obligations (including ISO 27001 and SOC 2), and maintain a culture of security accountability.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other individuals or entities (""Personnel"") who have been granted access to [Company Name]'s information assets, information systems, or physical premises. It covers all actions or omissions that constitute a violation of published security requirements.</p>

<p><strong>Major Violation:</strong> An act or omission that results, or could reasonably result, in significant harm to [Company Name]'s information assets, financial loss, legal liability, regulatory sanction, or severe reputational damage. Examples include malicious data exfiltration, deliberate circumvention of security controls, gross negligence leading to a data breach, or repeated minor violations.</p><p><strong>Minor Violation:</strong> An act or omission that constitutes a breach of policy but does not meet the threshold of a Major Violation, typically involving unintentional error, first-time lapses, or lack of awareness where no material harm occurred.</p><p><strong>Formal Disciplinary Action:</strong> A documented process resulting in consequences such as written warning, suspension, demotion, or termination of employment/contract.</p><p><strong>Investigation Authority:</strong> The designated individual or team (e.g., CISO, Human Resources, Legal) empowered to investigate policy violations and recommend disciplinary measures.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates strict adherence to all information security directives. Violations shall be investigated promptly, objectively, and confidentially. Disciplinary actions shall be applied consistently and proportionally to the severity of the violation, its intent, and the individual's history, in accordance with applicable labor laws and contractual agreements. Retaliation against individuals reporting violations in good faith is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Violation Identification and Reporting:</strong> All personnel shall immediately report suspected or actual policy violations to their manager, Human Resources, or via the designated anonymous reporting channel. Managers receiving such reports must escalate them to the Investigation Authority within one business day.</p><p><strong>5.2 Investigation Protocol:</strong> The Investigation Authority shall initiate a formal, documented investigation for all reported Major Violations and for patterns of Minor Violations. Investigations must preserve evidence, maintain confidentiality, and include interviews, log analysis, and forensic examination as required. The subject of the investigation shall be informed of the allegations and given an opportunity to respond, in line with legal requirements.</p><p><strong>5.3 Evidence Handling and Documentation:</strong> All evidence related to a policy violation investigation, including system logs, email records, witness statements, and forensic images, shall be collected and stored in a secure, tamper-evident manner to maintain a legally admissible chain of custody. A final investigation report shall be produced for all Major Violations.</p><p><strong>5.4 Disciplinary Action Matrix:</strong> Disciplinary actions shall be determined based on a pre-defined matrix aligning violation severity with consequences. For a <em>Minor Violation</em>, actions may include verbal counseling, mandatory re-training, or a written warning. For a <em>Major Violation</em>, actions must include formal written warning, suspension without pay, termination of employment/contract, and potential legal referral. All actions require documented approval from Human Resources and the CISO or equivalent.</p><p><strong>5.5 Communication and Enforcement of Actions:</strong> The outcome of disciplinary proceedings shall be communicated formally in writing to the individual concerned. Any required technical actions (e.g., revocation of access rights, disabling of accounts) must be executed by the IT Department immediately upon formal notification from the Investigation Authority.</p><p><strong>5.6 Record Retention:</strong> Complete records of all investigations and disciplinary actions, including the final report and evidence log, shall be retained in a secure repository for a minimum period as defined by legal and regulatory requirements, but not less than seven years.</p>

<p>This policy and its enforcement procedures are designed to satisfy specific control objectives from ISO 27001 (A.7.2.2 Information security awareness, education and training; A.7.3.1 Termination or change of employment responsibilities) and SOC 2 (CC6.1 Logical and physical access controls are implemented to protect against unauthorized access). All disciplinary processes shall be conducted in full compliance with local, state, and national employment laws. Legal counsel must be consulted prior to initiating any action that may result in termination or legal proceedings.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the policy, oversees the investigation of security violations, and approves disciplinary recommendations in conjunction with HR.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Owns the disciplinary process, ensures legal and procedural compliance, maintains personnel records, and executes formal employment actions.</p><p><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for enforcing security policies within their teams, reporting violations, and participating in investigations as required.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and complying with all security policies and for reporting violations.</p><p><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Provides guidance on the legal aspects of investigations and disciplinary actions to mitigate organizational risk.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and pursuit of civil or criminal legal remedies. Failure by managers or the Investigation Authority to enforce this policy constitutes a violation of the policy itself and may result in disciplinary action.</p>

<p>This Disciplinary Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or following significant changes in relevant laws, regulations, or the company's operational environment. All revisions require formal approval by [Company Name]'s executive management.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Disciplinary Policy establishes the formal framework for addressing violations of [Company Name]'s information security policies, standards, and procedures. Its purpose is to define clear, consistent, and legally defensible actions to deter misconduct, protect corporate assets, ensure compliance with regulatory obligations (including ISO 27001 and SOC 2), and maintain a culture of security accountability.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any other individuals or entities (\\\""Personnel\\\"") who have been granted access to [Company Name]'s information assets, information systems, or physical premises. It covers all actions or omissions that constitute a violation of published security requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Major Violation:</strong> An act or omission that results, or could reasonably result, in significant harm to [Company Name]'s information assets, financial loss, legal liability, regulatory sanction, or severe reputational damage. Examples include malicious data exfiltration, deliberate circumvention of security controls, gross negligence leading to a data breach, or repeated minor violations.</p><p><strong>Minor Violation:</strong> An act or omission that constitutes a breach of policy but does not meet the threshold of a Major Violation, typically involving unintentional error, first-time lapses, or lack of awareness where no material harm occurred.</p><p><strong>Formal Disciplinary Action:</strong> A documented process resulting in consequences such as written warning, suspension, demotion, or termination of employment/contract.</p><p><strong>Investigation Authority:</strong> The designated individual or team (e.g., CISO, Human Resources, Legal) empowered to investigate policy violations and recommend disciplinary measures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates strict adherence to all information security directives. Violations shall be investigated promptly, objectively, and confidentially. Disciplinary actions shall be applied consistently and proportionally to the severity of the violation, its intent, and the individual's history, in accordance with applicable labor laws and contractual agreements. Retaliation against individuals reporting violations in good faith is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Violation Identification and Reporting:</strong> All personnel shall immediately report suspected or actual policy violations to their manager, Human Resources, or via the designated anonymous reporting channel. Managers receiving such reports must escalate them to the Investigation Authority within one business day.</p><p><strong>5.2 Investigation Protocol:</strong> The Investigation Authority shall initiate a formal, documented investigation for all reported Major Violations and for patterns of Minor Violations. Investigations must preserve evidence, maintain confidentiality, and include interviews, log analysis, and forensic examination as required. The subject of the investigation shall be informed of the allegations and given an opportunity to respond, in line with legal requirements.</p><p><strong>5.3 Evidence Handling and Documentation:</strong> All evidence related to a policy violation investigation, including system logs, email records, witness statements, and forensic images, shall be collected and stored in a secure, tamper-evident manner to maintain a legally admissible chain of custody. A final investigation report shall be produced for all Major Violations.</p><p><strong>5.4 Disciplinary Action Matrix:</strong> Disciplinary actions shall be determined based on a pre-defined matrix aligning violation severity with consequences. For a <em>Minor Violation</em>, actions may include verbal counseling, mandatory re-training, or a written warning. For a <em>Major Violation</em>, actions must include formal written warning, suspension without pay, termination of employment/contract, and potential legal referral. All actions require documented approval from Human Resources and the CISO or equivalent.</p><p><strong>5.5 Communication and Enforcement of Actions:</strong> The outcome of disciplinary proceedings shall be communicated formally in writing to the individual concerned. Any required technical actions (e.g., revocation of access rights, disabling of accounts) must be executed by the IT Department immediately upon formal notification from the Investigation Authority.</p><p><strong>5.6 Record Retention:</strong> Complete records of all investigations and disciplinary actions, including the final report and evidence log, shall be retained in a secure repository for a minimum period as defined by legal and regulatory requirements, but not less than seven years.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy and its enforcement procedures are designed to satisfy specific control objectives from ISO 27001 (A.7.2.2 Information security awareness, education and training; A.7.3.1 Termination or change of employment responsibilities) and SOC 2 (CC6.1 Logical and physical access controls are implemented to protect against unauthorized access). All disciplinary processes shall be conducted in full compliance with local, state, and national employment laws. Legal counsel must be consulted prior to initiating any action that may result in termination or legal proceedings.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the policy, oversees the investigation of security violations, and approves disciplinary recommendations in conjunction with HR.</p><p><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Owns the disciplinary process, ensures legal and procedural compliance, maintains personnel records, and executes formal employment actions.</p><p><strong>All Managers/Supervisors:</strong> Responsible for enforcing security policies within their teams, reporting violations, and participating in investigations as required.</p><p><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for understanding and complying with all security policies and for reporting violations.</p><p><strong>Legal Department:</strong> Provides guidance on the legal aspects of investigations and disciplinary actions to mitigate organizational risk.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and pursuit of civil or criminal legal remedies. Failure by managers or the Investigation Authority to enforce this policy constitutes a violation of the policy itself and may result in disciplinary action.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Disciplinary Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or following significant changes in relevant laws, regulations, or the company's operational environment. All revisions require formal approval by [Company Name]'s executive management.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.883888,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:53:38.794,NULL
27,736300,Patch Management Policy,"<p>This Patch Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, evaluation, testing, deployment, and verification of software patches, updates, and security fixes across all [Company Name] information systems and assets. The purpose is to mitigate vulnerabilities, maintain system integrity, ensure operational stability, and comply with regulatory and contractual security obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, vendors, and third-party service providers who are responsible for the management, operation, or maintenance of any hardware, software, operating system, firmware, or application owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, servers, workstations, network devices, virtual environments, cloud-based infrastructure, and endpoint devices.</p>

<p><strong>Critical Patch:</strong> A software update that addresses a vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 9.0 or higher, or is actively being exploited in the wild.<br/><strong>Emergency Patch:</strong> A patch released to mitigate a zero-day exploit or a vulnerability causing widespread, severe impact.<br/><strong>Patch:</strong> A piece of software designed to update, fix, or improve a computer program or its supporting data.<br/><strong>Patch Cycle:</strong> The scheduled, recurring period (e.g., monthly, quarterly) during which non-emergency patches are evaluated and deployed.<br/><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive and risk-based approach to patch management. All systems within scope shall be maintained at a supported patch level. The unauthorized modification of system software or the deliberate delay of patch deployment without formal, documented exception is strictly prohibited.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability and Patch Identification</strong><br/>Automated tools shall continuously monitor and inventory all in-scope assets. A formal process must subscribe to and review vendor security advisories, CERT notifications, and threat intelligence feeds at least weekly.</p><p><strong>5.2 Risk Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br/>All identified patches shall be classified based on severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low), asset criticality, and exploit availability. A documented risk assessment must justify the deployment timeline for each patch classification.</p><p><strong>5.3 Patch Testing</strong><br/>All patches, excluding Emergency Patches, must be deployed to a dedicated, representative test environment prior to production deployment. Testing must validate functionality, performance, and interoperability. Emergency Patches require an abbreviated but documented test procedure.</p><p><strong>5.4 Deployment Procedures</strong><br/>Patch deployment shall be performed using automated enterprise management tools where feasible. All deployments must be scheduled during approved maintenance windows. A rollback plan must be documented and tested for all Critical and High severity patches.</p><p><strong>5.5 Verification and Reporting</strong><br/>Post-deployment, automated verification scans must confirm successful patch installation and the closure of the associated vulnerability. A centralized log of all patch management activities, including testing results and deployment status, shall be maintained for audit.</p><p><strong>5.6 Exception Management</strong><br/>Any deviation from mandated patch timelines requires a formal, risk-accepted exception. Exceptions must be documented, include a mitigation plan and an expiration date, and be approved by both the system owner and the CISO or designated authority.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.12.6.1, A.14.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC7.1). Patch management logs and exception records shall be retained for a minimum of two years and made available for internal and external audits. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties and breach of contractual agreements.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the policy.<br/><strong>System Owners:</strong> Responsible for authorizing patch deployment on their systems and approving exceptions.<br/><strong>IT Operations / Patch Management Team:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical processes defined in Section 5.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must report suspected vulnerabilities to the IT Service Desk immediately and shall not interfere with patch deployment activities.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of patch controls that leads to a security incident will be treated as gross misconduct.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or following a significant security incident or change in the threat landscape. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Patch Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for the systematic identification, evaluation, testing, deployment, and verification of software patches, updates, and security fixes across all [Company Name] information systems and assets. The purpose is to mitigate vulnerabilities, maintain system integrity, ensure operational stability, and comply with regulatory and contractual security obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, vendors, and third-party service providers who are responsible for the management, operation, or maintenance of any hardware, software, operating system, firmware, or application owned, leased, or operated by [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, servers, workstations, network devices, virtual environments, cloud-based infrastructure, and endpoint devices.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Critical Patch:</strong> A software update that addresses a vulnerability with a CVSS base score of 9.0 or higher, or is actively being exploited in the wild.<br/><strong>Emergency Patch:</strong> A patch released to mitigate a zero-day exploit or a vulnerability causing widespread, severe impact.<br/><strong>Patch:</strong> A piece of software designed to update, fix, or improve a computer program or its supporting data.<br/><strong>Patch Cycle:</strong> The scheduled, recurring period (e.g., monthly, quarterly) during which non-emergency patches are evaluated and deployed.<br/><strong>Vulnerability:</strong> A weakness in an information system, system security procedures, internal controls, or implementation that could be exploited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates a proactive and risk-based approach to patch management. All systems within scope shall be maintained at a supported patch level. The unauthorized modification of system software or the deliberate delay of patch deployment without formal, documented exception is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Vulnerability and Patch Identification</strong><br/>Automated tools shall continuously monitor and inventory all in-scope assets. A formal process must subscribe to and review vendor security advisories, CERT notifications, and threat intelligence feeds at least weekly.</p><p><strong>5.2 Risk Assessment and Prioritization</strong><br/>All identified patches shall be classified based on severity (Critical, High, Medium, Low), asset criticality, and exploit availability. A documented risk assessment must justify the deployment timeline for each patch classification.</p><p><strong>5.3 Patch Testing</strong><br/>All patches, excluding Emergency Patches, must be deployed to a dedicated, representative test environment prior to production deployment. Testing must validate functionality, performance, and interoperability. Emergency Patches require an abbreviated but documented test procedure.</p><p><strong>5.4 Deployment Procedures</strong><br/>Patch deployment shall be performed using automated enterprise management tools where feasible. All deployments must be scheduled during approved maintenance windows. A rollback plan must be documented and tested for all Critical and High severity patches.</p><p><strong>5.5 Verification and Reporting</strong><br/>Post-deployment, automated verification scans must confirm successful patch installation and the closure of the associated vulnerability. A centralized log of all patch management activities, including testing results and deployment status, shall be maintained for audit.</p><p><strong>5.6 Exception Management</strong><br/>Any deviation from mandated patch timelines requires a formal, risk-accepted exception. Exceptions must be documented, include a mitigation plan and an expiration date, and be approved by both the system owner and the CISO or designated authority.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to satisfy control objectives from ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.12.6.1, A.14.2.1) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC6.1, CC7.1). Patch management logs and exception records shall be retained for a minimum of two years and made available for internal and external audits. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties and breach of contractual agreements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority and accountability for the policy.<br/><strong>System Owners:</strong> Responsible for authorizing patch deployment on their systems and approving exceptions.<br/><strong>IT Operations / Patch Management Team:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical processes defined in Section 5.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must report suspected vulnerabilities to the IT Service Desk immediately and shall not interfere with patch deployment activities.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may face legal action. Deliberate circumvention of patch controls that leads to a security incident will be treated as gross misconduct.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or following a significant security incident or change in the threat landscape. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.088362,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-19 19:54:03.421,NULL
58,gdpr-privacy-policy,GDPR Privacy Policy,"<h1>Purpose</h1><p>This General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the lawful, fair, and transparent processing of personal data. Its purpose is to define the principles, controls, and procedures required to achieve and demonstrate compliance with Regulation (EU) 2016/679, thereby protecting the rights and freedoms of data subjects and mitigating organizational risk.</p>

<h1>Scope</h1><p>This policy applies to all processing of personal data, as defined by the GDPR, conducted by [Company Name], its employees, contractors, and any third-party processors acting on its behalf. It encompasses all systems, applications, databases, and manual filing systems where personal data is processed, regardless of geographic location, if the processing relates to offering goods or services to data subjects in the European Union (EU) or the monitoring of their behavior within the EU.</p>

<h1>Definitions</h1><ul><li><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person ('data subject').</li><li><strong>Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on personal data (e.g., collection, recording, storage, alteration, retrieval, disclosure).</li><li><strong>Data Controller:</strong> [Company Name], which determines the purposes and means of processing personal data.</li><li><strong>Data Processor:</strong> A natural or legal person which processes personal data on behalf of the controller.</li><li><strong>Special Categories of Data (Sensitive Data):</strong> Personal data revealing racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, genetic data, biometric data, health data, or data concerning a natural person's sex life or sexual orientation.</li><li><strong>Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA):</strong> A systematic process to identify and mitigate data protection risks in processing activities.</li></ul>

<h1>Policy Statement</h1><p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting the privacy and security of personal data. All processing activities shall adhere to the core principles of lawfulness, fairness, transparency, purpose limitation, data minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, and confidentiality. [Company Name] mandates a 'privacy by design and by default' approach to all systems and business processes.</p>

<h1>Policy Requirements</h1><h2>5.1 Lawful Basis for Processing</h2><p>Processing of personal data shall only be conducted under one of the following lawful bases, which must be documented prior to processing initiation: consent, contract performance, legal obligation, vital interests, public task, or legitimate interests. For Special Categories of Data, an additional condition under Article 9 must be met and recorded.</p><h2>5.2 Data Subject Rights Management</h2><p>Technical and procedural controls shall be implemented to facilitate and log the fulfillment of data subject rights within the mandated one-month timeframe. This includes automated systems for receiving, validating, and tracking requests for access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, and objection. All such requests and their outcomes must be logged in an immutable audit trail.</p><h2>5.3 Data Protection by Design and by Default</h2><p>All new systems, applications, and processes involving personal data must undergo a privacy review. Technical measures such as pseudonymization, encryption (using AES-256 or equivalent), and strict access controls (role-based access control - RBAC) shall be the default configuration. Data collection shall be minimized to what is strictly necessary for the specified purpose.</p><h2>5.4 Data Inventory and Mapping</h2><p>A centralized register of processing activities (ROPA) shall be maintained, documenting all processing operations, including: purposes, data categories, data subject categories, recipients, retention schedules, technical/organizational security measures, and international transfer mechanisms. This register must be kept current and be available for supervisory authority review.</p><h2>5.5 Vendor and Third-Party Processor Management</h2><p>All engagements with Data Processors require a GDPR-compliant Data Processing Agreement (DPA) prior to any data transfer. Due diligence must assess the processor's technical and organizational security measures. Ongoing monitoring shall include regular audits or reviews of certificates (e.g., ISO 27001, SOC 2 Type II).</p><h2>5.6 Security of Processing</h2><p>Personal data shall be protected against unauthorized or unlawful processing and against accidental loss, destruction, or damage. Mandatory controls include: encryption of data at rest and in transit, multi-factor authentication (MFA) for all administrative access, comprehensive logging of access and modifications with a 90-day minimum retention, and regular vulnerability scanning and penetration testing.</p><h2>5.7 Breach Notification Protocol</h2><p>A formal incident response plan must define the procedure for identifying, assessing, and reporting personal data breaches. Breaches likely to result in a risk to rights and freedoms shall be reported to the relevant supervisory authority within 72 hours of awareness. Notification to affected data subjects is required where the breach poses a high risk, without undue delay.</p><h2>5.8 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h2><p>Personal data shall not be kept in a form which permits identification of data subjects for longer than is necessary. Retention schedules, linked to specific data types and processing purposes, shall be enforced. Secure disposal methods (e.g., cryptographic erasure for storage media, shredding for physical records) must be used upon schedule expiration.</p><h2>5.9 International Data Transfers</h2><p>Transfers of personal data to third countries or international organizations are strictly prohibited unless appropriate safeguards under Chapter V of the GDPR are in place. This includes reliance on an Adequacy Decision, Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) supplemented by a Transfer Impact Assessment, or Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs).</p>

<h1>Compliance and Legal Requirements</h1><p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR). Adherence also supports compliance with related frameworks including national data protection laws, ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls (e.g., A.5.34, A.8.3, A.8.10), and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Privacy and Security. The Data Protection Officer (DPO) or designated privacy lead is responsible for monitoring regulatory developments and ensuring this policy is updated to reflect legal obligations.</p>

<h1>Roles and Responsibilities</h1><ul><li><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO)/Privacy Lead:</strong> Monitors compliance, advises on DPIA, cooperates with supervisory authorities, and acts as the point of contact.</li><li><strong>Executive Management:</strong> Accountable for resourcing and fostering a culture of data protection compliance.</li><li><strong>Information Technology Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical security controls specified in Section 5.</li><li><strong>Data Controllers (Business Unit Heads):</strong> Responsible for defining lawful basis, purpose, and data minimization for their processing activities and maintaining the ROPA.</li><li><strong>All Employees & Contractors:</strong> Mandated to complete annual data protection training and to process personal data only in accordance with this policy and documented instructions.</li></ul>

<h1>Enforcement</h1><p>Violation of this policy constitutes a serious breach of [Company Name]'s standards of conduct and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract. Deliberate or negligent violations that lead to a personal data breach may also result in personal liability and regulatory fines imposed on the individual by supervisory authorities.</p>

<h1>Review and Revision</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the Data Protection Officer (DPO) or designated privacy lead, or more frequently in response to significant changes in law, technology, business operations, or following a security incident. All revisions must be approved by [Company Name]'s executive management and communicated to all relevant personnel. The version control and revision history shall be maintained as part of the policy documentation.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Purpose</h1><p>This General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) Privacy Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the lawful, fair, and transparent processing of personal data. Its purpose is to define the principles, controls, and procedures required to achieve and demonstrate compliance with Regulation (EU) 2016/679, thereby protecting the rights and freedoms of data subjects and mitigating organizational risk.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Scope</h1><p>This policy applies to all processing of personal data, as defined by the GDPR, conducted by [Company Name], its employees, contractors, and any third-party processors acting on its behalf. It encompasses all systems, applications, databases, and manual filing systems where personal data is processed, regardless of geographic location, if the processing relates to offering goods or services to data subjects in the European Union (EU) or the monitoring of their behavior within the EU.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Definitions</h1><ul><li><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person ('data subject').</li><li><strong>Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on personal data (e.g., collection, recording, storage, alteration, retrieval, disclosure).</li><li><strong>Data Controller:</strong> [Company N