ong> Responsible for ensuring API functionality requests include security requirements and for approving data classification for API payloads.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any API found to be in violation of this policy will be subject to immediate remediation. The Information Security Team is authorized to disconnect or block access to non-compliant APIs from the production network until all security deficiencies are corrected. Violations by employees or contractors will be handled according to the [Company Name] Human Resources policies and may constitute grounds for disciplinary action. Violations by third-party providers will be escalated per the terms of the relevant service agreement.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This API Security Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Team at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to relevant compliance frameworks. All revisions require formal approval by the CISO. The latest approved version of this policy must be made accessible to all relevant personnel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:23.236925,"""[\""SOC 2\"",\""NIST CSF\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 05:59:05.029,NULL
10,5,Vendor Risk Management Policy,"<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to identify, assess, monitor, and mitigate risks associated with third-party vendors that have access to, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information assets or provide critical services. The policy ensures that vendor relationships are managed in a manner consistent with [Company Name]'s security, compliance, and business continuity objectives, and in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, and subsidiaries involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party vendors (""Vendors""). It encompasses all Vendor relationships where the Vendor has, or could have, access to [Company Name]'s information systems, non-public data (including Confidential, Proprietary, and Personal Data), or provides a service integral to [Company Name]'s operations. This includes, but is not limited to, cloud service providers (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), IT outsourcing, managed service providers, and business process outsourcing.</p>

<p><strong>Vendor:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, services, or technology to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Critical Vendor:</strong> A Vendor whose failure or security breach would cause significant operational disruption, financial loss, legal/regulatory impact, or reputational damage to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Due Diligence:</strong> The comprehensive assessment process conducted prior to engaging a Vendor to evaluate their security, financial, and operational controls.<br/><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The formal analysis to determine the level of risk a Vendor relationship poses, based on data sensitivity, service criticality, and control adequacy.<br/><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A contract defining the specific performance and availability metrics a Vendor must meet.<br/><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Vendor's controls, either directly or via independent reports (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Vendor relationships shall be governed by a formal risk management lifecycle. No department shall engage a Vendor that will have access to [Company Name] information assets without prior completion of a risk-based due diligence and approval process. Vendor contracts must incorporate explicit security, confidentiality, and compliance obligations. Ongoing monitoring and periodic re-assessment of Vendors, especially Critical Vendors, are strictly required.</p>

<h4>5.1 Vendor Classification and Risk Tiering</h4><p>All prospective Vendors shall be classified into risk tiers (e.g., High, Medium, Low) based on a standardized assessment of:<br/>• The sensitivity and classification of data to be accessed (e.g., PII, PHI, intellectual property).<br/>• The criticality of the service to business operations.<br/>• The level of integration with [Company Name]'s internal systems and networks.<br/>The risk tier shall dictate the rigor of the subsequent due diligence and ongoing monitoring requirements.</p><h4>5.2 Pre-Engagement Due Diligence</h4><p>Prior to contract signing, the responsible business unit, in conjunction with Information Security and Legal, must complete a Vendor Security Questionnaire (VSQ). For High and Medium-risk Vendors, this must be supplemented by one or more of the following:<br/>• Review of a current, Type II SOC 2 report (or equivalent) with a clean opinion.<br/>• Review of a valid ISO 27001 certification certificate and scope statement.<br/>• Completion of a third-party security assessment.<br/>Evidence of due diligence shall be formally documented and retained.</p><h4>5.3 Contractual Security Requirements</h4><p>All Vendor contracts, including amendments and statements of work, must include, at a minimum:<br/>• Explicit data protection and confidentiality clauses.<br/>• A Right-to-Audit clause permitting [Company Name] to request a SOC 2 report or conduct a security assessment.<br/>• Notification requirements for security incidents, including a defined timeframe (e.g., within 24 hours).<br/>• Data breach response and liability provisions.<br/>• Data return and destruction obligations upon contract termination.<br/>• Subcontractor management obligations, requiring Vendor to flow down equivalent security requirements.</p><h4>5.4 Continuous Monitoring</h4><p>High-risk (Critical) Vendors shall be subject to annual re-assessment. Monitoring activities shall include:<br/>• Annual review of the Vendor's most recent SOC 2 report or equivalent.<br/>• Verification of ongoing compliance with contractual SLAs and security requirements.<br/>• Tracking of Vendor-related security incidents and their resolution.<br/>• Re-assessment of the Vendor's risk tier following significant changes to the service or [Company Name]'s use of it.</p><h4>5.5 Termination and Offboarding</h4><p>Upon termination of a Vendor relationship, the responsible business unit must coordinate with IT and Information Security to:<br/>• Ensure all [Company Name] data is securely returned or verifiably destroyed, with evidence provided.<br/>• Revoke all system, network, and physical access privileges granted to the Vendor.<br/>• Archive contractual and due diligence documentation in accordance with records retention policy.</p>

<p>Vendor management activities shall be conducted to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and PCI-DSS. The policy is designed to satisfy control objectives within ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (e.g., A.15.1 & A.15.2 - Supplier Relationships) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity). Evidence of compliance with this policy, including completed assessments, contracts, and monitoring reports, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Business Unit/Procurement:</strong> Responsible for initial Vendor identification, completing the business justification, and initiating the due diligence process.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining the risk assessment methodology, reviewing security due diligence artifacts, classifying Vendor risk, and approving security controls.<br/><strong>Legal/Compliance Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring contractual terms meet policy and regulatory requirements.<br/><strong>Vendor Risk Management Committee (or designated authority):</strong> Responsible for final approval of High-risk Vendor engagements and overseeing the policy's implementation.<br/><strong>All Employees:</strong> Prohibited from bypassing the formal Vendor onboarding process or sharing sensitive data with unauthorized Vendors.</p>

<p>Any employee or contractor found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Engaging a Vendor without completing the mandated due diligence and approval process is strictly prohibited. The Information Security Department is authorized to suspend or terminate any Vendor connection that poses an immediate and severe risk to [Company Name]'s information assets.</p>

<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the Information Security Department and the Vendor Risk Management Committee. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, regulatory environment, business operations, or audit findings. All revisions require formal approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated executive.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to identify, assess, monitor, and mitigate risks associated with third-party vendors that have access to, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information assets or provide critical services. The policy ensures that vendor relationships are managed in a manner consistent with [Company Name]'s security, compliance, and business continuity objectives, and in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, and subsidiaries involved in the procurement, management, or oversight of third-party vendors (\\\""Vendors\\\""). It encompasses all Vendor relationships where the Vendor has, or could have, access to [Company Name]'s information systems, non-public data (including Confidential, Proprietary, and Personal Data), or provides a service integral to [Company Name]'s operations. This includes, but is not limited to, cloud service providers (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS), IT outsourcing, managed service providers, and business process outsourcing.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Vendor:</strong> Any external entity providing goods, services, or technology to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Critical Vendor:</strong> A Vendor whose failure or security breach would cause significant operational disruption, financial loss, legal/regulatory impact, or reputational damage to [Company Name].<br/><strong>Due Diligence:</strong> The comprehensive assessment process conducted prior to engaging a Vendor to evaluate their security, financial, and operational controls.<br/><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> The formal analysis to determine the level of risk a Vendor relationship poses, based on data sensitivity, service criticality, and control adequacy.<br/><strong>Service Level Agreement (SLA):</strong> A contract defining the specific performance and availability metrics a Vendor must meet.<br/><strong>Right-to-Audit Clause:</strong> A contractual provision granting [Company Name] the right to assess the Vendor's controls, either directly or via independent reports (e.g., SOC 2, ISO 27001).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Vendor relationships shall be governed by a formal risk management lifecycle. No department shall engage a Vendor that will have access to [Company Name] information assets without prior completion of a risk-based due diligence and approval process. Vendor contracts must incorporate explicit security, confidentiality, and compliance obligations. Ongoing monitoring and periodic re-assessment of Vendors, especially Critical Vendors, are strictly required.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Vendor Classification and Risk Tiering</h4><p>All prospective Vendors shall be classified into risk tiers (e.g., High, Medium, Low) based on a standardized assessment of:<br/>• The sensitivity and classification of data to be accessed (e.g., PII, PHI, intellectual property).<br/>• The criticality of the service to business operations.<br/>• The level of integration with [Company Name]'s internal systems and networks.<br/>The risk tier shall dictate the rigor of the subsequent due diligence and ongoing monitoring requirements.</p><h4>5.2 Pre-Engagement Due Diligence</h4><p>Prior to contract signing, the responsible business unit, in conjunction with Information Security and Legal, must complete a Vendor Security Questionnaire (VSQ). For High and Medium-risk Vendors, this must be supplemented by one or more of the following:<br/>• Review of a current, Type II SOC 2 report (or equivalent) with a clean opinion.<br/>• Review of a valid ISO 27001 certification certificate and scope statement.<br/>• Completion of a third-party security assessment.<br/>Evidence of due diligence shall be formally documented and retained.</p><h4>5.3 Contractual Security Requirements</h4><p>All Vendor contracts, including amendments and statements of work, must include, at a minimum:<br/>• Explicit data protection and confidentiality clauses.<br/>• A Right-to-Audit clause permitting [Company Name] to request a SOC 2 report or conduct a security assessment.<br/>• Notification requirements for security incidents, including a defined timeframe (e.g., within 24 hours).<br/>• Data breach response and liability provisions.<br/>• Data return and destruction obligations upon contract termination.<br/>• Subcontractor management obligations, requiring Vendor to flow down equivalent security requirements.</p><h4>5.4 Continuous Monitoring</h4><p>High-risk (Critical) Vendors shall be subject to annual re-assessment. Monitoring activities shall include:<br/>• Annual review of the Vendor's most recent SOC 2 report or equivalent.<br/>• Verification of ongoing compliance with contractual SLAs and security requirements.<br/>• Tracking of Vendor-related security incidents and their resolution.<br/>• Re-assessment of the Vendor's risk tier following significant changes to the service or [Company Name]'s use of it.</p><h4>5.5 Termination and Offboarding</h4><p>Upon termination of a Vendor relationship, the responsible business unit must coordinate with IT and Information Security to:<br/>• Ensure all [Company Name] data is securely returned or verifiably destroyed, with evidence provided.<br/>• Revoke all system, network, and physical access privileges granted to the Vendor.<br/>• Archive contractual and due diligence documentation in accordance with records retention policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Vendor management activities shall be conducted to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and PCI-DSS. The policy is designed to satisfy control objectives within ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (e.g., A.15.1 & A.15.2 - Supplier Relationships) and relevant SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity). Evidence of compliance with this policy, including completed assessments, contracts, and monitoring reports, shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Business Unit/Procurement:</strong> Responsible for initial Vendor identification, completing the business justification, and initiating the due diligence process.<br/><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining the risk assessment methodology, reviewing security due diligence artifacts, classifying Vendor risk, and approving security controls.<br/><strong>Legal/Compliance Department:</strong> Responsible for ensuring contractual terms meet policy and regulatory requirements.<br/><strong>Vendor Risk Management Committee (or designated authority):</strong> Responsible for final approval of High-risk Vendor engagements and overseeing the policy's implementation.<br/><strong>All Employees:</strong> Prohibited from bypassing the formal Vendor onboarding process or sharing sensitive data with unauthorized Vendors.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee or contractor found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Engaging a Vendor without completing the mandated due diligence and approval process is strictly prohibited. The Information Security Department is authorized to suspend or terminate any Vendor connection that poses an immediate and severe risk to [Company Name]'s information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Vendor Risk Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the Information Security Department and the Vendor Risk Management Committee. Revisions shall be made in response to changes in the threat landscape, regulatory environment, business operations, or audit findings. All revisions require formal approval from the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated executive.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:55.223023,"""[\""ISO 27001 / SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 05:59:40.731,NULL
48,924745,Visitor Access Policy,"<h1>Visitor Access Policy</h1>
<h2>1. Purpose</h2>
<p>The purpose of this Visitor Access Policy is to establish formal standards, procedures, and controls governing the authorization, monitoring, and management of all non-employee individuals granted physical or logical access to [Company Name] facilities, information systems, or sensitive data. This policy ensures that all visitor access aligns with the organization&#39;s security, safety, and compliance objectives.</p>
<h2>2. Scope</h2>
<p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, interns, vendors, and third-party representatives of [Company Name]. It further governs all visitors, including but not limited to clients, guests, service providers, and temporary personnel, who are granted access to company premises, networks, systems, or data resources. Compliance is mandatory for all individuals interacting with [Company Name] assets.</p>
<h2>3. Policy Statement</h2>
<p>[Company Name] is committed to maintaining the security, integrity, and confidentiality of its physical assets, information systems, and proprietary data. All visitor access must be pre-authorized, properly logged, actively monitored, and strictly limited to the minimum necessary for conducting approved business. Unauthorized access is expressly prohibited, and all visitors must adhere to company security protocols during their presence.</p>
<h2>4. Visitor Access Procedures</h2>
<h3>4.1 Pre-Arrival Authorization</h3>
<p>All visitors must receive advance authorization from an authorized employee sponsor. The sponsor must submit a visitor access request through the designated system, specifying the visitor&#39;s name, company, purpose, duration, and required access areas.</p>
<h3>4.2 Registration and Identification</h3>
<p>Upon arrival, all visitors must register at the designated reception or security point. They must present valid government-issued photo identification, which will be verified and logged. Visitors will be issued a temporary badge that must be visibly worn at all times while on premises.</p>
<h3>4.3 Escort Requirements</h3>
<p>Unless explicitly pre-authorized for unescorted access, visitors must be accompanied by their sponsor or a designated employee at all times. The escort is responsible for the visitor&#39;s conduct and adherence to all company policies.</p>
<h3>4.4 Access Limitations</h3>
<p>Visitor access is restricted to approved areas only. Access to sensitive zones, data centers, server rooms, and areas containing confidential information is prohibited unless specifically authorized in writing by the relevant department head.</p>
<h3>4.5 System and Network Access</h3>
<p>Logical access to [Company Name] networks, systems, or data by visitors is generally prohibited. Exceptions require formal approval from the Information Security Officer and must utilize segregated guest networks with appropriate security controls.</p>
<h3>4.6 Departure Procedures</h3>
<p>Upon departure, visitors must return all issued access badges, equipment, or materials to the reception or security point. The sponsor must confirm the visitor&#39;s departure and the return of all assets.</p>
<h2>5. Roles and Responsibilities</h2>
<h3>5.1 Employees/Sponsors</h3>
<p>Initiate and authorize visitor requests; escort visitors as required; ensure visitor compliance with policies; and confirm proper departure.</p>
<h3>5.2 Security/Reception Personnel</h3>
<p>Verify visitor identity; manage the registration and badging process; maintain access logs; and monitor visitor movement in accordance with this policy.</p>
<h3>5.3 Information Security Team</h3>
<p>Define technical controls for logical access; audit compliance; and investigate any policy violations related to system or data access.</p>
<h3>5.4 All Visitors</h3>
<p>Comply with all stated procedures; wear identification visibly; respect access restrictions; and report any security concerns immediately.</p>
<h2>6. Enforcement</h2>
<p>Violations of this policy by employees, sponsors, or visitors will result in appropriate disciplinary action, which may include revocation of access, termination of contracts, legal action, or reporting to relevant authorities. [Company Name] will investigate all suspected breaches and apply corrective measures to prevent recurrence.</p>
<h2>7. Policy Review and Maintenance</h2>
<p>This policy shall be reviewed annually by the Security and Compliance Committee to ensure its continued relevance, effectiveness, and alignment with industry standards and regulatory requirements. Updates will be communicated to all stakeholders upon approval.</p>
","""[\""Purpose\"",\""Scope\"",\""Terms and Definitions\"",\""Policy Requirements/Statements\"",\""Sign-in\"",\""Escorts\"",\""Badging\"",\""Enforcement/Violations\"",\""Review/Approval\""]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.302394,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-11 08:47:22.552259,NULL
11,6,Business Continuity Policy,"<p>This Business Continuity Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the resilience, continuity, and timely recovery of critical business operations, information assets, and supporting infrastructure in the event of a disruptive incident. The policy mandates the development, implementation, maintenance, and testing of a Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) to protect against, reduce the likelihood of, and ensure recovery from such incidents, thereby safeguarding stakeholder interests and organizational reputation.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all personnel (employees, contractors, and third-party agents), business processes, information systems, data, facilities, and technologies owned, operated, or under the contractual control of [Company Name] that are deemed critical to organizational operations. It encompasses all locations from which [Company Name] conducts business.</p>

<p><strong>Business Continuity Management System (BCMS):</strong> The holistic management process that identifies potential threats to the organization and provides a framework for building resilience and the capability for an effective response.<br><br><strong>Business Impact Analysis (BIA):</strong> A systematic process to determine and evaluate the potential effects of an interruption to critical business operations as a result of a disaster, accident, or emergency.<br><br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable length of time that a business process or system can be unavailable following a disruptive event.<br><br><strong>Recovery Point Objective (RPO):</strong> The maximum acceptable amount of data loss measured in time, defining the point to which information must be restored.<br><br><strong>Disruptive Incident:</strong> Any event, whether anticipated or unanticipated, that causes an interruption to the normal operation of a critical business process or system.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to maintaining operational resilience through a formally documented and regularly tested Business Continuity Management System. The organization shall proactively identify risks, establish recovery objectives, and implement robust continuity and recovery strategies to ensure the availability of critical services and the protection of information assets in alignment with business priorities, contractual obligations, and regulatory requirements.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Governance and Program Management</strong><br>A formal Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) shall be established, documented, and maintained. A designated Business Continuity Manager shall be appointed with the authority to oversee the BCMS lifecycle.</p><p><strong>5.2 Business Impact Analysis (BIA) and Risk Assessment</strong><br>A documented BIA shall be conducted at least annually, or upon significant organizational change, to identify and prioritize critical business processes, their interdependencies, and supporting resources. The BIA must define Recovery Time Objectives (RTO) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPO) for each critical process. A complementary risk assessment shall identify threats to these processes.</p><p><strong>5.3 Business Continuity Strategy and Plans</strong><br>Based on the BIA, documented recovery strategies shall be developed and approved to meet the defined RTOs and RPOs. This includes, but is not limited to, strategies for data backup, system redundancy, alternate work locations, and third-party service continuity. Detailed, actionable Business Continuity Plans (BCPs) and Disaster Recovery Plans (DRPs) shall be developed for all critical functions.</p><p><strong>5.4 Backup and Recovery Controls</strong><br>All data and system configurations deemed critical by the BIA shall be backed up according to their RPO. Backups must be encrypted, stored in a geographically separate location from the primary data center, and tested for integrity and recoverability. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for critical backups is mandated where feasible to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Testing and Exercises</strong><br>BCPs and DRPs shall be tested through structured exercises at least annually. Testing scenarios must be comprehensive, ranging from table-top walkthroughs to full-scale functional recovery tests. All test results, including successes and failures, shall be documented, reviewed by management, and used to update plans.</p><p><strong>5.6 Training and Awareness</strong><br>All personnel with defined roles in the BCP/DRP shall receive role-specific training upon plan assignment and following any significant plan update. General business continuity awareness training shall be provided to all employees annually.</p><p><strong>5.7 Communication and Notification</strong><br>A documented crisis communication plan shall be maintained, specifying internal and external notification procedures, communication channels, and spokespersons during a disruptive incident. Contact lists for key personnel, emergency services, and critical third parties must be reviewed and updated quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.8 Third-Party and Supply Chain Continuity</strong><br>Critical third-party service providers and suppliers shall be contractually obligated to maintain business continuity plans that meet or exceed [Company Name]'s RTO and RPO requirements for the services provided. Their compliance and test results shall be subject to review as part of vendor risk management.</p><p><strong>5.9 Plan Maintenance and Documentation</strong><br>All BCMS documentation, including the BIA, BCPs, DRPs, test reports, and training records, shall be maintained under version control. Plans shall be formally reviewed and updated at least annually, or immediately following a test, exercise, actual incident, or significant organizational change.</p>

<p>The BCMS shall be designed and operated to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to operational resilience and data availability. This policy aligns with the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.17) and SOC 2 (Availability and Security Criteria). Evidence of BIA, plan testing, and management review shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>

<p><strong>Executive Management / Board:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for approving this policy, providing resources for the BCMS, and ensuring organizational commitment.<br><br><strong>Business Continuity Manager:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day management, coordination, and maintenance of the BCMS, including overseeing the BIA, plan development, testing, and reporting.<br><br><strong>Process / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for participating in the BIA, providing input for recovery strategies, and executing specific recovery procedures for their owned processes/systems during an incident.<br><br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical recovery controls, including backup systems, redundant infrastructure, and the execution of the Disaster Recovery Plan.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for familiarizing themselves with general continuity procedures, participating in training and exercises, and following management directives during a disruptive incident.</p>

<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal or financial penalties for the individual and [Company Name]. Failure to participate in required BCP/DRP testing or training is considered a violation of this policy.</p>

<p>This Business Continuity Policy shall be reviewed by the Business Continuity Manager and relevant stakeholders at least annually. Revisions must be approved by Executive Management. The policy version history shall be maintained to track all changes.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Business Continuity Policy establishes the mandatory framework for [Company Name] to ensure the resilience, continuity, and timely recovery of critical business operations, information assets, and supporting infrastructure in the event of a disruptive incident. The policy mandates the development, implementation, maintenance, and testing of a Business Continuity Management System (BCMS) to protect against, reduce the likelihood of, and ensure recovery from such incidents, thereby safeguarding stakeholder interests and organizational reputation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all personnel (employees, contractors, and third-party agents), business processes, information systems, data, facilities, and technologies owned, operated, or under the contractual control of [Company Name] that are deemed critical to organizational operations. It encompasses all locations from which [Company Name] conducts business.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Business Continuity Management System (BCMS):</strong> The holistic management process that identifies potential threats to the organization and provides a framework for building resilience and the capability for an effective response.<br><br><strong>Business Impact Analysis (BIA):</strong> A systematic process to determine and evaluate the potential effects of an interruption to critical business operations as a result of a disaster, accident, or emergency.<br><br><strong>Recovery Time Objective (RTO):</strong> The maximum acceptable length of time that a business process or system can