RPOs. This includes, but is not limited to, strategies for data backup, system redundancy, alternate work locations, and third-party service continuity. Detailed, actionable Business Continuity Plans (BCPs) and Disaster Recovery Plans (DRPs) shall be developed for all critical functions.</p><p><strong>5.4 Backup and Recovery Controls</strong><br>All data and system configurations deemed critical by the BIA shall be backed up according to their RPO. Backups must be encrypted, stored in a geographically separate location from the primary data center, and tested for integrity and recoverability. The use of immutable or write-once-read-many (WORM) storage for critical backups is mandated where feasible to protect against ransomware or malicious deletion.</p><p><strong>5.5 Testing and Exercises</strong><br>BCPs and DRPs shall be tested through structured exercises at least annually. Testing scenarios must be comprehensive, ranging from table-top walkthroughs to full-scale functional recovery tests. All test results, including successes and failures, shall be documented, reviewed by management, and used to update plans.</p><p><strong>5.6 Training and Awareness</strong><br>All personnel with defined roles in the BCP/DRP shall receive role-specific training upon plan assignment and following any significant plan update. General business continuity awareness training shall be provided to all employees annually.</p><p><strong>5.7 Communication and Notification</strong><br>A documented crisis communication plan shall be maintained, specifying internal and external notification procedures, communication channels, and spokespersons during a disruptive incident. Contact lists for key personnel, emergency services, and critical third parties must be reviewed and updated quarterly.</p><p><strong>5.8 Third-Party and Supply Chain Continuity</strong><br>Critical third-party service providers and suppliers shall be contractually obligated to maintain business continuity plans that meet or exceed [Company Name]'s RTO and RPO requirements for the services provided. Their compliance and test results shall be subject to review as part of vendor risk management.</p><p><strong>5.9 Plan Maintenance and Documentation</strong><br>All BCMS documentation, including the BIA, BCPs, DRPs, test reports, and training records, shall be maintained under version control. Plans shall be formally reviewed and updated at least annually, or immediately following a test, exercise, actual incident, or significant organizational change.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The BCMS shall be designed and operated to ensure compliance with all applicable legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations related to operational resilience and data availability. This policy aligns with the requirements of ISO 27001 (Annex A.17) and SOC 2 (Availability and Security Criteria). Evidence of BIA, plan testing, and management review shall be maintained for audit purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Executive Management / Board:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for approving this policy, providing resources for the BCMS, and ensuring organizational commitment.<br><br><strong>Business Continuity Manager:</strong> Responsible for the day-to-day management, coordination, and maintenance of the BCMS, including overseeing the BIA, plan development, testing, and reporting.<br><br><strong>Process / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for participating in the BIA, providing input for recovery strategies, and executing specific recovery procedures for their owned processes/systems during an incident.<br><br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Responsible for implementing and maintaining the technical recovery controls, including backup systems, redundant infrastructure, and the execution of the Disaster Recovery Plan.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for familiarizing themselves with general continuity procedures, participating in training and exercises, and following management directives during a disruptive incident.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may lead to legal or financial penalties for the individual and [Company Name]. Failure to participate in required BCP/DRP testing or training is considered a violation of this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Business Continuity Policy shall be reviewed by the Business Continuity Manager and relevant stakeholders at least annually. Revisions must be approved by Executive Management. The policy version history shall be maintained to track all changes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:55.434025,"""[\""ISO 27001 / SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:00:17.133,NULL
13,8,Asset Management Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Asset Management Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for identifying, classifying, protecting, and managing all information assets owned, controlled, or processed by [Company Name]. This policy ensures that assets are appropriately protected throughout their lifecycle, commensurate with their value, sensitivity, and criticality to business operations. It provides the foundation for accountability, risk management, and compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other regulatory requirements.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets, regardless of format or location, that are owned, leased, managed, or otherwise under the custodianship of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, store, transmit, or dispose of company information assets. The scope encompasses physical assets (e.g., hardware, media), logical assets (e.g., software, data, licenses), and intangible assets (e.g., intellectual property, reputation).</p>

<p><strong>Asset:</strong> Any item that has value to the organization, including information, software, physical devices, and services.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> An individual or entity with approved management responsibility for an asset, accountable for its protection and lifecycle.<br><strong>Asset Custodian:</strong> An individual or entity responsible for the day-to-day safeguarding and operational management of an asset on behalf of the Owner.<br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> A body of information, defined and managed as a single unit, that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Asset Inventory:</strong> A formal register that records and tracks all assets, their attributes, classification, ownership, and location.<br><strong>Asset Lifecycle:</strong> The stages an asset passes through from planning and acquisition through to disposal.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be formally identified, classified, and managed according to their business value, sensitivity, and legal requirements. A centralized, accurate, and up-to-date asset inventory shall be maintained. All assets shall have a designated Owner responsible for defining protection requirements and authorizing access. Protection controls shall be implemented and maintained throughout the asset lifecycle to ensure confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>

<p><strong>5.1 Asset Identification and Inventory</strong><br>5.1.1 A comprehensive inventory of all information assets shall be established and maintained.<br>5.1.2 The inventory must, at a minimum, record: unique identifier, asset name, type, classification, owner, custodian, location, and acquisition/retirement dates.<br>5.1.3 The asset inventory shall be reviewed and verified for accuracy at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.2 Asset Classification and Labeling</strong><br>5.2.1 All information assets shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br>5.2.2 Classification must be based on legal requirements, value, criticality, and sensitivity to unauthorized disclosure or modification.<br>5.2.3 Assets containing or processing Confidential or Restricted data must be clearly labeled, either physically or electronically.<br><br><strong>5.3 Asset Ownership and Accountability</strong><br>5.3.1 Every asset recorded in the inventory must have a formally designated and documented Asset Owner.<br>5.3.2 The Asset Owner is responsible for approving access rights, defining acceptable use, and authorizing changes to the asset.<br>5.3.3 Ownership assignments shall be reviewed annually and upon any significant organizational change.<br><br><strong>5.4 Acceptable Use of Assets</strong><br>5.4.1 All use of company assets must be for authorized business purposes only.<br>5.4.2 Personal use of company information assets is strictly prohibited unless explicitly authorized in writing.<br>5.4.3 Users shall not install unapproved hardware or software on company assets.<br><br><strong>5.5 Asset Lifecycle Management</strong><br>5.5.1 Formal procedures shall govern the secure acquisition, deployment, maintenance, and disposal of all assets.<br>5.5.2 Prior to disposal, transfer, or reassignment, all data must be securely erased using approved methods (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization).<br>5.5.3 Physical assets shall be disposed of in a manner that prevents recovery of information and complies with environmental regulations.<br><br><strong>5.6 Protection of Mobile and Removable Assets</strong><br>5.6.1 Company-owned mobile devices (laptops, tablets, smartphones) and removable media (USB drives) must employ full-disk encryption.<br>5.6.2 The use of removable media for storing Confidential or Restricted information is forbidden unless encrypted and explicitly authorized by the Asset Owner.<br>5.6.3 All mobile assets must be capable of being remotely wiped if lost or stolen.<br><br><strong>5.7 Software Asset Management</strong><br>5.7.1 The installation and use of all software must comply with licensing agreements.<br>5.7.2 The use of unlicensed, trial, or shareware software for business purposes is strictly prohibited.<br>5.7.3 A software inventory shall be maintained and reconciled against purchase records at least annually.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), intellectual property rights, and software licensing agreements. Asset management practices shall support audit requirements for ISO 27001 (Annex A 8.1, 8.2) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6). Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action, regulatory penalties, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Senior Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provide ultimate accountability and resource commitment for the asset management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>Asset Owners:</strong> Define protection requirements, authorize access, and are accountable for their assigned assets.<br><strong>IT Department / Asset Custodians:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls, manage the asset inventory, and execute day-to-day operational procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Comply with this policy, use assets responsibly, and report any loss, theft, or misuse of assets immediately.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Violations include, but are not limited to: failure to maintain an accurate asset inventory, unauthorized use or modification of assets, removal of assets without authorization, and negligent handling leading to asset loss or data breach. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor compliance and investigate suspected violations.</p>

<p>This Asset Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CISO or designated authority. Revisions shall be made in response to significant security incidents, changes in business processes, technological developments, or updates to applicable laws and standards. All revisions require formal approval from the CISO and must be communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Asset Management Policy is to establish a comprehensive framework for identifying, classifying, protecting, and managing all information assets owned, controlled, or processed by [Company Name]. This policy ensures that assets are appropriately protected throughout their lifecycle, commensurate with their value, sensitivity, and criticality to business operations. It provides the foundation for accountability, risk management, and compliance with ISO 27001, SOC 2, and other regulatory requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets, regardless of format or location, that are owned, leased, managed, or otherwise under the custodianship of [Company Name]. This includes, but is not limited to, all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who create, access, process, store, transmit, or dispose of company information assets. The scope encompasses physical assets (e.g., hardware, media), logical assets (e.g., software, data, licenses), and intangible assets (e.g., intellectual property, reputation).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Asset:</strong> Any item that has value to the organization, including information, software, physical devices, and services.<br><strong>Asset Owner:</strong> An individual or entity with approved management responsibility for an asset, accountable for its protection and lifecycle.<br><strong>Asset Custodian:</strong> An individual or entity responsible for the day-to-day safeguarding and operational management of an asset on behalf of the Owner.<br><strong>Information Asset:</strong> A body of information, defined and managed as a single unit, that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Asset Inventory:</strong> A formal register that records and tracks all assets, their attributes, classification, ownership, and location.<br><strong>Asset Lifecycle:</strong> The stages an asset passes through from planning and acquisition through to disposal.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all information assets shall be formally identified, classified, and managed according to their business value, sensitivity, and legal requirements. A centralized, accurate, and up-to-date asset inventory shall be maintained. All assets shall have a designated Owner responsible for defining protection requirements and authorizing access. Protection controls shall be implemented and maintained throughout the asset lifecycle to ensure confidentiality, integrity, and availability.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>5.1 Asset Identification and Inventory</strong><br>5.1.1 A comprehensive inventory of all information assets shall be established and maintained.<br>5.1.2 The inventory must, at a minimum, record: unique identifier, asset name, type, classification, owner, custodian, location, and acquisition/retirement dates.<br>5.1.3 The asset inventory shall be reviewed and verified for accuracy at least quarterly.<br><br><strong>5.2 Asset Classification and Labeling</strong><br>5.2.1 All information assets shall be classified according to the corporate data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted).<br>5.2.2 Classification must be based on legal requirements, value, criticality, and sensitivity to unauthorized disclosure or modification.<br>5.2.3 Assets containing or processing Confidential or Restricted data must be clearly labeled, either physically or electronically.<br><br><strong>5.3 Asset Ownership and Accountability</strong><br>5.3.1 Every asset recorded in the inventory must have a formally designated and documented Asset Owner.<br>5.3.2 The Asset Owner is responsible for approving access rights, defining acceptable use, and authorizing changes to the asset.<br>5.3.3 Ownership assignments shall be reviewed annually and upon any significant organizational change.<br><br><strong>5.4 Acceptable Use of Assets</strong><br>5.4.1 All use of company assets must be for authorized business purposes only.<br>5.4.2 Personal use of company information assets is strictly prohibited unless explicitly authorized in writing.<br>5.4.3 Users shall not install unapproved hardware or software on company assets.<br><br><strong>5.5 Asset Lifecycle Management</strong><br>5.5.1 Formal procedures shall govern the secure acquisition, deployment, maintenance, and disposal of all assets.<br>5.5.2 Prior to disposal, transfer, or reassignment, all data must be securely erased using approved methods (e.g., NIST 800-88 media sanitization).<br>5.5.3 Physical assets shall be disposed of in a manner that prevents recovery of information and complies with environmental regulations.<br><br><strong>5.6 Protection of Mobile and Removable Assets</strong><br>5.6.1 Company-owned mobile devices (laptops, tablets, smartphones) and removable media (USB drives) must employ full-disk encryption.<br>5.6.2 The use of removable media for storing Confidential or Restricted information is forbidden unless encrypted and explicitly authorized by the Asset Owner.<br>5.6.3 All mobile assets must be capable of being remotely wiped if lost or stolen.<br><br><strong>5.7 Software Asset Management</strong><br>5.7.1 The installation and use of all software must comply with licensing agreements.<br>5.7.2 The use of unlicensed, trial, or shareware software for business purposes is strictly prohibited.<br>5.7.3 A software inventory shall be maintained and reconciled against purchase records at least annually.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), intellectual property rights, and software licensing agreements. Asset management practices shall support audit requirements for ISO 27001 (Annex A 8.1, 8.2) and SOC 2 (CC6.1, CC6.6). Non-compliance with this policy may result in legal action, regulatory penalties, and reputational damage to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Senior Management / Board of Directors:</strong> Provide ultimate accountability and resource commitment for the asset management program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy.<br><strong>Asset Owners:</strong> Define protection requirements, authorize access, and are accountable for their assigned assets.<br><strong>IT Department / Asset Custodians:</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls, manage the asset inventory, and execute day-to-day operational procedures.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Comply with this policy, use assets responsibly, and report any loss, theft, or misuse of assets immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. Violations include, but are not limited to: failure to maintain an accurate asset inventory, unauthorized use or modification of assets, removal of assets without authorization, and negligent handling leading to asset loss or data breach. The Information Security team is authorized to monitor compliance and investigate suspected violations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Asset Management Policy shall be reviewed at least annually by the CISO or designated authority. Revisions shall be made in response to significant security incidents, changes in business processes, technological developments, or updates to applicable laws and standards. All revisions require formal approval from the CISO and must be communicated to all relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:55.851538,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:00:53.88,NULL
19,14,Acceptable Use Policy,"<p>This Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory rules and behavioral standards for the appropriate and secure use of [Company Name]'s Information Assets, including but not limited to information systems, networks, data, software, and hardware. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate and customer data, mitigate risks, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and define the boundaries of acceptable conduct for all Users.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all Users, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals or entities granted access to [Company Name]'s Information Assets, irrespective of location or device ownership. It encompasses all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, data, and services, as well as the use of personal devices (BYOD) when accessing corporate resources or data.</p>

<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, network, software, hardware, or service owned, leased, operated by, or entrusted to [Company Name] that stores, processes, or transmits information.<br><strong>User:</strong> Any individual or system entity authorized to access [Company Name]'s Information Assets.<br><strong>Authorized Use:</strong> Use of Information Assets that is directly related to an individual's job responsibilities and in accordance with this policy and all other applicable policies.<br><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, customers, partners, or operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.<br><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned computing device used to access corporate resources.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to and use of its Information Assets is a privilege granted solely for conducting authorized business. All Users shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, data protection, and responsible conduct. Any use of Information Assets that violates this policy, compromises security, or is illegal, unethical, or for personal gain is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment/contract and legal prosecution.</p>

<h4>5.1 General Use and Access Controls</h4><p>5.1.1 Users shall only access Information Assets for which they have received explicit authorization. Sharing of credentials (usernames, passwords, tokens) is forbidden.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled for all remote access and privileged administrative accounts without exception.<br>5.1.3 Users must lock workstations and secure mobile devices with a password/PIN/biometric lock when unattended.<br>5.1.4 The installation of unauthorized software, including freeware, shareware, or trial versions, on company-owned devices is prohibited without prior written approval from IT Security.</p><h4>5.2 Data Protection and Handling</h4><p>5.2.1 Confidential Information must be classified and handled according to the Data Classification and Handling Policy. Transmission of Confidential Information over public networks requires encryption using company-approved protocols (e.g., TLS 1.2+).<br>5.2.2 Storing Confidential Information on unauthorized cloud storage services (e.g., personal Dropbox, Google Drive) or removable media without encryption is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Data must be disposed of securely using approved methods (e.g., certified data destruction, secure deletion tools) when no longer required for business purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Network and System Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Users shall not attempt to bypass network security controls, including firewalls, proxies, or intrusion prevention systems.<br>5.3.2 The connection of unauthorized network devices (e.g., wireless access points, routers, network switches) to the corporate network is strictly prohibited.<br>5.3.3 All wireless access to corporate networks must use company-provisioned and IT-managed infrastructure. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted wireless networks for corporate access is forbidden.</p><h4>5.4 Acceptable and Prohibited Activities</h4><p>5.4.1 <strong>Prohibited:</strong> Accessing, downloading, storing, or transmitting unlawful, offensive, or harassing material. Engaging in activities for personal financial gain. Running non-business related servers (e.g., game, file, web servers). Deliberately introducing malware or engaging in hacking activities.<br>5.4.2 <strong>Permitted with Caution:</strong> Limited personal use of email and internet is permitted provided it does not interfere with work duties, consume excessive bandwidth, or violate any other policy clause.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Privacy</h4><p>5.5.1 Users acknowledge that [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, inspect, and log all activity on its Information Assets without prior notice to ensure policy compliance, security, and operational integrity.<br>5.5.2 Users shall have no expectation of privacy regarding data stored on or transmitted through company-owned systems and networks.</p>

<p>All use of Information Assets must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), copyright laws, and industry-specific regulations. Users are responsible for understanding the legal requirements pertinent to their role. Any suspected violation of law must be reported immediately to the Legal or Compliance department.</p>

<p><strong>All Users:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this policy, safeguarding credentials, and reporting security incidents.<br><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their staff are aware of and adhere to this policy and for enforcing compliance within their teams.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and managing access rights in line with this policy.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Is the owner of this policy, responsible for its maintenance, interpretation, and overall enforcement.</p>

<p>Violations of this policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Depending on the severity and circumstances, enforcement actions may include, but are not limited to: formal reprimand, suspension of system access, disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All violations will be investigated by the IT Security and Human Resources departments.</p>

<p>This Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version is the authoritative version and is made available to all Users through the company policy portal.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory rules and behavioral standards for the appropriate and secure use of [Company Name]'s Information Assets, including but not limited to information systems, networks, data, software, and hardware. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate and customer data, mitigate risks, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and define the boundaries of acceptable conduct for all Users.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all Users, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, interns, and any other individuals or entities granted access to [Company Name]'s Information Assets, irrespective of location or device ownership. It encompasses all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, data, and services, as well as the use of personal devices (BYOD) when accessing corporate resources or data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Assets:</strong> Any data, system, network, software, hardware, or service owned, leased, operated by, or entrusted to [Company Name] that stores, processes, or transmits information.<br><strong>User:</strong> Any individual or system entity authorized to access [Company Name]'s Information Assets.<br><strong>Authorized Use:</strong> Use of Information Assets that is directly related to an individual's job responsibilities and in accordance with this policy and all other applicable policies.<br><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its employees, customers, partners, or operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.<br><strong>BYOD (Bring Your Own Device):</strong> A personally owned computing device used to access corporate resources.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that access to and use of its Information Assets is a privilege granted solely for conducting authorized business. All Users shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, data protection, and responsible conduct. Any use of Information Assets that violates this policy, compromises security, or is illegal, unethical, or for personal gain is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment/contract and legal prosecution.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 General Use and Access Controls</h4><p>5.1.1 Users shall only access Information Assets for which they have received explicit authorization. Sharing of credentials (usernames, passwords, tokens) is forbidden.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enabled for all remote access and privileged administrative accounts without exception.<br>5.1.3 Users must lock workstations and secure mobile devices with a password/PIN/biometric lock when unattended.<br>5.1.4 The installation of unauthorized software, including freeware, shareware, or trial versions, on company-owned devices is prohibited without prior written approval from IT Security.</p><h4>5.2 Data Protection and Handling</h4><p>5.2.1 Confidential Information must be classified and handled according to the Data Classification and Handling Policy. Transmission of Confidential Information over public networks requires encryption using company-approved protocols (e.g., TLS 1.2+).<br>5.2.2 Storing Confidential Information on unauthorized cloud storage services (e.g., personal Dropbox, Google Drive) or removable media without encryption is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 Data must be disposed of securely using approved methods (e.g., certified data destruction, secure deletion tools) when no longer required for business purposes.</p><h4>5.3 Network and System Security</h4><p>5.3.1 Users shall not attempt to bypass network security controls, including firewalls, proxies, or intrusion prevention systems.<br>5.3.2 The connection of unauthorized network devices (e.g., wireless access points, routers, network switches) to the corporate network is strictly prohibited.<br>5.3.3 All wireless access to corporate networks must use company-provisioned and IT-managed infrastructure. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted wireless networks for corporate access is forbidden.</p><h4>5.4 Acceptable and Prohibited Activities</h4><p>5.4.1 <strong>Prohibited:</strong> Accessing, downloading, storing, or transmitting unlawful, offensive, or harassing material. Engaging in activities for personal financial gain. Running non-business related servers (e.g., game, file, web servers). Deliberately introducing malware or engaging in hacking activities.<br>5.4.2 <strong>Permitted with Caution:</strong> Limited personal use of email and internet is permitted provided it does not interfere with work duties, consume excessive bandwidth, or violate any other policy clause.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring and Privacy</h4><p>5.5.1 Users acknowledge that [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, inspect, and log all activity on its Information Assets without prior notice to ensure policy compliance, security, and operational integrity.<br>5.5.2 Users shall have no expectation of privacy regarding data stored on or transmitted through company-owned systems and networks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>All use of Information Assets must comply with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), copyright laws, and industry-specific regulations. Users are responsible for understanding the legal requirements pertinent to their role. Any suspected violation of law must be reported immediately to the Legal or Compliance department.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>All Users:</strong> Are responsible for reading, understanding, and complying with this policy, safeguarding credentials, and reporting security incidents.<br><strong>Department Heads/Managers:</strong> Are responsible for ensuring their staff are aware of and adhere to this policy and for enforcing compliance within their teams.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Is responsible for implementing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and managing access rights in line with this policy.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Is the owner of this policy, responsible for its maintenance, interpretation, and overall enforcement.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Violations of this policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Depending on the severity and circumstances, enforcement actions may include, but are not limited to: formal reprimand, suspension of system access, disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All violations will be investigated by the IT Security and Human Resources departments.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, technology, or regulatory environment. Revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version is the authoritative version and is made available to all Users through the company policy portal.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:57.141012,"""[\""Both\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:01:27.204,NULL
29,569126,Secure Disposal Policy,"<p>This Secure Disposal Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure and permanent destruction of all [Company Name] information assets, regardless of media type, to prevent unauthorized disclosure, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data remanence. The policy mandates a consistent, auditable process for sanitizing and disposing of information that has reached the end of its lifecycle.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties acting on behalf of [Company Name] (""Personnel""). It governs the secure disposal of all information assets owned, leased, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: electronic media (hard drives, SSDs, USB drives, mobile devices, backup tapes, optical media), paper records, and any hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, multifunction devices, networking equipment).</p>

<p><strong>Clear:</strong> A logical sanitization technique using standard Read and Write commands to overwrite data with non-sensitive values (e.g., all zeros).<br><strong>Cryptographic Erasure:</strong> The process of securely deleting or rendering inaccessible the encryption key used to protect data on self-encrypting drives (SEDs), effectively destroying the data.<br><strong>Degaussing:</strong> The process of reducing or eliminating the magnetic field of storage media, rendering it unusable and data unrecoverable.<br><strong>Destruction:</strong> The physical disintegration, shredding, pulverizing, or incineration of media such that data recovery is infeasible.<br><strong>Media Sanitization:</strong> A general term referring to the process of irreversibly removing data from storage media.<br><strong>Purge:</strong> A sanitization method that protects against laboratory attack, typically involving multiple overwrite passes, degaussing, or cryptographic erasure.<br><strong>Removable Electronic Media:</strong> Storage devices designed to be easily removed from a computer, including USB flash drives, external hard drives, SD cards, and CDs/DVDs.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall ensure that all information assets are disposed of in a secure, controlled, and documented manner commensurate with their data classification. The unauthorized disposal, discarding, or transfer of any company information asset to an unapproved entity is strictly prohibited. All disposal activities must be performed in accordance with the requirements specified in this policy and associated procedures.</p>

<h4>5.1 Classification-Based Disposal Standard</h4><p>All media must be sanitized according to its highest classification level. The following standards shall be applied:<br><strong>Public/Internal Use Only:</strong> Media may be cleared via a single overwrite pass or securely erased using a software utility that meets NIST SP 800-88 Revision 1 guidelines.<br><strong>Confidential/Restricted:</strong> Media must be purged. This requires either cryptographic erasure (for SEDs) or overwriting with a minimum of three passes using a recognized algorithm (e.g., DoD 5220.22-M). Degaussing is required for magnetic media where overwriting is not feasible.<br><strong>Highly Confidential/Regulated (e.g., PHI, PII, Financial Data):</strong> Media containing such data must be physically destroyed. Hard drives and SSDs shall be shredded or disintegrated. Paper records shall be cross-cut shredded to a particle size not exceeding 5mm x 40mm.</p><h4>5.2 Inventory and Chain of Custody</h4><p>A formal, documented inventory of all assets pending disposal must be maintained. The chain of custody for media from decommissioning through final destruction shall be recorded and include: asset identifier, serial number, custodian name, date of transfer, and destruction certificate reference. Gaps in the chain of custody constitute a security incident.</p><h4>5.3 Approved Sanitization and Destruction Methods</h4><p>The use of unapproved software tools or ad-hoc destruction methods is forbidden. All sanitization software must be validated to produce verifiable logs. Physical destruction must be performed by an NAID AAA-certified vendor or using on-site equipment that meets DIN 66399 Level P-5 (paper) or E-3 (hard drives) standards. Degaussers must meet NSA/CSS EPL standards for the media type being sanitized.</p><h4>5.4 Verification and Certification</h4><p>For software-based sanitization, a verification log confirming successful completion must be generated and retained. For physical destruction, a formal Certificate of Destruction (CoD) must be obtained from the service provider for each batch. The CoD must include the destruction date, method, serial numbers (or range), and be signed by an authorized representative of the destruction vendor.</p><h4>5.5 Removable Media and End-of-Life Hardware</h4><p>All end-user removable electronic media issued by [Company Name] must be returned to IT for centralized, secure disposal. Personnel are strictly prohibited from disposing of such media through municipal waste or recycling. All hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, copiers) must have internal storage media sanitized or physically removed and destroyed prior to disposal, donation, or return to a lessor.</p><h4>5.6 Retention of Documentation</h4><p>All disposal records, including inventories, chain of custody logs, sanitization verification reports, and Certificates of Destruction, shall be retained for a minimum period of seven (7) years, or as required by specific regulatory mandates (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR).</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant sections of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.10, A.8.12, A.8.14), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality), HIPAA Security Rule (164.310(d)(1) Device and Media Controls), GDPR (Article 32, Security of Processing), and other data protection regulations. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage for [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, and oversight of this policy; approving disposal vendors and methods.<br><strong>IT Department/Asset Management:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical disposal process, maintaining the asset inventory and chain of custody, and securely storing media pending destruction.<br><strong>Department Heads/Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for ensuring all personnel within their area understand and comply with this policy, and for initiating the disposal request process for assets under their control.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding all [Company Name] information assets in their possession and returning them to IT for secure disposal at end-of-life.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Deliberate or negligent disposal of information assets in an unsecure manner, or the failure to report a suspected breach of this policy, shall be treated as a serious security incident.</p>

<p>This Secure Disposal Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes to technology, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the [Company Name] executive management team. The latest approved version is the only version considered valid.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Secure Disposal Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the secure and permanent destruction of all [Company Name] information assets, regardless of media type, to prevent unauthorized disclosure, ensure compliance with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data remanence. The policy mandates a consistent, auditable process for sanitizing and disposing of information that has reached the end of its lifecycle.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties acting on behalf of [Company Name] (\\\""Personnel\\\""). It governs the secure disposal of all information assets owned, leased, or managed by [Company Name], including but not limited to: electronic media (hard drives, SSDs, USB drives, mobile devices, backup tapes, optical media), paper records, and any hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, multifunction devices, networking equipment).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Clear:</strong> A logical sanitization technique using standard Read and Write commands to overwrite data with non-sensitive values (e.g., all zeros).<br><strong>Cryptographic Erasure:</strong> The process of securely deleting or rendering inaccessible the encryption key used to protect data on self-encrypting drives (SEDs), effectively destroying the data.<br><strong>Degaussing:</strong> The process of reducing or eliminating the magnetic field of storage media, rendering it unusable and data unrecoverable.<br><strong>Destruction:</strong> The physical disintegration, shredding, pulverizing, or incineration of media such that data recovery is infeasible.<br><strong>Media Sanitization:</strong> A general term referring to the process of irreversibly removing data from storage media.<br><strong>Purge:</strong> A sanitization method that protects against laboratory attack, typically involving multiple overwrite passes, degaussing, or cryptographic erasure.<br><strong>Removable Electronic Media:</strong> Storage devices designed to be easily removed from a computer, including USB flash drives, external hard drives, SD cards, and CDs/DVDs.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] shall ensure that all information assets are disposed of in a secure, controlled, and documented manner commensurate with their data classification. The unauthorized disposal, discarding, or transfer of any company information asset to an unapproved entity is strictly prohibited. All disposal activities must be performed in accordance with the requirements specified in this policy and associated procedures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Classification-Based Disposal Standard</h4><p>All media must be sanitized according to its highest classification level. The following standards shall be applied:<br><strong>Public/Internal Use Only:</strong> Media may be cleared via a single overwrite pass or securely erased using a software utility that meets NIST SP 800-88 Revision 1 guidelines.<br><strong>Confidential/Restricted:</strong> Media must be purged. This requires either cryptographic erasure (for SEDs) or overwriting with a minimum of three passes using a recognized algorithm (e.g., DoD 5220.22-M). Degaussing is required for magnetic media where overwriting is not feasible.<br><strong>Highly Confidential/Regulated (e.g., PHI, PII, Financial Data):</strong> Media containing such data must be physically destroyed. Hard drives and SSDs shall be shredded or disintegrated. Paper records shall be cross-cut shredded to a particle size not exceeding 5mm x 40mm.</p><h4>5.2 Inventory and Chain of Custody</h4><p>A formal, documented inventory of all assets pending disposal must be maintained. The chain of custody for media from decommissioning through final destruction shall be recorded and include: asset identifier, serial number, custodian name, date of transfer, and destruction certificate reference. Gaps in the chain of custody constitute a security incident.</p><h4>5.3 Approved Sanitization and Destruction Methods</h4><p>The use of unapproved software tools or ad-hoc destruction methods is forbidden. All sanitization software must be validated to produce verifiable logs. Physical destruction must be performed by an NAID AAA-certified vendor or using on-site equipment that meets DIN 66399 Level P-5 (paper) or E-3 (hard drives) standards. Degaussers must meet NSA/CSS EPL standards for the media type being sanitized.</p><h4>5.4 Verification and Certification</h4><p>For software-based sanitization, a verification log confirming successful completion must be generated and retained. For physical destruction, a formal Certificate of Destruction (CoD) must be obtained from the service provider for each batch. The CoD must include the destruction date, method, serial numbers (or range), and be signed by an authorized representative of the destruction vendor.</p><h4>5.5 Removable Media and End-of-Life Hardware</h4><p>All end-user removable electronic media issued by [Company Name] must be returned to IT for centralized, secure disposal. Personnel are strictly prohibited from disposing of such media through municipal waste or recycling. All hardware containing non-volatile memory (e.g., printers, copiers) must have internal storage media sanitized or physically removed and destroyed prior to disposal, donation, or return to a lessor.</p><h4>5.6 Retention of Documentation</h4><p>All disposal records, including inventories, chain of custody logs, sanitization verification reports, and Certificates of Destruction, shall be retained for a minimum period of seven (7) years, or as required by specific regulatory mandates (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant sections of ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.10, A.8.12, A.8.14), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Confidentiality), HIPAA Security Rule (164.310(d)(1) Device and Media Controls), GDPR (Article 32, Security of Processing), and other data protection regulations. Failure to comply may result in regulatory penalties, legal liability, and reputational damage for [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Security Officer (ISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, maintenance, and oversight of this policy; approving disposal vendors and methods.<br><strong>IT Department/Asset Management:</strong> Responsible for executing the technical disposal process, maintaining the asset inventory and chain of custody, and securely storing media pending destruction.<br><strong>Department Heads/Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for ensuring all personnel within their area understand and comply with this policy, and for initiating the disposal request process for assets under their control.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding all [Company Name] information assets in their possession and returning them to IT for secure disposal at end-of-life.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal action. Deliberate or negligent disposal of information assets in an unsecure manner, or the failure to report a suspected breach of this policy, shall be treated as a serious security incident.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Secure Disposal Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Officer at least annually, or following significant changes to technology, business processes, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be approved by the [Company Name] executive management team. The latest approved version is the only version considered valid.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.296933,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""HIPAA\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:02:05.015,NULL
47,902632,AI Acceptable Use Policy,"<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory governance, security, and ethical framework for the acquisition, development, deployment, and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to ensure that all AI/ML activities align with organizational values, comply with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data integrity, model bias, security, and operational reliability. It serves as the authoritative control document for AI governance, satisfying core requirements of ISO 27001 (A.6, A.8, A.12, A.14, A.15, A.16) and SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel of [Company Name] (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all third-party partners who access, develop, manage, or interact with [Company Name]'s AI/ML systems, data, or infrastructure. It encompasses all stages of the AI lifecycle: data sourcing and preparation, model development (including training, fine-tuning, and testing), model deployment (in production or pilot environments), model inference/operation, and model decommissioning. This policy governs all AI/ML systems, whether developed in-house, acquired as commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) software, accessed via third-party APIs (e.g., OpenAI, Anthropic), or embedded within other enterprise applications.</p>

<p><strong>AI/ML System:</strong> Any software system that employs machine learning (including deep learning, natural language processing, computer vision), statistical, or rule-based algorithms to perform tasks typically requiring human intelligence, such as prediction, classification, recommendation, or content generation.<br><br><strong>Production Model:</strong> An AI/ML model that is deployed in a live environment and whose outputs are used to inform business decisions, automate processes, or interact with customers or internal users.<br><br><strong>Training Data:</strong> The dataset used to train or fine-tune an AI/ML model. This includes both structured and unstructured data.<br><br><strong>Model Artifacts:</strong> The final outputs of the model development process, including the trained model weights, configuration files, and associated metadata.<br><br><strong>Shadow IT AI:</strong> The unauthorized use, procurement, or development of AI tools or services not approved by the designated governance body (e.g., AI Governance Committee).<br><br><strong>High-Risk AI Application:</strong> An AI/ML system whose failure or error could result in significant harm, including but not limited to: physical injury, substantial financial loss, legal liability, violation of individual rights, or material damage to [Company Name]'s reputation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning activities shall be conducted in a secure, ethical, compliant, and accountable manner. The use of AI/ML technologies is a privilege granted to support business objectives, not a right. All personnel and third parties are strictly prohibited from engaging in any AI-related activity that violates this policy, applicable laws, or ethical norms. [Company Name] reserves the right to monitor, audit, and immediately suspend any AI/ML system or user activity that is non-compliant.</p>

<h4>5.1 Governance & Pre-Approval</h4><p>5.1.1. All new AI/ML projects, including the procurement of third-party AI services, must undergo a formal risk assessment and receive written pre-approval from the AI Governance Committee prior to any development, procurement, or data collection activities.<br>5.1.2. A designated business owner and a technical owner must be assigned for every approved AI/ML system in production, responsible for its ongoing performance, compliance, and risk management.<br>5.1.3. The use of personal or ""shadow IT"" AI tools (e.g., unauthorized use of public generative AI chatbots for company data) on [Company Name] devices or networks is strictly forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Data Governance & Integrity</h4><p>5.2.1. Training and operational data for AI/ML systems must be sourced in accordance with the Data Classification and Handling Policy. The use of Sensitive or Confidential data requires explicit authorization and must be documented in the system's data provenance record.<br>5.2.2. All training datasets must be version-controlled. A data card or similar artifact must be maintained, documenting the dataset's origin, composition, preprocessing steps, and any known biases.<br>5.2.3. Data used for model training must be rigorously validated for quality, accuracy, and representativeness. Sampling bias that could lead to discriminatory outcomes must be identified and mitigated prior to model training.</p><h4>5.3 Model Development & Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Model development must occur in isolated, secure environments (e.g., approved cloud AI platforms or air-gapped research clusters). Development on local workstations using production data is prohibited.<br>5.3.2. All model code, configurations, and training scripts shall be managed within the organization's approved version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history must be preserved and shall not be altered.<br>5.3.3. Model artifacts must be cryptographically signed and stored in a secure, access-controlled model registry. Access to production model artifacts shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege.</p><h4>5.4 Testing, Validation & Bias Mitigation</h4><p>5.4.1. Prior to deployment, all models must pass a comprehensive validation suite, including performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall), robustness testing (for adversarial examples), and fairness assessments across protected attributes (e.g., race, gender, age) as relevant.<br>5.4.2. For High-Risk AI Applications, an independent third-party audit or red-team exercise must be conducted, and the results approved by the AI Governance Committee.<br>5.4.3. A model card must be created and maintained for every production model, detailing its intended use, performance characteristics, limitations, and known ethical considerations.</p><h4>5.5 Secure Deployment & Operations</h4><p>5.5.1. AI/ML models shall only be deployed to environments that meet [Company Name]'s hardened infrastructure standards, including network segmentation, intrusion detection, and vulnerability management.<br>5.5.2. All model inference endpoints (APIs) must be protected by strong authentication (OAuth 2.0, API keys) and encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.5.3. Inputs to and outputs from production models must be logged in a secure, immutable audit log. Logs must include a unique session/user ID, timestamp, and a hash of the input/output data for non-repudiation.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring, Observability & Human Oversight</h4><p>5.6.1. Continuous monitoring of model performance (e.g., prediction drift, data drift) and system health (latency, error rates) must be implemented. Automated alerts must be configured for significant degradation.<br>5.6.2. A human-in-the-loop (HITL) review process is mandatory for High-Risk AI Applications, where a qualified human must review and approve critical model outputs before action is taken.<br>5.6.3. All AI/ML systems must have a documented and tested rollback procedure to a previous known-good state in the event of failure or compromise.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party & External AI Services</h4><p>5.7.1. The use of external AI/ML APIs or services (e.g., cloud AI, generative AI platforms) requires a completed vendor security assessment and a signed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that addresses data ownership, confidentiality, and prohibits the vendor from using [Company Name] data for model training.<br>5.7.2. Prompt engineering for generative AI services must follow secure coding practices. Prompts must not contain Sensitive or Confidential information unless the service is explicitly approved for such data and is configured with data isolation guarantees.</p>

<p>All AI/ML activities must comply with applicable international, federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including but not limited to: data protection and privacy laws (GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, etc.), sector-specific regulations (HIPAA, GLBA, PCI-DSS where applicable), and emerging AI-specific legislation (e.g., EU AI Act, US Executive Order on AI). Systems that process personal data must implement Privacy by Design principles, including data minimization and purpose limitation. [Company Name] shall maintain records of AI system risk assessments, model cards, data cards, and audit logs as evidence of due diligence and compliance for a minimum period defined by the Records Retention Policy, or as required by law.</p>

<p><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Responsible for setting strategic direction, approving projects, defining risk tolerance, and overseeing policy compliance. Chaired by the CISO or designated executive.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the security of all AI/ML systems. Responsible for policy enforcement, security architecture review, and incident response.<br><br><strong>Data Owners & Business Unit Leaders:</strong> Accountable for the business use, ethical application, and risk management of AI systems within their domain. Must ensure adequate resources for monitoring and maintenance.<br><br><strong>AI/ML Developers & Data Scientists:</strong> Responsible for adhering to secure development practices, documenting their work, performing bias testing, and collaborating with security teams during design and deployment.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to complete mandatory AI ethics and security awareness training. Must report any suspected policy violations, security incidents, or unethical AI behavior through the established reporting channel.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Violations include, but are not limited to: deploying an AI system without approval, using prohibited data, circumventing security controls, or failing to perform required monitoring. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect, quarantine, or disable any AI/ML system that poses an imminent security threat or is operating in severe violation of this policy.</p>

<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed and updated by the AI Governance Committee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. A version history of this policy shall be maintained to demonstrate continuous improvement and governance.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) establishes the mandatory governance, security, and ethical framework for the acquisition, development, deployment, and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) systems by [Company Name]. The policy is designed to ensure that all AI/ML activities align with organizational values, comply with legal and regulatory obligations, and mitigate risks associated with data integrity, model bias, security, and operational reliability. It serves as the authoritative control document for AI governance, satisfying core requirements of ISO 27001 (A.6, A.8, A.12, A.14, A.15, A.16) and SOC 2 (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) trust service criteria.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies universally to all personnel of [Company Name] (including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and interns), all organizational units, and all third-party partners who access, develop, manage, or interact with [Company Name]'s AI/ML systems, data, or infrastructure. It encompasses all stages of the AI lifecycle: data sourcing and preparation, model development (including training, fine-tuning, and testing), model deployment (in production or pilot environments), model inference/operation, and model decommissioning. This policy governs all AI/ML systems, whether developed in-house, acquired as commercial-off-the-shelf (COTS) software, accessed via third-pa