configurations, and training scripts shall be managed within the organization's approved version control system (e.g., Git). Commit history must be preserved and shall not be altered.<br>5.3.3. Model artifacts must be cryptographically signed and stored in a secure, access-controlled model registry. Access to production model artifacts shall be restricted based on the principle of least privilege.</p><h4>5.4 Testing, Validation & Bias Mitigation</h4><p>5.4.1. Prior to deployment, all models must pass a comprehensive validation suite, including performance metrics (accuracy, precision, recall), robustness testing (for adversarial examples), and fairness assessments across protected attributes (e.g., race, gender, age) as relevant.<br>5.4.2. For High-Risk AI Applications, an independent third-party audit or red-team exercise must be conducted, and the results approved by the AI Governance Committee.<br>5.4.3. A model card must be created and maintained for every production model, detailing its intended use, performance characteristics, limitations, and known ethical considerations.</p><h4>5.5 Secure Deployment & Operations</h4><p>5.5.1. AI/ML models shall only be deployed to environments that meet [Company Name]'s hardened infrastructure standards, including network segmentation, intrusion detection, and vulnerability management.<br>5.5.2. All model inference endpoints (APIs) must be protected by strong authentication (OAuth 2.0, API keys) and encrypted in transit using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.5.3. Inputs to and outputs from production models must be logged in a secure, immutable audit log. Logs must include a unique session/user ID, timestamp, and a hash of the input/output data for non-repudiation.</p><h4>5.6 Monitoring, Observability & Human Oversight</h4><p>5.6.1. Continuous monitoring of model performance (e.g., prediction drift, data drift) and system health (latency, error rates) must be implemented. Automated alerts must be configured for significant degradation.<br>5.6.2. A human-in-the-loop (HITL) review process is mandatory for High-Risk AI Applications, where a qualified human must review and approve critical model outputs before action is taken.<br>5.6.3. All AI/ML systems must have a documented and tested rollback procedure to a previous known-good state in the event of failure or compromise.</p><h4>5.7 Third-Party & External AI Services</h4><p>5.7.1. The use of external AI/ML APIs or services (e.g., cloud AI, generative AI platforms) requires a completed vendor security assessment and a signed Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that addresses data ownership, confidentiality, and prohibits the vendor from using [Company Name] data for model training.<br>5.7.2. Prompt engineering for generative AI services must follow secure coding practices. Prompts must not contain Sensitive or Confidential information unless the service is explicitly approved for such data and is configured with data isolation guarantees.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>All AI/ML activities must comply with applicable international, federal, state, and local laws and regulations, including but not limited to: data protection and privacy laws (GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, etc.), sector-specific regulations (HIPAA, GLBA, PCI-DSS where applicable), and emerging AI-specific legislation (e.g., EU AI Act, US Executive Order on AI). Systems that process personal data must implement Privacy by Design principles, including data minimization and purpose limitation. [Company Name] shall maintain records of AI system risk assessments, model cards, data cards, and audit logs as evidence of due diligence and compliance for a minimum period defined by the Records Retention Policy, or as required by law.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>AI Governance Committee:</strong> Responsible for setting strategic direction, approving projects, defining risk tolerance, and overseeing policy compliance. Chaired by the CISO or designated executive.<br><br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate authority for the security of all AI/ML systems. Responsible for policy enforcement, security architecture review, and incident response.<br><br><strong>Data Owners & Business Unit Leaders:</strong> Accountable for the business use, ethical application, and risk management of AI systems within their domain. Must ensure adequate resources for monitoring and maintenance.<br><br><strong>AI/ML Developers & Data Scientists:</strong> Responsible for adhering to secure development practices, documenting their work, performing bias testing, and collaborating with security teams during design and deployment.<br><br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to complete mandatory AI ethics and security awareness training. Must report any suspected policy violations, security incidents, or unethical AI behavior through the established reporting channel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and may face legal action. Violations include, but are not limited to: deploying an AI system without approval, using prohibited data, circumventing security controls, or failing to perform required monitoring. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect, quarantine, or disable any AI/ML system that poses an imminent security threat or is operating in severe violation of this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This AI Acceptable Use Policy shall be reviewed and updated by the AI Governance Committee at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in technology, threat landscape, or regulatory requirements. All revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. A version history of this policy shall be maintained to demonstrate continuous improvement and governance.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:24.199895,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:02:56.071,NULL
16,11,Communications Security Policy,"<p>This Communications Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]'s information assets during electronic transmission over internal and external networks. The policy ensures that all communications are secured against unauthorized access, interception, modification, or denial of service, in alignment with business requirements and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, ""Personnel"") who access, use, or manage [Company Name]'s information systems, networks, or data. It encompasses all forms of electronic communication, including but not limited to: data transmitted over internal networks (wired and wireless), virtual private networks (VPNs), internet connections, email, instant messaging, voice-over-IP (VoIP), and any communication with external entities.</p>

<p><strong>Cryptographic Controls:</strong> The use of cryptographic techniques (e.g., encryption, digital signatures) to protect information.<br><strong>Electronic Communication:</strong> Any transmission of data, voice, or video via electronic means.<br><strong>Network Perimeter:</strong> The boundary between [Company Name]'s internal trusted network and external untrusted networks.<br><strong>Secure Channel:</strong> A communication path protected by encryption and authentication mechanisms.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security/Secure Sockets Layer, protocols for securing communications over a network.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all electronic communications containing sensitive or confidential information shall be protected using industry-standard cryptographic and security controls. The use of unsecured or unauthorized communication channels is strictly prohibited. All network infrastructure and communication services must be designed, configured, and operated to prevent unauthorized disclosure, alteration, or destruction of information.</p>

<h4>5.1 Network Encryption</h4><p>All sensitive data transmitted across internal networks, wide area networks (WANs), or the internet shall be encrypted using approved protocols. For web applications and services, TLS 1.2 or higher must be enforced, with weak cipher suites (e.g., RC4, DES, 3DES) explicitly disabled. Internal administrative traffic (e.g., SSH, RDP) must utilize strong encryption.</p><h4>5.2 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed by the Information Security or IT Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for business purposes is forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall implement WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be segregated from the corporate network via a dedicated VLAN and firewall rules.</p><h4>5.3 Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</h4><p>Remote access to [Company Name]'s internal network shall only be permitted via an approved corporate VPN. The VPN solution must support multi-factor authentication (MFA) and use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the VPN gateway for inspection and logging.</p><h4>5.4 Email Security</h4><p>All external email containing sensitive information must be encrypted in transit using TLS. For high-sensitivity data, end-to-end encryption or secure email gateways with encryption capabilities shall be employed. Email systems must be configured with anti-spoofing controls (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and robust anti-malware filtering.</p><h4>5.5 Voice and Video Communications</h4><p>Voice-over-IP (VoIP) and video conferencing systems used for business discussions involving confidential information must utilize encryption for both signaling and media streams. The use of consumer-grade, unsecured services for sensitive meetings is prohibited.</p><h4>5.6 Network Segmentation and Isolation</h4><p>Critical systems (e.g., databases, payment systems) shall be placed in logically isolated network segments (e.g., DMZs, private subnets) protected by firewalls. Communication between segments must be explicitly allowed based on the principle of least privilege and logged.</p><h4>5.7 Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All perimeter security devices (firewalls, VPN concentrators, proxies) and critical network infrastructure shall have logging enabled. Logs must capture source/destination IP, port, protocol, timestamp, and action taken (allow/deny). These logs shall be centrally collected, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and monitored for anomalous or malicious activity.</p>

<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls, specifically A.8.21 (Security of network services), A.8.22 (Segregation of networks), A.8.23 (Web filtering), A.8.24 (Use of cryptography), and A.8.25 (Secure development life cycle). It also addresses relevant requirements of SOC 2 (Security and Confidentiality criteria), GDPR, HIPAA, and other data protection regulations pertaining to data in transit. All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to the legal and contractual obligations related to the information they handle.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy's approval, implementation, and oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for designing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and investigating security incidents related to communications.<br><strong>IT/Network Operations:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of all network and communication infrastructure as defined in this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must use only approved communication channels and services, report suspected security weaknesses or incidents, and complete required security awareness training.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to block, disconnect, or restrict any network device, service, or connection that is found to be non-compliant and poses an immediate risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This Communications Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The version control and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Communications Security Policy establishes the mandatory controls and procedures to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]'s information assets during electronic transmission over internal and external networks. The policy ensures that all communications are secured against unauthorized access, interception, modification, or denial of service, in alignment with business requirements and regulatory obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") who access, use, or manage [Company Name]'s information systems, networks, or data. It encompasses all forms of electronic communication, including but not limited to: data transmitted over internal networks (wired and wireless), virtual private networks (VPNs), internet connections, email, instant messaging, voice-over-IP (VoIP), and any communication with external entities.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Cryptographic Controls:</strong> The use of cryptographic techniques (e.g., encryption, digital signatures) to protect information.<br><strong>Electronic Communication:</strong> Any transmission of data, voice, or video via electronic means.<br><strong>Network Perimeter:</strong> The boundary between [Company Name]'s internal trusted network and external untrusted networks.<br><strong>Secure Channel:</strong> A communication path protected by encryption and authentication mechanisms.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security/Secure Sockets Layer, protocols for securing communications over a network.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all electronic communications containing sensitive or confidential information shall be protected using industry-standard cryptographic and security controls. The use of unsecured or unauthorized communication channels is strictly prohibited. All network infrastructure and communication services must be designed, configured, and operated to prevent unauthorized disclosure, alteration, or destruction of information.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Network Encryption</h4><p>All sensitive data transmitted across internal networks, wide area networks (WANs), or the internet shall be encrypted using approved protocols. For web applications and services, TLS 1.2 or higher must be enforced, with weak cipher suites (e.g., RC4, DES, 3DES) explicitly disabled. Internal administrative traffic (e.g., SSH, RDP) must utilize strong encryption.</p><h4>5.2 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed by the Information Security or IT Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for business purposes is forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall implement WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be segregated from the corporate network via a dedicated VLAN and firewall rules.</p><h4>5.3 Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)</h4><p>Remote access to [Company Name]'s internal network shall only be permitted via an approved corporate VPN. The VPN solution must support multi-factor authentication (MFA) and use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the VPN gateway for inspection and logging.</p><h4>5.4 Email Security</h4><p>All external email containing sensitive information must be encrypted in transit using TLS. For high-sensitivity data, end-to-end encryption or secure email gateways with encryption capabilities shall be employed. Email systems must be configured with anti-spoofing controls (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) and robust anti-malware filtering.</p><h4>5.5 Voice and Video Communications</h4><p>Voice-over-IP (VoIP) and video conferencing systems used for business discussions involving confidential information must utilize encryption for both signaling and media streams. The use of consumer-grade, unsecured services for sensitive meetings is prohibited.</p><h4>5.6 Network Segmentation and Isolation</h4><p>Critical systems (e.g., databases, payment systems) shall be placed in logically isolated network segments (e.g., DMZs, private subnets) protected by firewalls. Communication between segments must be explicitly allowed based on the principle of least privilege and logged.</p><h4>5.7 Logging and Monitoring</h4><p>All perimeter security devices (firewalls, VPN concentrators, proxies) and critical network infrastructure shall have logging enabled. Logs must capture source/destination IP, port, protocol, timestamp, and action taken (allow/deny). These logs shall be centrally collected, retained for a minimum of 90 days, and monitored for anomalous or malicious activity.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy supports compliance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 controls, specifically A.8.21 (Security of network services), A.8.22 (Segregation of networks), A.8.23 (Web filtering), A.8.24 (Use of cryptography), and A.8.25 (Secure development life cycle). It also addresses relevant requirements of SOC 2 (Security and Confidentiality criteria), GDPR, HIPAA, and other data protection regulations pertaining to data in transit. All personnel are responsible for understanding and adhering to the legal and contractual obligations related to the information they handle.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the policy's approval, implementation, and oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for designing technical controls, monitoring compliance, and investigating security incidents related to communications.<br><strong>IT/Network Operations:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, maintenance, and monitoring of all network and communication infrastructure as defined in this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Must use only approved communication channels and services, report suspected security weaknesses or incidents, and complete required security awareness training.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Team is authorized to block, disconnect, or restrict any network device, service, or connection that is found to be non-compliant and poses an immediate risk to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Communications Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO or designated authority at least annually, or following significant changes to the threat landscape, technology infrastructure, or applicable regulations. Revisions must be formally approved and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The version control and revision history shall be maintained by the Information Security Team.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:56.491566,"""[\""ISO 27001\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:03:28.638,NULL
57,235550,Start-up Security Policy,"<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory security objectives, principles, and governance framework for [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all information assets, systems, and networks against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, destruction, or disruption. This policy serves as the foundational directive for all security-related activities and forms the basis for compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria and ISO/IEC 27001 requirements.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively, ""Personnel"") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information or utilize its information systems. It encompasses all information assets, regardless of format (electronic, physical, verbal), all company-owned or managed information technology resources (including endpoints, servers, network infrastructure, cloud services, and software), and all company facilities. This policy is in effect at all times and in all locations where company business is conducted.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service that has value to [Company Name].</li><li><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its customers, employees, or business operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.</li><li><strong>Information System:</strong> A discrete set of information resources organized for the collection, processing, maintenance, use, sharing, dissemination, or disposition of information.</li><li><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The types and amount of risk, on a broad level, that [Company Name] is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives.</li><li><strong>Baseline Security Controls:</strong> The minimum set of protective measures that shall be implemented across all in-scope systems and environments.</li><li><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that grants users and systems the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</li></ul>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing and maintaining a comprehensive information security management system (ISMS). Security is a shared responsibility integrated into all business processes. All Personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, defense-in-depth, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction of information assets is strictly prohibited. Management mandates full compliance with this policy and all supporting standards and procedures.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><h3>5.1 Access Control & Authentication</h3><p>5.1.1 All user accounts shall be provisioned based on documented authorization and the principle of least privilege. Generic or shared accounts are forbidden for individual human users in production environments.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote access (e.g., VPN, cloud consoles) and for all administrative access to critical systems.<br>5.1.3 Password complexity shall meet a minimum of 12 characters, combining uppercase, lowercase, numerals, and special symbols. Passwords must be hashed using a strong, adaptive algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, Argon2).<br>5.1.4 Access rights shall be reviewed and formally re-certified by system/data owners at least quarterly, with immediate revocation upon role termination.</p><h3>5.2 Data Protection & Encryption</h3><p>5.2.1 All Confidential Information at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. Encryption keys must be managed through a dedicated key management service, separate from the encrypted data.<br>5.2.2 All data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher with strong cipher suites. The use of SSLv3, TLS 1.0, or TLS 1.1 is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 A formal data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) shall be defined and applied to all information assets. Handling procedures must be defined per classification level.</p><h3>5.3 System Hardening & Configuration Management</h3><p>5.3.1 All systems (servers, workstations, network devices) shall be hardened against a recognized industry benchmark (e.g., CIS Benchmarks) before deployment into any environment.<br>5.3.2 A formal change management process must govern all modifications to production systems, applications, and security configurations. Emergency changes shall be documented and reviewed post-implementation.<br>5.3.3 Unnecessary services, ports, protocols, and software shall be disabled or removed from all systems. Only explicitly authorized software may be installed on company-managed endpoints.</p><h3>5.4 Network Security</h3><p>5.4.1 All network infrastructure devices (routers, switches, firewalls) must be configured to deny-all by default, with rules explicitly allowing only necessary traffic. Rule bases shall be reviewed semi-annually.<br>5.4.2 All wireless network access must be secured using WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks is strictly prohibited.<br>5.4.3 Production environments shall be logically segmented from development, testing, and corporate networks using firewalls or software-defined perimeters.</p><h3>5.5 Vulnerability & Patch Management</h3><p>5.5.1 All in-scope systems and applications shall be scanned for vulnerabilities using automated tools at least monthly. External penetration tests must be conducted annually by a qualified third party.<br>5.5.2 A risk-based patch management process shall be implemented. Critical and High severity security patches must be applied within 30 days of release or according to a stricter, risk-assessed timeline.<br>5.5.3 An inventory of all hardware and software assets, including dependencies, shall be maintained and updated automatically or reviewed quarterly.</p><h3>5.6 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h3><p>5.6.1 Security-relevant events (authentication successes/failures, configuration changes, data access, firewall denies) shall be logged centrally with immutable storage. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 90 days, with one year for critical systems.<br>5.6.2 A formal Security Operations Center (SOC) function or equivalent 24/7 monitoring capability must be established to detect and analyze anomalous activities.<br>5.6.3 A documented incident response plan shall be maintained, tested annually, and include procedures for containment, eradication, recovery, and communication. All incidents must be tracked to resolution.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>All security activities shall be conducted in accordance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and the requirements of the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for interpreting these requirements and ensuring controls are designed to meet them. Regular compliance assessments and internal audits shall be performed to validate control effectiveness.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Leadership:</strong> Ultimate accountability for information security; approves risk appetite and provides strategic resources.</li><li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the overall security program; reports on security posture and risks to leadership.</li><li><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Implements and maintains technical security controls as defined in this policy and supporting standards.</li><li><strong>System & Data Owners:</strong> Classify information assets, authorize access, and are accountable for the security of their assigned systems/data.</li><li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with all security policies and procedures; required to report security incidents or concerns immediately.</li></ul>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The CISO, in coordination with Human Resources and Legal departments, is authorized to investigate suspected violations. Corrective actions may include mandatory re-training, revocation of system access, or other measures deemed necessary to remediate risk and ensure future compliance. Enforcement actions shall be applied consistently and documented.</p>

<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or major business or regulatory changes. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The official version of this policy shall be maintained in a centralized document repository accessible to all Personnel. All revisions shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory security objectives, principles, and governance framework for [Company Name]. Its purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all information assets, systems, and networks against unauthorized access, disclosure, modification, destruction, or disruption. This policy serves as the foundational directive for all security-related activities and forms the basis for compliance with SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria and ISO/IEC 27001 requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] information or utilize its information systems. It encompasses all information assets, regardless of format (electronic, physical, verbal), all company-owned or managed information technology resources (including endpoints, servers, network infrastructure, cloud services, and software), and all company facilities. This policy is in effect at all times and in all locations where company business is conducted.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, system, hardware, software, or service that has value to [Company Name].</li><li><strong>Confidential Information:</strong> Any non-public information pertaining to [Company Name], its customers, employees, or business operations, the disclosure of which could cause harm.</li><li><strong>Information System:</strong> A discrete set of information resources organized for the collection, processing, maintenance, use, sharing, dissemination, or disposition of information.</li><li><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> The types and amount of risk, on a broad level, that [Company Name] is willing to accept in pursuit of its strategic objectives.</li><li><strong>Baseline Security Controls:</strong> The minimum set of protective measures that shall be implemented across all in-scope systems and environments.</li><li><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that grants users and systems the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.</li></ul>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing and maintaining a comprehensive information security management system (ISMS). Security is a shared responsibility integrated into all business processes. All Personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, defense-in-depth, and continuous monitoring. The unauthorized access, use, disclosure, disruption, modification, or destruction of information assets is strictly prohibited. Management mandates full compliance with this policy and all supporting standards and procedures.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><h3>5.1 Access Control & Authentication</h3><p>5.1.1 All user accounts shall be provisioned based on documented authorization and the principle of least privilege. Generic or shared accounts are forbidden for individual human users in production environments.<br>5.1.2 Multi-factor authentication (MFA) must be enforced for all remote access (e.g., VPN, cloud consoles) and for all administrative access to critical systems.<br>5.1.3 Password complexity shall meet a minimum of 12 characters, combining uppercase, lowercase, numerals, and special symbols. Passwords must be hashed using a strong, adaptive algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, Argon2).<br>5.1.4 Access rights shall be reviewed and formally re-certified by system/data owners at least quarterly, with immediate revocation upon role termination.</p><h3>5.2 Data Protection & Encryption</h3><p>5.2.1 All Confidential Information at rest shall be encrypted using AES-256 or equivalent. Encryption keys must be managed through a dedicated key management service, separate from the encrypted data.<br>5.2.2 All data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher with strong cipher suites. The use of SSLv3, TLS 1.0, or TLS 1.1 is forbidden.<br>5.2.3 A formal data classification scheme (e.g., Public, Internal, Confidential, Restricted) shall be defined and applied to all information assets. Handling procedures must be defined per classification level.</p><h3>5.3 System Hardening & Configuration Management</h3><p>5.3.1 All systems (servers, workstations, network devices) shall be hardened against a recognized industry benchmark (e.g., CIS Benchmarks) before deployment into any environment.<br>5.3.2 A formal change management process must govern all modifications to production systems, applications, and security configurations. Emergency changes shall be documented and reviewed post-implementation.<br>5.3.3 Unnecessary services, ports, protocols, and software shall be disabled or removed from all systems. Only explicitly authorized software may be installed on company-managed endpoints.</p><h3>5.4 Network Security</h3><p>5.4.1 All network infrastructure devices (routers, switches, firewalls) must be configured to deny-all by default, with rules explicitly allowing only necessary traffic. Rule bases shall be reviewed semi-annually.<br>5.4.2 All wireless network access must be secured using WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks is strictly prohibited.<br>5.4.3 Production environments shall be logically segmented from development, testing, and corporate networks using firewalls or software-defined perimeters.</p><h3>5.5 Vulnerability & Patch Management</h3><p>5.5.1 All in-scope systems and applications shall be scanned for vulnerabilities using automated tools at least monthly. External penetration tests must be conducted annually by a qualified third party.<br>5.5.2 A risk-based patch management process shall be implemented. Critical and High severity security patches must be applied within 30 days of release or according to a stricter, risk-assessed timeline.<br>5.5.3 An inventory of all hardware and software assets, including dependencies, shall be maintained and updated automatically or reviewed quarterly.</p><h3>5.6 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h3><p>5.6.1 Security-relevant events (authentication successes/failures, configuration changes, data access, firewall denies) shall be logged centrally with immutable storage. Logs must be retained for a minimum of 90 days, with one year for critical systems.<br>5.6.2 A formal Security Operations Center (SOC) function or equivalent 24/7 monitoring capability must be established to detect and analyze anomalous activities.<br>5.6.3 A documented incident response plan shall be maintained, tested annually, and include procedures for containment, eradication, recovery, and communication. All incidents must be tracked to resolution.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>All security activities shall be conducted in accordance with applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and the requirements of the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy) and ISO/IEC 27001:2022. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for interpreting these requirements and ensuring controls are designed to meet them. Regular compliance assessments and internal audits shall be performed to validate control effectiveness.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><ul><li><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Leadership:</strong> Ultimate accountability for information security; approves risk appetite and provides strategic resources.</li><li><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy and the overall security program; reports on security posture and risks to leadership.</li><li><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department:</strong> Implements and maintains technical security controls as defined in this policy and supporting standards.</li><li><strong>System & Data Owners:</strong> Classify information assets, authorize access, and are accountable for the security of their assigned systems/data.</li><li><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to comply with all security policies and procedures; required to report security incidents or concerns immediately.</li></ul>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>Any Personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The CISO, in coordination with Human Resources and Legal departments, is authorized to investigate suspected violations. Corrective actions may include mandatory re-training, revocation of system access, or other measures deemed necessary to remediate risk and ensure future compliance. Enforcement actions shall be applied consistently and documented.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Start-up Security Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or major business or regulatory changes. Proposed revisions must be approved by the executive leadership team. The official version of this policy shall be maintained in a centralized document repository accessible to all Personnel. All revisions shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-22 09:39:18.056999,"""[\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:04:13.04,NULL
17,12,Cryptography Policy,"<p>This Cryptography Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the use, management, and protection of cryptographic controls at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation of information assets in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations. The policy provides the authoritative framework for implementing cryptographic safeguards against unauthorized access, data breaches, and tampering.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including data at rest, in transit, and in use. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who develop, implement, manage, or interact with cryptographic systems or protected data. This includes, but is not limited to, applications, databases, network communications, cloud services, endpoints, and portable media.</p>

<p><strong>Cryptography:</strong> The discipline that embodies the principles, means, and methods for transforming data to hide its information content, establish its authenticity, prevent its undetected modification, and prevent its repudiation.<br><strong>Cryptographic Key:</strong> A parameter used in conjunction with a cryptographic algorithm that determines its operation (e.g., transformation of plaintext to ciphertext).<br><strong>Key Management:</strong> The activities involving the handling of cryptographic keys and related metadata during their entire lifecycle, including generation, storage, distribution, rotation, archival, and destruction.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security / Secure Sockets Layer, cryptographic protocols designed to provide communications security over a computer network.<br><strong>FIPS 140-2/3:</strong> Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 140-2/3, a U.S. government standard that specifies security requirements for cryptographic modules.<br><strong>Strong Cryptography:</strong> Cryptography that is based on industry-tested and accepted algorithms, along with strong key lengths and proper implementation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates the use of strong, approved cryptography to protect the confidentiality and integrity of its sensitive information. The implementation of cryptographic controls shall be risk-based, consistently applied, and managed through a formal lifecycle. The use of weak, deprecated, or non-compliant cryptographic algorithms, protocols, or key lengths is strictly prohibited.</p>

<h4>5.1 Algorithm and Protocol Standards</h4><p>All cryptographic implementations shall use algorithms and protocols approved by [Company Name]'s Information Security Department. As a minimum baseline:<br>- Symmetric encryption shall use AES with a key length of 256 bits.<br>- Asymmetric encryption and digital signatures shall use RSA (3072-bit or higher) or Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) with curves of at least 256 bits (e.g., P-256).<br>- Cryptographic hash functions shall use SHA-256 or stronger (e.g., SHA-384, SHA-512).<br>- The use of deprecated algorithms (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, DES, 3DES, RC4) for any security purpose is forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Transport Security</h4><p>All network communications transmitting sensitive or confidential data shall be encrypted in transit.<br>- External web applications and APIs must use TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are prohibited.<br>- TLS configurations must disable weak cipher suites (e.g., those using CBC mode, NULL, or anonymous authentication) and shall support perfect forward secrecy (PFS).<br>- Internal service-to-service communications within production environments must also be encrypted using mutually authenticated TLS (mTLS) or an equivalent approved protocol.</p><h4>5.3 Data at Rest Encryption</h4><p>Sensitive data stored on any medium must be encrypted.<br>- All databases containing sensitive information (e.g., PII, financial data, credentials) shall utilize application-layer encryption, transparent data encryption (TDE), or full-disk encryption.<br>- Encryption keys for data at rest shall not be stored on the same logical or physical system as the encrypted data they protect.<br>- Laptops, mobile devices, and removable media (e.g., USB drives) containing company data must employ full-disk encryption (e.g., BitLocker, FileVault) with a pre-boot authentication mechanism.</p><h4>5.4 Cryptographic Key Management</h4><p>A formal key management lifecycle policy shall be established and followed for all cryptographic keys.<br>- Keys shall be generated using approved, cryptographically secure random number generators.<br>- Secret and private keys shall be stored within a certified Hardware Security Module (HSM), cloud key management service (KMS), or a secure, dedicated key management server. Storage in configuration files, source code, or plaintext databases is strictly prohibited.<br>- Key distribution shall use secure channels. Symmetric keys shall never be transmitted in plaintext.<br>- Keys shall be rotated at least annually, or immediately upon suspicion of compromise. Key archival and secure destruction procedures must be documented and followed.</p><h4>5.5 Certificate Management</h4><p>All digital certificates used for authentication or encryption must be managed through a formal process.<br>- Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates shall be issued by a trusted, managed Certificate Authority (CA). Self-signed certificates are prohibited in production environments except for specific, approved internal use cases.<br>- Certificate lifetimes shall not exceed 13 months (398 days).<br>- A process for monitoring, renewing, and revoking certificates must be implemented to prevent service disruption or use of expired certificates.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Implementation</h4><p>Cryptographic functions shall not be implemented using custom or ""home-grown"" algorithms. Only vetted, industry-standard cryptographic libraries (e.g., OpenSSL, Bouncy Castle, platform-native APIs) shall be used.<br>- All cryptographic implementations, including selection of algorithms, modes of operation (e.g., GCM, CBC), and initialization vectors (IVs), must be reviewed by the Information Security Department prior to deployment.<br>- Cryptographic modules used for protecting regulated data should be FIPS 140-2/3 validated where applicable.</p>

<p>Cryptographic controls shall be implemented to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, PCI DSS, and SOX. The policy aligns with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls A.10 (Cryptography) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. The Information Security Department is responsible for interpreting the cryptographic requirements of such frameworks and mapping them to this policy's controls.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining cryptographic standards, maintaining the approved algorithms list, conducting security reviews of implementations, and auditing compliance.<br><strong>IT Operations/Cloud Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, management, and monitoring of cryptographic systems (e.g., HSMs, KMS, TLS terminators) in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for integrating approved cryptographic libraries and following secure coding practices as defined in section 5.6.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use cryptographic systems as mandated (e.g., encrypted VPNs, encrypted drives) and to report any suspected misuse or weakness in cryptographic controls.</p>

<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Department shall conduct regular audits and technical assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans, configuration reviews) to detect non-compliant cryptographic implementations. Systems found to be in violation shall be isolated from the network or decommissioned until remediated.</p>

<p>This Cryptography Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to cryptographic standards (e.g., NIST guidelines). Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Cryptography Policy establishes the mandatory requirements for the use, management, and protection of cryptographic controls at [Company Name]. Its purpose is to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation of information assets in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations. The policy provides the authoritative framework for implementing cryptographic safeguards against unauthorized access, data breaches, and tampering.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], including data at rest, in transit, and in use. It encompasses all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and third-party service providers who develop, implement, manage, or interact with cryptographic systems or protected data. This includes, but is not limited to, applications, databases, network communications, cloud services, endpoints, and portable media.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Cryptography:</strong> The discipline that embodies the principles, means, and methods for transforming data to hide its information content, establish its authenticity, prevent its undetected modification, and prevent its repudiation.<br><strong>Cryptographic Key:</strong> A parameter used in conjunction with a cryptographic algorithm that determines its operation (e.g., transformation of plaintext to ciphertext).<br><strong>Key Management:</strong> The activities involving the handling of cryptographic keys and related metadata during their entire lifecycle, including generation, storage, distribution, rotation, archival, and destruction.<br><strong>TLS/SSL:</strong> Transport Layer Security / Secure Sockets Layer, cryptographic protocols designed to provide communications security over a computer network.<br><strong>FIPS 140-2/3:</strong> Federal Information Processing Standards Publication 140-2/3, a U.S. government standard that specifies security requirements for cryptographic modules.<br><strong>Strong Cryptography:</strong> Cryptography that is based on industry-tested and accepted algorithms, along with strong key lengths and proper implementation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates the use of strong, approved cryptography to protect the confidentiality and integrity of its sensitive information. The implementation of cryptographic controls shall be risk-based, consistently applied, and managed through a formal lifecycle. The use of weak, deprecated, or non-compliant cryptographic algorithms, protocols, or key lengths is strictly prohibited.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Algorithm and Protocol Standards</h4><p>All cryptographic implementations shall use algorithms and protocols approved by [Company Name]'s Information Security Department. As a minimum baseline:<br>- Symmetric encryption shall use AES with a key length of 256 bits.<br>- Asymmetric encryption and digital signatures shall use RSA (3072-bit or higher) or Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) with curves of at least 256 bits (e.g., P-256).<br>- Cryptographic hash functions shall use SHA-256 or stronger (e.g., SHA-384, SHA-512).<br>- The use of deprecated algorithms (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, DES, 3DES, RC4) for any security purpose is forbidden.</p><h4>5.2 Transport Security</h4><p>All network communications transmitting sensitive or confidential data shall be encrypted in transit.<br>- External web applications and APIs must use TLS 1.2 or higher. TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are prohibited.<br>- TLS configurations must disable weak cipher suites (e.g., those using CBC mode, NULL, or anonymous authentication) and shall support perfect forward secrecy (PFS).<br>- Internal service-to-service communications within production environments must also be encrypted using mutually authenticated TLS (mTLS) or an equivalent approved protocol.</p><h4>5.3 Data at Rest Encryption</h4><p>Sensitive data stored on any medium must be encrypted.<br>- All databases containing sensitive information (e.g., PII, financial data, credentials) shall utilize application-layer encryption, transparent data encryption (TDE), or full-disk encryption.<br>- Encryption keys for data at rest shall not be stored on the same logical or physical system as the encrypted data they protect.<br>- Laptops, mobile devices, and removable media (e.g., USB drives) containing company data must employ full-disk encryption (e.g., BitLocker, FileVault) with a pre-boot authentication mechanism.</p><h4>5.4 Cryptographic Key Management</h4><p>A formal key management lifecycle policy shall be established and followed for all cryptographic keys.<br>- Keys shall be generated using approved, cryptographically secure random number generators.<br>- Secret and private keys shall be stored within a certified Hardware Security Module (HSM), cloud key management service (KMS), or a secure, dedicated key management server. Storage in configuration files, source code, or plaintext databases is strictly prohibited.<br>- Key distribution shall use secure channels. Symmetric keys shall never be transmitted in plaintext.<br>- Keys shall be rotated at least annually, or immediately upon suspicion of compromise. Key archival and secure destruction procedures must be documented and followed.</p><h4>5.5 Certificate Management</h4><p>All digital certificates used for authentication or encryption must be managed through a formal process.<br>- Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) certificates shall be issued by a trusted, managed Certificate Authority (CA). Self-signed certificates are prohibited in production environments except for specific, approved internal use cases.<br>- Certificate lifetimes shall not exceed 13 months (398 days).<br>- A process for monitoring, renewing, and revoking certificates must be implemented to prevent service disruption or use of expired certificates.</p><h4>5.6 Development and Implementation</h4><p>Cryptographic functions shall not be implemented using custom or \\\""home-grown\\\"" algorithms. Only vetted, industry-standard cryptographic libraries (e.g., OpenSSL, Bouncy Castle, platform-native APIs) shall be used.<br>- All cryptographic implementations, including selection of algorithms, modes of operation (e.g., GCM, CBC), and initialization vectors (IVs), must be reviewed by the Information Security Department prior to deployment.<br>- Cryptographic modules used for protecting regulated data should be FIPS 140-2/3 validated where applicable.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Cryptographic controls shall be implemented to ensure compliance with all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, PCI DSS, and SOX. The policy aligns with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 Annex A controls A.10 (Cryptography) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality. The Information Security Department is responsible for interpreting the cryptographic requirements of such frameworks and mapping them to this policy's controls.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for the approval, enforcement, and overall effectiveness of this policy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for defining cryptographic standards, maintaining the approved algorithms list, conducting security reviews of implementations, and auditing compliance.<br><strong>IT Operations/Cloud Teams:</strong> Responsible for the secure configuration, management, and monitoring of cryptographic systems (e.g., HSMs, KMS, TLS terminators) in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>Development Teams:</strong> Responsible for integrating approved cryptographic libraries and following secure coding practices as defined in section 5.6.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to use cryptographic systems as mandated (e.g., encrypted VPNs, encrypted drives) and to report any suspected misuse or weakness in cryptographic controls.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee, contractor, or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution. The Information Security Department shall conduct regular audits and technical assessments (e.g., vulnerability scans, configuration reviews) to detect non-compliant cryptographic implementations. Systems found to be in violation shall be isolated from the network or decommissioned until remediated.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Cryptography Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in the threat landscape, or updates to cryptographic standards (e.g., NIST guidelines). Revisions must be approved by the CISO. All changes shall be communicated to relevant stakeholders.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:56.703635,"""[\""Both\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:04:52.445,NULL
56,898107,Data Protection Policy,"<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory framework for protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all data assets owned, processed, or controlled by [Company Name]. It defines the principles, controls, and responsibilities required to safeguard data against unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration, destruction, or loss, and to ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations including, but not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), ISO 27001, and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, ""Personnel"") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. It encompasses all data, regardless of format (electronic, paper, etc.) or state (at-rest, in-transit, in-use), processed on all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, and facilities, including cloud services and personal devices used for business purposes (BYOD).</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person ('data subject'), as defined by the GDPR.<br><strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Non-public proprietary information, including trade secrets, financial data, and strategic plans.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> A subset of Personal Data requiring heightened protection (e.g., racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, health data, biometric data).<br><strong>Data Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on data, including collection, recording, organization, storage, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure, erasure, or destruction.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Controller.<br><strong>Encryption at Rest:</strong> The cryptographic protection of stored data using a minimum of AES-256.<br><strong>Encryption in Transit:</strong> The cryptographic protection of data traversing a network using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be protected according to its classification level, sensitivity, and associated legal requirements. All Personnel are strictly prohibited from accessing, using, or disclosing data except as necessary to perform authorized job functions. The organization is committed to implementing a defense-in-depth strategy, adhering to the principles of data minimization, purpose limitation, and accountability, and maintaining a state of continuous compliance with all relevant data protection frameworks.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><h2>5.1 Data Classification and Handling</h2><p>All data assets must be classified according to the corporate Data Classification Standard as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted. Handling procedures, including labeling, storage, transmission, and disposal requirements, shall be strictly enforced based on the assigned classification. Restricted and Confidential data shall not be stored on portable media (e.g., USB drives) without explicit written authorization and mandatory full-disk encryption.</p><h2>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h2><p>Access to data shall be granted on a strict need-to-know and least-privilege basis. All access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all personnel accessing systems containing Confidential or Restricted data, or when accessing corporate networks remotely. Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators) shall be subject to heightened monitoring, session recording, and just-in-time provisioning.</p><h2>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h2><p>All Restricted and Confidential data must be encrypted at rest using a minimum of AES-256. All data transmissions over public or untrusted networks (including email) must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. The use of deprecated protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) or weak ciphers is strictly forbidden. Encryption keys shall be managed through a dedicated Key Management Service (KMS) and stored separately from the encrypted data.</p><h2>5.4 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h2><p>Data shall not be retained beyond the period necessary for its defined business purpose or as required by law. A formal data retention schedule must be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data must be securely and irreversibly destroyed. For electronic media, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction. Paper documents containing Confidential or Restricted data must be shredded via cross-cut shredder or incinerated.</p><h2>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h2><p>All access to, and processing of, Confidential and Restricted data must be logged. Logs must include user identity, timestamp, action performed, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation. A formal Security Incident Response Plan must be maintained. Any suspected or confirmed data breach must be reported to the CISO or Data Protection Officer (DPO) immediately, triggering the incident response process.</p><h2>5.6 Third-Party and Cloud Security</h2><p>Before engaging any third-party vendor (Data Processor) that will handle [Company Name] data, a security assessment must be completed. A Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that contractually binds the vendor to equivalent protection standards is mandatory. For cloud services, the Shared Responsibility Model must be explicitly understood, and configuration baselines (e.g., disabling public read/write access on storage buckets) shall be enforced and audited quarterly.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] shall comply with all applicable data protection laws and regulations in jurisdictions where it operates, including but not limited to the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and sector-specific requirements. The organization shall maintain documented evidence of compliance for audits against ISO 27001:2022 Annex A controls (e.g., A.5, A.6, A.8, A.9, A.12, A.13, A.14, A.17, A.18) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for high-risk processing activities as mandated by the GDPR.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Board of Directors/Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate accountability for data protection governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy and the overall security program.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Required under GDPR; oversees compliance, acts as point of contact for supervisory authorities and data subjects.<br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Classify data, authorize access, and define retention periods for data within their domain.<br><strong>Data Custodians (IT/Security Teams):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls (access, encryption, backups) as directed by data owners.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to complete annual data protection training, adhere to this policy, and report any security incidents or policy violations immediately.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and potential legal or civil penalties. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor systems and network traffic to ensure compliance. Deliberate circumvention of security controls, unauthorized data exfiltration, or failure to report a data breach constitutes gross misconduct and will be dealt with accordingly.</p>

<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed and approved by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. All revisions must be formally version-controlled, communicated to all personnel, and archived. The effectiveness of controls outlined in this policy shall be validated through internal audits and penetration testing exercises.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy establishes the mandatory framework for protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all data assets owned, processed, or controlled by [Company Name]. It defines the principles, controls, and responsibilities required to safeguard data against unauthorized access, disclosure, alteration, destruction, or loss, and to ensure compliance with applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations including, but not limited to, the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), ISO 27001, and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy applies universally to all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties (collectively, \\\""Personnel\\\"") who access, process, store, or transmit [Company Name] data. It encompasses all data, regardless of format (electronic, paper, etc.) or state (at-rest, in-transit, in-use), processed on all company-owned or managed systems, networks, applications, and facilities, including cloud services and personal devices used for business purposes (BYOD).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Personal Data:</strong> Any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person ('data subject'), as defined by the GDPR.<br><strong>Confidential Data:</strong> Non-public proprietary information, including trade secrets, financial data, and strategic plans.<br><strong>Sensitive Data:</strong> A subset of Personal Data requiring heightened protection (e.g., racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, health data, biometric data).<br><strong>Data Processing:</strong> Any operation performed on data, including collection, recording, organization, storage, alteration, retrieval, consultation, use, disclosure, erasure, or destruction.<br><strong>Data Controller:</strong> The entity that determines the purposes and means of processing Personal Data.<br><strong>Data Processor:</strong> The entity that processes Personal Data on behalf of the Controller.<br><strong>Encryption at Rest:</strong> The cryptographic protection of stored data using a minimum of AES-256.<br><strong>Encryption in Transit:</strong> The cryptographic protection of data traversing a network using TLS 1.2 or higher.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] mandates that all data shall be protected according to its classification level, sensitivity, and associated legal requirements. All Personnel are strictly prohibited from accessing, using, or disclosing data except as necessary to perform authorized job functions. The organization is committed to implementing a defense-in-depth strategy, adhering to the principles of data minimization, purpose limitation, and accountability, and maintaining a state of continuous compliance with all relevant data protection frameworks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><h2>5.1 Data Classification and Handling</h2><p>All data assets must be classified according to the corporate Data Classification Standard as Public, Internal, Confidential, or Restricted. Handling procedures, including labeling, storage, transmission, and disposal requirements, shall be strictly enforced based on the assigned classification. Restricted and Confidential data shall not be stored on portable media (e.g., USB drives) without explicit written authorization and mandatory full-disk encryption.</p><h2>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h2><p>Access to data shall be granted on a strict need-to-know and least-privilege basis. All access requests must be formally approved by the data owner or their designated delegate. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all personnel accessing systems containing Confidential or Restricted data, or when accessing corporate networks remotely. Privileged access accounts (e.g., system administrators) shall be subject to heightened monitoring, session recording, and just-in-time provisioning.</p><h2>5.3 Encryption Requirements</h2><p>All Restricted and Confidential data must be encrypted at rest using a minimum of AES-256. All data transmissions over public or untrusted networks (including email) must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher. The use of deprecated protocols (e.g., SSLv3, TLS 1.0/1.1) or weak ciphers is strictly forbidden. Encryption keys shall be managed through a dedicated Key Management Service (KMS) and stored separately from the encrypted data.</p><h2>5.4 Data Retention and Secure Disposal</h2><p>Data shall not be retained beyond the period necessary for its defined business purpose or as required by law. A formal data retention schedule must be maintained and enforced. At the end of the retention period, data must be securely and irreversibly destroyed. For electronic media, this requires cryptographic erasure (NIST 800-88 Clear/Purge) or physical destruction. Paper documents containing Confidential or Restricted data must be shredded via cross-cut shredder or incinerated.</p><h2>5.5 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h2><p>All access to, and processing of, Confidential and Restricted data must be logged. Logs must include user identity, timestamp, action performed, and data identifier. Logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 90 days to support incident investigation. A formal Security Incident Response Plan must be maintained. Any suspected or confirmed data breach must be reported to the CISO or Data Protection Officer (DPO) immediately, triggering the incident response process.</p><h2>5.6 Third-Party and Cloud Security</h2><p>Before engaging any third-party vendor (Data Processor) that will handle [Company Name] data, a security assessment must be completed. A Data Processing Agreement (DPA) that contractually binds the vendor to equivalent protection standards is mandatory. For cloud services, the Shared Responsibility Model must be explicitly understood, and configuration baselines (e.g., disabling public read/write access on storage buckets) shall be enforced and audited quarterly.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>[Company Name] shall comply with all applicable data protection laws and regulations in jurisdictions where it operates, including but not limited to the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA), and sector-specific requirements. The organization shall maintain documented evidence of compliance for audits against ISO 27001:2022 Annex A controls (e.g., A.5, A.6, A.8, A.9, A.12, A.13, A.14, A.17, A.18) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability, Confidentiality, Processing Integrity, Privacy). Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) shall be conducted for high-risk processing activities as mandated by the GDPR.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p><strong>Board of Directors/Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate accountability for data protection governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Responsible for the development, implementation, and oversight of this policy and the overall security program.<br><strong>Data Protection Officer (DPO):</strong> Required under GDPR; oversees compliance, acts as point of contact for supervisory authorities and data subjects.<br><strong>Data Owners (Business Function Heads):</strong> Classify data, authorize access, and define retention periods for data within their domain.<br><strong>Data Custodians (IT/Security Teams):</strong> Implement and maintain technical controls (access, encryption, backups) as directed by data owners.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Mandated to complete annual data protection training, adhere to this policy, and report any security incidents or policy violations immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>Any violation of this policy may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual relationship, and potential legal or civil penalties. The Information Security Department is authorized to monitor systems and network traffic to ensure compliance. Deliberate circumvention of security controls, unauthorized data exfiltration, or failure to report a data breach constitutes gross misconduct and will be dealt with accordingly.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<h1>Data Protection Policy</h1><p>This policy shall be reviewed and approved by the CISO and Legal Department at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant changes in the threat landscape, business operations, or applicable regulations. All revisions must be formally version-controlled, communicated to all personnel, and archived. The effectiveness of controls outlined in this policy shall be validated through internal audits and penetration testing exercises.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-22 09:39:17.910448,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""GDPR\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:05:33.279,NULL
66,POL-INTEL-001,Intelligent Access Control Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Intelligent Access Control Policy is to establish a formal, risk-based framework for the provisioning, management, monitoring, and revocation of logical and physical access to [Company Name]'s information assets, systems, networks, and facilities. This policy mandates the principle of least privilege and ensures that access is granted based on verified identity, business need, and role, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with ISO 27001:2022 Annex A.5 (Information Security Policies) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all individuals, entities, and systems that interact with [Company Name]'s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary); contractors, consultants, and third-party service providers; all information systems, whether owned, operated, leased, or cloud-based; all data, regardless of classification or storage location; and all physical facilities owned or occupied by [Company Name].</p>

<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing services; and 2) enter specific physical facilities.<br><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access—or permissions—necessary to perform their authorized tasks.<br><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access to a resource.<br><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Any access that provides the ability to make significant configuration changes, override security controls, access sensitive data, or administer systems (e.g., root, administrator, domain admin, service accounts).<br><strong>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):</strong> A method of regulating access to computer or network resources based on the roles of individual users within an enterprise.<br><strong>Just-In-Time (JIT) Access:</strong> A privileged access management strategy where elevated permissions are granted for a specific, limited time period to perform a defined task, after which they are automatically revoked.</p>

<p>[Company Name] shall implement and maintain a comprehensive, intelligent access control system to protect its information assets from unauthorized access, modification, or disclosure. Access to all systems and data shall be strictly controlled, logged, and audited. The implementation of access controls shall be based on business requirements, security best practices, and a formal risk assessment. Violations of this policy are strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>

<h3>5.1 Identity and Authentication Management</h3><p>5.1.1 A unique user ID shall be assigned to each individual for accountability. Shared or generic accounts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.1.2 All user access to [Company Name]'s internal networks, cloud environments, and business applications must be authenticated. The use of weak or default passwords is forbidden.<br>5.1.3 <strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is mandatory</strong> for: all remote network access (VPN, RDP, SSH); all access to administrative consoles (cloud portals, infrastructure management); all access to systems containing sensitive or confidential data as defined by the Data Classification Policy; and for all privileged users, without exception.<br>5.1.4 Password policies shall enforce a minimum length of 14 characters, complexity, and a maximum age of 90 days. Password history shall prevent the reuse of the last 5 passwords.</p><h3>5.2 Authorization and Access Provisioning</h3><p>5.2.1 Access rights shall be granted based on the formal <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> and <strong>need-to-know</strong>.<br>5.2.2 A formal, documented user access review and approval process shall exist for all access requests. Approval must be obtained from the data/system owner or designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.2.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented where feasible to standardize access permissions based on job function.<br>5.2.4 Segregation of Duties (SoD) controls shall be implemented to prevent conflict of interest and fraud (e.g., a user who can request a payment cannot also approve it).</p><h3>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</h3><p>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., local administrators, domain admins, root, service accounts) shall be inventoried, and their use shall be strictly controlled and monitored.<br>5.3.2 Privileged sessions shall, where possible, be brokered through a dedicated PAM solution. Direct login with privileged credentials to critical systems is forbidden.<br>5.3.3 Just-In-Time (JIT) access elevation shall be implemented for privileged tasks, with approvals logged and sessions recorded.<br>5.3.4 All activity performed under a privileged account must be logged in an immutable audit trail, capturing command history, files accessed, and timestamps.</p><h3>5.4 Physical and Environmental Access</h3><p>5.4.1 Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via electronic access systems (e.g., card readers, biometrics). Access logs from these systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.<br>5.4.2 Access to secure areas shall be reviewed quarterly. Lost or stolen access cards must be reported immediately to the Security Team for deactivation.<br>5.4.3 Visitors to secure areas must be escorted at all times and their entry and exit logged.</p><h3>5.5 Access Review and Recertification</h3><p>5.5.1 Access rights for all users shall be formally reviewed and recertified by system/data owners at least semi-annually, or upon any role change.<br>5.5.2 Privileged access rights shall be reviewed and recertified quarterly.<br>5.5.3 Any access rights not explicitly re-approved during the recertification cycle shall be automatically revoked.</p><h3>5.6 Access Revocation and Termination</h3><p>5.6.1 Access rights for employees, contractors, and third parties shall be revoked immediately upon termination of employment or contract. This process shall be integrated with the Human Resources (HR) termination workflow.<br>5.6.2 For planned departures, access shall be revoked no later than the individual's last day of work. For unplanned or involuntary terminations, access shall be revoked concurrently with the termination notification.</p><h3>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Auditing</h3><p>5.7.1 All authentication events (successful and failed), privilege escalations, and access to critical data must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, source IP address, and action performed.<br>5.7.2 Access logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 365 days to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.<br>5.7.3 Automated monitoring shall be configured to alert the Security Operations Center (SOC) of anomalous access patterns, brute-force attacks, or policy violations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS for payment data), and contractual commitments to clients. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for mapping policy controls to specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 (notably A.5.1, A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.1-A.8.4) and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC1, CC5, CC6). Regular internal and external audits will be conducted to verify adherence to this policy and the aforementioned frameworks.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Provide overall governance and resource commitment for the access control program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, oversees its implementation, and reports on its effectiveness.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, and maintaining system-specific access procedures.<br><strong>Data Owners / Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests for their respective data and systems and performing periodic access recertifications.<br><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT and Security of employee status changes (hire, transfer, termination).<br><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, complying with access control procedures, and reporting any suspected unauthorized access immediately.</p>

<p>Any individual found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Unauthorized access, attempts to circumvent access controls, sharing of credentials, or failure to report known policy violations are considered serious misconduct. Legal action may be pursued in cases involving theft, fraud, or willful damage to [Company Name]'s information assets. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and Legal counsel, is authorized to investigate suspected violations and determine appropriate sanctions.</p>

<p>This Intelligent Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to legal and regulatory requirements. Proposed revisions shall be submitted to the Information Security Steering Committee for approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant personnel. The version control and revision history of this document shall be maintained by the Information Security Department.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Intelligent Access Control Policy is to establish a formal, risk-based framework for the provisioning, management, monitoring, and revocation of logical and physical access to [Company Name]'s information assets, systems, networks, and facilities. This policy mandates the principle of least privilege and ensures that access is granted based on verified identity, business need, and role, thereby protecting the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with ISO 27001:2022 Annex A.5 (Information Security Policies) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria for Security and Confidentiality.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all individuals, entities, and systems that interact with [Company Name]'s information assets, including but not limited to: all employees (full-time, part-time, temporary); contractors, consultants, and third-party service providers; all information systems, whether owned, operated, leased, or cloud-based; all data, regardless of classification or storage location; and all physical facilities owned or occupied by [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Access Control:</strong> The process of granting or denying specific requests to: 1) obtain and use information and related information processing ser