unts are strictly prohibited for human users, except where technically unavoidable for system functions, in which case they shall be tightly controlled and monitored.<br>5.1.2 All user access to [Company Name]'s internal networks, cloud environments, and business applications must be authenticated. The use of weak or default passwords is forbidden.<br>5.1.3 <strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) is mandatory</strong> for: all remote network access (VPN, RDP, SSH); all access to administrative consoles (cloud portals, infrastructure management); all access to systems containing sensitive or confidential data as defined by the Data Classification Policy; and for all privileged users, without exception.<br>5.1.4 Password policies shall enforce a minimum length of 14 characters, complexity, and a maximum age of 90 days. Password history shall prevent the reuse of the last 5 passwords.</p><h3>5.2 Authorization and Access Provisioning</h3><p>5.2.1 Access rights shall be granted based on the formal <strong>principle of least privilege</strong> and <strong>need-to-know</strong>.<br>5.2.2 A formal, documented user access review and approval process shall exist for all access requests. Approval must be obtained from the data/system owner or designated manager prior to provisioning.<br>5.2.3 Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) shall be implemented where feasible to standardize access permissions based on job function.<br>5.2.4 Segregation of Duties (SoD) controls shall be implemented to prevent conflict of interest and fraud (e.g., a user who can request a payment cannot also approve it).</p><h3>5.3 Privileged Access Management (PAM)</h3><p>5.3.1 Privileged accounts (e.g., local administrators, domain admins, root, service accounts) shall be inventoried, and their use shall be strictly controlled and monitored.<br>5.3.2 Privileged sessions shall, where possible, be brokered through a dedicated PAM solution. Direct login with privileged credentials to critical systems is forbidden.<br>5.3.3 Just-In-Time (JIT) access elevation shall be implemented for privileged tasks, with approvals logged and sessions recorded.<br>5.3.4 All activity performed under a privileged account must be logged in an immutable audit trail, capturing command history, files accessed, and timestamps.</p><h3>5.4 Physical and Environmental Access</h3><p>5.4.1 Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via electronic access systems (e.g., card readers, biometrics). Access logs from these systems shall be retained for a minimum of 90 days.<br>5.4.2 Access to secure areas shall be reviewed quarterly. Lost or stolen access cards must be reported immediately to the Security Team for deactivation.<br>5.4.3 Visitors to secure areas must be escorted at all times and their entry and exit logged.</p><h3>5.5 Access Review and Recertification</h3><p>5.5.1 Access rights for all users shall be formally reviewed and recertified by system/data owners at least semi-annually, or upon any role change.<br>5.5.2 Privileged access rights shall be reviewed and recertified quarterly.<br>5.5.3 Any access rights not explicitly re-approved during the recertification cycle shall be automatically revoked.</p><h3>5.6 Access Revocation and Termination</h3><p>5.6.1 Access rights for employees, contractors, and third parties shall be revoked immediately upon termination of employment or contract. This process shall be integrated with the Human Resources (HR) termination workflow.<br>5.6.2 For planned departures, access shall be revoked no later than the individual's last day of work. For unplanned or involuntary terminations, access shall be revoked concurrently with the termination notification.</p><h3>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Auditing</h3><p>5.7.1 All authentication events (successful and failed), privilege escalations, and access to critical data must be logged. Logs must include user ID, timestamp, source IP address, and action performed.<br>5.7.2 Access logs shall be centrally aggregated, protected from tampering, and retained for a minimum of 365 days to support forensic investigation and compliance audits.<br>5.7.3 Automated monitoring shall be configured to alert the Security Operations Center (SOC) of anomalous access patterns, brute-force attacks, or policy violations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure compliance with relevant legal, regulatory, and contractual obligations, including but not limited to data protection laws (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry standards (e.g., PCI DSS for payment data), and contractual commitments to clients. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is responsible for mapping policy controls to specific requirements of ISO 27001:2022 (notably A.5.1, A.5.15, A.5.16, A.5.17, A.8.1-A.8.4) and the SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (CC1, CC5, CC6). Regular internal and external audits will be conducted to verify adherence to this policy and the aforementioned frameworks.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Provide overall governance and resource commitment for the access control program.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Owns this policy, oversees its implementation, and reports on its effectiveness.<br><strong>Information Technology (IT) Department / System Owners:</strong> Responsible for the technical implementation of access controls, provisioning/de-provisioning user accounts, and maintaining system-specific access procedures.<br><strong>Data Owners / Business Unit Managers:</strong> Responsible for authorizing access requests for their respective data and systems and performing periodic access recertifications.<br><strong>Human Resources (HR):</strong> Responsible for promptly notifying IT and Security of employee status changes (hire, transfer, termination).<br><strong>All Users:</strong> Responsible for safeguarding their authentication credentials, complying with access control procedures, and reporting any suspected unauthorized access immediately.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any individual found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contractual agreement. Unauthorized access, attempts to circumvent access controls, sharing of credentials, or failure to report known policy violations are considered serious misconduct. Legal action may be pursued in cases involving theft, fraud, or willful damage to [Company Name]'s information assets. The CISO, in conjunction with Human Resources and Legal counsel, is authorized to investigate suspected violations and determine appropriate sanctions.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Intelligent Access Control Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in technology, or updates to legal and regulatory requirements. Proposed revisions shall be submitted to the Information Security Steering Committee for approval. Upon approval, the revised policy shall be communicated to all relevant personnel. The version control and revision history of this document shall be maintained by the Information Security Department.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2026-02-18 19:51:08.650427,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",1,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:06:20.893,"""[{\""id\"":\""usesCloud\"",\""question\"":\""Does the organization use cloud infrastructure (AWS/Azure/GCP)?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""defaultValue\"":true,\""category\"":\""Infrastructure\""},{\""id\"":\""mfaVendor\"",\""question\"":\""What is your primary MFA provider?\"",\""type\"":\""select\"",\""options\"":[\""Okta\"",\""Microsoft Authenticator\"",\""Duo\"",\""Google Authenticator\""],\""defaultValue\"":\""Okta\"",\""category\"":\""Security Tools\""}]"""
32,665128,Remote Access Policy,"<p>This Remote Access Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for all remote access connections to [Company Name]'s internal networks, systems, and data. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets by ensuring remote access is provisioned, monitored, and managed in a secure and compliant manner, in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party agents (collectively, ""Authorized Users"") who require access to internal corporate resources from any external or non-corporately managed location. It encompasses all remote access methods, including but not limited to Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), remote desktop protocols (RDP, VNC), cloud-based application access, and secure shell (SSH). This policy applies to all corporate-owned and personally-owned devices used for such access.</p>

<p><strong>Remote Access:</strong> Any connection to [Company Name]'s internal network or systems initiated from a location outside the physical corporate perimeter.<br/><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access.<br/><strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN):</strong> A secured, encrypted tunnel for network traffic between a remote device and the corporate network.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Access rights that allow for the configuration, management, or significant alteration of systems, applications, or data.<br/><strong>Endpoint Security Agent:</strong> Mandatory software installed on a device to enforce security policies (e.g., antivirus, host-based firewall, disk encryption).</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates that all remote access to corporate information resources shall be strictly controlled, centrally managed, and continuously monitored. Remote access is a privilege granted based on business necessity and adherence to the security requirements defined herein. Unauthorized remote access connections or the circumvention of defined security controls are strictly prohibited and constitute a violation of this policy.</p>

<h4>5.1 Authorization and Provisioning</h4><p>All requests for remote access must be formally approved by the employee's department head and the Information Security team prior to provisioning. Access shall be granted on a least-privilege basis and must be reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication Controls</h4><p>All remote access sessions must be protected by [Company Name]-approved Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The use of password-only authentication for remote access is forbidden. MFA shall be required at initial connection and may be required again for access to highly sensitive systems.</p><h4>5.3 Connection Security</h4><p>All remote network access must be routed through the corporate-approved Virtual Private Network (VPN) gateway. The VPN must use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256-GCM) and a secure key exchange protocol (e.g., IKEv2). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all client traffic must be routed through the VPN tunnel while connected.</p><h4>5.4 Endpoint Security Requirements</h4><p>Any device used for remote access must have the corporate Endpoint Security Agent installed, enabled, and reporting as compliant. Mandatory controls include: a host-based firewall set to deny-by-default, up-to-date anti-malware software, full-disk encryption for data-at-rest, and a supported operating system with all critical security patches applied within 14 days of release.</p><h4>5.5 Session Management and Timeouts</h4><p>Remote access sessions shall be configured to automatically lock after 15 minutes of user inactivity and terminate after a maximum of 12 hours of continuous connectivity, requiring re-authentication. All session establishment and termination events must be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Prohibited Activities and Environments</h4><p>Accessing corporate resources from public or untrusted wireless networks (e.g., airports, cafes) without the VPN is strictly prohibited. The use of corporate credentials on non-corporate devices or for personal services is forbidden. Storing sensitive corporate data on local drives of remote devices is not permitted unless encrypted and explicitly authorized.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific controls from ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (notably A.5.15, A.5.16, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria relevant to Security and Confidentiality. All remote access logging must be maintained to support audit requirements for a minimum of 90 days, with key security events retained for one year. Remote access activities are subject to monitoring and review to ensure compliance with this policy and relevant data protection regulations.</p>

<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, and monitoring of remote access security controls; conducting periodic reviews and audits.<br/><strong>IT Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the provisioning, maintenance, and technical operation of the remote access infrastructure (e.g., VPN concentrators).<br/><strong>Authorized Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, securing their remote access devices, and reporting any suspected security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads / Managers:</strong> Responsible for approving remote access requests based on business need and ensuring their staff are aware of and comply with this policy.</p>

<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security team is authorized to immediately disable the remote access of any user or device found to be non-compliant or posing a security risk to [Company Name].</p>

<p>This Remote Access Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or following significant changes to the technical infrastructure or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Remote Access Policy establishes the mandatory security controls and governance framework for all remote access connections to [Company Name]'s internal networks, systems, and data. The purpose is to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of corporate information assets by ensuring remote access is provisioned, monitored, and managed in a secure and compliant manner, in alignment with ISO 27001 and SOC 2 trust service criteria.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party agents (collectively, \\\""Authorized Users\\\"") who require access to internal corporate resources from any external or non-corporately managed location. It encompasses all remote access methods, including but not limited to Virtual Private Networks (VPNs), remote desktop protocols (RDP, VNC), cloud-based application access, and secure shell (SSH). This policy applies to all corporate-owned and personally-owned devices used for such access.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Remote Access:</strong> Any connection to [Company Name]'s internal network or systems initiated from a location outside the physical corporate perimeter.<br/><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA):</strong> An authentication method that requires the user to provide two or more verification factors to gain access.<br/><strong>Virtual Private Network (VPN):</strong> A secured, encrypted tunnel for network traffic between a remote device and the corporate network.<br/><strong>Privileged Access:</strong> Access rights that allow for the configuration, management, or significant alteration of systems, applications, or data.<br/><strong>Endpoint Security Agent:</strong> Mandatory software installed on a device to enforce security policies (e.g., antivirus, host-based firewall, disk encryption).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates that all remote access to corporate information resources shall be strictly controlled, centrally managed, and continuously monitored. Remote access is a privilege granted based on business necessity and adherence to the security requirements defined herein. Unauthorized remote access connections or the circumvention of defined security controls are strictly prohibited and constitute a violation of this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Authorization and Provisioning</h4><p>All requests for remote access must be formally approved by the employee's department head and the Information Security team prior to provisioning. Access shall be granted on a least-privilege basis and must be reviewed quarterly.</p><h4>5.2 Authentication Controls</h4><p>All remote access sessions must be protected by [Company Name]-approved Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). The use of password-only authentication for remote access is forbidden. MFA shall be required at initial connection and may be required again for access to highly sensitive systems.</p><h4>5.3 Connection Security</h4><p>All remote network access must be routed through the corporate-approved Virtual Private Network (VPN) gateway. The VPN must use strong encryption (e.g., AES-256-GCM) and a secure key exchange protocol (e.g., IKEv2). Split-tunneling is prohibited; all client traffic must be routed through the VPN tunnel while connected.</p><h4>5.4 Endpoint Security Requirements</h4><p>Any device used for remote access must have the corporate Endpoint Security Agent installed, enabled, and reporting as compliant. Mandatory controls include: a host-based firewall set to deny-by-default, up-to-date anti-malware software, full-disk encryption for data-at-rest, and a supported operating system with all critical security patches applied within 14 days of release.</p><h4>5.5 Session Management and Timeouts</h4><p>Remote access sessions shall be configured to automatically lock after 15 minutes of user inactivity and terminate after a maximum of 12 hours of continuous connectivity, requiring re-authentication. All session establishment and termination events must be logged.</p><h4>5.6 Prohibited Activities and Environments</h4><p>Accessing corporate resources from public or untrusted wireless networks (e.g., airports, cafes) without the VPN is strictly prohibited. The use of corporate credentials on non-corporate devices or for personal services is forbidden. Storing sensitive corporate data on local drives of remote devices is not permitted unless encrypted and explicitly authorized.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to satisfy specific controls from ISO 27001:2022 Annex A (notably A.5.15, A.5.16, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25) and SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria relevant to Security and Confidentiality. All remote access logging must be maintained to support audit requirements for a minimum of 90 days, with key security events retained for one year. Remote access activities are subject to monitoring and review to ensure compliance with this policy and relevant data protection regulations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Responsible for the design, implementation, and monitoring of remote access security controls; conducting periodic reviews and audits.<br/><strong>IT Department / Network Team:</strong> Responsible for the provisioning, maintenance, and technical operation of the remote access infrastructure (e.g., VPN concentrators).<br/><strong>Authorized Users:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all policy requirements, securing their remote access devices, and reporting any suspected security incidents immediately.<br/><strong>Department Heads / Managers:</strong> Responsible for approving remote access requests based on business need and ensuring their staff are aware of and comply with this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Any employee or third party found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal action. The Information Security team is authorized to immediately disable the remote access of any user or device found to be non-compliant or posing a security risk to [Company Name].</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Remote Access Policy shall be reviewed by the Information Security team at least annually, or following significant changes to the technical infrastructure or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) or designated authority.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.618477,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:06:51.802,NULL
67,POL-SAMPLE-001,Master Intelligent Security Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Master Intelligent Security Policy is to establish the authoritative, risk-based framework for the governance, implementation, and continuous assurance of [Company Name]'s information security program. This policy mandates the integration of intelligent security controls, including but not limited to artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and advanced analytics, to proactively protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. It is designed to ensure compliance with the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF).</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or entrusted to [Company Name], irrespective of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, all data, systems, networks, applications (including AI/ML models and supporting infrastructure), cloud services, and physical premises. The policy is binding on all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party entities accessing or processing [Company Name] information assets.</p>

<p><strong>Intelligent Security System (ISS):</strong> Any integrated set of security controls that utilizes AI, ML, automation, or predictive analytics to detect, prevent, or respond to security threats.<br><strong>AI/ML Model:</strong> A software artifact that implements a decision-making process derived from data through algorithmic training.<br><strong>Model Artifact:</strong> The complete set of files required to reconstruct and operate an AI/ML model, including training data, code, parameters, and configuration.<br><strong>Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR):</strong> A platform that integrates security tools and automates incident response workflows.<br><strong>Policy as Code (PaC):</strong> The management and deployment of security policies through machine-readable definition files, enabling automated enforcement and validation.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing a world-class, intelligent security posture. The organization shall architect its security controls to be proactive, adaptive, and data-driven. The deployment and operation of all Intelligent Security Systems are strictly governed by this policy to ensure they are effective, ethical, compliant, and aligned with business objectives. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is expressly forbidden without formal, documented exception approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO).</p>

<h4>5.1 Intelligent Security System Governance</h4><p>5.1.1 All proposed ISS deployments must undergo a formal security architecture review and risk assessment prior to procurement or development. Approval authority rests with the Information Security Department.<br>5.1.2 A centralized registry of all production ISS components, including AI/ML models, their intended purpose, data sources, and responsible owners, shall be maintained and reviewed quarterly.<br>5.1.3 The use of open-source or third-party AI/ML components within an ISS is forbidden unless a comprehensive software composition analysis and security review have been completed and documented.</p><h4>5.2 Data Security for Intelligent Systems</h4><p>5.2.1 All data used for training, testing, or operation of AI/ML models must be classified according to the Data Classification Policy. Use of Confidential or Restricted data requires explicit authorization.<br>5.2.2 Where technically feasible, synthetic data or differential privacy techniques shall be employed to minimize exposure of sensitive production data during model training.<br>5.2.3 All data in transit to and from ISS components must be encrypted using TLS 1.2 or higher. Data at rest within ISS storage must be encrypted using AES-256 or an approved equivalent.</p><h4>5.3 Identity and Access Management (IAM) for ISS</h4><p>5.3.1 Access to ISS administrative interfaces, model repositories, and training pipelines shall be strictly controlled via multi-factor authentication (MFA).<br>5.3.2 The principle of least privilege must be enforced. Service accounts for automated ISS tasks shall use short-lived, scoped credentials managed by a privileged access management (PAM) solution.<br>5.3.3 All access events, including model training initiation, configuration changes, and data queries by automated agents, must be logged to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.</p><h4>5.4 Secure Development and Deployment of AI/ML Models</h4><p>5.4.1 AI/ML model development must follow a secure development lifecycle (SDLC) integrated with version control for all model artifacts.<br>5.4.2 All models must undergo adversarial robustness testing and bias/fairness evaluation before promotion to a production environment.<br>5.4.3 Deployment of models shall be conducted via automated pipelines that include static and dynamic security scanning of container images and deployment manifests.</p><h4>5.5 Monitoring, Logging, and Automated Response</h4><p>5.5.1 All ISS components shall generate audit logs detailing system health, performance metrics, security events, and all decision-making actions with high confidence scores.<br>5.5.2 Logs must be ingested into the corporate SIEM and retained for a minimum of 90 days for active analysis and one year for compliance purposes.<br>5.5.3 Security playbooks for common threat scenarios (e.g., model poisoning, data exfiltration via API) must be codified and deployed within the SOAR platform. Automated containment actions for high-fidelity alerts are mandated.</p><h4>5.6 Policy as Code and Continuous Compliance</h4><p>5.6.1 Security policies for cloud infrastructure hosting ISS (e.g., network segmentation, encryption settings) shall be defined, versioned, and enforced as code.<br>5.6.2 Continuous compliance scanning tools must be configured to validate the runtime state of ISS environments against Policy as Code definitions at least every 24 hours.<br>5.6.3 Any drift from the defined secure state shall generate a high-priority alert and trigger an automated remediation workflow or isolate the non-compliant asset.</p>

<p>The design and operation of Intelligent Security Systems shall adhere to all applicable laws, regulations, and contractual obligations. This includes, but is not limited to, data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA), industry-specific mandates, and intellectual property rights. The Information Security Department, in conjunction with Legal and Compliance, is responsible for conducting periodic reviews to ensure ISS activities remain compliant. Any use of AI/ML for automated decision-making that has a legal or significant effect on individuals must include provisions for human review and explanation, as required by law.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimate accountability for this policy's approval, implementation, and effectiveness. Owns the intelligent security strategy.<br><strong>Information Security Department:</strong> Responsible for policy execution, architecture reviews, threat modeling for ISS, monitoring, and incident response.<br><strong>Data Science & AI Engineering Teams:</strong> Responsible for implementing secure development practices, model testing, and maintaining the integrity of model artifacts.<br><strong>Cloud & Infrastructure Teams:</strong> Responsible for provisioning secure, compliant infrastructure for ISS as defined by Policy as Code.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Required to report any suspected security weaknesses or anomalous behavior related to intelligent systems immediately.</p>

<p>Any personnel found to have violated this policy may be subject to disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment and legal prosecution. Third-party vendors in violation of policy requirements stipulated in their contracts may be subject to penalties, contract termination, and liability for damages. The Information Security Department is authorized to immediately disconnect or isolate any ISS component deemed to be operating in an insecure or non-compliant manner that poses an imminent risk to the organization.</p>

<p>This Master Intelligent Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO and the Information Security Steering Committee at least annually, or following any significant security incident, technological change, or update to relevant compliance frameworks. Revisions must be formally versioned, approved, and communicated to all stakeholders. The policy's effectiveness shall be measured through key risk indicators (KRIs) and metrics reported to executive management.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Master Intelligent Security Policy is to establish the authoritative, risk-based framework for the governance, implementation, and continuous assurance of [Company Name]'s information security program. This policy mandates the integration of intelligent security controls, including but not limited to artificial intelligence (AI), machine learning (ML), and advanced analytics, to proactively protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. It is designed to ensure compliance with the requirements of ISO/IEC 27001, SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria, and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, operated, or entrusted to [Company Name], irrespective of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, all data, systems, networks, applications (including AI/ML models and supporting infrastructure), cloud services, and physical premises. The policy is binding on all personnel, including employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and any third-party entities accessing or processing [Company Name] information assets.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Intelligent Security System (ISS):</strong> Any integrated set of security controls that utilizes AI, ML, automation, or predictive analytics to detect, prevent, or respond to security threats.<br><strong>AI/ML Model:</strong> A software artifact that implements a decision-making process derived from data through algorithmic training.<br><strong>Model Artifact:</strong> The complete set of files required to reconstruct and operate an AI/ML model, including training data, code, parameters, and configuration.<br><strong>Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR):</strong> A platform that integrates security tools and automates incident response workflows.<br><strong>Policy as Code (PaC):</strong> The management and deployment of security policies through machine-readable definition files, enabling automated enforcement and validation.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] is committed to implementing a world-class, intelligent security posture. The organization shall architect its security controls to be proactive, adaptive, and data-driven. The deployment and operation of all Intelligent Security Systems are strictly governed by this policy to ensure they are effective, ethical, compliant, and aligned with business objectives. Any deviation from the controls specified herein is expressly forbidden without formal, documented exception approved by the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO).</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Intelligent Security System Governance</h4><p>5.1.1 All proposed ISS deployments must undergo a formal security architecture review and risk assessment prior to procurement or development. Approval authority rests with the Information Security Department.<br>5.1.2 A centralized registry of all production ISS components, including AI/ML models, their intended purpose, data sources, and responsible owners, shall be maintained and reviewed quarterly.<br>5.1.3 The use of open-source or third-party AI/ML components within an ISS is forbidden unless a com