""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""NIST CSF\""]""",1,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:07:32.862,"""[{\""id\"":\""cloud_environment\"",\""question\"":\""Primary Cloud Infrastructure Provider?\"",\""type\"":\""select\"",\""options\"":[\""AWS\"",\""Azure\"",\""Google Cloud\"",\""On-Premises\""],\""defaultValue\"":\""AWS\""},{\""id\"":\""biometrics_enabled\"",\""question\"":\""Is biometric authentication (FaceID/Fingerprint) enabled for mobile devices?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""staff_count\"",\""question\"":\""Approximate total headcount (contractors included)?\"",\""type\"":\""number\"",\""defaultValue\"":25},{\""id\"":\""data_privacy_officer\"",\""question\"":\""Designated Data Privacy Officer (DPO) Full Name?\"",\""type\"":\""text\"",\""defaultValue\"":\""John Doe\""}]"""
6,1,Information Security Policy,"<p>The purpose of this Information Security Policy is to establish the framework for protecting the information assets of [Company Name] from all threats, whether internal or external, deliberate or accidental. This policy mandates the implementation of a comprehensive Information Security Management System (ISMS) to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with business objectives, regulatory requirements, and the standards set forth by ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic data, physical records, software, hardware, and network infrastructure. The policy is binding on all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who access, use, or manage [Company Name]'s information assets or information processing facilities.</p>

<p><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, document, or resource that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> The property that information is not made available or disclosed to unauthorized individuals, entities, or processes.<br><strong>Integrity:</strong> The property of safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of assets.<br><strong>Availability:</strong> The property of being accessible and usable upon demand by an authorized entity.<br><strong>ISMS:</strong> Information Security Management System, the systematic approach to managing sensitive company information.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process of selecting and implementing measures to modify risk.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The risk remaining after risk treatment.</p>

<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting its information assets through a risk-based approach. Information security is the responsibility of every individual within the organization. All personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and defense-in-depth. Any activity that compromises the security of information assets is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>

<h4>5.1 Access Control</h4><p>5.1.1. Access to information assets shall be granted based on the principle of least privilege and must be formally authorized.<br>5.1.2. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all remote access and administrative accounts.<br>5.1.3. User access rights shall be reviewed and recertified at least quarterly. All dormant accounts (inactive for 90 days) shall be disabled or removed.<br>5.1.4. Privileged access sessions shall be logged, monitored, and subject to periodic audit.</p><h4>5.2 Cryptography</h4><p>5.2.1. All data classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or an equivalent approved algorithm.<br>5.2.2. Data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.2.3. Cryptographic keys shall be managed through a dedicated key management system, with key generation, storage, rotation, and destruction procedures formally documented.</p><h4>5.3 Physical and Environmental Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via badge access and logged.<br>5.3.2. Environmental controls (temperature, humidity, fire suppression) must be maintained and monitored 24/7 for all critical infrastructure locations.</p><h4>5.4 Operations Security</h4><p>5.4.1. All systems must be hardened according to a defined baseline configuration before deployment.<br>5.4.2. A formal change management process shall govern all modifications to hardware, software, or network configurations.<br>5.4.3. Anti-malware software with automatic signature updates must be deployed on all endpoints and servers.</p><h4>5.5 Communications Security</h4><p>5.5.1. All wireless network infrastructure must be approved and configured by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for company business is strictly forbidden.<br>5.5.2. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate sensitive environments (e.g., production, development) from general corporate networks.</p><h4>5.6 System Acquisition, Development, and Maintenance</h4><p>5.6.1. Security requirements shall be defined and integrated into all phases of the system development lifecycle (SDLC).<br>5.6.2. All application code must undergo static and dynamic application security testing (SAST/DAST) prior to release.</p><h4>5.7 Supplier Relationships</h4><p>5.7.1. All third-party suppliers with access to [Company Name] information assets must be subject to a security assessment and bound by a contract containing explicit information security obligations.</p><h4>5.8 Information Security Incident Management</h4><p>5.8.1. A formal incident response plan shall be maintained and tested annually.<br>5.8.2. All suspected security incidents must be reported immediately to the Information Security team. Containment, eradication, and recovery actions shall be documented.</p><h4>5.9 Information Security Aspects of Business Continuity Management</h4><p>5.9.1. Business continuity and disaster recovery plans must include specific procedures for the restoration of information security controls.<br>5.9.2. Backups of critical data and systems shall be performed regularly, stored securely off-site, and tested for integrity at least semi-annually.</p><h4>5.10 Compliance</h4><p>5.10.1. Systems shall be configured to collect and retain security event logs for a minimum of 90 days to support monitoring and investigation.<br>5.10.2. Technical controls shall be implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration and to ensure data processing complies with relevant privacy regulations.</p>

<p>This policy is designed to ensure [Company Name]'s compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual requirements related to information security and data protection, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and the control frameworks of ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria). The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying and communicating changes in the regulatory landscape that impact this policy.</p>

<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for information security governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall ownership of the ISMS, policy development, and security program oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Implementation, monitoring, and operational management of security controls.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Secure configuration and maintenance of IT infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all security policies and procedures, and for reporting security incidents.</p>

<p>Violations of this Information Security Policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Individuals found to have violated this policy will be subject to disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the breach, which may include verbal or written warnings, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All enforcement actions shall be documented.</p>

<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. All revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>The purpose of this Information Security Policy is to establish the framework for protecting the information assets of [Company Name] from all threats, whether internal or external, deliberate or accidental. This policy mandates the implementation of a comprehensive Information Security Management System (ISMS) to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information in alignment with business objectives, regulatory requirements, and the standards set forth by ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all information assets owned, managed, or processed by [Company Name], regardless of format or location. This includes, but is not limited to, electronic data, physical records, software, hardware, and network infrastructure. The policy is binding on all employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third parties who access, use, or manage [Company Name]'s information assets or information processing facilities.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Information Asset:</strong> Any data, document, or resource that has value to the organization.<br><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> The property that information is not made available or disclosed to unauthorized individuals, entities, or processes.<br><strong>Integrity:</strong> The property of safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of assets.<br><strong>Availability:</strong> The property of being accessible and usable upon demand by an authorized entity.<br><strong>ISMS:</strong> Information Security Management System, the systematic approach to managing sensitive company information.<br><strong>Risk Treatment:</strong> The process of selecting and implementing measures to modify risk.<br><strong>Residual Risk:</strong> The risk remaining after risk treatment.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] is committed to protecting its information assets through a risk-based approach. Information security is the responsibility of every individual within the organization. All personnel shall adhere to the principles of least privilege, need-to-know, and defense-in-depth. Any activity that compromises the security of information assets is strictly prohibited and may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and legal prosecution.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Access Control</h4><p>5.1.1. Access to information assets shall be granted based on the principle of least privilege and must be formally authorized.<br>5.1.2. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all remote access and administrative accounts.<br>5.1.3. User access rights shall be reviewed and recertified at least quarterly. All dormant accounts (inactive for 90 days) shall be disabled or removed.<br>5.1.4. Privileged access sessions shall be logged, monitored, and subject to periodic audit.</p><h4>5.2 Cryptography</h4><p>5.2.1. All data classified as Confidential or higher must be encrypted at rest using AES-256 or an equivalent approved algorithm.<br>5.2.2. Data in transit over public or untrusted networks must be protected using TLS 1.2 or higher.<br>5.2.3. Cryptographic keys shall be managed through a dedicated key management system, with key generation, storage, rotation, and destruction procedures formally documented.</p><h4>5.3 Physical and Environmental Security</h4><p>5.3.1. Physical access to data centers, server rooms, and network closets shall be controlled via badge access and logged.<br>5.3.2. Environmental controls (temperature, humidity, fire suppression) must be maintained and monitored 24/7 for all critical infrastructure locations.</p><h4>5.4 Operations Security</h4><p>5.4.1. All systems must be hardened according to a defined baseline configuration before deployment.<br>5.4.2. A formal change management process shall govern all modifications to hardware, software, or network configurations.<br>5.4.3. Anti-malware software with automatic signature updates must be deployed on all endpoints and servers.</p><h4>5.5 Communications Security</h4><p>5.5.1. All wireless network infrastructure must be approved and configured by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for company business is strictly forbidden.<br>5.5.2. Network segmentation shall be implemented to isolate sensitive environments (e.g., production, development) from general corporate networks.</p><h4>5.6 System Acquisition, Development, and Maintenance</h4><p>5.6.1. Security requirements shall be defined and integrated into all phases of the system development lifecycle (SDLC).<br>5.6.2. All application code must undergo static and dynamic application security testing (SAST/DAST) prior to release.</p><h4>5.7 Supplier Relationships</h4><p>5.7.1. All third-party suppliers with access to [Company Name] information assets must be subject to a security assessment and bound by a contract containing explicit information security obligations.</p><h4>5.8 Information Security Incident Management</h4><p>5.8.1. A formal incident response plan shall be maintained and tested annually.<br>5.8.2. All suspected security incidents must be reported immediately to the Information Security team. Containment, eradication, and recovery actions shall be documented.</p><h4>5.9 Information Security Aspects of Business Continuity Management</h4><p>5.9.1. Business continuity and disaster recovery plans must include specific procedures for the restoration of information security controls.<br>5.9.2. Backups of critical data and systems shall be performed regularly, stored securely off-site, and tested for integrity at least semi-annually.</p><h4>5.10 Compliance</h4><p>5.10.1. Systems shall be configured to collect and retain security event logs for a minimum of 90 days to support monitoring and investigation.<br>5.10.2. Technical controls shall be implemented to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration and to ensure data processing complies with relevant privacy regulations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy is designed to ensure [Company Name]'s compliance with relevant legal, statutory, regulatory, and contractual requirements related to information security and data protection, including but not limited to GDPR, CCPA/CPRA, HIPAA (if applicable), and the control frameworks of ISO/IEC 27001 and SOC 2 (Trust Services Criteria). The Legal and Compliance departments are responsible for identifying and communicating changes in the regulatory landscape that impact this policy.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Board of Directors / Executive Management:</strong> Ultimate responsibility for information security governance and resource allocation.<br><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Overall ownership of the ISMS, policy development, and security program oversight.<br><strong>Information Security Team:</strong> Implementation, monitoring, and operational management of security controls.<br><strong>IT Department:</strong> Secure configuration and maintenance of IT infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to all security policies and procedures, and for reporting security incidents.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Violations of this Information Security Policy will be treated with the utmost seriousness. Individuals found to have violated this policy will be subject to disciplinary action commensurate with the severity of the breach, which may include verbal or written warnings, suspension of system access, termination of employment or contract, and pursuit of legal remedies. All enforcement actions shall be documented.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or more frequently in response to significant security incidents, changes in business operations, or updates to relevant standards and regulations. All revisions must be approved by executive management. The most current version of this policy shall be made accessible to all personnel covered by its scope.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-19 06:15:54.290528,"""[\""ISO 27001 / SOC 2\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:08:11.071,"""[{\""id\"":\""q_1771452393824\"",\""question\"":\""New Question\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""defaultValue\"":false}]"""
31,507790,Network Security Policy,"<p>This Network Security Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]'s information assets and network infrastructure. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures required to safeguard network resources from unauthorized access, misuse, modification, or destruction, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>

<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively ""Personnel""). It encompasses all network infrastructure owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: data centers, cloud environments, corporate offices, remote access connections, wireless networks, and all connected devices (servers, workstations, network appliances, IoT devices). This policy extends to any system that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data.</p>

<p><strong>DMZ (Demilitarized Zone):</strong> A physical or logical subnet that contains and isolates externally-facing services from the internal trusted network.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform their authorized functions.<br/><strong>Network Segmentation:</strong> The practice of dividing a network into smaller, isolated subnetworks to control traffic flow and contain security breaches.<br/><strong>Stateful Inspection Firewall:</strong> A firewall that monitors the state of active connections and makes filtering decisions based on the context of the traffic.<br/><strong>Zero Trust Architecture:</strong> A security model that mandates strict identity verification for every person and device attempting to access resources on a private network, regardless of location.</p>

<p>[Company Name] mandates a defense-in-depth approach to network security, integrating multiple layers of physical, technical, and administrative controls. All network access shall be governed by the principles of least privilege and need-to-know. The design, implementation, and operation of the corporate network must ensure the secure processing and transmission of information, protect against internal and external threats, and maintain compliance with all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements.</p>

<h4>5.1 Network Architecture and Segmentation</h4><p>[Company Name] shall implement a logically segmented network architecture. A minimum of three security zones (e.g., untrusted, DMZ, trusted internal) must be established and enforced by next-generation firewalls. All user workstations shall reside in a segment separate from server and infrastructure segments. Micro-segmentation must be applied within data center and cloud environments to restrict east-west traffic.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>Access to all network resources shall be granted based on authenticated identity and role. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative access and remote network access (e.g., VPN). Network device configurations must enforce role-based access control (RBAC). All service accounts used for network device management must have complex, unique credentials that are rotated at least every 90 days.</p><h4>5.3 Perimeter and Internal Security Controls</h4><p>Stateful inspection or next-generation firewalls must be deployed at all network boundaries, including internet egress points and inter-zone boundaries. Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS) shall be deployed in-line to monitor and block malicious traffic. All inbound and outbound traffic must be explicitly allowed by firewall rules; a default-deny rule shall be in place for all rule sets.</p><h4>5.4 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed centrally by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for corporate data or access is strictly forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall use WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be isolated in a separate VLAN with no connectivity to the internal corporate network.</p><h4>5.5 Remote Access Security</h4><p>Remote access to the corporate network shall only be permitted through the approved corporate Virtual Private Network (VPN) solution. Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the corporate VPN gateway. VPN sessions must timeout after a maximum of 12 hours of inactivity and require re-authentication.</p><h4>5.6 Vulnerability Management and Patching</h4><p>A formal inventory of all network devices (firewalls, routers, switches, wireless controllers) must be maintained and updated automatically. Critical and high-severity security patches for network infrastructure must be applied within 30 days of vendor release. Vulnerability scans of the internal and external network perimeter must be conducted at least quarterly, with findings remediated according to risk-based timelines.</p><h4>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h4><p>All network security devices (firewalls, IPS, VPN concentrators) must generate audit logs. Logs must record all allowed and denied traffic, configuration changes, and authentication events. These logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days. The Security Operations Center (SOC) shall monitor these logs 24/7/365 for indicators of compromise.</p>

<p>Network security controls shall be designed and operated to meet the requirements of relevant frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.20, A.8.21, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25, A.8.26), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Protect, Detect). This policy also supports compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) by enforcing controls for the secure transmission of personal data. Any deviation from this policy for legal or investigative purposes requires written authorization from the CISO and General Counsel.</p>

<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, approval, and overall governance of this policy.<br/><strong>Network Engineering Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, and maintenance of the network infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the 24/7 monitoring of network security events, incident triage, and initial response.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy, reporting suspected security incidents or policy violations, and using corporate network resources only for authorized purposes.</p>

<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department and SOC shall implement automated technical controls (e.g., NAC, firewall rules) to enforce policy requirements. Regular audits and automated compliance scans will be conducted to verify adherence. Any exception to this policy requires a formal, documented risk acceptance approved by the CISO.</p>

<p>This Network Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following any significant security incident or major change in the network architecture or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained in a central document repository.</p>","""[{\""title\"":\""1. Purpose\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Network Security Policy establishes the mandatory framework and technical controls to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of [Company Name]'s information assets and network infrastructure. It defines the principles, standards, and procedures required to safeguard network resources from unauthorized access, misuse, modification, or destruction, in alignment with business objectives and regulatory obligations.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""2. Scope\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This policy applies to all [Company Name] employees, contractors, consultants, temporary staff, and other third-party personnel (collectively \\\""Personnel\\\""). It encompasses all network infrastructure owned, operated, or leased by [Company Name], including but not limited to: data centers, cloud environments, corporate offices, remote access connections, wireless networks, and all connected devices (servers, workstations, network appliances, IoT devices). This policy extends to any system that processes, stores, or transmits [Company Name] data.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""3. Definitions\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>DMZ (Demilitarized Zone):</strong> A physical or logical subnet that contains and isolates externally-facing services from the internal trusted network.<br/><strong>Least Privilege:</strong> The security principle that users and systems shall be granted the minimum levels of access necessary to perform their authorized functions.<br/><strong>Network Segmentation:</strong> The practice of dividing a network into smaller, isolated subnetworks to control traffic flow and contain security breaches.<br/><strong>Stateful Inspection Firewall:</strong> A firewall that monitors the state of active connections and makes filtering decisions based on the context of the traffic.<br/><strong>Zero Trust Architecture:</strong> A security model that mandates strict identity verification for every person and device attempting to access resources on a private network, regardless of location.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""4. Policy Statement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>[Company Name] mandates a defense-in-depth approach to network security, integrating multiple layers of physical, technical, and administrative controls. All network access shall be governed by the principles of least privilege and need-to-know. The design, implementation, and operation of the corporate network must ensure the secure processing and transmission of information, protect against internal and external threats, and maintain compliance with all applicable legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""5. Policy Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<h4>5.1 Network Architecture and Segmentation</h4><p>[Company Name] shall implement a logically segmented network architecture. A minimum of three security zones (e.g., untrusted, DMZ, trusted internal) must be established and enforced by next-generation firewalls. All user workstations shall reside in a segment separate from server and infrastructure segments. Micro-segmentation must be applied within data center and cloud environments to restrict east-west traffic.</p><h4>5.2 Access Control and Authentication</h4><p>Access to all network resources shall be granted based on authenticated identity and role. Multi-factor authentication (MFA) is mandatory for all administrative access and remote network access (e.g., VPN). Network device configurations must enforce role-based access control (RBAC). All service accounts used for network device management must have complex, unique credentials that are rotated at least every 90 days.</p><h4>5.3 Perimeter and Internal Security Controls</h4><p>Stateful inspection or next-generation firewalls must be deployed at all network boundaries, including internet egress points and inter-zone boundaries. Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS) shall be deployed in-line to monitor and block malicious traffic. All inbound and outbound traffic must be explicitly allowed by firewall rules; a default-deny rule shall be in place for all rule sets.</p><h4>5.4 Wireless Network Security</h4><p>All wireless infrastructure and access points must be approved and managed centrally by the Information Technology (IT) Department. The use of WEP, WPA-Personal (PSK), or open/unencrypted networks for corporate data or access is strictly forbidden. Corporate wireless networks shall use WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X authentication. Guest wireless access must be isolated in a separate VLAN with no connectivity to the internal corporate network.</p><h4>5.5 Remote Access Security</h4><p>Remote access to the corporate network shall only be permitted through the approved corporate Virtual Private Network (VPN) solution. Split-tunneling is prohibited; all remote user traffic must be routed through the corporate VPN gateway. VPN sessions must timeout after a maximum of 12 hours of inactivity and require re-authentication.</p><h4>5.6 Vulnerability Management and Patching</h4><p>A formal inventory of all network devices (firewalls, routers, switches, wireless controllers) must be maintained and updated automatically. Critical and high-severity security patches for network infrastructure must be applied within 30 days of vendor release. Vulnerability scans of the internal and external network perimeter must be conducted at least quarterly, with findings remediated according to risk-based timelines.</p><h4>5.7 Logging, Monitoring, and Incident Response</h4><p>All network security devices (firewalls, IPS, VPN concentrators) must generate audit logs. Logs must record all allowed and denied traffic, configuration changes, and authentication events. These logs must be forwarded in real-time to a centralized Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system and retained for a minimum of 90 days. The Security Operations Center (SOC) shall monitor these logs 24/7/365 for indicators of compromise.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""6. Compliance and Legal Requirements\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Network security controls shall be designed and operated to meet the requirements of relevant frameworks including ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (A.8.20, A.8.21, A.8.22, A.8.23, A.8.24, A.8.25, A.8.26), SOC 2 Trust Services Criteria (Security, Availability), and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (Protect, Detect). This policy also supports compliance with data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR, CCPA) by enforcing controls for the secure transmission of personal data. Any deviation from this policy for legal or investigative purposes requires written authorization from the CISO and General Counsel.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""7. Roles and Responsibilities\"",\""content\"":\""<p><strong>Chief Information Security Officer (CISO):</strong> Ultimately responsible for the development, approval, and overall governance of this policy.<br/><strong>Network Engineering Team:</strong> Responsible for the secure design, implementation, and maintenance of the network infrastructure in accordance with this policy.<br/><strong>Security Operations Center (SOC):</strong> Responsible for the 24/7 monitoring of network security events, incident triage, and initial response.<br/><strong>All Personnel:</strong> Responsible for adhering to this policy, reporting suspected security incidents or policy violations, and using corporate network resources only for authorized purposes.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""8. Enforcement\"",\""content\"":\""<p>Compliance with this policy is mandatory. Violations may result in disciplinary action, up to and including termination of employment or contract, and may lead to civil or criminal liability. The IT Department and SOC shall implement automated technical controls (e.g., NAC, firewall rules) to enforce policy requirements. Regular audits and automated compliance scans will be conducted to verify adherence. Any exception to this policy requires a formal, documented risk acceptance approved by the CISO.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true},{\""title\"":\""9. Review and Revision\"",\""content\"":\""<p>This Network Security Policy shall be reviewed by the CISO at least annually, or following any significant security incident or major change in the network architecture or threat landscape. Revisions must be approved by the CISO and communicated to all relevant stakeholders. The policy version and revision history shall be maintained in a central document repository.</p>\"",\""defaultEnabled\"":true}]""",2025-12-20 21:17:22.508899,"""[\""ISO 27001\"",\""SOC 2\"",\""NIST CSF\""]""",NULL,t,NULL,2026-02-20 06:08:47.174,"""[{\""id\"":\""q_1771455702503_ji8s43ypg\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization handle, process, or store any regulated data types subject to specific compliance frameworks (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR, CCPA, PCI-DSS)?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455702503_ntfwtvguk\"",\""question\"":\""What is the primary method for remote employee access to internal network resources?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[\""VPN only\"",\""Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA)\"",\""Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (VDI)\"",\""Direct access with multi-factor authentication\"",\""Other\""],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455702503_qtc3q2cte\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization operate in a hybrid or multi-cloud environment requiring network segmentation between on-premises and cloud resources?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455727570_r94clw8lt\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization utilize Internet of Things (IoT) devices or operational technology (OT) networks that require integration with your corporate IT network?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455727570_opg4upzx9\"",\""question\"":\""What is the primary method for securing wireless network access for employees and guests?\"",\""type\"":\""select\"",\""options\"":[\""WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X\"",\""WPA2/WPA3-Personal with PSK\"",\""Captive portal with separate VLAN\"",\""Certificate-based authentication\"",\""Other\""],\""defaultValue\"":\""\""},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455727570_b1vngcr2t\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization conduct regular penetration testing or vulnerability assessments of your network infrastructure?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455752415_57wtsnyo1\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization use network-connected industrial control systems (ICS) or supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) systems?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455752415_5s18vopnc\"",\""question\"":\""What is the primary method for network traffic monitoring and intrusion detection?\"",\""type\"":\""select\"",\""options\"":[\""Commercial SIEM/SOAR platform\"",\""Open-source tools (e.g., Wazuh, Suricata)\"",\""Managed Security Service Provider (MSSP)\"",\""Basic firewall logging only\"",\""None\""],\""defaultValue\"":\""\""},{\""id\"":\""q_1771455752415_lc3rqxd91\"",\""question\"":\""Does your organization allow employees to connect personal devices (BYOD) to the corporate network?\"",\""type\"":\""boolean\"",\""options\"":[],\""defaultValue\"":false}]"""
